INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (7, 1, 'luv capabilities', 900, 'Part 1 of `plan 1: masterplan`: find out what luv can carry for a solar-system flight game, by building luv and running its programs, tests and screenshot paths, not only by reading. The findings ground `plan 1: masterplan` parts 2 to 5 (skeleton, architecture, design, story), so every claim in the deliverables says how it was checked (ran, read, or assumed).

## Ground rules
- `../luv` is read-only: build and run it from its flake (`nix build`/`nix run` on `github:mbrock/luv` or `path:../luv`), or with build outputs in a temp dir; never edit or commit there, and leave no new files that `git -C ../luv status` would show (its `result` symlink already exists; leave it).
- Note the luv revision examined (`git -C ../luv rev-parse HEAD`) at the top of `docs/luv-capabilities.md`; part 2 pins the same revision in `flake.lock`.
- Every window opened is closed (`./sly stop-playing`, or `kill $!` by PID); no image or game left running; never kill by name pattern.
- Docs style per `AGENTS.md`: intro paragraph, then concise bullets.
- Headless/no-display findings matter: record what works without a display (agents run in such sessions).

## Reading list (in `../luv`)
- `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `Makefile`, `flake.nix` (packages, apps, devShells, `applicationPackage`, the luv nix cache), `luv.asd`, `luft.asd`, `luvcraft.asd` (system names and dependencies).
- `wiki/`: `index.org`, `gpu-architecture.org`, `metal-backend.org`, `mathematical-shaders.org`, `sky-and-light.org`, `temporal-antialiasing.org`, `frame-performance.org`, `physics-and-simd.org`, `sb-simd.org`, `quantities-and-measurement.org`, `field-notes-measures.org`, `world-terminal.org`, `slug-bezier.org` (text), `technical-debt.org`, `luft-star-figures.org` (stars), `application-workbench.org`.
- Code: `hal/` (`gpu.lisp`, `canvas.lisp`, `application-lifecycle.lisp`, `application-capture.lisp`, `capture-specification.lisp`, `png.lisp`, `sdl/`, `vulkan/`, `metal/`, `shader/`), `luv/`, `terminal/`, `arithmetic/`, `luft/star-*.lisp`, `luvcraft/` (game loop, input, physics, sky), `scripts/` (`test.lisp`, `captures.lisp`, `readme-screenshots.lisp`, `build-application`), `sly`, `sly-init.lisp`.

## Run (each run noted in the doc with command, time, outcome)
- `nix run --accept-flake-config github:mbrock/luv` (Luvcraft) and `#luft`: does a window open here, how fast from the cache, what input it takes; close it.
- `nix build` of `#luvcraft`, `#luft`, `#luv-shaderc`; `luv-shaderc` on `hal/shaderc/examples/sky.lisp` into a temp dir.
- The test suite: `make test` / `make test-suite` / `make luft-test` inside `nix develop ../luv`, outputs kept out of `../luv` where possible; if a target can only write into `../luv/build` (ignored build output), note it.
- The live image: `./sly start`, `./sly play luft`, `./sly status`, `./sly screenshot /tmp/…png` (read the PNG), `./sly eval`, redefining a shader or function while playing, `./sly stop-playing`, stopping the image by PID. Find out whether `./sly` can serve a different checkout (this repo) or how part 2 should copy the setup.
- Screenshots and headless: `scripts/captures`, `hal/application-capture.lisp`: can a frame be rendered offscreen to PNG without a window or display (for tests and the screenshot command)?
- A tiny probe program loaded from a temp dir against luv''s ASDF systems: open a window, draw a sphere mesh with a Lisp shader, take a screenshot, close. This answers which systems a game loads (`luv`, `luv/shader`, `luv/terminal`, `luv/arithmetic`, …) and seeds part 2 (walking skeleton); keep its source in the doc or an appendix, not as repo code.

## Capabilities to report
- Rendering: meshes and vertex formats, Lisp shaders (stages, uniforms, textures, compute), lighting, shadows, sky/sun, bloom, fog, TAA, in-world text (Slug glyphs), terminal screens (Ghostty) and whether they can show game-generated text (instrument panels) without a shell.
- Windowing and input: SDL3, keyboard, mouse (relative mode), gamepad/joystick (SDL3 has it; does luv expose it?), resizing, fullscreen.
- Live image and dev loop: `./sly`, Swash, failure parking and `./sly resume`.
- Screenshots and headless runs, tests (Parachute), how luv runs its own tests.
- Physics and SIMD: what luvcraft''s physics is (voxel collision, not orbital), `sb-simd`, use of `double-float`; the quantities/units library and whether it fits SI orbital mechanics.
- Packaging: `applicationPackage` in luv''s flake, whether this repo can reuse it through the luv flake input (`lib`, overlays) or must copy it; the binary cache (`luv.swa.sh`).
- Platforms: Linux x86-64/ARM64 (Vulkan), macOS Apple Silicon (Metal 4); what was tested on this machine vs. only read.
- Performance: frame budget numbers from `frame-performance.org` or measured.

## Gaps to assess, each with a workaround in this repo
- Depth range across 10^12 m: reversed-Z with float depth, logarithmic depth, or layered passes (far bodies, near bodies, cockpit); check what depth format and clear luv uses and whether a game can set them.
- Double-precision positions: physics in `double-float` in Lisp, camera-relative single-float positions sent to the GPU.
- Spheres and planet surfaces: UV/icosphere generation, an Earth shader (texture vs. procedural continents, clouds, atmosphere rim), texture loading (which image formats luv loads; licences of public Earth textures, e.g. NASA Blue Marble).
- Starfield: luft''s star tables, or a catalogue (e.g. HYG, Yale BSC; licence noted) drawn as points or sprites.
- Sun glare and brightness: bloom from luvcraft''s sky, a sun disc, exposure across Earth-lit and deep-space scenes.
- Orbit lines and markers: does luv draw lines? otherwise thin quads or ribbons.
- HUD/2D overlay: screen-space text and shapes over the 3D view, vs. everything drawn on in-world cockpit screens.
- Audio: luv''s main shell has none (`sdl3-mixer` only in a side shell); SDL3 audio via cl-sdl3, or cut.
- Save files: luvcraft''s save path; a small s-expression save under `$XDG_DATA_HOME`.
- Anything else found (e.g. fixed physics step decoupled from frame rate, instancing many objects).

## Deliverables
- `docs/luv-capabilities.md`: luv revision, one section per capability area (what it offers, how it was checked, limits), the gaps each with its workaround, and a short "what this means for luv-in-space" summary that parts 2 to 4 of `plan 1: masterplan` build on.
- `docs/luv-wishlist.md`: what would better live in luv than here (e.g. a reversed-Z option, double-precision camera helpers, sphere meshes, line rendering, audio), each with why.
- Decisions recorded with `pm decision record`, at least: which luv ASDF systems the game depends on; how the game consumes luv (flake input, which package or lib output); the depth strategy and the precision approach as findings-backed recommendations (part 3, architecture, may supersede them); which platforms are targeted and tested.
- Follow-up tasks (`pm task add`) for gaps needing work outside this part, in the plan they belong to.

## Suggested split
- Read and summarise luv''s docs and systems (no running).
- Build and run luv''s programs, tests, live image and screenshots; record outcomes.
- Probe program: a sphere in a window plus a screenshot, from a temp dir against luv.
- Assess the gaps with workarounds; write `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`; record the decisions.', '2026-10-14T16:13:00Z', 8);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (8, 1, 'walking skeleton', 800, 'Part 2 of `plan 1: masterplan`: an ASDF system in this repo, built against luv pinned as a flake input, that opens a window and draws something recognisable, with a headless test and a screenshot command an agent can use, and `AGENTS.md` "Commands" filled in. It runs after `plan 7: luv capabilities` (priority 900) and builds on its findings and decisions: which luv ASDF systems to load, how a luv program is built and started (`applicationPackage`, `scripts/build-application`), how screenshots and headless runs work (`hal/application-capture.lisp`, `./sly screenshot`), and its probe program (sphere in a window plus a screenshot), which seeds this skeleton.

## Ground rules
- Read `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and the decisions of `plan 7: luv capabilities` first (`pm decision list --answered --grep luv --grep depth --grep platform`); they are binding unless superseded.
- `../luv` is read-only reference; the build uses luv from the flake input only, at the revision noted in `docs/luv-capabilities.md`.
- Every window opened is closed (by PID or `./sly stop-playing`); no process left running.
- Out of scope: game features. The scene is a placeholder, marked `PLACEHOLDER(<task id>)` in code and on screen.
- Before each commit the build and the test pass.

## Flake
- `flake.nix`: add `luv` as an input (`github:mbrock/luv`, pinned in `flake.lock`; `nixpkgs.follows` only if luv tolerates it, else keep luv''s nixpkgs for its cache); `nixConfig` with luv''s binary cache (`luv.swa.sh`) as luv''s flake does.
- `packages.default` (`luv-in-space`): built with luv''s `applicationPackage` if the input exposes it (`lib` or similar), else a copy adapted here, noted in `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.
- `apps.default`: starts the game (`nix run` from the repo root).
- `devShells.default`: luv''s dev shell environment (SBCL with luv''s Lisp deps, Vulkan/SDL3 libraries) plus `pm` and `git`; `direnv` (`.envrc` with `use flake`) keeps working.
- `checks.<system>.default`: the headless test, if it can run inside the nix sandbox (no GPU); otherwise the test is a dev-shell command and the reason is noted.
- Platforms: those decided in `plan 7: luv capabilities`; tested on this machine (Linux x86-64), others only evaluated (`nix flake check --no-build` or `nix eval`).

## ASDF system
- `luv-in-space.asd` at the repo root: system `luv-in-space` (sources under `src/`), `luv-in-space/tests` (under `tests/`, Parachute as luv uses it).
- `src/package.lisp`, `src/main.lisp` (entry point: open a window, run the frame loop, quit on Escape or window close), `src/scene.lisp` (the placeholder scene).
- The scene: a lit sphere (Earth placeholder, procedural colour, no texture) against a dark background with a few points or a sun direction; a visible `PLACEHOLDER(<task id>)` label (in-world text or terminal screen, whatever `plan 7: luv capabilities` found cheapest), the task id being the task that builds the scene.
- Shaders written in Lisp the way luv''s programs do; nothing that pre-empts the depth and precision decisions of part 3 (architecture), beyond what the probe already did.

## Test
- A headless test command (e.g. `make test` or `scripts/test`, run inside the dev shell): loads `luv-in-space/tests` and runs it with a non-zero exit on failure.
- Tests: the system loads; a pure function (e.g. sphere mesh generation: vertex count, unit radius) is correct; if luv supports offscreen rendering without a display, one frame renders to a PNG with the expected size and non-background pixels at the sphere''s centre.

## Screenshot
- A screenshot command (e.g. `scripts/screenshot out.png`): starts the game, renders some frames, writes a PNG, exits; works for an agent (from a terminal, without a human). Offscreen if luv allows it, else in a window that it closes itself.
- Optional `--frames N` or a time argument; the default shows the scene.

## Live image
- If `./sly` (luv''s Slynk-based live image) can load this repo''s system (from `../luv` or via the flake''s dev shell), a `./sly`-like wrapper here or documented steps: start the image, load `luv-in-space`, play, screenshot, redefine a function while playing, stop. If it can''t be reused, say why in `AGENTS.md` and add a follow-up task.

## AGENTS.md "Commands"
- Fill in: dev shell, build (`nix build`), run (`nix run`), test, screenshot, live image; each command one bullet.
- Verify from a clean checkout: `git clone` into a temp dir, run each command there, delete the clone; note the time a cold build takes.
- Record a decision for the lasting choices (`pm decision record`): the layout of systems and directories, the test and screenshot commands.

## Suggested split
- Flake: luv input, dev shell, package and `apps.default` building an empty system that opens a window.
- The placeholder scene with its `PLACEHOLDER` label.
- Headless test command and the screenshot command.
- Live-image dev loop (or the reason it''s skipped).
- `AGENTS.md` "Commands", verified from a clean clone; decisions recorded.', '2026-10-14T16:13:35Z', 9);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (9, 1, 'architecture', 700, 'Part 3 of `plan 1: masterplan`: write `docs/architecture.md`, the technical ground every later milestone builds on. It runs after `plan 7: luv capabilities` and `plan 8: walking skeleton` and rests on `docs/luv-capabilities.md`, the skeleton in `src/`, `tests/`, `scripts/`, `flake.nix`, and the decisions they recorded. It writes down how the game is built on luv and the technical choices that are still open (units, frames, integrator, time warp, module layout, testing), and records those choices as decisions so the design (`task 11: write plan: game design`) and `milestone 2: Flight core: Newtonian flight in near-Earth orbit` can implement from it.

## Ground rules
- Read first: `docs/luv-capabilities.md` (especially "Gaps and workarounds" and "What this means for luv-in-space"), `docs/luv-wishlist.md`, `AGENTS.md`, the skeleton''s code, and `pm decision list --answered`.
- Binding decisions, cited in the document, not reopened: `decision 1: How the game consumes luv`, `decision 2: Screenshots and GPU test: offscreen capture on a hidden canvas, headless on lavapipe`, `decision 3: luv ASDF systems the game depends on`, `decision 4: Depth strategy`, `decision 5: Precision of positions`, `decision 6: Target platforms`, `decision 7: Placeholder labels: dot-matrix overlay quads`, `decision 8: Live image: ./sly wraps luv''s client from the flake input`, `decision 9: Layout of systems and directories`, `decision 10: Test and screenshot commands`, `decision 11: Render pipelines are luv live shader pipelines`.
- The document describes; it does not implement. No game code beyond what a check needs (e.g. a throwaway numeric experiment in `/tmp` to pick an integrator step is fine; it is not committed).
- Where a choice depends on the game design (`docs/design.md`, written later by the plan from `task 11: write plan: game design`), state the architecture''s default and its constraint on the design (e.g. "thrust only below warp x10"), so the design picks within it.
- Style per `AGENTS.md`: intro paragraph under each top-level heading, then concise bullets; units in SI; cite decisions by id and subject.
- Out of scope: game features; the design and story documents.

## docs/architecture.md: contents
### Dependency on luv
- Flake input pinned in `flake.lock`, dev shell and package from `luv.lib.environmentFor`, the copied application recipe, binary cache, launcher without `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` (`decision 1`).
- ASDF systems: `luv-in-space` depends on `luv` only; tests add `luv/test-support` and Parachute; how a new luv system becomes a dependency (recorded decision first, `decision 3`); candidates already known: `luv/production` for a trajectory-prediction worker, `luv/arithmetic` quantities at boundaries.
- What the game builds on from luv: HAL (devices, pipelines, buffers, textures), the shader language with live shader pipelines (`decision 11`), the SDL3 canvas and its events, `capture-application-screenshot`, `audio-sink`; what it writes itself (camera, projection, impostors, stars, ribbons, text/screens, HDR, physics, audio mixer, saves), each pointing to its section in `docs/luv-capabilities.md`.
- Dev loop: `./sly` live image (start, play, eval to redefine functions and shaders while playing, screenshot, stop), `scripts/test`, `scripts/screenshot`, `nix build`/`nix run`, `nix flake check`; what to keep live-redefinable (no closures captured over functions the loop calls; state in the `game` object).
- luv wishlist: how a gap moves to `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

### Module layout
- Target layout of `src/` as files and packages, extending the skeleton (`package`, `shaders`, `scene`, `main`, `capture`) per `decision 9`; one line each on purpose and dependencies, in load order, e.g.:
  - math (double-float vec3/quaternion on the CPU, single-float conversion at the GPU boundary), units/constants (G·M of bodies, radii, J2000 epoch);
  - bodies and ephemerides (on-rails positions of Sun, Earth, Moon, planets as functions of time);
  - physics (state, gravity, integrator, Kepler propagation, orbital elements), ship (rigid body, mass, thrusters, propellant), simulation clock and time warp;
  - rendering: camera and projection, sphere impostors, stars, ribbons/markers, meshes (ship, asteroids), HDR and tone map, the 2D/text pass for instruments;
  - cockpit and instruments, autopilot (orbit targeting, presets), input mapping, audio, story/quests, save/load, main loop, capture;
  - tests mirroring the modules (`tests/<module>.lisp`).
- Package split: one game package plus `luv-in-space.shaders` as now, or more; decide and say why.
- Which milestone first creates each module (2 to 6), consistent with the masterplan''s milestone list.
- Assets: where textures (Blue Marble) and the star table (HYG ≤ 6.5) live, how they are fetched or committed (size limits, Nix fetch vs. git), how attribution and the CC BY-SA table are kept (from `docs/luv-capabilities.md`).

### Units and coordinates
- Unit of length: metre (SI throughout: m, s, kg, N, m/s); state why not km or AU (precision of double over 10^12 m: ~1e-4 m at 1 AU, ample); constants as μ = GM (m^3/s^2) from a named source (e.g. IAU/JPL values).
- Time: simulation time as double seconds since an epoch (e.g. J2000 TDB, or a game-start epoch with a fixed calendar date); resolution of double seconds over decades of game time.
- Frames: the frame of integration (heliocentric inertial, ecliptic J2000? or the dominant body''s inertial frame with re-centring on sphere-of-influence change); planet-centred inertial frames for orbital elements and instruments; body-fixed rotating frame for Earth''s surface (crash detection, ground track, the texture''s longitude); ship-local body frame (x forward, y, z) for thrusters and cockpit; the camera frame. Give the transforms between them and where each is used.
- Rendering: camera-relative positions computed in double, then single float (`decision 5`); the projection function in one place (reversed-Z, infinite far, near 0.1 m, `decision 4`), including the Vulkan clip-y flip; cockpit in the same pass; stars at infinity first.
- Floating-origin pitfalls to name: subtracting large positions before converting, cancelling terms computed in double on the CPU (ray-sphere `h(2r+h)`), orbit-line vertices made relative to the camera each frame.

### Time and integration
- The clock: the frame adds `elapsed x warp` to a double accumulator and runs fixed steps, capped per frame (sim slows rather than stalls); render the latest state or interpolate.
- The integrator for the ship: choose and justify (e.g. symplectic velocity Verlet/leapfrog at a fixed step for coasting and thrust, or RK4/RK45 with step control); step size for LEO (orbital period ~5,500 s) with the error it gives; thrust and attitude (quaternion, angular velocity, torques from RCS) integrated in the same step.
- Gravity model: point masses (Earth, Moon, Sun as the design needs); patched conics vs. n-body for the ship: choose (e.g. n-body with on-rails bodies, the dominant body''s frame for precision; patched conics only for prediction and the autopilot); Earth J2 in or out, with the reason.
- On rails: planets and moons from ephemerides (choose: Keplerian elements with secular rates, e.g. JPL "Approximate Positions of the Planets", vs. a truncated series; the Moon''s model); the ship on rails (Kepler propagation from elements) while coasting at high warp in one body''s field.
- Time warp: levels (e.g. x1 to x100,000 or more), when each is allowed (no thrust above a level; altitude limits near a body; drop out of warp before an SOI change, periapsis below the surface, a scheduled burn); switching between integrated and on-rails motion without a jump (state → elements → state round-trip).
- Collisions: crash detection against body spheres (and terrain height if ever), done per step with a swept test so high warp can''t tunnel; the Sun''s heat as a function of distance (the rule itself is the design''s).
- Trajectory prediction for the map, instruments and the autopilot: where it runs (frame thread with a budget, or a worker via `luv/production`, then a recorded dependency), its horizon and resolution.
- Determinism: same inputs and steps give the same trajectory (for tests and scripted runs).

### Testing
- Headless physics tests as `milestone 2` requires: energy and angular-momentum drift bounds for a circular and an eccentric Earth orbit over N orbits at each warp level and in rails mode; set concrete bounds (e.g. relative energy drift < 1e-6 per 100 orbits; state the numbers the integrator choice makes reachable) and how they''re measured.
- Other tests: Kepler round-trip (state ↔ elements), ephemeris spot checks against JPL Horizons values (a handful, committed as constants with the source), frame transforms, warp transitions without jumps, crash detection, save/load round-trip.
- Scripted runs: driving the game from a script (an input sequence or a control function) for a burn, an orbit raise, a deorbit, each ending with a screenshot (`scripts/screenshot` or `./sly screenshot`); how `milestone 2`''s "Done when" is demonstrated by an agent.
- GPU tests stay as `decision 2`/`decision 10`: one frame on a hidden canvas with lavapipe, image checks rather than pixel-exact comparisons.
- How agents build, run, test and screenshot: point to `AGENTS.md` "Commands" rather than duplicating.

## Decisions to record (`pm decision record`)
- Unit of length (and SI units throughout) and the time epoch.
- Coordinate frames: the frame of integration, re-centring rule, body-fixed frames.
- Integrator and step size; gravity model (n-body on rails vs. patched conics; J2).
- Ephemerides source and model for the bodies.
- Time-warp rules (levels, thrust limit, rails switching).
- Module layout and package split.
- Asset storage and attribution (if decided here rather than left to the design).
- Depth strategy and precision are already `decision 4` and `decision 5`: cite, don''t re-record; supersede only with a reason.

## Done when
- `docs/architecture.md` covers the sections above, agrees with `docs/luv-capabilities.md`, `AGENTS.md` and the skeleton, and cites the decisions it relies on.
- The new decisions are recorded; follow-ups the document reveals (e.g. a missing luv wishlist entry, a test the skeleton lacks) are added as tasks in the plan they belong to.

## Suggested split
- Dependency on luv, dev loop and module layout (incl. assets).
- Units, frames and time; decisions recorded.
- Integration, gravity model, ephemerides, time warp, collisions and prediction; a throwaway numeric check of the integrator''s drift at the chosen step; decisions recorded.
- Testing section with concrete drift bounds; final read-through against the other documents.', '2026-10-14T17:31:46Z', 10);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (10, 1, 'game design', 600, 'Part 4 of `plan 1: masterplan`: write `docs/design.md`, the game design written for implementers. It runs after `plan 9: architecture` and stays inside what `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/architecture.md` say luv and the architecture can carry. `milestone 2: Flight core: Newtonian flight in near-Earth orbit` to `milestone 6: Release: one command to play, a README for people` each implement "what `docs/design.md` assigns" to them, so the document''s scope list is their specification; `task 12: write plan: story` writes `docs/story.md` on top of it and may use only features the scope list puts in milestones 2 to 5.

## Ground rules
- Read first: `docs/luv-capabilities.md` ("Gaps and workarounds", "What this means for luv-in-space"), `docs/architecture.md` (all of it; "Time and integration" leaves choices to the design), `docs/luv-wishlist.md`, `AGENTS.md`, the milestones (`pm milestone show 2` … `6`) and `pm decision list --answered`.
- Binding, cited, not reopened: `decision 4: Depth strategy`, `decision 5: Precision of positions`, `decision 6: Target platforms`, `decision 7: Placeholder labels: dot-matrix overlay quads`, `decision 12: Module layout and package split`, `decision 13: Asset storage and attribution`, `decision 14: Units and time epoch`, `decision 15: Coordinate frames`, `decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock`, `decision 17: Gravity model`, `decision 18: Ephemerides`, `decision 19: Time-warp rules`, `decision 20: Trajectory prediction on the frame thread`, `decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts`. Where the architecture offers the design a choice within limits (thrust limit under warp, crash radii, SOI drop-out, burn lead time, the Sun''s rule, drag, audio''s milestone, Ghostty, gamepad), the design picks within them and says so; going beyond a limit needs a superseding decision with the reason.
- Realism over spectacle, scope over realism (`milestone 1`): a feature luv can''t carry, or that costs far more than it shows, is cut with the reason.
- Numbers in SI with display units in brackets where they help (km, km/s, t); every physical figure (masses, thrust, Isp, Δv, orbits) is computed, not guessed: state the formula or source next to it. A throwaway calculation in `/tmp` is fine; nothing of it is committed.
- Real data with sources named (title, publisher or URL, date read): orbital elements of stations or asteroids, engine figures, body data. Bodies'' μ and radii come from `docs/architecture.md` "Units" (`decision 14`), not new sources.
- Names of things in code and docs are consistent across the document (module names as in `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order").
- Style per `AGENTS.md`: intro paragraph under each top-level heading, then concise bullets; tables where they help (ship parts, places, scope list).
- The document describes; no game code. Out of scope: the story itself (`docs/story.md`), beyond what the design needs to name (e.g. that quests exist and how messages reach the cockpit).

## docs/design.md: contents
### The game in one page
- What the player does, minute to minute and hour to hour; the start (the ship in LEO, the date per `decision 14`, final choice with the story); what makes it a showcase of luv (live shaders, Lisp, a real Earth).
- Pillars (e.g. the ship is a tool, Newton is the game, the cockpit is home-made) used later to justify cuts.

### The ship(s)
- The player''s ship: a rugged DIY tug. Parts as a table: steel truss frame, life-support module (crew 1, maybe 2), propellant tanks, main engine(s), RCS thruster blocks, grapple/docking gear (if assigned), radiators, solar panels or other power, batteries; each with mass (kg), dimensions (m), position in ship body axes (FRD, `decision 15`).
- Propulsion: choose realistic, near-future technology (e.g. a storable or methalox chemical main engine, cold gas or hydrazine RCS, maybe a separate electric drive for slow tug work): thrust (N), Isp (s), propellant and its density, tank volumes; sourced from real engines of the class.
- Mass budget and Δv: dry mass, propellant, wet mass, Δv by the rocket equation (Δv = Isp g₀ ln(m₀/m₁)) empty and with a towed load (e.g. a 50 t asteroid); thrust-to-weight and burn times for typical burns (LEO raise, TLI, capture); check against the Δv each place in "The places" needs, so every destination in scope is reachable (with refuelling where needed) and the story can rely on it.
- Inertia: the principal moments (kg m²) from the parts (`decision 16` integrates them), how they change with propellant; RCS torques and translation forces; resulting rotation and translation accelerations, so manual control feels right.
- Refuelling and resupply: where (depots in "The places"), how (docked and a key, or as designed), cost if any (an economy or none, say which).
- Damage: what can be damaged (by collision speed with stations/asteroids, by heat near the Sun, by over-stress while towing?), its effects (lost thruster, leak, dead screen), repair (at a station, or none); keep it minimal and say what is cut.
- Other ships (traffic, the story''s characters): which kinds exist, built from the same parts; whether they move on rails only (`decision 19`).
- How they look: built from simple generated meshes (`meshes.lisp`: tubes, boxes, cylinders, spheres), materials (bare steel, foil, painted markings), lights; recognisable silhouettes, function obvious at a glance; a sketch per ship as a parts list with dimensions.

### Flight
- Controls: a complete key map (keyboard and mouse, luv''s canvas events) for attitude (pitch, yaw, roll), translation (RCS), throttle and engine cut, attitude holds (prograde, retrograde, normal, anti-normal, radial in/out, target, inertial), warp up/down, views, map, autopilot, pause, quit; mouse for looking around in the cockpit and pressing switches; gamepad: assign a milestone or cut (`docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Gamepad and joystick").
- Control modes: direct RCS (rate) vs. rate damping (SAS-like stabiliser, a realistic flight computer function), holds; what the player feels at x1 and x4 (fine step).
- Views: cockpit (eye point, head look), outside chase/orbit camera, map/orbit view (Earth or the current body with orbit lines, the prediction ribbon, targets, at which zoom); what each shows and when it is available; how the camera handles 10^12 m (zoom range, focus body).
- Instruments: the list (altitude, speed, apoapsis/periapsis, inclination, period, attitude ball or equivalent, propellant and Δv remaining, throttle, warp actual/nominal, clock and date, target distance and closing speed, heat, alarms); which milestone each comes in (the minimal readout of `milestone 2`, the full set of `milestone 3`).
- Time warp: the levels the game offers (from `decision 19`), the thrust limit under warp (≤ x1,000), SOI drop-out (stop or continue), burn lead time (default 60 s), how warp is shown and refused.
- The autopilot: what it can do per milestone: circularise, raise/lower apoapsis or periapsis, change plane, rendezvous with a point of interest and stop at a set range (`milestone 3`), transfers (TLI, Hohmann to an asteroid or planet, capture; `milestone 4`); how the player picks a preset, sees the planned burns (on the map and the screens), watches and interrupts them; safety rules (never plans a periapsis below the crash radius; aborts on low propellant); it writes `controls` like the player (`milestone 3` constraint: same physics, same fuel).
- Close-range manoeuvring: docking or grappling: which one, how it''s flown (RCS translation, a docking camera/range finder), tolerances (closing speed, alignment), what happens on contact; assigned to a milestone or cut.
- Towing: attaching to an asteroid or cargo, the combined mass and inertia, how thrust is applied to the stack.
- Dying and what follows: crash (crash spheres per body; Earth''s 100 km entry interface default or another value; contact speed rule near stations and asteroids), the Sun (pick the rule: hull temperature from `docs/architecture.md` "Collisions and the Sun" with warning and death thresholds, and the distance that gives in AU for the player''s ship), running out of life support if any; what the player sees (crash view, a message) and what follows (restart from the last checkpoint/save, or a fresh start); interaction with quests and saves (`milestone 5`).
- Saving: when (checkpoints at quest steps, docking, a key?), what (`saves.lisp` per `docs/architecture.md`); the milestone.
- Sound: what is heard (only what travels through the hull: engine rumble, RCS puffs, valves, alarms, radio static), synthesised per `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Audio"; assign a milestone (architecture default 6) or cut.

### The cockpit
- Its DIY look: a welded frame inside the life-support module, mismatched screens and gauges bolted on, taped labels, exposed cable runs, a window and its frame; a layout drawing as a list of panels with positions (m, ship body axes) relative to the eye point, so the cockpit mesh can be built from it.
- Each instrument: what it shows, how it is drawn (an in-world screen rendered to a texture with Slug text, `text.lisp`; a needle gauge as a mesh; a lamp; a dot-matrix display; a small orbit plot on a screen), its update rate, and its milestone.
- Ghostty terminal screen: keep (e.g. a ship computer''s text console or the radio log, with what it costs per `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "HUD and instruments") or cut, with the reason; if kept, a decision extending `decision 3: luv ASDF systems the game depends on` is needed later.
- Interaction: switches pressed with the mouse or keys; whether the player can fly from the cockpit alone (the `milestone 3` outcome) and what the outside view adds.
- Readability rules: minimum text size at 1280x800, contrast in sunlight and shadow, colour use.
- How messages and radio reach the player (screens, a printer strip, a lamp; the story uses these).

### The places
- Earth: the Blue Marble (`decision 13`), atmosphere rim, night side (Black Marble or cut), clouds (cut or not), what can be seen from LEO; atmospheric drag: cut or an exponential atmosphere as a force (`decision 17` lists it as the design''s option), with the reason.
- Earth-orbit points of interest (`milestone 3` presets): each with name, purpose (station, propellant depot, debris field, a captured asteroid being moved, a satellite to service), orbit (a, e, i, Ω, ω or altitude/inclination, epoch; real where a real object exists, e.g. the ISS''s orbit from a named TLE source and date, otherwise plausible and stated as invented), Δv from the start orbit, and how it looks; 4 to 6 places so the autopilot test of `milestone 3` stays small.
- The Moon: texture source and licence (e.g. NASA/USGS LRO mosaic; checked per `decision 13`), lunar orbits of interest, landing: in or cut (the ship design decides; a tug without landing gear says cut), Δv for TLI, LOI.
- The asteroid belt and near-Earth asteroids: which real objects (e.g. a small NEA with known elements from JPL SBDB, named with designation and the date read), how they are shown (instanced meshes near, markers far, the belt not as a dense field), what work the ship does there (moving a small asteroid); Δv and travel times by Hohmann for the ship.
- Planets in scope: choose (e.g. Venus and Mars as flyby/transfer destinations, outer planets shown on the map only, or as designed) with travel times and Δv that the ship can do; their look (flat albedo impostors or textures with sources).
- The Sun: its look (impostor, bloom, glare per "Sun glare and brightness"), the danger zone in AU from the chosen heat rule, warnings.
- Real orbital data: list sources and the date read; game time stays before 2050-01-01 (`decision 18`).

### Scope
- A prioritised feature list as one table: feature, priority (must/should/could), milestone (2 flight core, 3 cockpit and autopilot, 4 the solar system, 5 story and quests, 6 release) or "cut", reason; covering every feature named above and in `milestone 1`''s developer ideas (rugged ships, low-tech weapons, autopilot presets, Moon, belt, planets, Sun death, DIY cockpit, story), plus title screen, intro, controls help, credits screen (`decision 13`), README, performance target, saves, audio, gamepad.
- Weapons: low-tech weapons only if the story needs them; decide in or cut here (`docs/story.md` follows).
- Consistent with the milestones'' Outcomes and Done-whens (e.g. `milestone 2` needs the outside view, the minimal readout and crash into Earth; `milestone 4` needs Sun death tested; `milestone 5` needs saves); where a milestone''s text and the design disagree, the design wins inside the milestone''s intent, and a real contradiction is a `pm decision add`.
- What `docs/story.md` may use, by milestone: a short list for `task 12: write plan: story`.

## Decisions to record (`pm decision record`)
- Ship and propulsion: the player''s ship (parts, masses), main engine and RCS technology with Isp and thrust, propellant, Δv budget.
- How death works: crash rules and radii, the Sun''s heat rule and thresholds, what follows (restart/checkpoint).
- Places in scope: Earth-orbit points of interest, the Moon (landing in or out), which asteroids, which planets.
- Docking or grappling, towing; damage model (or none).
- Control scheme and views; warp thrust limit and drop-out choices within `decision 19`.
- Cockpit instrument technology (Slug screens, Ghostty in or out); audio''s milestone; gamepad''s milestone; drag in or out; weapons in or out.
- Supersede an architecture decision only with a reason, and then update `docs/architecture.md` in the same task.

## Done when
- `docs/design.md` covers the sections above, agrees with `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/architecture.md`, and cites the decisions it relies on.
- Every feature is in the scope table with a milestone 2 to 6 or cut with a reason; every destination in scope has its Δv checked against the ship''s.
- The decisions above are recorded; follow-ups the document reveals (e.g. an asset whose licence must be checked, a wishlist item, an architecture section to adjust) are added as tasks in the plan they belong to.

## Suggested split
- The ship(s): parts, propulsion, masses, inertia, Δv, looks; decisions recorded.
- Flight: controls, views, instruments, warp choices, autopilot, docking/towing, death, saving, sound; decisions recorded.
- The cockpit: layout, instruments and how each is drawn, messages.
- The places: Earth, points of interest, Moon, asteroids, planets, Sun, with data and sources and Δv checked against the ship; decisions recorded.
- Scope table and read-through against `docs/luv-capabilities.md`, `docs/architecture.md` and the milestones; the list for the story.', '2026-10-14T17:57:23Z', 11);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (11, 1, 'story', 500, 'Part 5 of `plan 1: masterplan`: write `docs/story.md`, the setting, characters, premise and linear quest line of the prelude. It runs after `plan 10: game design` and builds on what `docs/design.md` "Scope", "What the story may use" puts in milestones 2 to 5. `milestone 5: Story and quests: the playable prelude` implements the document (`quests.lisp`, RADIO/JOB, static texts, saves, a headless play-through), and `milestone 6: Release: one command to play, a README for people` takes its short introduction from it, so it must be specific enough to implement and to test from.

## Ground rules
- Read first: `docs/design.md` (all of it, above all "The game in one page", "The places", "Messages and radio", "Dying and what follows", "Scope"), `docs/architecture.md` ("Files in load order": `quests.lisp`, `saves.lisp`; "Scripted runs"; "Rails"; "Re-entry of objects"), `AGENTS.md`, `pm milestone show 5` and `pm decision list --answered`.
- Binding, cited, not reopened: `decision 22: The player''s ship and its propulsion`, `decision 24: Grapple, not docking; towing in`, `decision 25: Damage model`, `decision 26: Refuelling and economy`, `decision 27: Other ships`, `decision 33: Grapple tolerances, contact and berthing`, `decision 34: How death works`, `decision 35: Saving and audio`, `decision 37: Message channels the story may use`, `decision 38: Start orbit: polar`, `decision 39: Places in scope`, `decision 41: Weapons: cut`, `decision 42: Scope: what each milestone builds`.
- Only features `docs/design.md` assigns to milestones 2 to 5; nothing cut (weapons, fights, landing, money, EVA, docking ports, voices, faces, reply choices, destinations beyond the Moon and the passing rock). A quest built on a "should" (the passing rock, story ships moving) says so and names what the quest line does if it is dropped (the quest is skipped or replaced, the line stays complete).
- Hard sci-fi, early 2040s, all technology known: no aliens, no conspiracy tech, no secret drives. Danger comes from the trade (a spinning derelict, a heavy load, a long transfer, the Sun) and people''s conflicts told by RADIO (`docs/design.md` "Weapons").
- Every quest flyable by the Mule: its Δv within the propellant the pilot can have at that point (6.65 km/s empty, 1.74 km/s with 50 t, 1.02 km/s with 100 t; refuelling only berthed at Fulmar Depot, Highport, Peary Depot), its duration within 180 person-days with restocking at Tern Station, Highport or Peary Depot; take the figures from `docs/design.md` "Δv for the standard legs", "The places", "Δv checked against the ship", don''t recompute them unless a quest needs a leg not listed (then state the formula).
- Names: the story may rename the places (working names, `decision 39`); if it does, the same task updates `docs/design.md` (and `docs/architecture.md` where named) in step and records the renaming.
- The document is the story and the quest specification; no game code. Style per `AGENTS.md`: intro paragraph under each top-level heading, then concise bullets; a table for the quest line.

## docs/story.md: contents
### Setting
- The world of the early 2040s as the game shows it: who works in near-Earth space (the polar-plane economy of `docs/design.md` "The places": Tern Station, Fulmar Depot, Highport and its water plant, Skua Yard, Peary Depot), who owns what, what a tug pilot''s life is like; plausible, small, sober.
- The start date (`*start-date*`, `decision 14: Units and time epoch`; default 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC, before 2050-01-01 by `decision 18: Ephemerides`): chosen so the quest line''s windows work (the Moon''s best month from Highport''s perigee, `docs/design.md` "The Moon"; the passing rock''s perigee date and designation, "Asteroids"); the start orbit''s phase if the story changes it (`docs/design.md` "The start").
### Characters
- The pilot (the player; no gender or face needed, nothing shown but text addressed to them), the ship''s name and its previous owner if any, the employer(s), the people at the places who speak by RADIO (each with a call sign, a role, a voice in text, where they are, so the light-time delay is right).
- Other ships the story names (from `decision 27`: Mule tugs in other liveries, the tanker, the crew capsule, the rock mover), which are scenery and which move between places on rails (milestone 5 "should").
- Static texts: the ship''s name above the window and up to three tape notes (exact wording, `decision 37`).
### Premise
- Why the pilot is in this orbit with this tug, what they want, what goes on around them, and where the prelude ends: an ending that opens the longer adventure (a hook, not a resolution) without needing anything cut.
- The short introduction for `milestone 6` (a few sentences, exact text, shown before the first flight).
### The quest line
- 5 to 10 quests in a fixed order that teach piloting one step at a time, from manual attitude and a small burn in the start orbit, through rendezvous, grapple, berthing and refuelling, the derelict K-17 pushed to re-entry by hand (`docs/design.md` "The derelict''s re-entry"), towing a Skua Yard rock, the trip to Highport, a lunar transfer to Peary Depot, to the passing rock if built and a close call with the Sun''s warning zone only if it fits the Δv and life support (otherwise the Sun stays a danger told about, not a destination).
- Per quest: title, giver (RADIO sender), goal, place(s), skills taught (which controls, holds, autopilot presets, instruments), the milestone that makes it playable (2 to 5), the propellant and time it needs from the expected state at its start, its steps (each a JOB card: title and up to three lines; which steps need Y), the RADIO messages with their exact text, sent at which trigger, and the completion condition per step.
- Completion conditions machine-checkable from the simulation state (`docs/architecture.md`: berthed at a fixture, grappled object id, orbit elements within tolerances, an object removed with its cause, a body''s sphere entered, time elapsed), so `quests.lisp` and the headless play-through of `milestone 5` can test them; no condition on what the player reads or looks at, except Y acknowledgements.
- Failure: death never fails a quest; its step is flown again from the last checkpoint (`decision 34`); say where each quest takes a checkpoint (at quest steps, `decision 35`). A step that can be made impossible (e.g. a rock pushed away, propellant spent far from a depot) says how the game recovers (the step''s objects reset on restart from its checkpoint, a tanker or the employer''s offer by RADIO), without new mechanics.
- No quest needs a feature cut or not yet built at its milestone; the autopilot never flies what `docs/design.md` says it refuses (the derelict''s push below 150 km is by hand).
### How the story is told
- Which channel carries what (RADIO, JOB, SHIP stays the game''s, static texts), the tone of the radio (terse, working people, numbers where pilots would say them), what the COMMS screen shows between quests, how light time shows at the Moon.
- What the player sees of other ships at their work (scenery at places, departures and arrivals on rails out of view).
### Names and terms
- One list of every name the story fixes (characters, call signs, ship, company, places, the passing rock''s designation), so code (`places.lisp`, `quests.lisp`) and the other docs use them consistently.

## Decisions to record (`pm decision record`)
- Setting and premise (the pilot''s situation, the employer, where the prelude ends).
- Names: the ship, the characters and call signs, the companies, the places (renamed or kept), the passing rock''s designation.
- The start date (and the start orbit''s phase if changed).
- The quest line: its quests in order, each with its milestone; what happens if a "should" it relies on is dropped.

## Done when
- `docs/story.md` has the sections above, 5 to 10 quests each with every per-quest item, and uses only features `docs/design.md` assigns to milestones 2 to 5.
- Every quest''s Δv and duration checked against the ship''s propellant, refuelling and 180 person-days, citing `docs/design.md`.
- `docs/design.md` and `docs/architecture.md` agree with the names and the start date the story fixes; the decisions above are recorded.
- Follow-ups the story reveals (e.g. a Δv leg the design lacks, a place detail, a flight script `milestone 5` needs) added as tasks in the plan they belong to.

## Suggested split
- Setting, characters, premise, the start date and the names; decisions recorded; `docs/design.md` and `docs/architecture.md` updated for renamings and the date.
- The quest line: quests, steps, completion conditions, Δv and time checks, checkpoints; decision recorded.
- How the story is told: every RADIO message and JOB card in exact text, the tape notes, the introduction; a read-through of `docs/story.md` against `docs/design.md` "What the story may use".', '2026-10-14T19:33:18Z', 12);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (12, 1, 'consistency check and sign-off', 400, 'Part 6 of `plan 1: masterplan`: make the Done when of `milestone 1: Scope and design: what luv can carry, what the game is` true. The four documents (`docs/luv-capabilities.md`, `docs/architecture.md`, `docs/design.md`, `docs/story.md`) and `docs/luv-wishlist.md` were written by many tasks, with later decisions (`decision 46` to `decision 59`) refining earlier text; this plan checks that they agree with each other, with the decision log and with the skeleton, that the scope is complete, and that the skeleton works from a clean checkout.

## Ground rules for every task
- Each check task fixes what it finds in the documents itself when the fix is small and the answer is already decided (a stale number, a name, a missing cross-reference); it cites the decision or section the fix follows.
- A fix that changes a lasting choice supersedes its decision (`pm decision record`, naming the decision it supersedes) and updates every document that cites it.
- A gap too large for the task (a missing design section, a figure that needs a new calculation) becomes a follow-up task in this plan, with a body another worker can act on.
- Work only a later milestone can do is not a task here: it goes into the `docs/design.md` "Scope" feature list or the section that milestone''s masterplan reads (the masterplans of milestones 2 to 6, tasks 2 to 6, are not yet written).
- Each task ends with a short list of what it checked and what it changed, in its done note.
- No game features; the skeleton is changed only to fix a command that fails.

## Task: figures, dates and names agree across the documents
- The ship''s figures in every document match `docs/design.md` "Parts", "Mass budget and Δv": 28 t of LH2/LOX, v_e 4,410 m/s, 6,650 m/s empty, 1,738 m/s with 50 t, 1,017 m/s with 100 t, 180 person-days, 10 kg/s transfer.
- Δv per leg in `docs/story.md` (quest table, per-quest "Propellant" lines, "Running checks") against `docs/design.md` "Δv for the standard legs", "Earth-orbit points of interest", "The Moon", "Δv checked against the ship", "Rendezvous: search, phasing and the approach" and `decision 55: Meeting 2040 BQ7 and the lunar arrival from the polar plane`. Known suspect: quest 7''s "1,378 + 1,353" in the quest table, while `docs/design.md` gives 1,273 m/s for Highport → Peary Depot (1,353 with margin); recompute or correct, and the "Running checks" propellant and the flight scripts'' `:dv-below` and `:propellant-above` that follow from it.
- Dates and times: start 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC, TLI 2040-01-20 06:01, Moon at Highport''s apogee 2040-01-26 02:36, BQ7 found 2040-01-19, meeting ~2040-03-10 23:00, perigee 2040-03-12 12:00, TANKER 3 leaving Fulmar 2040-03-06 06:00; the same in story, design, architecture''s flight scripts and `decision 50`, `decision 55`, `decision 58`.
- Names and ids: place names, call signs, ship names and the ids of `docs/story.md` "Names and terms" ("Proposed ids") used identically in `docs/design.md` (places, fixtures, rigid bodies) and `docs/architecture.md` (flight scripts, start states, removal causes `:burned-up`, `:delivered`).
- Units, frames and epoch: SI and `double-float` world positions, frames (heliocentric, planet-centred, ship-local FRD), the epoch and constants of `decision 14: Units and time epoch`; the same wherever the documents give formulas or figures.

## Task: the documents agree with the decision log
- `pm decision list --answered`: every answered decision is reflected in the document it concerns, and no document states the superseded part of a decision (e.g. `decision 50` partly superseded by `decision 55`; `decision 19` by `decision 43`).
- Decisions are cited by id and current subject; a subject in a citation that no longer matches is corrected.
- Decisions recorded by tasks but missing from the documents, and choices in the documents that no decision records although they are lasting (unit of length, frames, integrator, depth strategy, propulsion, death, planets in scope, names, premise), are listed; missing records are added with `pm decision record`.

## Task: luv capabilities, wishlist and architecture agree
- Every gap in `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Gaps and workarounds" has its workaround named in `docs/architecture.md` (the file that holds it in "Files in load order") and, where it would serve other luv programs, a line in `docs/luv-wishlist.md`; every wishlist line rests on a finding in `docs/luv-capabilities.md`.
- The luv systems, rendering paths and choices the architecture relies on (reversed-Z infinite far projection, camera-relative `single-float` rendering, Slug text, the overlay''s dot-matrix labels, render targets for the grapple camera, `sdl3-image`, the audio sink, no Ghostty per `decision 36`) are ones `docs/luv-capabilities.md` found working, or are marked as built in this repo.
- The performance budgets in `docs/design.md` "Performance target" and `docs/architecture.md` ("Integrator", "Trajectory prediction", the play-through''s run time) agree with the measurements in `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Performance".

## Task: every feature assigned, and milestones agree with the scope
- Every feature named in a section of `docs/design.md` (ship, flight, controls, views, instruments, autopilot, time warp, dying, saving, sound, cockpit, places) has a row in "Scope" "Feature list" with a priority and milestone 2 to 6, or is cut with its reason; rows without a section, and sections without a row, are fixed.
- `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" gives each file the milestone the feature list gives its features (e.g. `audio.lisp` and sound, `hazards.lisp` and the Sun''s heat, `saves.lisp` and saves, `title.lisp`), and "Scripts by milestone" matches the Done when rows.
- `pm milestone show 2` to `6`: each milestone''s Outcome and Done when is covered by "must" rows of that milestone, as "Each milestone against its Outcome and Done when" claims; a real contradiction with a milestone is a `pm decision add` (the only human decision this plan may raise).

## Task: the story uses only what is in scope by milestone 5
- Every place, object, skill, instrument, channel and action a quest of `docs/story.md` uses exists in the feature list in milestones 2 to 5 (or 2 to 4 for what makes a quest flyable, as its "Milestone" column says), and nothing cut (weapons, landing, EVA, docking ports, money, save slots, Ghostty, recorded voices) appears.
- Every "should" a quest relies on (the passing rock, the moving story ships) has its fallback in "If a ''should'' is dropped", and the architecture''s play-through covers that fallback.
- Mechanical checks, by a throwaway script (not committed): JOB card lines ≤ 26 characters, title plus step id fits; every RADIO message id unique, its trigger names an existing step or date, its sender a call sign of "Names and terms"; every step referenced by a flight script exists in the story.
- The quest play-through scripts in `docs/architecture.md` "The quest play-through" use only the actions, waits and start states defined in "Scripted runs", and their end states match each next quest''s "Expected state at start" in `docs/story.md`.

## Task: the skeleton from a clean checkout, and the skeleton against the documents
- `git clone` this repo into a temp dir; there, run every command of `AGENTS.md` "Commands" as written: `nix develop` (or `direnv allow`), `nix build`, `nix run` (stopped by PID after a few seconds), `scripts/test`, `scripts/screenshot out.png`, `./sly start`, `./sly status`, `./sly screenshot`, `./sly eval` of a shader, `./sly stop`, `nix flake check`; record the times and update those `AGENTS.md` gives where they are off. Read the screenshot. Remove the temp dir and leave no process running.
- The skeleton agrees with `docs/architecture.md`: flake input and pin, ASDF systems and packages (`luv-in-space`, `luv-in-space.shaders`, `luv-in-space.tests`), files `package`, `shaders`, `scene`, `main`, `capture`, `PLACEHOLDER(23)` in code and on screen, test and screenshot commands as "Dependency on luv" and "Dev loop" describe them.
- A failing command is fixed in this task if the fix is small; otherwise a follow-up task in this plan.

## Task: sign-off (last; priority 390)
- Runs after every other task of this plan and their follow-ups.
- Walks the Done when of `milestone 1`: the four documents exist, agree with each other (the check tasks'' notes), the feature list assigns every feature; the skeleton builds and runs from a clean checkout and its test passes (the clean-checkout task''s note, and `scripts/test` once more on the current tree).
- Style pass over all docs (`AGENTS.md`: intro paragraph under each top-level heading, concise bullets) and no stale text ("TODO", "TBD", references to tasks as still open that are done).
- What later milestones need from the documents and is still missing: a follow-up task here if the documents must say it, or a row or note in `docs/design.md` "Scope" for that milestone''s masterplan.
- When nothing is left, a short note in its done note that `milestone 1` is ready for the developer''s reading; closing the milestone is the developer''s.', '2026-10-14T21:18:28Z', 13);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (13, 2, 'Simulation foundations: math, units, bodies, orbits', 700, 'Part 1 of `plan 2: masterplan`: the simulation''s ground, four files that every later part builds on and that never call the HAL or SDL. `math.lisp` gives double vectors and quaternions and the one single-float boundary, `units.lisp` the constants, the calendar and display conversions, `bodies.lisp` the Sun, Earth and Moon with their rotations and ephemerides, `orbits.lisp` osculating elements, Kepler propagation, apsides and radius crossings. `plan 2`''s part 2 (ship, integrator, flight computer) and part 3 (clock, hazards, prediction) depend on it; nothing here integrates the ship.

## Sources and decisions
- Files and load order: `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" (`decision 12: Module layout and package split`): `math`, `units`, `bodies`, `orbits`, right after `package` and before `shaders` in `luv-in-space.asd`; tests `tests/math.lisp`, `tests/units.lisp`, `tests/bodies.lisp`, `tests/orbits.lisp` in the same order after `tests/package.lisp` in `luv-in-space/tests`.
- Units, time, frames: `docs/architecture.md` "Units", "Time", "Frames", "Floating-origin pitfalls"; `decision 5: Precision of positions`, `decision 14: Units and time epoch`, `decision 15: Coordinate frames`.
- Ephemerides and gravity constants: "Ephemerides", "Gravity model"; `decision 17: Gravity model`, `decision 18: Ephemerides`.
- Orbits: "Rails", "Hyperbolic rails", "Removal of objects" (the radius crossing); `decision 46: Rails across spheres, hyperbolas and re-entry` (milestone 4 builds the rails on what this part writes).
- Crash radii: `decision 34: How death works`; start orbit: `decision 38: Start orbit: polar`, `docs/design.md` "The start".
- Recorded for this plan: `decision 65: Vectors and quaternions in math.lisp`, `decision 66: Calendar dates on the 1/64 s grid`, `decision 67: Ephemeris cache and the Moon''s series frame`, `decision 68: Orbit elements: one struct for every conic`.
- Tests: `docs/architecture.md` "Testing" → "Rules for tests", "Rails, orbits and frames"; this part''s tests together stay under 0.5 s.

## Rules for every piece
- Pure functions on `double-float` (`decision 14`), typed and fast enough for the integrator (part 2 calls `bodies` four times per step); no mutable globals except the ephemeris cache (`decision 67`); tunables `defparameter`, constants `defconstant` only for numbers that never change (μ, radii) or `defparameter` where `./sly eval` should reach them.
- Every constant cites its source in a short comment beside it (`docs/architecture.md` "Units"); reference values in tests come from closed forms or committed sources, never from a previous run.
- Each piece adds its files to `luv-in-space.asd` (both systems), passes `scripts/test`, and updates `docs/architecture.md` where it refines it (the line given per piece). `AGENTS.md` "Layout": the first piece changes the `src/` bullet from "so far the walking skeleton" to the skeleton plus the simulation files built so far, citing "Files in load order"; later pieces keep it true.
- No luv gap is expected (luv''s `vec3` and `luv/arithmetic` are not used, `decision 65`); one met goes to `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

## Pieces, in the order they run

### 1. `math.lisp`: vectors, quaternions, the single-float boundary
- `decision 65`: vec3 `(simple-array double-float (3))`, quaternion `(simple-array double-float (4))` as (w x y z); constructors `vec3`, `quat`; `vec3-add`, `-sub`, `-scale`, `-dot`, `-cross`, `-norm`, `-normalize`, `-lerp`, `axpy`-style `vec3-add-scaled`, each with an in-place `!` twin (destination first); 3×3 matrices as `(simple-array double-float (9))` row-major with `mat3*vec3`, transpose, product, inverse of a symmetric matrix (for the inertia tensor in part 2), `mat3-from-quat`.
- Quaternions: product, conjugate, normalise in double, `quat-rotate` (v_inertial = q v_body q*), `quat-from-axis-angle`, `quat-slerp` (shortest arc, for render interpolation), `quat-derivative` q̇ = ½ q ⊗ (0, ω) with ω in body axes (`docs/architecture.md` "Integrator"), a frame from three axes (`quat-from-basis`, for holds and cameras).
- Boundary: `(relative-single p origin &optional out (index 0))`: subtract in double, then coerce, into a single-float array at `index` ("Floating-origin pitfalls").
- Tests `tests/math.lisp` (moved here from "Frame transforms", which names `tests/physics.lisp`): body → inertial → body round trip within 1e-15 for random unit quaternions from a fixed seed list; FRD signs: integrating `quat-derivative` (small fixed steps, or the exact exponential) with a positive pitch rate turns body +x towards inertial −z when q = 1, a positive yaw rate towards +y, a positive roll rate +y towards +z; `quat-slerp` at 0, ½ and 1; `relative-single` of two points 6.8×10^6 m from the origin and 1.25 m apart returns 1.25 exactly while coercing each first does not; symmetric inverse × matrix = identity within 1e-15.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Frame transforms" points the quaternion tests at `tests/math.lisp`; "Files in load order" for `math.lisp` names the layout of `decision 65`.
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval ''(quat-rotate (quat-from-axis-angle (vec3 0 1 0) 0.1d0) (vec3 1 0 0))'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` gives a vector with negative z.

### 2. `units.lisp`: constants, calendar, display conversions
- Constants with sources ("Units"): μ Sun 1.32712440041279419×10^20, Earth 3.98600435507×10^14, Moon 4.902800118×10^12 m³/s² (DE440, Park et al. 2021; piece 4 verifies the digits against the paper); radii Sun 6.957×10^8 m (IAU 2015 B3), Earth equatorial 6,378,136.6 m, Moon mean 1,737,400 m (IAU WGCCRE 2015); Earth''s J2 1.08263×10^-3 (EGM2008); g₀ 9.80665 m/s²; obliquity ε 84381.448″; the AU 149,597,870,700 m (IAU 2012 B2); TT − UTC 69.184 s; day 86,400 s, Julian century 36,525 days; the fine step 1/64 s as `+fine-step+`.
- Calendar (`decision 66`): `(utc->t year month day hour minute second)` by Fliegel–Van Flandern with integer days and seconds kept apart; `(utc->grid-t …)` rounds to the nearest 1/64 s; `(t->utc t)` returns the six fields (seconds as a double, truncation to whole seconds is the caller''s); `*start-date*` = `(2040 1 1 0 0 0)` and `(start-t)`; `(t->ut1-days t)` = JD_UT1 − 2451545.0 for Earth''s rotation; `(t->julian-centuries t)` for the series (TDB = TT, "Time").
- Display conversions (instruments only, "Units"): metres to km, m/s to km/s, radians to degrees and back, a duration in seconds to fields (days, hours, minutes, seconds) for countdowns, `format-utc` as `2040-01-01 00:00:00`; the readout''s row layout is part 9''s.
- Tests `tests/units.lisp` ("Units" in "Rails, orbits and frames"): J2000.0 (2000-01-01 11:58:55.816 UTC) gives `t` within 1e-9 s of 0; round trips date → `t` → date for J2000.0, the start date and 2040-02-29 12:34:56; `(start-t)` is a multiple of 1/64 s and within 1/128 s of the exact `utc->t`; `format-utc` of `(start-t)` is `2040-01-01 00:00:00`; one day after the start formats as 2040-01-02; the start date lies inside 1800–2050 (`decision 18`); a degree/radian round trip.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Time" states the grid rounding and `(start-t)` (`decision 66`).
- Done: the tests pass; `units.lisp` cites a source beside every constant.

### 3. `bodies.lisp`, part one: bodies, rotations, body-fixed frames
- A `body` struct and a fixed list (no hash-table iteration): `:sun`, `:earth`, `:moon` with name, μ, radius, crash radius (`decision 34`: Earth R + 100 km, Moon R + 0, Sun R), parent (`:sun` for Earth, `:earth` for the Moon), the parent orbit''s mean semi-major axis and the Laplace sphere a (m/M)^(2/5) (Earth ~9.25×10^8 m, Moon ~6.6×10^7 m; used from milestone 4, here only defined), J2 for Earth only; `(body key)` looks one up.
- Earth''s rotation ("Frames"): Earth Rotation Angle θ = 2π (0.7790572732640 + 1.00273781191135448 D_U), D_U from `t->ut1-days`, the fraction reduced before multiplying by 2π (keep the integer part of D_U apart for precision); inertial → Earth-fixed and back as rotations about ICRF z.
- The Moon''s rotation: IAU WGCCRE 2015 leading terms, α₀ = 269.9949° + 0.0031° T, δ₀ = 66.5392° + 0.0130° T, W = 38.3213° + 13.17635815° d − 1.4×10^-12° d² (T centuries, d days from J2000 TDB), inertial → body-fixed R_z(W) R_x(90° − δ₀) R_z(90° + α₀); the periodic terms are left out until milestone 4 textures the Moon (a `PLACEHOLDER` is not needed: nothing visible depends on them).
- `(body-fixed-matrix body t)` (a mat3, double; the GPU''s single copy is made by the renderer), `(latitude-longitude body r t)` for a body-centred inertial position, `(body-texture-uv direction)` ("Frames": u = 0.5 + atan2(y, x)/(2π), v = 0.5 − asin(z)/π) for the crash marker and the tests.
- Tests `tests/bodies.lisp` ("Frame transforms"): θ at J2000.0 UT1 is 0.7790572732640 rev within 1e-12 rev and advances 1.00273781191135448 rev per UT1 day within 1e-12; body-fixed x maps to (u, v) = (0.5, 0.5), the north pole to v = 0; an inertial point over latitude 0, longitude 0 when θ = 0 is at longitude 90.246° W (0.25 × 1.00273781191135448 rev) 6 UT1 hours later, within 1e-9°; the Laplace spheres within 1 % of the figures above; the Moon''s synchronous rotation is checked in piece 4, which brings its ephemeris.
- Docs: none beyond keeping "Files in load order" true.
- Done: the tests pass.

### 4. `bodies.lisp`, part two: ephemerides and the Hermite cache (`--web`: Horizons vectors and the DE440 digits)
- Models (`decision 18`, `decision 67`): Sun fixed at the heliocentric origin; the Earth–Moon barycentre from Standish Table 1 (its EMB row only; the planets are milestone 4''s), Kepler''s equation by Newton to 1e-14 rad, analytic velocity, ecliptic → ICRF by ε; the Moon from Meeus ch. 47 (tables 47.A and 47.B in full, mean geometric, no nutation), precessed to the J2000 ecliptic by Meeus eq. 21.5, then ICRF; Moon velocity by central difference ±60 s; Earth = EMB − μ_Moon/(μ_Earth + μ_Moon) × Moon.
- Cache (`decision 67`): nodes every 4,096 s of (r, v) for the EMB and the geocentric Moon, each a pure function of its index; cubic Hermite position and its derivative for velocity; direct-mapped, 64 slots per quantity.
- API: `(body-state key t &optional (origin :earth))` → `(values r v)` in the origin-centred ICRF frame, with an in-place variant `(body-state-into r-out v-out key t origin)` for part 2''s gravity; origins `:sun` and `:earth` now (`:moon` too: the geocentric Moon negated plus nothing heliocentric), built by `decision 67`''s rule; `(body-state-series …)` bypasses the cache for tests.
- Tests `tests/bodies.lisp` ("Ephemeris spot checks"):
  - JPL Horizons vectors (geometric, ICRF, TDB) committed as constants with the query in a comment (target, centre, the date fetched): the EMB from the Sun (target 3, centre 500@10) and the Moon from Earth (target 301, centre 500@399), at 2026-01-01, 2040-01-01 and 2049-06-01 00:00 TDB; bounds: EMB direction within 1′ and distance within 1e-4 relative; Moon direction within 1′, distance within 50 km. Mars and Jupiter come with the planets in milestone 4.
  - The Sun from Earth at the start date near right ascension 281°, declination −23° within 0.5° (`docs/design.md` "The start").
  - The cache: Hermite positions match the series within 2 cm for the Moon (and the EMB) over 200 random times in 2040 from a fixed list; a node is bit for bit the same whether computed first or after 100 others (clear the cache between).
  - Earth heliocentric minus EMB is μ_Moon/(μ_Earth + μ_Moon) times the geocentric Moon within 1e-6 m; the Moon from the Sun equals Earth from the Sun plus the Moon from Earth within 1e-4 m.
  - The Moon''s prime meridian (body-fixed +x) within 10° of the direction to Earth at the three dates (piece 3''s rotation).
- Also: check μ of Sun, Earth and Moon digit by digit against Park et al. 2021 (DE440) and the IAU radii; fix `units.lisp` and say so in the done note if one differs.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Ephemerides": the cached quantities, the global cache and the precession of `decision 67` (replacing "kept in a small table in the `game` object"); "Ephemeris spot checks" notes Mars and Jupiter wait for milestone 4.
- Done: the tests pass with every Horizons query reproducible from its comment; the cost of a cached `body-state-into` is measured (a few hundred ns at most) and written in "Ephemerides".

### 5. `orbits.lisp`: ellipses, Kepler, apsides, radius crossings
- `decision 68`''s `orbit` struct; `(state->orbit r v mu t)` and `(orbit-state orbit t)` → `(values r v)`, through the argument of latitude and true longitude where ω or Ω are conventional; elliptic Kepler''s equation by Newton from a robust start (E₀ = M + e sin M, or π for e > 0.8), to 1e-15 relative.
- Derived: a, p, n, period, apoapsis and periapsis radii, `orbit-energy`, `(orbit-next-apsis orbit t :periapsis|:apoapsis)` times, `(orbit-radius-crossings orbit r t)`: the next inbound and outbound times at radius r after t in closed form ("Removal of objects": θ = ∓arccos((p/r − 1)/e), its eccentric and mean anomaly), nil when r is out of [q, Q].
- `(orbit-j2-rates orbit body)`: Ω̇ = −(3/2) n J2 (R/p)² cos i, ω̇ = (3/4) n J2 (R/p)² (5 cos² i − 1), Ṁ − n = (3/4) n J2 (R/p)² √(1 − e²)(3 cos² i − 1); used by rails from milestone 3 and part 3''s node test.
- Tests `tests/orbits.lisp` ("Kepler round trip"):
  - state → orbit → state within 1e-9 relative (position and velocity) for circular equatorial, circular polar (the start orbit of `decision 38`: a = 6,778,136.6 m, i 90°, Ω 270°, u 0), retrograde i = 150°, e = 0.5 and e = 0.9, each at eight anomalies; orbit → state → orbit likewise in the non-singular combinations of `decision 68`.
  - Kepler propagation t₀ → t₁ → t₀ returns the state within 1e-9 relative; the residual of Kepler''s equation below 1e-14 for e up to 0.99.
  - The start orbit''s period 92.6 min and speed 7,669 m/s (`docs/design.md` "The start") within 0.1 min and 1 m/s; apsides of an 800 × 90 km orbit (radii R + h).
  - Radius crossing: Derelict K-17''s 800 × 90 km orbit (a = 6,823,136.6 m, e = 0.052029) crosses R + 100 km 2,603.07 s after apogee at θ = −14.35° within 0.01 s and 0.01° (the reference of "Removal of objects"; its 1e-6 s test on rails is milestone 3''s); none for periapsis 1 m above the radius.
  - J2 rates: Ω̇ for 400 km at 51.6° about −5.0°/day within 0.1°/day and equal to the closed form within 1e-12 relative; |Ω̇| < 1e-15 rad/s at i = 90°.
  - The start''s lighting (needs piece 4): with the Sun from Earth at `(start-t)`, the start orbit''s β is −10° within 1° and the ship is 25° from the Sun within 1° (`docs/design.md` "The start").
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Rails" names the `orbit` struct (`decision 68`); "Kepler round trip" lists the hyperbolic cases as piece 6''s.
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval ''(orbit-period (state->orbit …))''` works on a hand-made state.

### 6. `orbits.lisp`: hyperbolas for the osculating display of an escape
- The same `orbit` struct for e > 1 (`decision 46`, "Hyperbolic rails"): state ↔ orbit, the hyperbolic Kepler equation M = e sinh H − H by Newton from H₀ = sign(M) ln(2|M|/e + 1.8) to 1e-14 relative; position from the perifocal forms of "Hyperbolic rails"; periapsis only (apoapsis and period nil, so the readout shows "—"); `orbit-radius-crossings` for r ≥ q; `(orbit-points orbit t-start t-end n)` for both conics, the map''s ribbon vertices in double around the body (part 8 draws them).
- Milestone 4 keeps: the near-parabolic series for |H| < 0.5, the |e − 1| < 1e-6 nudge, segments across spheres and the passing rock''s tests; until then a state with |e − 1| < 1e-6 is treated as e = 1 + 1e-6 or 1 − 1e-6 on its side without changing the state (display only).
- Tests `tests/orbits.lisp`: round trips for e = 1.5 and e = 3 at H = 0, ±0.5, ±2 within 1e-9 relative; propagation there and back; Kepler''s residual below 1e-14 for |M| from 1e-6 to 1e3; speed at periapsis √(v∞² + 2μ/q) within 1e-12 relative; `orbit-points` of an ellipse lie on it within 1e-6 m of the conic''s radius.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Hyperbolic rails" says which forms milestone 2 built and which wait for milestone 4.
- Done: the tests pass; the whole part''s tests run in under 0.5 s in `scripts/test`.

## What later parts take from here
- Part 2 (`physics.lisp`, `ship.lisp`): `math` in place forms, `body-state-into` for the Sun and the Moon, μ, J2 and R; the drift tests'' exact Kepler solution from `orbit-state`.
- Part 3 (`clock.lisp`, `hazards.lisp`, `prediction.lisp`): `+fine-step+`, `(start-t)`, crash radii, `orbit-radius-crossings` and `orbit-next-apsis` for events, `latitude-longitude` for the impact point, `orbit-j2-rates` for the polar node test.
- Parts 4–9: `relative-single`, `body-fixed-matrix`, `body-state` for the Sun''s direction and the Moon, `orbit-points`, display conversions and `format-utc`.', '2026-10-14T22:03:09Z', 72);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (14, 2, 'Ship, integrator and flight computer', 680, 'Part 2 of `plan 2: masterplan`: the ship as a rigid body under Newtonian physics and the flight computer that flies it. `physics.lisp` holds the ship''s state, the gravity model, the RK4 step and the step rule; `ship.lisp` the Mule''s parts, mass properties, engine and RCS layout; `controls.lisp` the control state that input, scripts and the autopilot write, the flight computer (direct, rate damping, holds, fine control) and the RCS mixer, and one function that advances the ship by a step. It builds on `plan 13: Simulation foundations: math, units, bodies, orbits` (math, units, bodies, orbits) and is what `plan 2`''s part 3 (clock, warp, hazards, prediction) drives; nothing here knows warp levels, frames or keys.

## Sources and decisions
- Files and load order: `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" (`decision 12: Module layout and package split`): `physics`, `ship`, `controls` after `orbits` and before `shaders` in `luv-in-space.asd`; tests `tests/physics.lisp`, `tests/ship.lisp`, `tests/controls.lisp` in the same order after `tests/orbits.lisp` in `luv-in-space/tests`.
- Integrator and gravity: `docs/architecture.md` "Integrator", "Measured drift", "Gravity model"; `decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock`, `decision 17: Gravity model`, `decision 15: Coordinate frames` (FRD, q body → inertial, ω in body axes).
- Warp''s demands on the integrator: `decision 19: Time-warp rules` (kinematic attitude above ×4, thrust cap), `decision 31: Warp choices within the time-warp rules`.
- The ship: `docs/design.md` "The ship(s)" ("Body axes and reference point", "Parts", "Propulsion", "Mass budget and Δv", "Inertia", "RCS forces, torques and accelerations"); `decision 22: The player''s ship and its propulsion`. Running dry does not kill (`decision 34: How death works`).
- Controls: `docs/design.md` "Controls" (the key rules), "Control modes"; `decision 29: Control scheme: key map, control modes, gamepad`; the start''s prograde hold (`decision 38: Start orbit: polar`).
- From part 1 (`plan 13`): `decision 65: Vectors and quaternions in math.lisp` (vec3, quat, mat3, in-place `!` twins, `quat-derivative`, `quat-from-basis`), `body-state-into` (`decision 67: Ephemeris cache and the Moon''s series frame`), μ, R, J2, g₀ and `+fine-step+` from `units.lisp`, `orbit-state` for exact Kepler references (`decision 68: Orbit elements: one struct for every conic`).
- Recorded for this plan: `decision 69: Ship state, step rule and kinematic attitude in physics.lisp`, `decision 70: Flight computer: per-step control laws, holds and the RCS mixer`, `decision 71: The Mule''s mass model in ship.lisp`.
- Tests: `docs/architecture.md` "Testing" → "Rules for tests", "Drift bounds" (`decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts`); this part''s tests together stay under 3 s, leaving part 3 its share of the ~5 s physics budget.

## Rules for every piece
- Simulation files: no HAL, SDL or `*game*`; pure functions on `double-float`, typed and declaimed inline where hot; the step path (gravity, RK4, mass properties, flight computer, mixer) conses nothing (checked by a test with `sb-ext:get-bytes-consed`).
- State only in structs that part 3 copies into its simulation state (`ship-state`, `controls`); each has a deep copy function and `equalp` compares two bit for bit. No mutable globals except caches that are pure functions of their key (the ephemeris cache of `plan 13`).
- Tunables (`5°/s`, `0.5 s`, `0.8`, the settle thresholds) are `defparameter`s with the design section cited beside them; the Mule''s figures are data in `ship.lisp`, each part''s row citing "Parts".
- Each piece adds its files to `luv-in-space.asd` (both systems), passes `scripts/test`, and keeps `AGENTS.md` "Layout" (the `src/` bullet lists the simulation files built so far) and `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" true.
- No luv gap is expected (nothing here touches luv); one met goes to `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

## Pieces, in the order they run

### 1. `physics.lisp`: ship state, gravity, the step rule
- `ship-state` struct (`decision 69`): `r`, `v` (vec3, Earth-centred ICRF in milestone 2), `q` (quat body → inertial), `w` (vec3, body rad/s), `m-p` (propellant kg), `dv` (odometer, m/s); `copy-ship-state` deep; a constructor from (r, v, q, w, m-p).
- `(gravity-into! a r t model)`: `model` `:none`, `:point` (Earth μ), `:j2` (Earth with J2 = 1.08263×10⁻³ and R⊕ equatorial, the pole along ICRF z, in closed form in the frame''s own axes), `:full` (`:j2` plus Sun and Moon as third bodies, μ_b ((d − r)/|d − r|³ − d/|d|³), d from `body-state-into … :earth` at `t`; "Gravity model"). Earth frame only (`plan 2`''s rules); an `ecase` on the central body leaves room for milestone 4''s frames.
- `(coarse-step r t thrusting)`: h = η min over Earth, Moon, Sun of √(d³/μ), η = 1/256, rounded down to a power of two, clamped to [1/64 s, 16,384 s], and ≤ 1 s when thrusting ("Integrator"); `+fine-step+` from `units.lisp` is the fine one. `docs/architecture.md` "Drift bounds" says the rule is `physics.lisp`''s, called by the clock.
- Tests `tests/physics.lisp`:
  - J2 against closed forms: at the equator (r = R + 400 km) the radial acceleration is −μ/r² (1 + 1.5 J2 (R/r)²), over the pole −μ/r² (1 − 3 J2 (R/r)²), each within 1e-14 relative; no tangential component at either; the acceleration is symmetric under z → −z.
  - Third bodies: the Moon''s and Sun''s terms at three points (towards, away from and across the body''s direction at 7×10⁶ m) equal the same formula evaluated in rationals within 1e-9 relative; along the Earth–Moon line the Moon''s term is the tidal 2 μ_☾ r / d³ within 2 %.
  - Step rule: 2 s at 400 km, 512 s at 2×10⁸ m (translunar coast, "Integrator"), 1 s cap when thrusting in LEO''s 2 s, 1/64 s at the clamp (r = R), 16,384 s at the upper clamp; every result a power of two.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Drift bounds" (step rule from `physics.lisp`), "Files in load order" (`physics.lisp` also holds the step rule).
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval ''(coarse-step (vec3 6778136.6d0 0 0) 0d0 nil)'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` gives 2.0d0.

### 2. `physics.lisp`: the RK4 step and the drift tests
- `(rk4-step! state h t model force-body torque-body mdot inertia inertia-inverse &optional attitude-fn)`: classical RK4 on r, v, q, ω, m_p ("Integrator"): ṙ = v; v̇ = gravity + q (F_body / m) q* with m = m_dry + m_p supplied by the caller (a `mass` argument or folded into the inputs, the worker''s choice, consing nothing); q̇ = `quat-derivative`; I ω̇ = τ − ω × I ω with the full tensor and its inverse held over the step; ṁ_p = −ṁ. After the step: q renormalised in double; the odometer adds |F| / ṁ ln(m₀/m₁), or |F| h / m when ṁ = 0 (`decision 69`).
- Propellant exhaustion: when ṁ h > m_p, every thruster''s output is scaled by m_p / (ṁ h) for this step, so m_p ends at 0.0d0 exactly and the step stays on the grid; at m_p = 0 force, torque and ṁ are zero (no death, `decision 34`). The scale applies to force, torque and ṁ together.
- Kinematic attitude (`decision 69`): with `attitude-fn` (a function of t, r, v returning a quat and the frame rate ω_t in inertial axes) the stages take q from it for the thrust direction, ω and q are not integrated, and the step ends with q and ω (body axes) from the function at t + h; torque is ignored.
- Tests `tests/physics.lisp` ("Drift bounds"):
  - The table of "Drift bounds", two-body (`:point`), periapsis 6,778 km, i 51.6°, Ω 30°, ω 40°, from periapsis, attitude integrated alongside with I = diag(1, 2, 3)×10⁴ kg m² and ω = 0: circular 10 orbits at 1/64 s and e = 0.5 2 orbits at 1/64 s (ΔE/E, ΔL/L < 1e-11, position < 0.05 m), coarse step (`coarse-step`) circular 1,000 orbits (< 1e-10, 5 m), e = 0.5 1,000 orbits (< 1e-9, 100 m), e = 0.9 200 orbits (ΔE/E < 1e-8, ΔL/L < 1e-9, 5 km); errors against `orbit-state` at whole periods, the maximum kept. If the table''s runs don''t fit in ~2 s, the worker shortens the fine-step rows (5 circular orbits, 1 at e = 0.5, bounds unchanged) and says so in the test''s comment.
  - Rotation: torque-free I = diag(1, 2, 3)×10⁴ kg m², |ω| = 0.1 rad/s off the principal axes, 1,000 s at 1/64 s: rotational energy and |I ω| in inertial axes within 1e-9 relative, |q| within 1e-12 of 1; the same with I = R diag(1, 2, 3)×10⁴ Rᵀ, R 10° about body y.
  - Exhaustion: a step with ṁ h = 2 m_p ends with m_p = 0.0d0 exactly and Δv equal to the rocket equation over the burned mass within 1e-12 relative.
  - Kinematic: with an attitude function holding body x along v in a circular orbit, after 100 coarse steps q''s x axis is along v within 1e-12 rad.
  - Allocation: 10,000 steps with `:full` cons less than 1 KB; the same start and inputs twice give `equalp` states.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Integrator": the exhaustion rule''s scaling of all thrusters, the odometer, the kinematic step (`decision 69`); the cost line with this code''s measured ns per step (`:point`, `:full`, with and without attitude) replacing the stand-in measurement.
- Done: the tests pass inside the budget; the measured cost is in "Integrator".

### 3. `ship.lisp`: the Mule''s parts, mass properties, engine and RCS layout
- Parts (`decision 71`): the "Parts" table as data (name, mass, centre from R in FRD, shape and size), cabling and plumbing at 199 kg so the dry sum is 7,961 kg; precomputed dry mass, first and second moments about R.
- `(ship-mass-properties m-p &optional load)` into preallocated outputs: total mass, CoM (body, from R), tensor about the CoM (full symmetric, parallel-axis rule I = Σ (I_i + m_i (|d|² 𝟙 − d dᵀ))) and its inverse; propellant as "Inertia" says (two solid cylinders r 1.52 m from x 0.50 and 9.76 over f × 8.86 and f × 3.72 m, MR 5.5). `load` is nil in milestone 2: the argument and a `PLACEHOLDER` are not needed, milestone 3''s grapple adds it.
- Engine ("Propulsion"): F = 101,800 N, Isp 449.7 s, v_e = Isp g₀, ṁ = throttle F / v_e; throttle 0 or 0.1–1.0 (`ship-engine-force`, `ship-engine-mdot`); thrust along +x through the CoM, no torque; `(ship-trim-angle m-p)` = atan((z_cm − z_g)/(x_cm − x_g)), gimbal at (0, 0, −0.5), for the meshes of part 6 and the readout.
- RCS ("RCS forces, torques and accelerations"): the 24 thrusters as data, eight blocks at x 0.5 and 14.5, y ±1.8, z ±1.8, each with an axial nozzle (aft blocks +x, front −x) and a y and a z nozzle pushing towards the axis; 445 N, Isp 360 s; per thruster position (from R), direction; `(rcs-matrix! b com)`: the 6×24 force and torque columns about a CoM; the couples (pitch, yaw 2 × 445 × 14.0 N m, roll 4 × 445 × 1.8 N m) and `(rcs-translation-capability axis com)` of `decision 70`.
- `(ship-dv-remaining m-p)`: v_e ln(m / m_dry) ("Mass budget and Δv").
- Tests `tests/ship.lisp`:
  - Mass: dry 7,961 kg and wet 35,961 kg exactly; CoM for the rows Wet, Half (14,000 kg) and Dry of "Inertia" within 0.01 m; I_xx, I_yy, I_zz within 5 % and I_xz within 10 % of the table, the principal tilt within 1° (the table is an independent script''s, `decision 71`); the tensor symmetric, positive definite, tensor × inverse = 𝟙 within 1e-12.
  - Closed forms: a single box part gives m (b² + c²)/12 …; two point masses give the parallel-axis tensor exactly.
  - Trim angle: wet 1.6°, half 0.9°, dry −3.0° within 0.1°, all inside ±4°.
  - RCS: couples 12,460 N m and 3,204 N m; the matrix''s columns sum to zero force and torque for opposed pairs; translation capability ±x 1,780 N, ±y and ±z 1,266 N wet and 1,391 N dry within 1 N.
  - Δv: `ship-dv-remaining` at 28,000 kg is 6,650 m/s within 1 m/s.
  - Thrust ("Drift bounds", Thrust): a constant full-throttle burn in field-free space (`:none`) from wet for 100 s matches Δv = Isp g₀ ln(m₀/m₁) within 1e-9 relative and equals the odometer within 1e-12; a burn of 1,300 s reaches m_p = 0.0d0 exactly in the step containing 28,000 / ṁ = 1,213.2 s and stays there, the mass at 7,961 kg.
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "Parts": cabling and plumbing 199 kg (the dry sum then matches its 7,961 kg); "Inertia": one line naming the shapes `ship.lisp` uses (`decision 71`).
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval ''(ship-mass-properties 28000d0)'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` returns 35,961 kg and CoM (10.34, 0, −0.22).

### 4. `controls.lisp`, part one: control state, the mixer, direct and translation, `flight-step`
- `controls` struct (`decision 70`): throttle lever, rotation keys (−1, 0, +1 per axis: pitch, yaw, roll), translation keys (per body axis), fine flag, attitude mode (`:direct`, `:rate-damping`, `:hold`), hold kind (`:prograde` … `:radial-in`, `:inertial`), inertial target quat, roll offset, settled flag, the last duty cycles (for the meshes'' and the readout''s use); `copy-controls` deep; `(new-controls)` gives rate damping with a prograde hold, throttle off (the start, `decision 38`).
- Commands that input (part 7), flight scripts (part 10) and the autopilot (milestone 3) call, each a function of `controls` with the rule of "Controls": `controls-set-throttle` (below 0.1 is 0), `controls-throttle-full`, `controls-throttle-cut`, `controls-set-rotation-key`, `controls-set-translation-key`, `controls-toggle-fine`; the hold and damping commands follow in piece 5. Throttle ramps in real time are input''s (part 7), refusals above ×4 the clock''s (part 3).
- Mixer (`decision 70`): `(rcs-mix! u b tau-c f-c)`: weighted BVLS with Householder QR, fixed iteration limit, duty cycles in [0, 1], zero command gives zero duty; its cost measured.
- Direct mode: a held rotation key commands its axis''s full couple (×0.1 fine). Translation: the axis''s nominal force from `rcs-translation-capability` (×0.1 fine), with the rotation command, in one mix.
- `(flight-step! state controls h t model &optional attitude-fn)`: mass properties from `m-p`, flight computer (piece 5 adds the closed loops), mixer, force, torque and ṁ of engine plus RCS (ṁ_p = −Σ F_i / (Isp_i g₀)), then `rk4-step!`; with `attitude-fn` (above ×4) no RCS and no flight computer, engine only. This is what part 3''s clock calls once per step.
- Tests `tests/controls.lisp`:
  - Mixer: for 50 commanded torques from a fixed list within 90 % of the couples (wet, half and dry CoM), the resulting torque equals the command within 1e-6 × the couple; all duties in [0, 1]; zero command gives all zeros; a pure +x force command uses only the four aft axial nozzles at 1,780 N with zero torque within 1e-6 N m; a 10 kN +y command with zero torque gives zero torque within 1e-6 N m and a force within 2 % of 1,266 N (wet).
  - Direct taps ("Control modes" table): one fine step of direct pitch from rest changes ω by I⁻¹ τ h within 1e-9 relative (0.027°/s per 1/60 s wet, 0.060 dry); one fine step of +x translation 0.77 mm/s wet within 1 %.
  - Propellant: one second of all-RCS firing burns Σ u_i 445 / (360 g₀) kg within 1e-12 relative; engine and RCS share one pool.
  - The mixer''s cost per call is measured and written in `docs/architecture.md` "Integrator" (a few tens of µs at most; above that the worker records the profile and a follow-up task).
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "RCS forces, torques and accelerations": the mixer runs per fine step, not per frame (`decision 70`); `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order": `controls.lisp` also holds the flight computer, the mixer and `flight-step!`.
- Done: the tests pass; a `./sly eval` of 64 `flight-step!`s in direct mode with pitch held from the start state shows ω_y = 12,460 N m / I_yy × 1 s within 1e-6 relative (about 0.028 rad/s wet).

### 5. `controls.lisp`, part two: rate damping, holds 1–6 and R, fine control, kinematic holds
- Rate damping (`decision 70`, "Control modes"): τ = I (ω_c − ω) / 0.5 s + ω × I ω, clamped per axis to the couple (×0.1 fine), ω_c 5°/s (0.5°/s fine) on held axes.
- Holds: `(hold-attitude kind r v roll-offset)` → target quat and frame rate from the orbital frame ("Frames": prograde along v, normal along r × v, radial out completing the triple; default roll of `decision 70`); `:inertial` keeps the stored quat; the slew and settle law and the settled hysteresis of `decision 70`.
- Commands with the rules of "Controls": `controls-set-hold` (pressing the active hold again releases to rate damping), `controls-toggle-rate-damping` (T; releases a hold to rate damping), `controls-inertial-hold` (R: the current attitude); a pitch or yaw key releases any hold; a roll key during a hold turns the roll offset at the rate-damping rate.
- `(controls-attitude-fn controls)`: the active hold''s attitude function for `rk4-step!`''s kinematic mode above ×4 (part 3 sets an inertial hold first when there is none, `decision 19`).
- Tests `tests/controls.lisp` (the design''s numbers from "Control modes"):
  - Rate damping: from rest with pitch held, ω reaches 95 % of 5°/s within 3.5 s wet and 2.0 s dry (the saturated ramp plus the 0.5 s tail); released, |ω| < 0.02°/s within 6 s wet; fine control reaches 0.5°/s and never exceeds it by more than 1 %.
  - Holds: from a start 90° off prograde in the 400 km polar orbit (`:full` gravity), the prograde hold reports settled within 40 s wet and 35 s dry (the times measured go in the test''s comment), the angle then stays < 0.1° and the rate error < 0.02°/s for 120 s as the orbital frame turns; settling within 40 s for retrograde, normal, anti-normal, radial out and in, and R after a tumble at 2°/s; the default roll puts body −z within 0.1° of radial out (towards normal for the radial holds); a roll key held 2 s during a prograde hold changes the roll by 10° within 0.5° and the hold keeps it.
  - Key rules: pressing 1 twice gives rate damping; W releases a hold; T releases a hold to rate damping and toggles direct otherwise.
  - Kinematic burn ("Drift bounds", Thrust): a 60 s full-throttle prograde burn from the start orbit at the coarse step with the 1 s cap and the kinematic hold matches the same burn at the fine step flown by the hold within 1e-6 relative in Δv and 1 m in apoapsis radius.
  - Determinism: 120 s of a scripted key sequence (fixed list keyed by step) run twice gives `equalp` states and controls.
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "Control modes": one bullet naming the slew law, the settle hysteresis and the roll rule of `decision 70`; `docs/architecture.md` "Testing": a "Controls" bullet list (`tests/controls.lisp`) with the bounds above, beside "Drift bounds".
- Done: the tests pass; part 2''s tests together run under 3 s in `scripts/test` (measured and stated in the done note).

## What later parts take from here
- Part 3 (`clock.lisp`, `hazards.lisp`, `prediction.lisp`): `ship-state` and `controls` inside its simulation state, `flight-step!` per step, `coarse-step`, `controls-attitude-fn` and `controls-inertial-hold` for warp above ×4, the settled flag and |ω| for refusals, `copy-ship-state` and `copy-controls` for checkpoints, the odometer for `:after-dv`.
- Part 6 (`meshes.lisp`): `ship-mass-properties` (the CoM, to place R at r − q c), the parts table, `ship-trim-angle`, the last duty cycles and throttle for the engine glow.
- Part 7 (`input.lisp`): the `controls-…` commands, one per key of "Controls"; Part 9: throttle, propellant, attitude mode and settled for the readout; Part 10: the same commands for script actions.
- Milestone 3: loads (`ship-mass-properties` with a load), the RCS capability with the CoM ahead of the front blocks, target holds 7–0.', '2026-10-14T22:09:56Z', 73);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (15, 2, 'Clock, warp, hazards and events', 660, 'Part 3 of `plan 2: masterplan`: what turns the ship of part 2 into a running simulation. `clock.lisp` holds the simulation state as one copyable object, the start state, the one step function with every rule, the frame accumulator, the eight warp levels with their refusals, thrust limits, drop-outs and pause, and the start-state checkpoint; `hazards.lisp` the swept crash test against Earth and the Moon and death; `prediction.lisp` the milestone-2 events (apsides, impact, a scheduled cut-off) for drop-outs, the readout''s alarm and flight scripts. It builds on `plan 13: Simulation foundations: math, units, bodies, orbits` and `plan 14: Ship, integrator and flight computer`; parts 4 (the frame in `main.lisp`), 7 (warp and pause keys), 9 (readout, messages, LOST panel) and 10 (flight scripts) call it. Nothing here draws or reads keys.

## Sources and decisions
- Files and load order: `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" (`decision 12: Module layout and package split`): `clock`, `hazards`, `prediction` after `controls` and before `shaders` in `luv-in-space.asd`; tests `tests/clock.lisp`, `tests/hazards.lisp`, `tests/prediction.lisp` in the same order after `tests/controls.lisp`. `clock.lisp` calls `hazards.lisp` and `prediction.lisp` at run time (loaded later; ASDF''s compilation unit defers the undefined-function warnings).
- Clock, steps, warp: `docs/architecture.md` "The clock", "Integrator", "Time warp", "Determinism", "Checkpoints"; `decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock`, `decision 19: Time-warp rules` (with `decision 43: Warp drop-outs for contact and life support`: its events are milestones 3–4''s), `decision 31: Warp choices within the time-warp rules`; `docs/design.md` "Time warp".
- Crash and death: `docs/architecture.md` "Collisions and the Sun"; `decision 34: How death works`; `docs/design.md` "Dying and what follows" (milestone 2: Earth R + 100 km, the Moon R; the start state is the only checkpoint, Return and N the same).
- Prediction: `docs/architecture.md` "Trajectory prediction"; `decision 20: Trajectory prediction on the frame thread`. Script waits and cut-offs: "Scripted runs" (`decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts`, `decision 56: Flight-script actions, start states and the quest play-through`).
- The start: `decision 38: Start orbit: polar`, `docs/design.md` "The start"; `decision 66: Calendar dates on the 1/64 s grid` (`(start-t)`).
- From part 1 (`plan 13`): `+fine-step+`, `(start-t)`, crash radii and `body-state` (`bodies.lisp`), `latitude-longitude`, `state->orbit`, `orbit-state`, `orbit-radius-crossings`, `orbit-next-apsis`, `orbit-j2-rates`, display conversions and duration fields (`units.lisp`). From part 2 (`plan 14`): `ship-state`, `copy-ship-state`, `controls`, `copy-controls`, `new-controls`, `flight-step!`, `coarse-step`, `controls-attitude-fn`, `controls-inertial-hold`, `hold-attitude`, the settled flag, the odometer, `ship-engine-force` and `ship-engine-mdot` (`decision 69: Ship state, step rule and kinematic attitude in physics.lisp`, `decision 70: Flight computer: per-step control laws, holds and the RCS mixer`).
- Recorded for this plan: `decision 72: Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp`, `decision 73: Warp levels above x4: entry, holds and lowering`, `decision 74: Crash test on the step''s Hermite and the death state`, `decision 75: Prediction in milestone 2: the step function on a copy`.
- Tests: `docs/architecture.md` "Testing" → "Rules for tests", "Drift bounds" (through the clock), "Rails, orbits and frames" (the polar node test), "Warp, events and collisions"; this part''s tests together stay under 1.5 s (part 2 has 3 s of the ~5 s physics budget).

## Rules for every piece
- Simulation files: no HAL, SDL or `*game*`; no wall clock except the frame''s 4 ms cap through `*frame-step-time-limit*` (tests bind it to nil, `decision 72`). The step path (`sim-step!`, the crash test, a prediction step) conses nothing (a test with `sb-ext:get-bytes-consed`, as part 2''s).
- State: the `sim` struct is the simulation state; the `clock` and the `prediction` structs are not part of it and checkpoints don''t copy them. Every double in `sim` compares bit for bit with `equalp`.
- Tunables (3 s, the levels, the limits ×10 / ×100, 0.01 rad/s, 4,096 steps, 4 ms, the horizon) are `defparameter`s citing their decision; the warp levels a list in order, no hash tables.
- Each piece adds its files to `luv-in-space.asd` (both systems), passes `scripts/test`, and keeps `AGENTS.md` "Layout" (the `src/` bullet lists the simulation files built so far) and `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" true.
- No luv gap is expected (nothing here touches luv); one met goes to `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

## Pieces, in the order they run

### 1. `clock.lisp`, part one: the simulation state, the start state, the step, checkpoints
- `sim` struct (`decision 72`): `t`, `body` (`:earth` in milestone 2), `ship` (`ship-state`), `controls`, `model` (`:full`; tests set `:point` or `:j2`), `cutoff` (nil or the odometer value at which the throttle is cut), `loss` (nil or the loss record of piece 2), `steps` (count); `copy-sim` deep (through `copy-ship-state`, `copy-controls`) and `sim-assign!` copying into an existing sim without consing.
- `(new-game-sim)`: `t` = `(start-t)`; r, v from the start orbit (a = 6,778,136.6 m, e 0, i 90°, Ω 270°, u 0 at `(start-t)`) through `orbit-state`; q and ω from `hold-attitude :prograde` with the default roll (ω in body axes = the orbital frame''s rate); m_p 28,000 kg; `(new-controls)` (rate damping, prograde hold, settled, throttle off). Flight scripts'' `(:start :new-game)` and N use it.
- `(sim-step! sim level &key until)`, one step with every rule (`decision 72`):
  - h: `+fine-step+` at ×1 and ×4; above, `(coarse-step r t thrusting)` with `controls-attitude-fn` (kinematic attitude, no RCS). Entering a coarse step with no hold engages an inertial hold first (`controls-inertial-hold`, `decision 73`); the warp-level checks that may refuse or lower warp are piece 5''s.
  - Landing: h shortened so that `t + h` ≤ `until` (a grid time: a script action, a date, an apsis wait) and ≤ the cut-off''s grid time; shortened steps are multiples of 1/64 s, so `t` stays on the grid.
  - Cut-off: with `cutoff` armed and the throttle on, the cut lands on the first 1/64 s grid point where the odometer reaches it: at the fine step after the step that crosses it; at a coarse step h = m₀/ṁ (1 − exp(−Δv_left ṁ / |F|)) (engine only, as above ×4) rounded up to the grid; then throttle 0 and `cutoff` nil.
  - Then `flight-step!` with `(sim-model sim)`; `t += h`, `steps += 1`. A sim with a `loss` takes no step (returns nil).
  - `sim-step!` returns h. Piece 2 adds the crash test after `flight-step!`.
- Checkpoints ("Checkpoints"): `(take-checkpoint sim)` = `copy-sim`; `(restore-checkpoint! sim checkpoint)` = `sim-assign!`; `(checkpoint-safe-p sim)`: throttle off, no loss (piece 3 adds "no impact predicted"); milestone 2 takes one checkpoint, the start state.
- Tests `tests/clock.lisp`:
  - Start state: `state->orbit` of it gives a, e, i, Ω, u within 1e-9 relative (1e-12 for e); body +x along v within 1e-12 rad and −z along radial out within 1e-12 rad; |ω| = n within 1e-12 relative; `t` = `(start-t)`; the hold settled.
  - Grid: 1,000 mixed steps (fine and coarse, with `until` on odd grid times) leave `t` an exact multiple of 1/64 s and land on every `until` exactly.
  - Cut-off: a prograde full-throttle burn with `cutoff` 25 m/s at ×1 and at ×100: each ends on the grid, Δv delivered ≥ 25 m/s and below 25 m/s + one fine step''s Δv; the two cut times differ by at most 1/64 s.
  - Checkpoint: restore then N = 2,000 steps (fine and coarse mixed) equals N steps from the original bit for bit (`equalp` on the sims).
  - The polar node (`docs/architecture.md` "Rails with J2", `decision 38`): the start state with `:j2`, 7 days at the coarse step (×1,000 through `sim-step!`): Ω stays within 1e-6° of 270°; `orbit-j2-rates` gives |Ω̇| < 1e-15 rad/s.
  - Allocation: 10,000 `sim-step!`s cons less than 1 KB.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Checkpoints" (milestone 2''s `sim` fields, `decision 72`), "The clock" (`sim-step!` as the one step; landing on scheduled times and the cut-off rule), "Files in load order" (`clock.lisp` also holds the start state).
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval ''(let ((s (new-game-sim))) (dotimes (i 64) (sim-step! s 1)) (sim-t s))'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE` gives `(start-t)` + 1.

### 2. `hazards.lisp`: the swept crash test and death
- `(crash-test! sim r0 v0 t0)` after each step, called by `sim-step!` with the step''s start state (kept in a preallocated buffer; nothing consed), against Earth (R + 100 km, the central body) and the Moon (R, a third body, its geocentric state from `body-state` at both ends), as `decision 74` sets: the relative cubic Hermite p(s); chord closest approach minus max(|p''''(0)|, |p''''(1)|)/8 against the crash radius; only then Newton on d|p|²/ds, and the first crossing |p(s)| = R_c by bracketed Newton to 1e-9 in s. The earliest crossing over both bodies wins.
- Death (`decision 74`): `loss` struct (cause `:crash`, body, `t`, speed relative to the body, latitude, longitude from `latitude-longitude` at the impact `t`); the sim''s ship at the Hermite state of the crossing (attitude slerped, ω and m_p of the step''s end), `t` the impact time; no step after. `(sim-lost-p sim)`.
- Tests `tests/hazards.lisp` ("Crash detection"):
  - Radial free fall from rest at 1,000 km (`:point`): impact at R + 100 km within 1e-3 s of the closed form t = √(r₀³/(2μ)) (√(x(1 − x)) + arccos √x), x = r_c/r₀, at the fine step (×1) and the coarse step (×1,000).
  - Periapsis 50 km below the crash radius (`:point`, an orbit from 400 km apoapsis), at the coarse step: the impact on the first pass, its time within 1e-2 s of `orbit-radius-crossings`, latitude and longitude within 0.01° of the closed-form point (the Kepler position at that time through `latitude-longitude`); the speed equal to the vis-viva speed at R_c within 1e-3 m/s. (The same through the clock at ×1,000,000 is piece 5''s.)
  - Grazing: periapsis 1 m above the crash radius, two orbits at the fine and the coarse step: no impact.
  - The Moon: a ship set 50,000 km from the Moon (in Earth''s frame, `:full`, with `t` 2040-01-01) at 3 km/s relative, aimed with an impact parameter of 0.9 R_☾, at the coarse step: caught, body `:moon`, |p| at the crossing = R_☾ within 1e-3 m; aimed so that the selenocentric hyperbola''s periapsis (computed in the test) is 1 km above R_☾: no impact.
  - Death stops: after the impact `sim-step!` returns nil and the sim is unchanged; a checkpoint restored brings back a sim that flies to the same impact bit for bit.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Collisions and the Sun" (the exact sag bound and the death state, `decision 74`); "Testing" → "Crash detection" with the Moon case as written.
- Done: the tests pass; the cost of the crash test per step is measured (a few tens of ns when nothing is near) and written in "Integrator"''s cost line.

### 3. `prediction.lisp`: the milestone-2 events
- `prediction` struct (`decision 75`): its own `sim` copy, a preallocated buffer of 20,000 steps (t, r, v), the horizon (one osculating period; two on request for the map), the events found, a validity state and the controls'' signature it was made with.
- `(prediction-update! pred sim)`: restart from the flown sim when the signature (throttle, mode, hold kind, roll offset, inertial target, keys, fine, cutoff) differs, while the throttle is on (each call), when the flown r is more than 1 m from the buffer''s Hermite at the flown t, when the flown t passes the buffer''s end, after a restore or a death; otherwise extend the tail to the horizon from the current t. At most `*prediction-steps-per-frame*` `sim-step!`s per call at ×1,000; `(prediction-run! pred sim)` runs it to completion (tests, scripts).
- Events: `(prediction-event pred kind)` → time (and for `:impact` the copy''s loss record) or nil; `:apoapsis`, `:periapsis` from r·v sign changes on the stored Hermite, root to 1e-6 s; `:impact` from the copy''s crash; `:cutoff` when the copy cuts the throttle. `(prediction-dropout-event pred)` → the earliest drop-out event (milestone 2: `:impact`) with its kind; `(prediction-complete-p pred)`. `checkpoint-safe-p` adds: no impact predicted.
- A wait on an event ends at the first 1/64 s grid point at or after its predicted time (`decision 75`); `(grid-ceiling t)` here, used by part 10''s waits as `sim-step!`''s `until`.
- Tests `tests/prediction.lisp` ("Prediction"):
  - Coasting at ×1,000 (`:full`): the prediction''s state one orbit ahead equals the flown sim''s at that `t` bit for bit; a prediction computed over frames of 500 steps equals one computed at once bit for bit.
  - Apsides (`:point`, e = 0.1): `:periapsis` and `:apoapsis` within 1e-3 s of `orbit-next-apsis`; the flight at ×1 then meets r·v = 0 within the fine step that contains the predicted time.
  - Impact: the deorbit case of piece 2 (periapsis 50 km below the crash radius): the predicted impact within 1e-2 s of the flight''s at ×1,000 and within 0.05 s at ×1 (fine steps under rate damping differ by millimetres).
  - Cut-off: a prograde burn with `cutoff` 25 m/s: `:cutoff` at the flight''s cut time at ×100 exactly; the apoapsis after it matches the flown one within 1 m.
  - Restart rules: a throttle change, a hold change and a 2 m displacement each restart; coasting 100 frames does not, and only the tail grows.
  - Budget: one call never takes more than `*prediction-steps-per-frame*` steps; a full LEO prediction conses nothing after the first.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Trajectory prediction" (milestone 2''s form, the step budget, `decision 75`); "Scripted runs" → "Steps": an event wait ends at the first grid point at or after the event.
- Done: the tests pass; `./sly eval` of `(prediction-run! …)` on the start state with a 100 m/s retrograde burn applied lists apoapsis, periapsis and no impact.

### 4. `clock.lisp`, part two: frames, warp levels, pause, the actual rate
- `clock` struct (`decision 72`): level index into ×1, ×4, ×10, ×100, ×1,000, ×10,000, ×100,000, ×1,000,000; accumulator (double); paused; the rate meter (real and game time of the frames in the last second, a fixed ring); the message list (piece 5); the previous ship state and `t` for interpolation (preallocated, filled before each step); `hook` (nil or a lambda called with the clock and the sim before each step, returning a grid `until` or nil: flight scripts); the `prediction`.
- `(clock-frame! clock sim elapsed)`: `prediction-update!` first; `acc += min(elapsed, 0.1) × level`; while `acc ≥` the next h: the hook, `sim-step!` with its `until`, `acc −= h`; at most 4,096 steps and `*frame-step-time-limit*` (checked every 64 steps); the rest dropped (`acc` clamped below one step). Paused: no steps, `acc` 0, the level kept. Lost: no steps.
- Commands for input (part 7) and scripts (part 10): `clock-warp-up`, `clock-warp-down`, `clock-warp-reset` (never refused), `clock-toggle-pause`; a change takes effect at the next step boundary and never touches the sim. `(clock-actual-rate clock)`, `(clock-kinematic-p clock)` (level above ×4: input ignores rotation and translation keys with decision 31''s message), `(clock-previous-state clock)` for part 4''s interpolation.
- Tests `tests/clock.lisp` ("Drift bounds", through the clock; two-body `:point`, the test orbit of "Drift bounds", direct mode with no keys so the mixer''s zero command short-cuts):
  - At every level, fed frames of 1/60 s with the time limit off: the state at each step equals bit for bit a direct `sim-step!` loop at the same level; ×1 and ×4 over 600 s, the six coarse levels over 10 circular orbits; at ×1,000,000 every frame stops at 4,096 steps and the states still match.
  - Frames of 1/144 s and a fixed list of irregular elapsed times (including 0.5 s, clamped to 0.1) give the same states at ×1, ×4 and ×1,000.
