- Once live: `./sly start`, `./sly play`, `./sly eval ''(fly "raise-orbit")''` and `./sly screenshot`s at the first burn and after the raise, then `(fly "deorbit")` to the LOST panel, a synthetic Return (as `plan 21`''s piece 3 does) and a screenshot of the start state; `./sly stop`. Then `nix run` started in the background, closed by its PID (`kill $!`) after a few seconds: the window opens without error. No process left running.
- By hand, as a player, once (the Outcome''s "the player flies it by hand"): in `./sly play`, synthetic key events through `./sly eval` (prograde hold, Shift to raise the throttle, X to cut, warp up and down, M and Tab), each followed by a `./sly screenshot`, the readout''s values moving as expected.
- Docs against what was built: `AGENTS.md` ("Commands": every command listed runs as written with the times stated; "Layout": every file and directory; the `src/` bullet no longer says walking skeleton or `PLACEHOLDER(23)`); `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" against `luv-in-space.asd` and `src/`, "Testing" against `tests/` (each named test exists or its row says which milestone writes it), "Scripted runs" against `flights.lisp`; `docs/design.md` "Feature list": every milestone-2 must row built, each should row built or noted as not built with a follow-up task; `grep -rn ''PLACEHOLDER('' src tests` lists only tasks of later milestones (each id checked with `pm show`); `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md` list the luv gaps met in milestone 2.
- The screenshots are not committed (`decision 87`); the done note lists the commands and the times measured. Temporary clones and directories deleted.
- Done: every check above passes, or each failure has a follow-up task named in the done note; the done note of this task is the milestone''s evidence.

## What later milestones take from here
- `milestone 3`: pilot actions, `(:start :state …)`, the terminal approach and the autopilot conditions in `flights.lisp`; their read-time rejections ("NOT BEFORE MILESTONE 3") become implementations; `scripts/fly` serves the preset and rendezvous scripts.
- `milestone 4`: `:soi`, `:dominant`, the Sun and hull conditions, `sun-dive`.
- `milestone 5`: `saves.lisp`''s round trip for `(:start :after …)` and `:file`, `scripts/fly --from`, `<name>.end.sexp`, the quest play-through (`scripts/play-through`).', '2026-10-14T22:54:02Z', 81);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (23, 3, 'Objects on rails and the Earth-orbit places', 700, 'Part 1 of `plan 3: masterplan`: the rigid-body objects of `milestone 3` on Kepler rails, their promotion to integration near the ship, their removal, and the Earth-orbit places that are made of them. Everything later in the milestone stands on it: contact and grappling (`task 158: write plan: Grapple, contact, berthing, towing, deliveries and refuelling`), targets and predicted trajectories (`task 159`), rendezvous (`task 161`), meshes (`task 163`) and the map (`task 164`) read the objects through the API below.

The specification: `docs/design.md` "Earth-orbit points of interest", "Rigid bodies: attitude, mass, shapes and fixtures", "One plane for near-Earth space", "Other ships"; `docs/architecture.md` "Rails", "Rails across spheres of influence" (only what `milestone 3` needs), "Removal of objects", "Checkpoints", "Determinism", "Testing". Decisions it builds on: `decision 19: Time-warp rules` (rails, promotion within 100 km, demotion beyond 200 km), `decision 44: Contact, capture and berthing in the simulation`, `decision 46: Rails across spheres, hyperbolas and re-entry`, `decision 47: Rigid bodies of the places`, `decision 27: Other ships`, `decision 38: Start orbit: polar`, `decision 53: Deliveries, the stranded offer, checkpoint floors and Skua Yard''s layout`, `decision 63: POD 14 and Skua Yard''s layout from milestone 3`, `decision 68: Orbit elements: one struct for every conic`, `decision 72: Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp`, `decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock`; and this plan''s own `decision 110: Objects in the simulation: places.lisp''s load position, the object struct and promotion checks` and `decision 111: Fixture axes of Derelict K-17 and Skua Yard''s rocks`.

## Scope and limits
- In: the object type, rails (one Earth-centred segment per object, J2''s secular rates, an end at the crash sphere), attitude on rails and promoted, promotion and demotion, promoted objects stepped with the ship, re-entry removal, the removal record and its API, the twelve objects and five places, the sim holding them, checkpoints copying them.
- Out, to later parts: contact, capture, the grapple, berthing, the stack, deliveries (`task 158`; it calls this part''s `object-remove!` with `:delivered` and inserts contact into the step order below); SHIP messages for removals ("K-17 BURNED UP", `task 158`''s message queue); targets, closest approach, the prediction''s contact events and its step budget with objects (`task 159`); drawing (`task 163`, `task 164`).
- Out, to `milestone 4`: segments across spheres of influence, the child-sphere search, backward segments, the near-parabolic forms, the passing rock, Peary Depot; the story ships and their schedules (`milestone 5`). The segment list is a list from the start, so `milestone 4` adds segments, not a new type. A demotion to a hyperbola in `milestone 3` gives one two-body hyperbolic segment to the horizon (2050-01-01).

## Files, in load order
- `src/physics.lisp` (extended): the object type and rails.
- `src/ship.lisp`: unchanged; `shape-tensor` and `add-mass!` are reused by the tests that check the places'' tensors.
- `src/places.lisp` (new; loads after `ship.lisp`, before `controls.lisp`, `decision 110`): the definitions.
- `src/clock.lisp` (extended): the sim holds the objects; the step order; promotion, demotion; checkpoints.
- `src/hazards.lisp` (extended): the crash test of promoted objects and the removal.
- `luv-in-space.asd`: `places` after `ship` in both systems; `tests/places.lisp` after `tests/ship.lisp`.

## The object (`physics.lisp`, `decision 110`)
- `object-def` (immutable, shared by every copy): key (`:tern-station`, `:fulmar-depot`, `:k-17`, `:highport`, `:skua-yard-1` … `:skua-yard-6`, `:pod-14`, `:rock-mover`), display name ("TERN STATION", "K-17", "SKUA YARD 3", "POD 14", "ROCK MOVER"), kind (`:towable`, `:berth`, `:fixed`: immovable like a berth object, no usable fixture), mass, tensor and its inverse (mat3, body axes FRD about the CoM), shapes (each `:box` centre and size, or `:sphere` centre and radius), fixtures (each name, pin, outward unit axis, kind `:towable`, `:berth` or `:receiving` with a free/occupied flag: Highport''s plant is `:berth` and receiving, `decision 53`), attitude mode (`:lvlh`, `:sun-pointing`, `:inertial` with a quaternion, or `:spin` with q₀, a body axis and a rate), bound (m), and the definition''s rails.
- `object` (the mutable part, in the sim): the def, status `:rails`, `:promoted` or `:removed`, the rails segment list (immutable; a demotion replaces it whole, so copies share it), its attitude mode (the def''s, or the one a demotion set; immutable, replaced whole), a `ship-state` for the promoted state (r, v, q, ω; m_p 0), the next promotion check time, the removal record (nil, or time, body, cause, point). `object-assign!` copies in place, consing nothing; `copy-object`; doubles compare bit for bit under `equalp`.
- Rails segment: start, end, central body (`:earth` only in `milestone 3`), an `orbit` (`decision 68`) at the epoch, J2''s secular rates (`orbit-j2-rates`; ellipses around Earth only, else zero), how it ends (`:crash`, `:horizon`). `rails-state-into! r v segment time`: Ω and ω advanced by their rates, M by n + Ṁ, Kepler from `orbits.lisp`; conses nothing.
- Building rails (`make-rails orbit start`): one segment from `start` to the first inbound crossing of Earth''s crash radius (R + 100 km, `decision 34: How death works`) in closed form, θ_c = −arccos((p/r_c − 1)/e) through the eccentric anomaly and n + Ṁ, to 1e-6 s ("Removal of objects"), or to the horizon 2050-01-01 (`decision 18: Ephemerides`); a periapsis radius ≥ r_c has no crossing.
- Attitude, a pure function of the state and t, for rails and promoted alike (`object-attitude-into! q ω-inertial def r v time`):
  - `:lvlh`: x along the horizontal in the direction of motion, z to Earth''s centre, y = z × x; ω = h / r² about −y.
  - `:sun-pointing`: x towards the Sun from the object (`body-state-into` of the Sun), z the ecliptic south pole (0, sin ε, −cos ε) made perpendicular to x, y = z × x; ω from the direction''s rate (the Sun''s geocentric velocity minus the object''s).
  - `:inertial`: the fixed quaternion, ω = 0.
  - `:spin`: q₀ ⊗ (rotation about the body axis by rate × (t − t₀)), ω = rate about that axis.
- `(object-state-into! r v q w object time)`: rails from the segment containing `time`; promoted the integrated state (valid only at the sim''s t); nil for a removed object or a time past its crash end (`place-state` in the docs). Also `object-def-of`, `object-key`, `object-removed-p`, `object-promoted-p`.

## The places (`places.lisp`)
- `*objects*`: the twelve definitions in this fixed order: Tern Station, Fulmar Depot, Derelict K-17, Highport, Skua Yard 1–6, POD 14, the rock mover; values from the tables of `docs/design.md` "Rigid bodies: attitude, mass, shapes and fixtures" (masses, tensors with their off-diagonal entries, shapes with the berthed scenery ships, fixtures, bounds; `decision 47`) and the rocks'' table, as data (`decision 110`).
- Elements, epoch the start date, i 90°, Ω 270° (`decision 38`): Tern 420 km circular u₀ 20°; Fulmar 500 km circular u₀ 180°; K-17 800 km circular u₀ 270°; Highport a 41,878.1 km, e 0.82382, ω 336°, M₀ 180°; the yard on Highport''s a, e, ω with the M₀ of "Layout" (Skua Yard 3 179.87880°, POD 14 179.87587°, Skua Yard 5 179.87293°, Skua Yard 1 179.87000°, Skua Yard 2 179.86707°, Skua Yard 4 179.86413°, Skua Yard 6 179.86120°, the rock mover 179.85239°). Circular orbits take u₀ as the mean anomaly (ω = 0 by `decision 68`).
- Attitudes: Tern `:lvlh`; Fulmar and Highport `:sun-pointing`; the rock mover `:inertial` (its LVLH at the epoch); POD 14 `:inertial` (its LVLH at the epoch turned 180° about z, no spin); K-17 `:spin` 0.3°/s with +x along its orbit''s anti-normal; the rocks `:spin` at their tabled rates with +x as POD 14''s (`decision 111`).
- `*places*`: the five presets, each a name and a target: Tern Station, Fulmar Depot, Derelict K-17 and Highport their objects; Skua Yard its drop point, Skua Yard 3''s slot (rails on Highport''s elements at M₀ 179.87880°, no shapes; `docs/design.md` "The terminal path and crowded targets"), so "Rendezvous: Skua Yard" has a point to stop at after Skua Yard 3 is towed away. `(place key)`, `(place-target-state-into! r v place sim)`.
- THRIFT, TANKER 3, BAGGER 3 and Peary Depot are not defined here (`milestone 5`, `milestone 4`).

## The sim and the step (`clock.lisp`, `hazards.lisp`)
- `sim-objects`: a simple-vector of `object`s in `*objects*`'' order; `new-game-sim` builds them on their definition rails, unpromoted, each with its first check time; `copy-sim` and `sim-assign!` copy them (assign conses nothing); `(sim-object sim key)`.
- Step order in `sim-step-ticks!` (`decision 110`): the ship''s `flight-step!`; each promoted object stepped with `rk4-step!` over the same h and gravity model (towable: zero force, torque and ṁ, m-dry its mass, attitude integrated torque-free with its tensor; berth and fixed: its mode''s attitude as the kinematic attitude function); `t += h`; the ship''s crash test; each promoted object''s crash test; rails removals (an object whose segment ends `:crash` at t_c ≤ t is removed at t_c); then promotion and demotion. `task 158` inserts contact after the crash tests. A ship crash leaves the objects at the step''s end (the sim is lost; no more steps).
- Sharing the third bodies: `rk4-step!` gets the Moon''s and the Sun''s positions at t, t + h/2, t + h read once per step (an optional buffer the ship''s step fills and the objects'' steps reuse), bit for bit the same ship trajectory as now (`tests/physics.lisp`''s existing tests unchanged).
- Promotion (`decision 19`): a rails object whose next check time is ≤ t gets its distance d to the ship''s r; d ≤ 100 km promotes it (state and attitude from rails and its mode at t, exact at that instant); otherwise the next check is t + max(0, d − 100 km) / 25 km/s rounded down to the grid, or the next step while |v_ship| > 12 km/s (`decision 110`). Demotion: a promoted object beyond 200 km gets rails from its osculating elements at t (`state->orbit`, plus J2''s rates), its attitude mode kept for berth and fixed objects; a towable object''s becomes `:spin` with q₀ its q at t and its ω (body axes, which `:spin` takes as an axis and rate, +x for the definitions) when ω is within 1° of a principal axis, else `:inertial` with its q at t: a tumble is frozen while on rails, beyond 200 km and out of view, and a re-promotion starts from it.
- Crash of a promoted object (`hazards.lisp`): the ship''s swept test on the step''s Hermite (`hermite-set!`, `hermite-sag`, `hermite-crossing`), factored so ship and objects call one function; a crossing removes the object at that time.
- Removal: `(object-remove! sim object time cause &optional point)`; causes `:burned-up` (Earth''s crash sphere; the point latitude and longitude from `latitude-longitude` at t_c), `:delivered` (for `task 158`; the point the receiving object and fixture), `:impact` (`milestone 4`). A removed object is never stepped, promoted, crash-tested or returned by `object-state-into!`; its record stays (`docs/architecture.md` "Removal of objects"). Checkpoints carry it; a restore before t_c brings the object back.

## Pieces, in order
Each piece is one task: code, its tests, its docs; `nix build` and `scripts/test` pass. Physics tests together stay under ~5 s (`docs/architecture.md` "Rules for tests"); each piece''s new tests ≤ ~0.5 s, sized with `:point` or `:j2` and coarse steps.

1. **Rails and attitude modes** (`physics.lisp`, `tests/physics.lisp`): `object-def`, the rails segment, `make-rails`, `rails-state-into!`, the four attitude modes, `object-state-into!` for rails. Tests ("Rails, orbits and frames"): Kepler propagation against the integrator (two-body, coarse step, 10 orbits of the drift tests'' 51.6° orbit) within the coarse position bound; rails with J2 against the ship integrated with `:j2` from the same 400 km 51.6° state, node within 0.05° after one day (short-period terms averaged over the last orbit), Ω̇ within 1e-9 relative of −(3/2) n J2 (R/p)² cos i; a polar rails orbit keeps Ω = 270° exactly; K-17''s re-entry end on an 800 × 90 km orbit from apogee at 2,603.07 s within 1e-6 s two-body, at the rates'' time with J2; perigee 1 m above r_c has no end; each mode''s ω matches a central difference of q within 1e-9 rad/s; `rails-state-into!` conses nothing. Done: the tests pass; `docs/architecture.md` "Rails" names the functions as built.
2. **The places** (`places.lisp` new, `luv-in-space.asd`, `tests/places.lisp` new): `*objects*` and `*places*` as above. Tests: every mass, tensor entry, shape, fixture and bound equals the design''s table; rocks and the cargo pod''s tensors equal their formulas ((2/5) m r², the box) within 1e-9 relative and the rocks'' radii (3m/4πρ)^(1/3) within 0.005 m; Tern Station''s tensor recomputed from its parts list in "Rigid bodies" with `shape-tensor` and `add-mass!` within 2 % per diagonal entry (a miss is a follow-up task against the design, not a silent change); each bound ≥ the farthest shape corner and every pin + 0.8 m, within 0.1 m of the table; each fixture plate 0.3 m proud of its object''s shapes along its axis; every towable spin about +x and ≤ 0.4°/s; every object at i 90°, Ω 270°; the yard''s offsets at the first apogee 666.7 m apart within 1 m and Skua Yard 1 29.5 km behind Highport within 0.5 km (`docs/design.md` "Layout"); POD 14''s +x anti-parallel to its velocity at apogee within 0.01°; K-17''s +x along its orbit''s anti-normal at t₀ and after a day within 1e-9 rad. Done: tests pass; `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order" moves `places.lisp` after `ship.lisp` with the reason (`decision 110`) and lists what it holds; `AGENTS.md` "Layout" lists `places.lisp`; `docs/design.md` "Rigid bodies" states K-17''s and the rocks'' axes (`decision 111`).
3. **The sim holds the objects; promotion, demotion, stepping** (`clock.lisp`, `physics.lisp` for the shared third-body reads, `tests/clock.lisp`, `tests/physics.lisp`): `sim-objects`, `new-game-sim`, `copy-sim`, `sim-assign!`, the step order, promotion with next-check times, demotion, promoted objects stepped. Tests ("Rails, orbits and frames", "Determinism"): promoting an object at t and demoting it in the next step moves it < 1 mm; Tern Station promoted beside a parked ship (1 km ahead, `:full`, fine step) stays within 1 m of a reference integration of the station alone for 1 hour game time at ×4 steps (~0.4 s; at the coarse step if it does not fit, saying so in the test); the ship''s trajectory with objects present equals today''s without them bit for bit (no object promoted); a run through `clock-frame!` at two frame rates and a checkpoint restore give the same objects'' states bit for bit; `sim-assign!` and 10,000 steps with Skua Yard''s nine objects promoted cons nothing; the start state''s first checks schedule no promotion for ~1,000 s (Tern 2,370 km ahead). Measure and record in `docs/architecture.md` "Integrator" the cost of a coarse step at Skua Yard with nine objects promoted (target ≤ 5 µs; `task 159`''s prediction budget uses the figure). Done: tests pass; `docs/architecture.md` "Checkpoints" lists the sim''s new fields as built, "The clock" the step order.
4. **Removal and re-entry** (`hazards.lisp`, `clock.lisp`, `tests/hazards.lisp`): the shared swept crash test, removal on rails and promoted, `object-remove!`, the record. Tests ("Re-entry"): K-17 placed on 800 × 90 km at apogee on rails two-body: removed 2,603.07 s later within 1e-6 s, cause `:burned-up`; promoted, integrated two-body at the fine and at the coarse step: within 1e-2 s of the closed form, point within 0.01°; after removal `object-state-into!` nil, never promoted, no crash test; a checkpoint restored before t_c brings it back and it is removed at the same t_c bit for bit; perigee 1 m above r_c: never removed; `object-remove!` with `:delivered` leaves the same record shape. Done: tests pass; `docs/architecture.md` "Removal of objects" names the functions as built and the tests.

## Docs and `AGENTS.md`
- `docs/architecture.md`: "Files in load order" (`places.lisp` moved and described, `physics.lisp`, `clock.lisp`, `hazards.lisp` extended as built), "Rails", "Removal of objects", "Checkpoints", "The clock" (step order), "Integrator" (cost with objects), "Testing" (where each test lives). Each piece updates its sections.
- `docs/design.md` "Rigid bodies": the two axes of `decision 111`.
- `AGENTS.md`: "Layout" lists `places.lisp` and the object type in `physics.lisp`; the intro paragraph stays until the milestone''s last part.

## Done when
- The four pieces are done; `nix build` and `scripts/test` pass with `scripts/test` near its ~15 s.
- From the start state, every object of `*objects*` has a state from `object-state-into!` at any t up to 2050 except after its removal; flying the ship near any of them promotes it, and leaving demotes it, deterministically.', '2026-10-15T06:08:37Z', 157);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (24, 3, 'Grapple, contact, berthing, towing, deliveries and refuelling', 690, 'Part 2 of `plan 3: masterplan`: everything that happens when the Mule touches an object. The grapple, contact with bounce and death, capture into a stack or onto a berth, release, towing, deliveries at Highport''s plant, propellant transfer, SHIP messages from the simulation and the in-memory checkpoint on berthing. It stands on `plan 23: Objects on rails and the Earth-orbit places` (the object type, `sim-objects`, promotion, `object-remove!`, the step order of `clock.lisp`). Later parts read it: targets and the prediction (`task 159`), the autopilot''s contact and latch aborts and its checkpoint (`task 160`), the flight scripts'' `:grapple`, `:release`, `:transfer`, `:approach` (`task 162`), the grapple''s meshes and the latched load drawn (`task 163`), the grapple camera, lamps and COMMS log (`task 166`).

The specification: `docs/design.md` "Grapple and towing", "Refuelling and resupply", "Grappling and berthing", "Towing", "Dying and what follows", "Controls", "Time warp" (drop-outs), "Messages and radio" (SHIP), "Rigid bodies: attitude, mass, shapes and fixtures", "Inertia"; `docs/architecture.md` "Collisions and the Sun" (contact), "Removal of objects" (delivery), "Time warp" (the contact drop-out), "Checkpoints", "The clock", "Testing" ("Warp, events and collisions": Contact, Berthing, Delivery, Checkpoints). Decisions: `decision 24: Grapple, not docking; towing in`, `decision 26: Refuelling and economy`, `decision 33: Grapple tolerances, contact and berthing`, `decision 34: How death works`, `decision 43: Warp drop-outs for contact and life support`, `decision 44: Contact, capture and berthing in the simulation`, `decision 53: Deliveries, the stranded offer, checkpoint floors and Skua Yard''s layout`, `decision 37: Message channels the story may use`, `decision 72: Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp`, `decision 74: Crash test on the step''s Hermite and the death state`, `decision 81: Input in milestone 2: held keys per frame, taps, gating and the shared key map`, `decision 86: Death view, LOST panel and restart in milestone 2`, `decision 110: Objects in the simulation: places.lisp''s load position, the object struct and promotion checks`; and this plan''s own `decision 112: Grapple, stack and berth in the sim: state, notices to the clock, the latest checkpoint` and `decision 113: Grapple and contact details the design leaves open`.

## Scope and limits
- In: the ship''s collision shapes and bound, the stack''s mass properties, the grapple''s states and G, the range finder values, contact (broad and narrow phase, grapple tip, bounce, death), capture (towable, berth), the berthed state, release and the excluded object, propellant transfer and B, deliveries, SHIP notices and the clock''s SHIP log, Y, the latest checkpoint and the LOST panel''s Return, the contact drop-out and the "no contact predicted" check, the contact death''s panel and flash.
- Out, to later parts: targets and the target''s relative state (`task 159`); the autopilot''s latch and contact aborts and its own checkpoint request (`task 160`, through this part''s flag); flight-script actions and `:approach` (`task 162`); meshes of the claws, floodlight, load and places (`task 163`); the grapple camera, MSTR and MSG lamps, the COMMS log''s drawing (`task 166`).
- Out, to `milestone 4`: restocking consumables (no life support before it; Tern Station''s berth only holds the ship), Peary Depot''s berth as a depot and receiving fixture. To `milestone 5`: checkpoint floors, files.

## Files, in load order
- `src/ship.lisp`: collision shapes, the stack''s mass properties, the range finder.
- `src/places.lisp`: a `depot` flag on `object-def` (Fulmar Depot, Highport).
- `src/clock.lisp`: the sim''s new fields, the step order''s additions, notices to messages and the SHIP log, the latest checkpoint.
- `src/hazards.lisp`: contact, capture, bounce, death by contact, release, deliveries, the berthed state held.
- `src/prediction.lisp`: the `:contact` drop-out event; `checkpoint-safe-p` (in `clock.lisp`) asks for it.
- `src/input.lisp`: G, B, Y in `*key-map*`; the locked engine''s message; the window title.
- `src/overlay.lisp`: the LOST panel for `:contact` and the Return line.
- `src/main.lisp`: Return and N restore, the contact death''s flash.
- Tests in `tests/ship.lisp`, `tests/clock.lisp`, `tests/hazards.lisp`, `tests/prediction.lisp`, `tests/input.lisp`, `tests/overlay.lisp`. No new file.

## The ship''s shapes and the stack (`ship.lisp`)
- Collision shapes (`*ship-collision-boxes*`, "Grappling and berthing"; centres in FRD from R): truss with tanks and engine 17.0 × 3.6 × 3.6 centred (6.5, 0, 0) (x −2.0 to 15.0, as THRIFT''s row in "Rigid bodies"); cab 4.5 × 3.0 × 3.0 at (12.75, 0, −3.5); grapple cone 1.6 × 1.6 × 1.6 at (15.8, 0, 0). The cone''s tip `*grapple-tip*` (16.6, 0, 0).
- `ship-mass-properties` takes the load (`decision 112`): the object''s mass, its tensor turned into ship axes by the relative quaternion, its CoM in ship axes from R; first += m p, second += R I Rᵀ + m (|p|² 𝟙 − p pᵀ); conses nothing (stack-allocated scratch as now). The design''s pose ("Inertia": the fixture plate at the tip, rock centre at 16.9 + r) is `stack-design-pose`, for tests.
- `stack-bound`: the largest distance from the stack''s CoM to a corner of the ship''s boxes and to the load''s bound sphere; recomputed per step from the current CoM (cheap: 24 corners).
- Range finder (`decision 113`): `(grapple-range-into! out sim)` → nil or a preallocated `grapple-reading` (object, fixture, range, closing speed, offset y and z, angle, relative rotation, and per value in tolerance or not) for the nearest free fixture ahead within 500 m; conses nothing; a pure function of the sim, for the cockpit, the overlay and `:approach`.

## The sim (`clock.lisp`, `decision 112`)
- New fields, copied by `copy-sim` and `sim-assign!` (nothing consed by the latter), `equalp` bit for bit: `grapple` (state `:stowed`/`:armed`/`:closing`/`:latched`, object index, fixture index, relative pose: position vec3 and quaternion, closing end time), `berthed`, `transfer`, `excluded` (object index or nil), `delivery` (nil or load, receiver, fixture, release time; immutable list), `notices` (list of (key text)), `checkpoint-request`.
- Step order in `sim-step-ticks!`, around `plan 23`''s: berthed, no `flight-step!` (the ship is set from the station after the objects'' steps: r = r_s + q_s p, v = v_s + ω_s × (q_s p), q = q_s ⊗ q_rel, ω in ship axes from the station''s), transfer m_p += min(10 h, 28,000 − m_p) exactly; else `flight-step!` with the stack''s mass properties; promoted objects stepped; a latched load''s stored state written from the stack; `t += h`; crash tests; contact (`hazards.lisp`); the delivery check; rails removals; promotion and demotion. A pending delivery''s due time (release + 600 s) is a landing time for `step-h` like the Δv cut-off (`decision 72`).
- `clock-step!` after each step: notices to `clock-message!` (key per kind) and to the SHIP log (`clock-log`: the last 200 (t text) entries, every `clock-message!` too; `clock-log-read`, the read mark), cleared from the sim; a checkpoint request taken as `clock-checkpoint` when `checkpoint-safe-p` (throttle off, not lost, no impact and no contact predicted), then cleared. The prediction''s copy clears notices and requests after each step.
- `clock-restart!` keeps `clock-checkpoint`; a new game clears it.

## Contact (`hazards.lisp`, `decision 44`, `decision 113`)
- As `docs/architecture.md` "Collisions and the Sun" sets it, after the crash tests, against every promoted object that is not latched, not the berth station while berthed and not excluded: broad phase on the relative Hermite (the crash test''s `hermite-set!`, `hermite-sag`) against `stack-bound` + the object''s bound; narrow phase on substeps of ≤ 0.25 m relative motion (translation plus |ω| × bound for each body), attitudes by slerp; the grapple tip first while armed (a free fixture''s plate crossed within 0.8 m of the pin), then boxes and spheres (15 separating axes box–box, closest point box–sphere; the stack''s shapes are the ship''s boxes plus the load''s); the first overlap bisected to 1e-4 s; normal of least penetration, the contact point the midpoint of the deepest points; only approaching contacts.
- Response at the grid point g at or before t_c: the step''s start restored from the scratch (saved only when a sphere is within reach, `decision 112`) and the step redone with `until` g (g = the step''s start: the response on the start state); then:
  - capture at a towable fixture: perfectly inelastic coupling (mass, CoM, v by mass; L about the new CoM kept, ω = I_stack⁻¹ L in ship axes), the object `:latched` at the pose of the moment, grapple `:closing` until t + 2 s then `:latched`; notice "LATCHED <NAME>";
  - capture at a berth fixture: the ship takes the fixture point''s velocity and the station''s rotation, `berthed`, throttle cut, rate damping, transfer off; notice "BERTHED <NAME>"; checkpoint request;
  - missed capture, or any other contact at a normal speed ≤ 1.0 m/s: the impulse of restitution 0.3 to both bodies'' v and ω (a berth or fixed object''s terms zero; while berthed the ship is part of the station); "NO CAPTURE: …" or the rating''s message as `decision 113` orders them;
  - above 1.0 m/s: death at t_c, the state from the Hermite, the `loss` cause `:contact` with the object and the normal speed (the loss struct gains `object`).
- `t` stays on the grid after every response but death (as the crash''s).
- Release (`(grapple-key! sim)` → nil or a refusal text; G): stowed ↔ armed; latched: refused above 0.5°/s or with the engine on; otherwise both bodies take the stack''s motion at their CoMs (v + ω × (r_i − r_cm), the same ω), the load back to `:promoted` (or the ship back to integration with the fixture point''s velocity), the grapple `:armed`, the object excluded until its shapes and the stack''s no longer overlap (a separating-axes test each step while excluded); a towable release starts the delivery check.
- Deliveries (`docs/architecture.md` "Removal of objects", `decision 53`): at a towable release, the load''s CoM within 100 m of a receiving fixture''s pin and ≤ 0.05 m/s relative to the receiver''s velocity at the pin: `delivery` pending; after each step the load within 150 m (its `object-state-into!`, rails or promoted), else cleared; cleared too by a new grapple of it or the receiver''s removal; at release + 600 s `object-remove!` with `:delivered`, notice "<NAME> DELIVERED". `object-remove!` writes "<NAME> BURNED UP" for `:burned-up`.
- Transfer (`(transfer-key! sim)` → nil or a refusal text; B): on only berthed at a depot''s fixture; off at full ("TANKS FULL") and at release.

## The contact drop-out (`prediction.lisp`, `decision 43`, `decision 113`)
- After each step of its copy the prediction finds the first time the stack''s and a promoted object''s bounding spheres come to meet on the step''s relative Hermite (the broad phase''s function, its sag bound), with the exclusions above; kept as a `:contact` event (object, time) beside `:impact`; `prediction-dropout-event` returns the earlier; the clock''s lowering message "WARP ×L: CONTACT IN <duration>" (`format-duration`). Objects on rails come into it as the copy promotes them.
- `checkpoint-safe-p` also requires no `:contact` event.

## Input, the panel and the flash
- `input.lisp`: G (`:grapple`) and B (`:transfer`) act unpaused and not lost at every level, ignored while a flight runs (`decision 87`''s gate); their refusal texts and "ENGINE LOCKED: BERTHED" (throttle or RCS keys while berthed, once per press) go to `clock-message!`; Y (`:acknowledge`) at any time but lost sets `clock-log-read`. The window title gains "G grapple · B refuel · Y ack".
- `overlay.lisp`: `lost-panel-lines` for `:contact` ("LOST: CONTACT", "<OBJECT NAME>  <UTC>", "SPEED <normal speed>"), and "RETURN  RESTART FROM CHECKPOINT" when the clock holds one.
- `main.lisp`: Return restores `clock-checkpoint` (else the start), N the start (clearing it); a contact death''s white flash in the emissive slot (`*contact-flash-colour*` #FFFFFF, radiance 1.0 ÷ exposure, full 0.1 s, 0 at 1 s).

## Pieces, in order
Each piece is one task: code, its tests, its docs; `nix build` and `scripts/test` pass. This part''s tests together stay under ~1.5 s (`docs/architecture.md` "Rules for tests"): `:point` gravity unless J2 or the Moon matters, objects placed promoted by hand in a 500 km orbit (a test helper in `tests/hazards.lisp`), runs of at most a few thousand fine steps or one coarse orbit.

1. **The stack and the ship''s shapes** (`ship.lisp`, `tests/ship.lisp`): collision boxes, tip, `ship-mass-properties` with a load, `stack-design-pose`, `stack-bound`, `grapple-range-into!`. Tests: wet + 50 t, dry + 50 t and wet + 100 t at the design pose against "Inertia" (mass exact, CoM within 0.005 m, I_xx, I_yy, I_zz within 0.5 %, I_xz within 1×10³ kg m²); with no load bit for bit today''s values; a load turned 90° about x gives the same tensor as its turned tensor added by hand within 1e-12 relative; conses nothing with a load; the range finder in front of Skua Yard 3''s fixture at 50 m, 0.2 m off, 5° and 0.3°/s gives those values within 1e-9 and flags each tolerance edge; a fixture behind the tip or beyond 500 m gives nil. Done: tests pass; "Files in load order" (`ship.lisp`) lists them as built.
2. **The sim''s fields, notices, the SHIP log, the latest checkpoint, G B Y** (`clock.lisp`, `places.lisp`, `input.lisp`, `overlay.lisp`, `main.lisp`; their tests): the fields of `decision 112`, copy and assign, notices drained by `clock-step!`, `clock-log`, `clock-checkpoint` on a request, Return and N, `grapple-key!` for stowed ↔ armed and its refusals, `transfer-key!`''s refusals, the depot flag, the key map and title, `object-remove!`''s "BURNED UP" notice. Tests: `copy-sim` `equalp` with every field set, `sim-assign!` conses nothing; a notice written in a step shows in `clock-messages` and the log with the step''s t, and not from the prediction''s copy; a request takes a checkpoint when safe and not with the throttle on; Return after a death restores it bit for bit, N the start, and `lost-panel-lines` reads "RESTART FROM CHECKPOINT"; G, B, Y through synthetic events (gating while paused, lost, a flight running); K-17''s removal writes "K-17 BURNED UP" once. Done: tests pass; `docs/architecture.md` "Checkpoints" lists the sim''s fields as built, "The clock" the notices and the log; `docs/design.md` "Controls" the title; `AGENTS.md` "Commands" ("Run") names G, B, Y.
3. **Contact: detection, bounce and death** (`hazards.lisp`, `clock.lisp`, `overlay.lisp`, `main.lisp`; `tests/hazards.lisp`, `tests/overlay.lisp`): broad and narrow phase, separating axes, bisection, the redo on the grid, the bounce impulse, the exclusions, death `:contact`, the panel, the flash. Tests ("Contact" in "Testing"): the truss box head-on into a 50 t sphere at 0.50 m/s: normal speed after 0.3 × before within 1e-9 relative, linear and angular momentum within 1e-12; against Tern Station the ship reverses to 0.15 m/s and the station''s state is bit for bit its own run''s; 0.99 m/s bounces, 1.01 m/s kills with t_c within 1e-4 s of straight-line motion; no tunnelling (the cone''s box past a 2.7 m sphere at 100 m/s, 1.0 m off centre, caught at the fine step and in one 2 s coarse step); touching or receding: no impulse; every response leaves `t` on the grid; 10,000 steps beside a promoted object out of reach cons nothing. Done: tests pass; "Collisions and the Sun" says "as built" with the functions; `docs/design.md` "Dying and what follows" the contact panel and flash.
4. **Capture, the stack, berthing** (`hazards.lisp`, `clock.lisp`; `tests/hazards.lisp`): the grapple tip test, the tolerances and messages, towable coupling, `:closing` and `:latched`, the latched load''s state, berth capture, the berthed state held, locked engine and RCS, the berth''s checkpoint request. Tests: capture inside tolerance (50 t fixture axis, 0.10 m/s, 0.1 m, 5°): stack mass 85,961 kg, CoM and tensor equal `ship-mass-properties` at the capture pose within 1e-9; momentum (0.2 m off, 0.25 m/s, both rotating 0.3°/s): linear and angular momentum about the system''s CoM within 1e-12 relative, kinetic energy falls; one tolerance just outside each (0.31 m/s, 0.31 m, 10.5°, 0.6°/s), 0.005 m/s, and Skua Yard 6 (150 t): a bounce with its message, no capture; berthed at Tern Station then one orbit at ×1,000: the pose relative to the fixture within 1e-6 m and 1e-9 rad, throttle and keys change nothing; a latched load''s `object-state-into!` follows the stack within 1e-9 m; G while closing refused. Done: tests pass; `docs/design.md` "Grappling and berthing" and `docs/architecture.md` "Collisions and the Sun" as built.
5. **Release, transfer and the berth checkpoint** (`hazards.lisp`, `clock.lisp`; `tests/hazards.lisp`, `tests/clock.lisp`): release of a load and of a berth, exclusion, transfer. Tests: after a towable release both bodies'' v equal the stack''s at their CoMs within 1e-12 relative and share ω; after a berth release the ship''s v equals the station''s at the fixture point within 1e-12 relative and, coasting 100 s, stays within 1 cm of the held pose''s path; no contact with the released object until the shapes separate, then contact again; refused at 0.6°/s and with the engine on; transfer at Fulmar Depot: m_p rises 10 kg/s × t exactly to 28,000 kg and stops ("TANKS FULL"), the same at ×1,000 on the grid; refused at Tern Station and unberthed; berthing at Fulmar Depot takes a checkpoint, and a death after it restores it bit for bit through Return. Done: tests pass; `docs/design.md` "Refuelling and resupply" as built.
6. **Deliveries** (`hazards.lisp`, `clock.lisp`; `tests/hazards.lisp`): the pending delivery, its landing time, the 150 m rule, the clearing rules, the removal and notice. Tests ("Delivery" in "Testing"): a 50 t load released by a wet Mule beside a promoted Highport (two-body), 80 m from the plant''s pin at 0.04 m/s: removed `:delivered` at release + 600 s exactly, the same `t` at the fine step and at ×1,000; at 0.06 m/s, 110 m out, a drift across 150 m before 600 s, or grappled again at 300 s: never removed; a checkpoint restored before the removal gives the same `t` bit for bit; "SKUA YARD 3 DELIVERED" once. Done: tests pass; "Removal of objects" as built.
7. **The contact drop-out** (`prediction.lisp`, `clock.lisp`; `tests/prediction.lisp`, `tests/clock.lisp`): the `:contact` event, the lowering message, `checkpoint-safe-p`. Tests: a ship coasting at 2 m/s towards a promoted 50 t sphere 5 km away at ×1,000 through `clock-frame!`: every step above ×1 at least 3 s × level from the event (less 0.05 s), ×1 at it, messages at each lowering; warp up refused within 3 s of it; berthed, latched and just released: no event; an overlap at the prediction''s start: no event; a rails object promoted on the copy is seen. Done: tests pass; "Time warp" and "Trajectory prediction" in `docs/architecture.md` as built.

## Docs and `AGENTS.md`
- `docs/architecture.md`: "Files in load order" (`ship.lisp`, `clock.lisp`, `hazards.lisp`, `prediction.lisp`, `input.lisp`, `overlay.lisp`, `main.lisp` as built), "Collisions and the Sun", "Removal of objects", "Time warp", "Trajectory prediction", "Checkpoints", "The clock", "Testing" (where each test lives and its measured time). Each piece updates its sections.
- `docs/design.md`: "Controls" (title, G B Y gating), "Grappling and berthing", "Refuelling and resupply", "Dying and what follows" (contact panel and flash, Return''s checkpoint), each as built, citing `decision 112` and `decision 113`.
