INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (29, 3, 'Meshes of the places, other ships and the grapple', 480, 'Part 7 of `plan 3: masterplan`: the looks of everything the Mule meets in Earth orbit. Meshes of the five places and their objects (Tern Station with AURORA 11, Fulmar Depot with TANKER 2, Highport with STUBBORN and THISTLE, Derelict K-17, Skua Yard''s six rocks, POD 14 and GRINDSTONE), their lights and lit fixtures, the Mule''s grapple at work (claws, floodlight, range finder), a latched load drawn with the stack, and the renderer drawing objects camera-relative from their double states with the exposure counting them. It stands on `plan 23: Objects on rails and the Earth-orbit places` (the object definitions: shapes, fixtures, attitude modes, `sim-objects`, `object-state-into!`) and `plan 24: Grapple, contact, berthing, towing, deliveries and refuelling` (the grapple''s states and relative pose, `berthed`, the stack''s mass properties with a load). Later parts read it: the map''s markers (`task 164`), the cockpit''s window view and the grapple camera (`task 165`, `task 166`), the screenshots of the Done when (`task 169`).

The specification: `docs/design.md` "How they look", "Other ships", "Earth-orbit points of interest", "Rigid bodies: attitude, mass, shapes and fixtures", "Grappling and berthing", "Views" (outside view, exposure); `docs/story.md` "Other ships" (names, registrations, liveries); `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order", "Rendering precision", "Floating-origin pitfalls", "The clock" (render state), "GPU tests", "Rules for tests". Decisions it builds on: `decision 27: Other ships`, `decision 47: Rigid bodies of the places`, `decision 80: The Mule''s meshes in numbers: geometry, draws, materials and lights`, `decision 100: The lights'' clock and the glows'' numbers`, `decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure`, `decision 79: Sunlight at a point and the sunlit ship in the exposure rule`, `decision 93: Exposure rule as built: hidden Sun, coverage within the view`, `decision 99: Earthshine from the lit part of the visible cap`, `decision 82: Outside view camera: angles in a frame, toggles that keep the picture, distance and easing`, `decision 4: Depth strategy`, `decision 5: Precision of positions`, `decision 11: Render pipelines are luv live shader pipelines`, `decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset`, `decision 110: Objects in the simulation: places.lisp''s load position, the object struct and promotion checks`, `decision 111: Fixture axes of Derelict K-17 and Skua Yard''s rocks`, `decision 112: Grapple, stack and berth in the sim: state, notices to the clock, the latest checkpoint`; and this plan''s own `decision 124: Meshes of the places: models, scenery liveries, lights, far dots, the grapple''s claws, floodlight and range finder`, which holds every number below that the docs leave open.

## Scope and limits
- In: one model per object definition with its scenery ships baked in, the standard fixture mesh, the new materials and the decal atlas, the places'' lights and lit fixtures, far objects as dots, objects in the render state (rails, promoted, latched, berthed), their draws and glows, the exposure and the outside view''s least distance with objects and a load, the Mule''s claws, floodlight and range-finder light, the Mule drawn about the stack''s CoM.
- Out, to later parts: the map''s object markers and labels (`task 164`); the cockpit, its view and the window''s glass from inside (`task 165`); the grapple camera''s render target (`task 166`; it draws this part''s scene from the cone and so sees the floodlight and the fixture''s target mark); screenshots of the presets (`task 169`).
- Out, to `milestone 4`: asteroid meshes (the rocks stay plain spheres in nets), Peary Depot, BAGGER 3; to `milestone 5`: THRIFT and TANKER 3 flying free (their models come for free from this part''s builders, their drawing with their slots).

## Files, in load order
- `src/clock.lisp`: the clock keeps the objects'' previous states (`clock-previous-objects`, a preallocated copy of `sim-objects`'' mutable parts, assigned with `object-assign!` where `clock-previous` is assigned today); rendering''s only need of the sim side; it changes no sim state.
- `src/meshes.lisp`: builders, the fixture, materials, the decal atlas, the places'' models and lights, the object draws and glows, the claws, floodlight and range finder.
- `src/post.lisp`: `exposure-target` counts objects and the load.
- `src/camera.lisp`: `outside-min-distance` with the load.
- `src/render.lisp`: the render state''s objects and grapple, `encode-frame` draws the objects after the Mule.
- `src/shaders.lisp`: the procedural materials (net, scorched, peeling foil, fixture target, Boreal band via material rows), the floodlight term in the hull and instanced fragment shaders, the claws'' per-frame instances.
- Tests in `tests/meshes.lisp`, `tests/render.lisp` (CPU and GPU), `tests/post.lisp`, `tests/camera.lisp`, `tests/clock.lisp`. No new file.

## Models (`meshes.lisp`, `decision 124`)
- Builders: `mule-model` becomes one case of `truss-ship-model` (truss length and bays, tank pairs with their x spans, cab or none, grapple or nose fixture, engines, RCS blocks, wings, radiators, decal row); the Mule''s output stays the same (7,302 hull vertices, 102 tubes, 11 boxes, 24 cones, `ship-bounding-radius` 12.57 m wet, 11.70 m dry). `tanker-model` from `docs/design.md` "Rigid bodies" (TANKER 3, parts: 24 m, 16 bays, two pairs LH2 x 0.50–8.10 and 12.20–19.80, LOX 8.50–11.80 and 20.20–23.50, nose fixture at 24.15). `capsule-model` (frustum Ø 1.5 → 3.9 over 2.5 m, service section Ø 3.9 × 2.0, two wings 1.0 × 3.0 m). `bake-model! out model xform` appends a model''s hull and instance groups (bases composed) and its lights under a transform.
- `fixture-mesh` (`decision 124`): plate, pin, guides, stand-off, the `fixture` material with its target mark; placed from a fixture''s pin and axis in `places.lisp`, so mesh and contact plate coincide.
- The places'' models, each in its object''s body axes about the CoM, from the parts of "Rigid bodies" and "Earth-orbit points of interest":
  - Tern Station: three modules Ø 4.2 × 8 at x −10.92, −2.92, 5.08, the node Ø 4.2 × 4 at 11.08, AURORA 11 nose-in on the node from x 13.08 to 17.58, the truss 1.5 × 28 × 1.5 across the middle module, radiators 4 × 10 m at y ±8, wings 8 × 25 m at y ±26.5, the berth fixture at (−10.92, 0, −2.40) −z; Boreal livery.
  - Fulmar Depot: the 30 m spine, two LH2 tanks Ø 6 × 14 and two LOX tanks Ø 4.5 × 6 at y ±3.9 in foil, the gold sunshade disc Ø 16 at the sunward end, cryocooler fins 6 × 2.5 at z ±2, two arms along ±z; fixture A free at (−13.98, 0, 4.30) +z, TANKER 2 berthed at B, its nose on (−13.98, 0, −4.30), its axis along +z.
  - Highport: the open truss 6.5 × 60 × 6.5 along y as instanced tubes, four tanks Ø 6 × 12 along y at y ±7 and ±21, the water plant 6 × 6 × 10 below it with two radiator fins 6 × 20 edge-on to the Sun, wings 40 × 20 at y ±50, three arms; the plant fixture free at (−3.28, 0, 3.41) −x; STUBBORN and THISTLE berthed at the arms (0.02, ±20.0, −11.39), grapple tip on the pin, their +x along +z.
  - Derelict K-17: the tank Ø 3.05 × 12.7, the bell 1.5 m out of the aft end (the Mule''s engine mesh), the adapter ring r 1.4 at x 5.80 (6.1 from the tank''s centre; the CoM sits 0.30 ahead of it, as the shapes'' box from −8.15 to 6.05 gives), the bright fixture at (6.35, 0, 0) +x; scorched and peeling.
  - POD 14 (the standard cargo pod): a box 4.0 × 2.5 × 2.5 with panel seams, the fixture at (2.30, 0, 0) +x, "POD 14" stencilled.
  - Skua Yard 1–6: spheres of their tabled radii under the net, the net''s frame (a square of four tubes 1.2 m round the stand-off) and the fixture at (r + 0.3, 0, 0); one model per rock.
  - GRINDSTONE (the rock mover): two 20 m Mule trusses at y ±1.8, two engines, two tanks Ø 3.04 × 17, the Mule cab on top at x 15.5–20 (the cairn only), two grapples, the netted rock Ø 10 at its tabled centre.
- `(object-model key)`: the model for a definition key, made once and cached; `model-extent` (the farthest vertex from the CoM, for culling and dots).
- Lights per model (`model-lights`: kind `:red`, `:green`, `:strobe`, `:lamp`, position, the object''s strobe phase offset), the free berth fixtures'' four lamps, as `decision 124` lists them.
- Size budget: each place''s hull ≤ 40,000 vertices and ≤ 2,000 instances; measured and written in "Files in load order".

## Objects in the render state (`clock.lisp`, `render.lisp`)
- `render-state` gains `objects`, a preallocated vector of `object-render` (r, v, q, w double, drawn flag, sun-fraction at the object), one per `sim-objects` entry, and the grapple (`render-state-grapple`: state, object index, the claws'' shown angle, floodlight and range finder on); filled by `render-state-update!`, consing nothing:
  - on rails at both ends of the step: `object-state-into!` at the render time t_r (exact);
  - promoted (or promoted or demoted in the last step): the cubic Hermite and slerp of `render-state-hermite!` between the previous state (the previous copy''s promoted state, or its rails at t_prev) and the current;
  - latched, and the station while berthed: the ship''s render pose composed with the grapple''s relative pose (`decision 112`), so neither moves against the Mule;
  - removed: not drawn.
- The claws'' angle: from the sim''s grapple state and closing end time at t_r; between stowed and armed it eases at 90° per real second in `main.lisp`''s `draw` beside `lights-t`, snapping in captures (`decision 124`).

## Draws (`meshes.lisp`, `render.lisp`, `shaders.lisp`)
- `encode-ship` takes the stack''s CoM (`ship-mass-properties` with the latched load), so R = r − q c stays right with a load.
- `object-gpu`: per model its hull and instance buffers, made once with the renderer (`make-frame-renderer`); per target an `object-slot` per object (its block, bind group, glow buffer) in the frame slot; the `:ship-mesh` and `:ship-instanced` pipelines and the shared material table and decal atlas serve all of them; no new pipeline but what the claws need.
- `object-uniforms!`: the ship block''s layout for an object: rows of its q, its CoM relative to the eye in double then single (`decision 5`), the Sun with (1 AU / d)² × its own `sun-visible-fraction`, earthshine from its position (`earth-lit-cap`), the eye in its body axes, lights on, no emission, identity trim; pure, consing nothing.
- `encode-objects` after the Mule in the scene pass, every view: each drawn object whose projected `model-extent` reaches 2 px and lies in front of the near plane gets its mesh; below 2 px its dot (`decision 124`); its lights through the `:ship-glow` pipeline (strobes offset per object, fixture lamps, scenery ships'' lights); depth against Earth and the Mule as the ship''s draws.
- The floodlight: two vec4s more in the ship block (lamp position and intensity, direction and cone cosines in the drawn body''s axes, zero when off), added in the hull and instanced fragment shaders of every draw; its lamp glow; the range finder''s aperture glow and its spot from `range-finder-spot` (a CPU ray from the tip along +x against the promoted objects'' shapes within 500 m, consing nothing).
- The claws: three instances written per frame from the shown angle into a small per-target instance buffer, drawn with the box group''s primitive; the static box group keeps the RCS blocks.
- `post.lisp`: `exposure-target` counts each drawn object within the view like the ship (`decision 79`): a sphere of its bound, coverage × its sun-fraction ≥ 0.005 gives the sunlit exposure; the latched load joins the ship''s term. `camera.lisp`: `outside-min-distance` is 1.2 × the larger of today''s box-corner radius and the load''s CoM distance from the stack''s CoM plus its bound.
- Frame cost: measure the CPU encode time at Skua Yard (nine objects promoted, eight in view) and at Highport; target ≤ 1 ms; write it in "Files in load order" (`render.lisp`).

## Pieces, in order
Each piece is one task: code, its tests, its docs; `nix build` and `scripts/test` pass. CPU tests in `tests/meshes.lisp` stay under ~0.5 s together (models built once per test run); the GPU checks join `render-frames`'' one hidden canvas at 320×200 (`with-capture-game`), ≤ ~1 s more in all, so `scripts/test` stays near ~15 s.

