INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (139, 14, 'physics.lisp: fewer ephemeris reads per :full RK4 step', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-14, src/physics.lisp, docs/architecture.md: rk4-step! reads Moon and Sun once per distinct stage time (6 reads, not 8) via earth-gravity-into!; bit-for-bit identical after 200k :full steps; :full 530→470 ns integrated, 460→395 ns kinematic; scripts/test passes (2321)', '`rk4-step!` in `src/physics.lisp` with `:full` gravity costs ~530 ns per step (measured by `task 99: physics.lisp: the RK4 step, exhaustion, kinematic attitude and the drift tests`, `docs/architecture.md` "Integrator"), ~350 ns of it the eight ephemeris-cache reads (`body-state-into` for the Moon and the Sun at each of the four RK4 stages, via `gravity-into!`). `docs/architecture.md` "Rules for tests" sized test runs at ~200 ns per full ship step, and `plan 14: Ship, integrator and flight computer` keeps its tests under 3 s (task 99''s already take ~1.9 s), so `controls.lisp`''s hold tests at `:full` are tight.

- Make `:full` cheaper without changing results bit for bit where possible: stages 2 and 3 share t + h/2, so read the Moon and Sun once for both (six reads instead of eight); consider a `gravity-into!` variant that takes the third bodies'' positions, read by `rk4-step!` once per distinct stage time.
- Keep the step path consing nothing (`tests/physics.lisp` `rk4-cons-nothing-and-deterministic`), and the drift and gravity tests passing unchanged.
- Measure again with the loop described in "Integrator" (1M inlined steps, thrust and torque, best of 5) and update the cost line there.', '2026-10-14T23:40:40Z', '2026-10-15T00:12:43Z', 99, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (140, 15, 'tests/physics.lisp: rk4-cons-nothing-and-deterministic measured 32,768 bytes once', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-14, tests/package.lisp, tests/physics.lisp, tests/controls.lisp, tests/clock.lisp, tests/ship.lisp, tests/hazards.lisp, tests/prediction.lisp, docs/architecture.md: a measurement artefact, not real consing. SBCL 2.6.7 counts allocation per 32 KB region (one 48-byte make-array reads 0 or 32,768); full-steps'' setup (vec3s, mat3-inverse) conses a few hundred bytes per call, so 3 of 300 measurements read 32,768; the step itself conses nothing. Every conses-nothing test now uses least-bytes-consed (the least of three runs; stateful runs split into three chunks with the reference run chunked alike); docs/architecture.md ''Rules for tests'' says so. 20 consecutive scripts/test runs passed (2675 checks each). Follow-up: task 141 (the Moon consing test is too short to see per-step consing).', 'Found by `task 103: clock.lisp: the sim state, the start state, sim-step! and checkpoints`: in one of four `scripts/test` runs (2026-10-14) `rk4-cons-nothing-and-deterministic` (`tests/physics.lisp`) failed with "32768 bytes" (bound 1,024); the next three runs passed with no change in between. 32,768 bytes is one allocation region, so it looks like a measurement artefact (a region opened, or another thread consing between the two `sb-ext:get-bytes-consed` reads) rather than the step consing.

- Reproduce: run `scripts/test` in a loop (~6 s each) until it fails, or run that test alone many times in one image after the others.
- Find out whether `rk4-step!` / `full-steps` really conse (e.g. `sb-aprof` or `(sb-ext:get-bytes-consed)` over 100 repeats) or whether the measurement is noisy.
- If noisy: make every "conses nothing" test in `tests/physics.lisp`, `tests/controls.lisp` and `tests/clock.lisp` robust in the same way (e.g. take the minimum over three measured runs, or run `(sb-ext:gc)` before measuring) and keep the 1 KB bound.
- Done when: 20 consecutive `scripts/test` runs pass.', '2026-10-15T00:19:39Z', '2026-10-15T00:54:20Z', 103, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (141, 15, 'tests/hazards.lisp: crash-test-cons-nothing-near-the-moon is too short to see per-step consing', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-14, tests/hazards.lisp: the Moon allocation test runs 2,048 fine steps per measured run (was 33 coarse); checked that one boxed double per step in the Moon branch fails it (32,640+ bytes), reverted; the test takes 21 ms; scripts/test passes (2,675)', 'Found by `task 140: tests/physics.lisp: rk4-cons-nothing-and-deterministic measured 32,768 bytes once`: SBCL 2.6.7 counts allocation per 32 KB region, so `least-bytes-consed` (`tests/package.lisp`, `docs/architecture.md` "Rules for tests") only sees per-step consing once a run has 2,048 steps or more (16 B × 2,048 = 32 KB). `crash-test-cons-nothing-near-the-moon` (`tests/hazards.lisp`) runs 3 × 33 coarse steps (before task 140: 100), so a crash test that consed 16 bytes per step near the Moon would still pass.

- Make each of the three measured runs at least 2,048 steps with the Moon branch active every step: e.g. at a finer step (×1 or ×4, still within the Moon''s least distance) or from a start far enough out that 2,048 coarse steps don''t reach the Moon; keep the 1 KB bound and the "not lost" check.
- Check it catches consing: temporarily cons one double-float per step in the Moon branch, and the test must fail; then revert.
- Keep within the part''s 1.5 s test budget (`plan 15: Clock, warp, hazards and events`).
- Done when: `scripts/test` passes.', '2026-10-15T00:54:06Z', '2026-10-15T00:56:09Z', 140, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (142, 16, 'render.lisp and gpu.lisp: release per-target frame resources and colour views of swapchain textures dropped by a resize', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-14, src/render.lisp (note-swapchain-target: on a new canvas-extent, forget-target the old swapchain textures, frame slots and colour views), src/main.lisp (draw calls it), tests/render.lisp (three resize-canvas calls on the hidden canvas: frame slots and colour views stay at the image count, 4; 16 without the fix), docs/architecture.md, docs/luv-capabilities.md, docs/luv-wishlist.md (swapchain generation). scripts/test passes, 11.9 s. ./sly play: the tiling WM pins the window at 959x1010 so resize-canvas did not change the extent there; counts stayed 4/4. Not covered: a swapchain rebuild at the same extent (no public signal in luv).', '`render.lisp`''s `frame-renderer` keeps each target''s uniform buffer and bind group in a luv `canvas-frame-resource-cache` (`frame-resources`), and `gpu.lisp`''s `color-view` caches a view per texture. Capture targets are released in `luv:cleanup-capture` (`forget-target`, `src/main.lisp`), but when the window is resized and the swapchain''s textures are replaced, the old textures'' entries stay until `stop` (a small leak per resize).
- Find how luv signals swapchain recreation (`../luv/hal/canvas.lisp`, `../luv/hal/vulkan/canvas.lisp`; how Luvcraft evicts its drawable-keyed state, `../luv/wiki/gpu-architecture.org` #5OROXO) and evict stale keys through `forget-target` (e.g. when `luv:canvas-extent` changes, clear the cache and the colour views of foreign textures before encoding).
- Test: a CPU-free check is hard; at least a GPU test in `tests/render.lisp` that resizes a hidden canvas, if luv allows it, and counts `luv:canvas-frame-resource-count` staying at the swapchain''s image count.
- Done: `scripts/test` passes; `./sly play`, resizing the window a few times, `luv:canvas-frame-resource-count` of the renderer''s cache stays bounded.', '2026-10-15T01:11:25Z', '2026-10-15T01:38:26Z', 109, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (143, 16, 'tests: *earth-crash-radius* used in tests/clock.lisp before tests/hazards.lisp defines it', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-14, tests/package.lisp, tests/hazards.lisp: *earth-crash-radius* defined in package.lisp; scripts/test passes (2796), no Undefined variable warning', 'Loading `luv-in-space/tests` warns "Undefined variable: LUV-IN-SPACE.TESTS::*EARTH-CRASH-RADIUS*": `tests/clock.lisp` (the dive tests, around lines 352 and 386) and `tests/prediction.lisp` use it, but `tests/hazards.lisp` (loaded after `clock`) defines it with `defparameter`. The tests pass because it is bound by run time. (Filed in this plan because `plan 15: Clock, warp, hazards and events` is closed.)
- Move the `defparameter` to `tests/package.lisp` (or to the top of `tests/clock.lisp`) and remove it from `tests/hazards.lisp`.
- Done: `scripts/test` passes and its output has no "Undefined variable" warning.', '2026-10-15T01:11:29Z', '2026-10-15T01:38:59Z', 109, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (144, 16, 'gpu.lisp: destroy-renderer leaves colour views of swapchain and capture textures to the GC', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-14, src/gpu.lisp: destroy-renderer destroys cached colour views (decision recorded superseding decision 91''s foreign-view clause); scripts/test 2796 passed, 0 ''Leaked GPU'' lines; scripts/screenshot fine', '`destroy-renderer` (`src/gpu.lisp`) clears `renderer-color-views` without destroying the views ("views of foreign textures go with the device", `decision 91: Render targets in gpu.lisp: cached per name`). luv''s leak check then reports them when a GC reclaims them: since `task 110: assets.lisp, the Blue Marble and its packaging` decodes a 58 MB texture array at every renderer start, `scripts/test` triggers a GC between the two hidden canvases and prints four times `Leaked GPU VULKAN-GPU-TEXTURE-VIEW: it was reclaimed by the garbage collector instead of being destroyed explicitly.` (the first canvas''s swapchain and capture views). Before task 110 no GC ran there, so the message did not appear.

- Fix: in `destroy-renderer` (or `destroy-frame-renderer` in `src/render.lisp`, before the canvas closes) `luv:destroy` each cached colour view, then `clrhash`; check that luv''s deferred destroy is safe while the device still lives (`stop` in `src/main.lisp` closes the canvas first, then destroys the renderer, then the device — reorder if destroying a view of a gone swapchain image fails).
- Supersede decision 91''s sentence on foreign views with `pm decision record` if the rule changes.
- Related: `task 142: render.lisp and gpu.lisp: release per-target frame resources and colour views of swapchain textures dropped by a resize` covers the resize case; do this together if that task is still open.
- Done: `scripts/test 2>&1 | grep -c "Leaked GPU"` prints 0; tests pass.', '2026-10-15T01:19:32Z', '2026-10-15T01:39:55Z', 110, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (145, 17, 'post.lisp: a round Sun glare instead of the bloom chain''s rounded square', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, src/shaders.lisp (disc-form: round 19-tap kernel for bloom-up), src/post.lisp, tests/render.lisp (glare roundness check: row vs diagonal x sqrt 2 within 5 %; before 8 % at 320x200, now 2 %), docs/architecture.md, docs/design.md, docs/luv-capabilities.md; decision 98: Bloom''s way up: a round 19-tap kernel. scripts/test 13.6 s, 2969 passed; scripts/screenshot fine.', 'The Sun''s glare from `src/post.lisp`''s bloom chain (`decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure`; the Sun from `task 114: impostors.lisp part one: impostor machinery, the Sun, the Moon, sun-visible-fraction`) is a white rounded square about 14° wide in the 50° outside view at the sunlit exposure: the 4.6e4 disc saturates everything within the 1/16 level''s 3×3 tent, and the tent''s square footprint shows. Seen with `./sly play` and a camera-fn looking at the Sun, and in captures of `(body-camera :sun 50)` from `tests/render.lisp` at u = 340° (`start-sim-at 340`). `docs/design.md` "The Sun" wants a round disc with glare.

- Make the glow round: e.g. a radially symmetric upsample (a 13-tap or disc-shaped kernel instead of the 3×3 tent), one more level (1/32), or per-level weights that fall off so the far levels do not saturate; keep `decision 78: Sky in numbers: stars, Sun, Moon and Earth''s look` (glare only from bloom, no threshold) and `*bloom-strength*` unless a decision changes them.
- Check: `tests/render.lisp`''s glare check (pixels 20 px from the Sun''s centre more than 30 above the background) and bloom checks still pass; a new check that the glow''s extent along a row and along a diagonal (×√2) from the Sun''s centre differ by under 15 %; `scripts/test` under ~15 s.
- Docs: `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Sun glare and brightness" (the shape as built), `docs/design.md` "The Sun".', '2026-10-15T02:11:24Z', '2026-10-15T02:28:39Z', 114, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (146, 19, './sly play: close the window when the game requests a quit', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-14, src/main.lisp (play with a quit watcher thread, stop idempotent under a lock), src/package.lisp, scripts/sly-client.lisp (play calls luv-in-space:play), AGENTS.md; checked live: Escape and Control+Q through call-on-canvas-thread/handle-event end the game, ./sly status says nothing is playing, play works again', 'Escape, Control+Q and the window''s close button only set `game-quit-requested-p` (`src/input.lisp` `handle-event`, task 119: input.lisp part one: the shared held-key map, the event path, taps, focus and quit). `nix run` closes because `run` (`src/main.lisp`) polls `running-p`; in `./sly play` (`scripts/sly-client.lisp` `run-play` calls `luv-in-space:start` and returns) nothing polls it, so the window stays open and `./sly status` still says playing.

- Make a quit request close the game in the live image too: e.g. `start` takes a `:on-quit` or the play form starts a thread that waits for `(not (running-p))` and then calls `stop` off the canvas thread (`stop` must not run on the canvas thread, see the comment above `handle-event`), and sets `*game*` nil so `./sly play` works again.
- Check: `./sly start`, `./sly play`, `./sly eval` of a synthetic Escape press through `call-on-canvas-thread` and `handle-event`; within a second `./sly status` says nothing is playing; same for Control+Q (`:q` with `:control`); `./sly stop`. No process left running.', '2026-10-15T03:18:29Z', '2026-10-15T03:32:31Z', 119, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (147, 20, 'Non-consing state->orbit for the map''s per-frame ribbon', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, src/orbits.lisp (state->orbit-into!, writable orbit slots, inline wrap-angle/wrap-signed/true->eccentric/true->hyperbolic, wrap-signed''s fround as an inline ties-to-even), src/ribbons.lisp (ribbon''s orbit slot, encode-ribbon conses nothing), tests/orbits.lisp, docs/architecture.md. Checked bit for bit against the old state->orbit on 52 states (ellipses, circular, equatorial, hyperbolic, near-parabolic); scripts/test 5936 passed', '`src/ribbons.lisp` `encode-ribbon` (task 125) calls `state->orbit` (`src/orbits.lisp`) every map frame, which conses an `orbit` struct and several vec3s (a few hundred bytes a frame). `plan 20: The basic map` wants per-frame paths that cons nothing.

- Add a non-consing variant, e.g. `(state->orbit-into! out r v mu time)` writing a preallocated orbit (the struct''s slots are `:read-only`; make them writable or add a mutable twin) using `vec3-cross!` and scratch vec3s, same results bit for bit as `state->orbit` (test in `tests/orbits.lisp` over ellipses, circular, equatorial and hyperbolic states).
- Use it in `encode-ribbon` with an orbit kept in the `ribbon` struct or the frame renderer; the map markers (task 127) can share it.
- Test: 1,000 calls of the variant cons nothing (as `tests/ribbons.lisp` checks points and instances).
- `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order": drop the note that `state->orbit` conses each frame in the `ribbons.lisp` entry.', '2026-10-15T03:56:23Z', '2026-10-15T04:25:02Z', 125, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (148, 20, 'Map labels readable over bright ground', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, src/overlay.lisp (label-backing!, map-marker-vertices! draws backings, then shapes, then glyphs; decision 104: Map labels on a near-black backing), tests/overlay.lisp label-backing, tests/render.lisp marker-checks backing pixel, docs/design.md Views -> Map labels. scripts/test 5945 passed; scripts/screenshot ok. No live ./sly check run; the GPU check covers the backing pixel.', 'The map''s marker labels (`task 127`, `src/overlay.lisp` `map-marker-vertices!`, `decision 84: Map ribbons and markers: osculating orbit, adaptive segments, overlay markers and visibility`) are paper white #E8E4D8 dot-matrix text with no backing. Seen in `./sly play` with the map open on the start state: the "AP 400 KM" label over Antarctica''s sunlit ice is barely readable.

- Choose a backing that keeps colours exact (the overlay has no blending): e.g. a 1 px near-black #0A0C0C outline around each lit dot, or an opaque #0A0C0C box behind the label as the readout has (`decision 85: Minimal readout: layout, formats, colours and alarms`); record the choice as a decision.
- Draw it in `map-marker-vertices!` before the label''s glyphs; `place-map-labels!` (`decision 103: Map labels: placement order and overlap`) keeps its boxes as they are or grows them by the backing.
- Tests: `tests/overlay.lisp` (the backing''s pixels around a label are #0A0C0C, the dots still #E8E4D8; conses nothing); the GPU check in `tests/render.lisp` `marker-checks` still passes.
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "Views" → Map, the labels line.', '2026-10-15T04:20:31Z', '2026-10-15T04:27:36Z', 127, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (149, 20, 'render.lisp: define *marker-max-vertices* before frame-renderer uses it', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-14, src/render.lisp: *marker-max-vertices* moved above defclass frame-renderer; forced load of luv-in-space prints no caught WARNING; scripts/test 5945 passed', 'Compiling `luv-in-space` warns `undefined variable: LUV-IN-SPACE::*MARKER-MAX-VERTICES*`: `src/render.lisp` uses it in `frame-renderer`''s `marker-overlay` `:initform` (line ~131) but its `defparameter` comes later (line ~151). It works at run time, but the full warning hides new ones in the build output.

- Move the `defparameter *marker-max-vertices*` above the `defclass frame-renderer` (or into `overlay.lisp` / `ribbons.lisp` next to the marker parameters).
- Done: `(asdf:load-system "luv-in-space" :force t)` prints no `caught WARNING` from `src/render.lisp`; `scripts/test` passes.', '2026-10-15T04:25:02Z', '2026-10-15T04:28:30Z', 147, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (150, 21, 'Rate meter: no ACT caution for a second after each warp change', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-14, src/clock.lisp (rate-meter-reset! at each warp change, pause/resume and clock-restart!; clock-actual-rate nil until a second is metered), src/overlay.lisp (ACT only with a metered rate), tests/clock.lisp, tests/overlay.lisp (x1 to x4 after 60 frames shows no ACT one frame later, nor after pause/resume; 490,000 still does), docs/design.md Instruments, docs/architecture.md frame. scripts/test 6219 passed, ~21 s (task 153).', '`clock-actual-rate` (`src/clock.lisp`) averages the rate meter''s last second of frames, so after every warp change the readout''s `! ACT ×<rate>` (`readout-lines`, `src/overlay.lisp`, `decision 85: Minimal readout: layout, formats, colours and alarms`) flashes amber for about a second: seen live in task 129, `WARP ×4 ! ACT ×1.000` right after `clock-warp-up` from ×1.
- Fix: have the meter count only frames at the current level (reset the ring in `clock-set-level-index!`, or record the level per frame and stop summing at a different one), or have `readout-lines` show ACT only once the meter holds a full second at the level.
- Pause/unpause likewise: frames while paused record 0 game seconds.
- Test (`tests/clock.lisp` or `tests/overlay.lisp`): a clock warped up from ×1 to ×4 after 60 frames at ×1, one frame later, shows no `ACT`; one metered at 490,000 over a second at ×1,000,000 still does.', '2026-10-15T04:35:05Z', '2026-10-15T04:52:29Z', 129, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (151, 21, 'Captures show WARP PAUSED in the readout because the capture pauses the clock', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-14, src/main.lisp, src/capture.lisp, tests/render.lisp, docs/architecture.md; decision 106: Captures freeze the game, not the clock''s pause; scripts/test 6220 passed (~19 s, task 153 tracks it); scripts/screenshot shows WARP ×1', 'Follow-up of `task 130: Draw the readout in the overlay pass, capture-state :readout, remove PLACEHOLDER(80)`.

Captures (`capture-state`, `capture-game-state`, `src/capture.lisp`) pause the game''s clock so the picture shows exactly the given state (`decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset`). Since the readout is drawn, every capture''s WARP row reads `WARP ×1 PAUSED` (see `scripts/screenshot /tmp/r.png`), though the player never paused; part 10''s flight-script screenshots (`plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when`) would all say PAUSED.

## What
- Decide and record how a capture shows the warp row: e.g. `capture-game-state` draws the readout from the clock as it was before the capture paused it (a `:paused` key on the capture, default the sim''s own: not paused), or the capture freezes the sim by another means than `clock-paused` (the canvas thread not stepping while capturing).
- Keep the capture showing exactly the given state (no steps taken).
- Test in `tests/render.lisp` or `tests/overlay.lisp`: the start state''s capture readout''s WARP line is `WARP ×1` (compute via `readout-lines` on the game''s state as `readout-checks` does).', '2026-10-15T04:40:04Z', '2026-10-15T04:54:03Z', 130, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (152, 21, 'Death glow reads yellow-white, not orange: retune *death-glow-radiance*', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, src/main.lisp, tests/overlay.lisp, docs/design.md: *death-glow-radiance* 4.0 → 1.0 (decision 107: Death glow radiance 1.0, partly superseding decision 86). Captures of a lost sim at the peak, lit and in Earth''s shadow, at 4.0/1.5/1.0/0.75/0.5: 1.0 is orange in shadow and on the sunlit truss, wings, tanks and engine; the sunlit white cab stays pale (emission adds to sunlight). scripts/test passes (6220), task 132''s redness check included.', '`task 131: Death record, game-restart! and the orange death glow in main.lisp` built the glow as `decision 86: Death view, LOST panel and restart in milestone 2` sets it: `*death-glow-colour*` #FF6A1A × `*death-glow-radiance*` 4.0 ÷ the exposure into the ship''s emissive slot (`death-glow!` in `src/main.lisp`). After the tone map that is 4 × the colour, so the ACES curve saturates it: a capture of a lost sim at the peak shows the Mule glowing yellow-white with an orange fringe from bloom, not orange.

- Reproduce: a `new-game-sim` with v scaled by 0.9, `sim-step!` at ×1000 until `sim-lost-p`, `capture-state` of it (the death time is the capture''s first frame, so the glow is at its peak).
- Retune `*death-glow-radiance*` (try 0.5–1.5, `./sly eval` shows it on the next frame) until the peak reads orange over both the lit and the shadowed ship, keeping the curve (full 1 s, linear to 0 at 3 s); record the new number by superseding the radiance in `decision 86` (`pm decision record`), update `docs/design.md` "Dying and what follows" → "What the player sees" and the 4.0 / 2.0 values in `tests/overlay.lisp` `death-glow-radiance`.
- Keep `task 132`''s GPU check (the ship redder at `:death-elapsed` 0 than at 3.5 s) passing.', '2026-10-15T04:44:19Z', '2026-10-15T04:56:29Z', 131, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (153, 21, 'scripts/test takes ~17 s; bring it under ~15 s and fix AGENTS.md''s ~5 s', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, tests/render.lisp (ribbon-checks: near-test marks a pixel mask once instead of searching 2048 ring points per pixel, 2 s -> ~0), tests/bodies.lisp (lens-area against a 100,000-slice midpoint integral within 1e-6 instead of 1e6 Monte Carlo samples within 2e-3), tests/assets.lisp (blue-marble-eighth reuses blue-marble-decodes'' decode, typed channel-means), AGENTS.md (Test ~12 s), docs/architecture.md ''Rules for tests'' (measured time). scripts/test 16.9 s -> 11.6-11.7 s wall (two runs), 6220 passed; in-image suite 11.9 -> 9.6 s, render-frames 4.8 -> 3.1 s; physics drift tests (~2 s) left at their table''s runs. nix flake check passes (33 s with the image rebuilt).', '`plan 21: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel` asks that `scripts/test` stay under ~15 s; measured on 2026-10-14 (task 132) it takes ~17 s wall with compiled fasls (19 s before task 132, which did not add to it), while `AGENTS.md` "Commands" → Test still says ~5 s.

- Profile the suite per test (Parachute''s report or `(time …)` around each `define-test`), find the slow ones (likely long time-warp physics runs in `tests/hazards.lisp`, `tests/clock.lisp`, `tests/prediction.lisp`, and the GPU `render-frames` in `tests/render.lisp`).
- Shorten them without weakening what they check (shared fixtures computed once, e.g. the deorbit loss built by `tests/render.lisp`''s `lost-sim` and `tests/input.lisp`''s `lost-game`; fewer frames per capture), or move very long runs behind an opt-in.
- Update `AGENTS.md` "Commands" → Test (and `docs/architecture.md` "Testing" if it states a time) with the measured time.', '2026-10-15T04:50:45Z', '2026-10-15T05:02:17Z', 132, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (154, 22, 'Flight screenshots: the impact marker and red arc in deorbit''s map PNG, the AP marker in raise-orbit''s', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, flights/deorbit.sexp (entry-map on the map after entry: red arc and IMPACT marker visible, AP 380 KM), flights/raise-orbit.sexp (at-apoapsis on the map after the :before 18.3 wait: AP 793 KM beside the ship), both with a PNG list in their comment; docs/architecture.md Scripts by milestone, AGENTS.md (five PNGs for raise-orbit, ~4 s unchanged). PNGs checked from scripts/fly into /tmp, deleted; scripts/test passes (6372 checks, ~16 s).', 'Found by `task 138: Milestone 2 Done when: check from a clean checkout`. `plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when` asks `scripts/fly`''s PNGs to show "the map''s ring and ellipses, the impact marker and red arc"; two map PNGs miss what they are meant to show.

## What fails
- `scripts/fly deorbit <dir>`: `02-falling.png` (the map, taken right after the deorbit burn at T+78.7 s) shows the cyan ring and "AP 400 KM" at the ship, but no red arc and no IMPACT marker: the orbit''s low part (below the 100 km crash radius) and the impact point lie on the far side, behind Earth from the map''s opening pose (`decision 83`: az −45°, el 30° from the ship''s r), so `map-markers` drops the impact as hidden and the ribbon''s red segments are depth-occluded. Not a rendering bug.
- `scripts/fly raise-orbit <dir>`: `02-transfer.png` (right after the first burn) shows the ring and "PE 400 KM" but not the AP marker (behind Earth), so the 800 km apoapsis shows only in the readout.
- Checked live: in `./sly play`, M pressed at the `entry` point of `deorbit` (altitude 150 km, ~4 min 47 s before the impact) shows the red arc and the IMPACT marker plainly, AP 380 KM on the near side.

## To do
- `flights/deorbit.sexp`: add `(:screenshot "entry-map" :view :map)` right after `(:screenshot "entry")` (or make the map shot there), keeping `falling`; the opening comment lists what each PNG shows.
- `flights/raise-orbit.sexp`: add a map screenshot where the AP marker is visible, e.g. `(:screenshot "at-apoapsis" :view :map)` right after `(:wait :apoapsis :before 18.3)` (the ship at its AP, the marker beside it), before the second burn.
- Look at the new PNGs (`scripts/fly deorbit /tmp/x`, `scripts/fly raise-orbit /tmp/y`): the red arc and IMPACT marker, and "AP 8xx KM", visible; delete the directories after.
- Keep the scripts'' tests green (`tests/flights.lisp` `flights-scripts`: records equal twice, every expectation passing; any test counting checkpoints); `scripts/test`.
- Docs: `docs/architecture.md` "Scripts by milestone" → `milestone 2` lists the new screenshots; `AGENTS.md` "Commands" (the PNG count for `raise-orbit`: "four PNGs" becomes five) and the time if it changes.', '2026-10-15T05:55:44Z', '2026-10-15T05:57:28Z', 138, 0);
