INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (254, 35, 'Live fly with screenshots: fly :shots, the pending shot and the fly-shots thread', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 4 of `plan 35: Flight screenshots, the live runs and the Done when`. Needs piece 1. Rules: `decision 131: Flight screenshots and live runs in milestone 3: the screen state, screenshot waits, live shots, the cockpit capture script`, `decision 109: Live fly: the restart at once, Return and N ignored, the record in ./sly log`, `decision 87: Flight scripts in milestone 2: additions, apsis conditions, live fly and scripts/fly`, `decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset`.

Why: a `./sly screenshot` cannot be timed at ×100,000; the in-game Done when needs each preset flown once with PNGs.

## What
- `src/flights.lisp`: `(fly name &key (game *game*) shots)`; without `shots` as now (no pause). With `shots` a directory:
  - at a `:screenshot` step (and `:approach`''s 20 m shots) the hook records the entry as headless, sets the flight''s pending shot and the clock''s pause;
  - a thread of the image, started by `fly`, named `fly-shots`, waits for a pending shot, calls `fly-live-shot!`, clears the pending shot, unpauses;
  - `(flight-stop)` ends the thread and unpauses; at the end the record lines and the PNG paths go to `./sly log`; `(flight-status)` returns both (paths written so far).
- `src/main.lisp`: `clock-frame!` takes no more steps in a frame once the hook paused it; the render state shows the sim''s state (the clock''s previous state set to the current, as `decision 77`''s rule for no previous state).
- `src/capture.lisp`: `fly-live-shot!`: sets the game''s view to the entry''s as `fly-screenshots` does (outside at its default pose, cockpit head at Home, map framed on opening), waits 10 frames, writes `<shots>/<nn>-<label>.png` by `screenshot`. The view stays the shot''s afterwards.
- `src/input.lisp`: P ignored while a shot is pending.
- The record and end sim equal `run-flight`''s, shots or not: the pause only delays frames.

## Tests (`scripts/test`)
- `tests/flights.lisp`: piece 1''s `set-ap` script flown through `clock-frame!` as `flights-live-frames` does (a game-less clock, frames of 1/60 s), the driver taking each pending shot after 3 frames (clearing it, unpausing): end sim and record equal `run-flight`''s; no step taken while a shot is pending.
- `tests/input.lisp`: P while a shot is pending leaves the clock paused.

## Check, by hand
- `./sly start`, `./sly play`, `./sly eval ''(fly "approach-k-17" :shots "/tmp/live-k17")'' --package LUV-IN-SPACE`, poll `(flight-status)` until `END`; one PNG per screenshot entry, each looked at; record lines equal `scripts/fly approach-k-17`''s; `./sly stop`; no process left; directory deleted. Note the wall time.

## Docs
- `AGENTS.md` "Commands" → "Live image": `fly … :shots`, `(flight-status)` with paths, `approach-k-17`''s live wall time.
- `docs/architecture.md` "Running scripts" (live shots, wall times; `decision 131`), "Files in load order" (`flights.lisp`, `main.lisp`, `capture.lisp`, `input.lisp`).

## Done when
- `nix build`, `scripts/test`, `scripts/test-slow` pass; the hand check done.', '2026-10-15T07:54:41Z', NULL, 182, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (255, 35, 'Check milestone 3''s Done when from a clean checkout', 'work', 'open', NULL, NULL, 'Piece 5 of `plan 35: Flight screenshots, the live runs and the Done when`, after every other task of `milestone 3` (pieces 1–4 of this plan and plans 23–34). Not code: an agent checks `milestone 3`''s Done when, as `plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when`''s piece 4 did for `milestone 2`. A failure becomes a follow-up task (`pm task add` in the plan of the part that owns it); this task stays open until they are done, or is dropped with the reason. Screenshots not committed (`decision 87: Flight scripts in milestone 2: additions, apsis conditions, live fly and scripts/fly`).

## From a clean checkout
`git clone` into a temporary directory (no `build/`, no `result`), times noted:
- `nix build`; `scripts/test` (passes, ~15 s); `scripts/test-slow` (passes; every preset reached by autopilot from the start orbit: the headless Done when); `nix flake check` (both checks).
- `scripts/screenshot` with `--view outside`, `cockpit`, `map`.
- `scripts/fly` of every script in `flights/` (`burn`, `raise-orbit`, `deorbit` and the eleven of `milestone 3`): exit 0; look at every PNG against its script''s comment.

## In the running game
- Each Earth-orbit preset once (the in-game Done when): `./sly start`, `./sly play`; for each of the five `preset-<place>`, `(fly "preset-<place>" :shots "/tmp/live-<place>")`, polled with `(flight-status)` until `END PASSED`; record lines equal `scripts/fly`''s; every PNG looked at; wall time noted. `./sly stop`.
- As a player, from the cockpit alone (the Outcome''s "the player can fly from the cockpit alone"): in `./sly play`, synthetic key and mouse events through `./sly eval` (as `plan 22`''s piece 4): ] to target Tern Station, O and the AUTOPILOT menu by clicks to "Rendezvous: Tern Station", Return twice, a warp up, G to arm and back, Y on MSTR, V to the outside view and back; a `./sly screenshot` after each, the screens'' values moving as expected.
- `nix run` in the background, closed by its PID (`kill $!`) after a few seconds: the window opens in the cockpit without error. No process left running.

## Docs against what was built
- `AGENTS.md`: the intro paragraph (`milestone 3` built: the cockpit, the autopilot, the five places, grappling, berthing and towing; next `milestone 4`); "Commands" (every command runs as written, with the times: `scripts/test-slow`, `scripts/fly`, `scripts/screenshot --view`, live `fly` with `:shots`, the window title''s keys); "Layout" (every file in `src/`, `tests/`, `flights/`, `assets/`, `scripts/`).
- `docs/architecture.md`: "Files in load order" against `luv-in-space.asd` and `src/`; "Testing" against `tests/` (each named test exists, or its row names the milestone that writes it); "Scripted runs" against `flights.lisp` and `flights/`.
- `docs/design.md` "Feature list": every milestone-3 must row built; each should row (the sunlit patch, night lights and clouds, …) built or noted not built with a follow-up task; "Controls" against `*key-map*` and the window title.
- `rg -n ''PLACEHOLDER\('' src tests`: only tasks of later milestones (TEMP, LIFE, the hull gauge, `SOI --:--`), each id checked with `pm show`.
- `docs/luv-capabilities.md`, `docs/luv-wishlist.md`: the luv gaps met in `milestone 3`.

## Done when
- Every check passes, or each failure has a follow-up task named in the done note; the done note lists the commands, the times and the follow-ups and is the milestone''s evidence. Temporary clones and directories deleted.', '2026-10-15T07:54:41Z', NULL, 182, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (256, 24, 'RCS translation capability with the stack''s CoM ahead of the front blocks', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, src/ship.lisp (rcs-translation-capability: CoM ahead of the front blocks), tests/ship.lisp, tests/controls.lisp (mixer-lateral-loaded, translation-loaded), docs/design.md, docs/architecture.md; decision 142 corrects the design''s ~1,077 N to the measured 1,065 N. A first scripts/test run hit the intermittent cons-nothing failures (rails, map markers) of task 257; the rerun passed.', '`rcs-translation-capability` (`src/ship.lisp`) gives the torque-free ±y/±z force from the front and aft blocks'' arms, 2F (1 + min(a_f, a_r)/max(a_f, a_r)). Since `task 190: Capture, the stack and berthing`, `flight-step!` runs with a latched load''s mass properties, and a heavy load moves the stack''s CoM ahead of the front blocks (x 14.5 m): wet + 100 t at the design pose puts it near x 16 m, so a_f < 0 and the formula no longer means anything (its docstring already says "milestone 3 adds a CoM ahead of the front blocks").

- `docs/design.md` "RCS forces, torques and accelerations" gives the rule: "plus the axial nozzles'' ±1.8 m arms when the CoM lies ahead of the front blocks", with its numbers (1,266 N, 1,391 N, ~1,077 N for its three cases).
- Do: implement that case in `rcs-translation-capability`; check `flight-commands!` and `rcs-mix!` with a 50 t and a 100 t load at `stack-design-pose` (the mixer''s force fit and zero torque within its usual bounds).
- Tests in `tests/ship.lisp` / `tests/controls.lisp`: the design''s three numbers within 1 N; a translation key held with 100 t latched gives the capability''s acceleration and no rotation build-up beyond the mixer''s residual.', '2026-10-15T10:45:32Z', '2026-10-15T11:21:59Z', 190, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (257, 24, 'Intermittent rails-cons-nothing failure in the full test run', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, tests/physics.lisp, docs/architecture.md: the consing was the test''s own, not another thread: rails-attitude-loop made (+ time (* 7d0 i)) and full-called object-state-into!, boxing 16 B a call, so every other run filled a 32 KB region; the times are now boxed beforehand in a simple-vector; single runs read 0 for every mode; scripts/test passed 10 runs in a row (16-22 s); nix build passes.', '`tests/physics.lisp` `rails-cons-nothing` asserts `(<= consed 0)` over 1,000 `object-state-into!` calls on rails. In `scripts/test` runs during `task 190: Capture, the stack and berthing` it failed in two of five full runs with odd amounts (768 B to 19 KB, different attitude kinds each time) and passed every time run alone (`(parachute:test ''rails-cons-nothing)`), so something else conses while it measures (`sb-ext:get-bytes-consed` is global: a canvas thread left from the GPU tests in `tests/render.lisp`, or a finaliser) rather than the function.

- Do: find what conses concurrently (run the suite a few times, log `sb-thread:list-all-threads` before the test), then make the measurement robust: the other threads stopped before, or the bound the other cons tests use (`< 1024` with `least-bytes-consed`''s least of three runs, as `tests/package.lisp` explains).
- Done: `scripts/test` passes 10 runs in a row.', '2026-10-15T10:45:32Z', '2026-10-15T11:28:33Z', 190, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (258, 24, 'Bring scripts/test back under its ~15 s budget', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, src/render.lisp (*earth-pixels*), src/capture.lisp (wait-for-frames polls 1 ms), tests/assets.lisp, tests/render.lisp, tests/physics.lisp, tests/clock.lisp, tests/flights.lisp, docs/architecture.md, AGENTS.md: scripts/test 16.2-16.5 s -> 14.3 s wall over three runs; tests 13.6 -> 10.5 s in a live image', '`scripts/test` measured 16.4 s and 18.6 s wall on 2026-10-15 (`task 194: Plan 24''s Done when: a headless round at every place`), over the ~15 s budget of `docs/architecture.md` "Testing" → "Rules for tests" (~13 s when it was set). The tests alone take 14.2 s inside a live image (`./sly`), the rest is `nix develop` and loading.

Slowest tests then (s, live image): `render-frames` 3.65, `flights-scripts` 0.71, `offscreen-frame` 0.66, `clock-tern-beside-parked-ship` 0.61, `drift-coarse-circular` 0.57, `drift-coarse-e0.5` 0.55, `round-at-every-place` 0.40, `clock-levels-equal-direct-steps` 0.36, `clock-warp-round-trip` 0.35, `drift-fine-circular` 0.33, `reentry-promoted` 0.27.

- Do: shorten the slowest runs without losing what they check (fewer steps, shorter flights, sharing state as the Blue Marble tests share one decode), or move long runs to `scripts/test-slow` (`task 217: Approach and rendezvous scripts and scripts/test-slow` creates it) where the bounds stay meaningful; record each change''s reason in the test''s comment.
- Measure in a live image: `./sly eval` timing each test of `(parachute:package-tests :luv-in-space.tests)`.
- Done: `scripts/test` under ~15 s wall over three runs; "Rules for tests" and `AGENTS.md` "Commands" ("Test") give the new measured time.', '2026-10-15T11:17:14Z', '2026-10-15T11:47:25Z', 194, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (259, 25, 'Closing speed and relative velocity to a rails target: the position''s drift', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, src/controls.lisp, src/render.lisp, tests/render.lisp, docs/design.md, docs/architecture.md; decision 148', 'Found by `task 196: Target holds 7–0`; see `decision 144: The target hold''s rails target: the position''s drift beside the Kepler velocity`.

- On rails (`physics.lisp` `rails-state-into!`) the position moves with J2''s secular rates (Ω̇, ω̇, Ṁ), but v is the Kepler velocity of the elements: ~11 m/s from the position''s rate in LEO (`rails-rate-into!` gives the rate).
- `target-state-into!` (`clock.lisp`) returns that Kepler v for a rails target, so `target-relative-into!`''s closing speed ("CLS" on the TARGET screen, `task 166`) disagrees with the range''s actual rate of change by up to ~11 m/s for a target beyond 100 km (promoted targets are not affected).
- Decide and record: whether the instruments'' closing speed (and the F R D relative velocity) of a rails target should use the position''s rate (`rails-rate-into!` after `target-state-into!` via `target-rails-segment`), or stay on the Kepler v (the velocity a promoted target gets, and the one to be matched); at least the closing speed should equal d(range)/dt.
- Test: Tern Station on rails from the start orbit: the closing speed against the range''s centred difference over ±1/64 s within 1e-3 m/s.
- Docs: `docs/design.md` "Instruments and how each is drawn" (TARGET''s CLS), `docs/architecture.md` "Testing".', '2026-10-15T12:14:01Z', '2026-10-15T13:10:38Z', 196, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (260, 25, 'The prediction''s per-step cost and a ~1 ms frame budget', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, src/physics.lisp, src/bodies.lisp, src/math.lisp, src/hazards.lisp, src/clock.lisp, src/prediction.lisp, tests/prediction.lisp, docs/architecture.md, docs/design.md; decision 149: The prediction''s work budget stays 4,096 per frame after task 260''s cheaper step', 'Follow-up of `task 198: The prediction of milestone 3: horizons, the plan, the target buffer and the budget`, which set `*prediction-work-per-frame*` to 4,096 (`decision 146: The prediction''s work budget: 4,096 per frame, not 1 ms`) instead of the ~1 ms `decision 116: Prediction in milestone 3: horizon, target nodes, closest approach, relative nodes, budget` asked for.

## Measured (task 198, the benchmark form in `tests/prediction.lisp`''s comment)
- Start orbit, `:full`, two periods: 1.13 µs per work unit (one per copy step); with Tern Station targeted (rails) 0.68 µs per unit (2 per step); Skua Yard, nine objects promoted, Skua Yard 3 targeted: 0.66 µs per unit (~9.2 per step). `decision 20: Trajectory prediction on the frame thread` measured ~180 ns per step.
- At 900 units (~1 ms) `clock-dropout-to-impact` and `clock-dropout-to-contact` (`tests/clock.lisp`, `tests/prediction.lisp`) fail: the clock steps up to `*frame-step-limit*` 4,096 steps per frame, so the prediction falls behind the flight at high warp; and under thrust the prediction restarts every frame and one LEO period (~2,800 steps) never completes.

## What
- Profile one copy step of `prediction-extend!` (`sb-sprof`) at the start orbit: `sim-step-ticks!` (`contact-save!`, `third-bodies-step-into!`, `crash-test!`, `object-crash-tests!`, `contact-test!`, `sim-update-objects!`) and the prediction''s own `contact-starts!`, `contact-crossing!`, `apsis-search!`, `push-target!`; cut what costs without changing results (the bit-for-bit tests in `tests/prediction.lisp` and `tests/clock.lisp` must stay).
- Then either lower `*prediction-work-per-frame*` to ~1 ms with rules that keep the drop-outs safe (e.g. the clock never stepping past the prediction''s computed extent while it runs; under thrust keeping the last complete prediction until the restarted one completes), recording a decision that supersedes `decision 146`, or record why 4,096 stays.
- Update `docs/architecture.md` "Trajectory prediction" (the budget and figures) and the docstring of `*prediction-work-per-frame*`.

## Done when
- `nix build`, `scripts/test` pass; the measured figures and the chosen budget are in `docs/architecture.md` and a decision.', '2026-10-15T12:30:30Z', '2026-10-15T13:22:23Z', 198, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (261, 25, 'Bring scripts/test back under its ~15 s budget after plan 25', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, tests/controls.lisp (target-hold-promoted over an eighth orbit), tests/clock.lisp (clock-levels-equal-direct-steps coarse levels over 3 orbits, clock-warp-round-trip over half an orbit), tests/physics.lisp (drift-fine-circular over 3 orbits), tests/prediction.lisp (closest-approach scan every 1/4 s refined by the parabola''s vertex, agreeing within 2e-6 s), docs/architecture.md, AGENTS.md, docs/luv-wishlist.md (pixels without the PNG: 0.53 s of render-frames): scripts/test 15.1-15.8 s -> 14.2-14.4 s wall over four quiet runs (14.7-15.0 s at load ~2); tests 13.0 -> 12.1-12.6 s in one image; no bound loosened', 'Found by `task 200: Plan 25''s Done when: targets, holds, burns and prediction live and headless`: after `plan 25: Predicted trajectories, targets and target holds` `scripts/test` takes 16.0 s wall (three runs, 15.9–16.2 s), ~1 s over its ~15 s budget (`docs/architecture.md` "Rules for tests"; `task 258: Bring scripts/test back under its ~15 s budget` had it at 14.3 s).

## Measured (task 200, each test by `parachute:test` in one image after loading `luv-in-space/tests`)
- Plan 25''s 31 tests together 1.31 s; the largest: `target-hold-promoted` 0.38 s (`tests/controls.lisp`, a quarter orbit of fine steps at `:j2`), `prediction-closest-approach` 0.27 s (`tests/prediction.lisp`, the 1/16 s brute-force scan over two periods), `target-holds-settle` 0.19 s.
- The rest of the suite grew too: find the largest tests over all files (the same loop over every test) before cutting.

## What
- Bring `scripts/test` back to ≤ ~15 s wall without loosening a bound: shorter runs (e.g. `target-hold-promoted` over an eighth orbit, a coarser brute-force scan where the 0.05 s time bound still holds by the Hermite), shared setups, or work moved out of per-step checks; each shortened test''s comment says what and why, as `task 258` did.
- Record the new figures in `docs/architecture.md` "Rules for tests" and `AGENTS.md` "Commands" ("Test").

## Done when
- `nix build` and `scripts/test` pass; `scripts/test` ≤ ~15 s wall over three runs, recorded.', '2026-10-15T13:06:07Z', '2026-10-15T13:35:19Z', 200, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (262, 26, 'Bring scripts/test back under its ~15 s budget after plan 26', 'work', 'done', NULL, '2026-10-15, tests/package.lisp (make-instance notinline: 2.5 s of per-call-site constructor compiles), docs/architecture.md, docs/luv-wishlist.md, AGENTS.md; scripts/test 13.4-13.9 s wall at load ~2 (16.3-17.2 s before); decision 155', '`scripts/test` measured 16.2–16.8 s wall over three runs at load average ~2.3 after `plan 26: Autopilot: planner, orbit manoeuvres, execution and safety` (`task 206`, 2026-10-15), against its ~15 s budget (`docs/architecture.md` "Testing", "Time budget"; `task 261` measured 14.7–15.0 s at load ~2 before plan 26).

## What
- Measure each test''s time in one image (load `luv-in-space/tests`, run each test twice with `parachute:test … :report ''parachute:quiet`, time the second round) and find the largest; plan 26''s 32 autopilot tests are 1.13 s together, the most `autopilot-flown-through-clock` 0.26 s, `autopilot-replan` 0.21 s, `autopilot-trim` 0.15 s; `render-frames` was 2.65 s.
- Shorten the costliest runs without losing what they check (as `task 258` and `task 261` did: fewer orbits, coarser sampling, sharing setup), each change''s reason in the test''s comment; or move runs to `scripts/test-slow` if `task 217: Approach and rendezvous scripts and scripts/test-slow` has built it.
- Record the new wall times (three runs, with load average) in `docs/architecture.md` "Testing", "Time budget".

## Done when
- `scripts/test` near ~15 s wall at load average ~2; `nix build` and `scripts/test` pass; "Testing" updated.', '2026-10-15T15:18:07Z', '2026-10-15T15:30:50Z', 206, 0);
INSERT INTO task (id, plan_id, subject, kind, status, priority, note, body, created_at, retired_at, created_by_task, web) VALUES (263, NULL, 'write an executive summary', 'exec', 'open', 10000, NULL, 'Write an executive summary of the project for the human: where it stands, in a few minutes of reading. Look, don''t
change: no code, files, tasks, plans or decisions; stage nothing.
* the previous summary: `pm summary show` (none: this is the first, cover the project so far)
* what happened since it: `git log --stat <its commit>..HEAD` (pm''s commits name the tasks and decisions), `pm status`,
  `pm milestone list`, `pm runs`, `pm decision list`, `pm decision list --answered`, `pm show <id>` where it matters

Markdown, for the human, who knows the project but not the last days. Lead with:
* **Needs you**: pending decisions and sign-offs, what each blocks; failures, stalls
* **Since the last summary**: the delta: what got done, what changed course (new plans, revisions, dropped tasks,
  decisions answered or recorded by workers), runs, failures and cost
* **Where it stands**: each open milestone: progress, what comes next
* **Risks**: what may go wrong, take longer or need a decision soon

Bullets, ids with subjects (`task 12: <subject>`), no padding. Store it: `pm summary add < summary.md`, delete
summary.md, then close your task.', '2026-10-15T15:49:28Z', NULL, NULL, 0);

INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (1, 'How the game consumes luv', 'answered', '[]', 'Findings of `task 17: Build and run luv''s programs and test suite; record the runs` (docs/luv-capabilities.md, Packaging and Runs).
- luv''s flake exports apps, devShells, packages and lib; lib has only environmentFor, which returns everything the packaging needs (pkgs, lisp, applicationEnvironment/Packages, developmentEnvironmentHook, slynkSource, swashPackage, …). No overlays.
- applicationPackage (../luv/flake.nix:637) is not an output and is hard-wired to src = self, so it is copied (~120 lines) with src = this repo; noted for docs/luv-wishlist.md (export a lib.applicationPackage taking src).
- Do not set nixpkgs.follows: luv''s own pkgs keep the dependency closure identical to what luv.swa.sh/nix-cache serves (the dev shell''s Mesa, Vulkan tools, ffmpeg etc. came from it); the cache holds no x86_64-linux luv outputs at this revision, so the Lisp images are built locally (~40 s each).
- Pin the same revision as docs/luv-capabilities.md; bump deliberately, rerunning luv''s make test.
- Inherited LD_LIBRARY_PATH from the host broke Luvcraft (host alsa-lib needing glibc 2.43); our launcher and scripts unset it.
Part 3 (architecture) may supersede this.', 'Flake input github:mbrock/luv pinned to 6abc9d10ec8991c2cfe92888b84946372d6030b9; dev shell and app built from luv.lib.environmentFor (pkgs, lisp, developmentEnvironmentHook, applicationEnvironment) with a copy of luv''s applicationPackage recipe adapted here; luv''s sources on CL_SOURCE_REGISTRY from the input; luv''s binary cache in nixConfig; launchers unset LD_LIBRARY_PATH.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:33:38Z', '2026-10-14T16:33:38Z', 17, NULL, 'rule', 17, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (2, 'Screenshots and GPU test: offscreen capture on a hidden canvas, headless on lavapipe', 'answered', '[]', 'Found by task 18 (docs/luv-capabilities.md, runs 24–28): a probe outside luv captured correct PNGs in a window, on a hidden canvas, with no DISPLAY/WAYLAND_DISPLAY (offscreen + lavapipe), and inside the nix build sandbox (no /dev/dri) in 21 s including compiling luv; Luvcraft''s full 3D frame also renders headless on lavapipe. Hidden canvases keep the requested size; visible windows get the compositor''s size, so tests should not use them. Binds task 24 (screenshot command) and task 25 (headless test); the architecture may supersede it.', 'Screenshots and the GPU test use luv''s capture-application-screenshot on a hidden SDL canvas (:visible-p nil); without a display the luv shell hook picks SDL''s offscreen driver and Mesa lavapipe; the GPU test runs as a nix flake check in the sandbox, built with lib.environmentFor''s slim development environment and hook.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:42:58Z', '2026-10-14T16:42:58Z', 18, NULL, 'rule', 18, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (3, 'luv ASDF systems the game depends on', 'answered', '[]', 'The sphere probe of `task 19: Probe program: a lit sphere in a luv window plus a screenshot` (docs/luv-capabilities.md, "Appendix: probe program", runs 30–31) opened a window, drew a lit UV sphere with shaders in luv''s shader language, used a depth buffer and took an offscreen screenshot, visible and headless, with `:depends-on ("luv")` alone. Shader files use a package with (:use #:cl #:luv.shader) and (:shadowing-import-from #:luv.shader #:step). luvcraft/core is not loadable as a library (it pulls in Ghostty, libav and the whole game), so its sky, bloom and world-text code is copied and adapted here. luv/ghostty needs the full dev shell, not slim, which the GPU test (`decision 2: Screenshots and GPU test: offscreen capture on a hidden canvas, headless on lavapipe`) uses; adding it later is a separate choice.', 'The game system depends on "luv" only (it brings luv/shader, the SPIR-V/MSL lowerings and luv/arithmetic); tests add "luv/test-support". luv/ghostty, luv/terminal, luv/production and luvcraft/* are not dependencies until a feature needs one, and then each is recorded.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:48:14Z', '2026-10-14T16:48:14Z', 19, NULL, 'rule', 19, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (4, 'Depth strategy', 'answered', '[]', 'Findings of task 20: Assess luv''s rendering gaps for solar-system scale, each with a workaround (docs/luv-capabilities.md, "Gaps and workarounds"). luv offers only depth32-float but any compare op and clear value. The standard projection lost Earth''s far side from orbit and drew nothing at the Moon''s distance; infinite reversed-Z with near 0.1 m drew both, and a textured Earth impostor, an orbit ribbon and stars in one pass (runs 30-33). Logarithmic depth via frag-depth costs early-Z for no gain; layered passes cost a pass per layer. A separate cockpit pass with its own depth clear stays the fallback. docs/architecture.md may supersede this.', 'Reversed-Z: depth32-float, an infinite far plane (depth = near/z, near 0.1 m), compare :greater, clear 0.0, in every pass; stars at infinity drawn first with compare :always and no depth write; planets as ray-traced impostors writing their depth through :frag-depth.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:56:15Z', '2026-10-14T16:56:15Z', 20, NULL, 'rule', 20, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (5, 'Precision of positions', 'answered', '[]', 'Findings of task 20: Assess luv''s rendering gaps for solar-system scale, each with a workaround (docs/luv-capabilities.md, "Gaps and workarounds"). luv''s shaders have no 64-bit floats and write-buffer takes single-float arrays; base luv has no camera, and luv.arithmetic''s vec3 holds any real. Single floats give ~0.5 m at Earth''s radius and ~60 km at 1 AU. The sphere and gap probes computed the camera in double and sent camera-relative singles; Earth from 629 km and 34 Mm and the Moon from 384,400 km rendered correctly (runs 30-33). docs/architecture.md may supersede this, e.g. with per-body frames for the integrator.', 'World state in double-float on the CPU; each frame positions are made camera-relative in double and only then converted to single-float for the GPU; no world-space positions or matrices reach shaders; quantities that cancel in single float (e.g. |c|^2 - r^2 of a ray-sphere test) are computed in double on the CPU.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:56:15Z', '2026-10-14T16:56:15Z', 20, NULL, 'rule', 20, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (6, 'Target platforms', 'answered', '[]', 'luv builds for exactly these three systems and has no Windows backend (docs/luv-capabilities.md, Platforms). Only x86_64-linux was available to run luv''s builds, tests, programs and probes (runs 1-33); the developer''s luv wiki measures on Apple Silicon, so aarch64-darwin is a target but untested here. Luft''s mesh-shader requirement failed on the Intel iGPU (runs 7, 17), so the game avoids mesh and task shaders. Clip-space y and other backend differences are kept in one projection function so a Metal check later touches one place.', 'Targets: x86_64-linux and aarch64-linux (Vulkan 1.4) and aarch64-darwin (Metal 4), the systems luv''s flake supports; no Windows. Tested: x86_64-linux only (this machine, Intel iGPU, NVIDIA and lavapipe). The others are evaluated (nix flake show/eval) until a machine runs them. No mesh or task shaders, so any Vulkan 1.4 iGPU works.', NULL, '2026-10-14T16:58:45Z', '2026-10-14T16:58:45Z', 21, NULL, 'rule', 21, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (7, 'Placeholder labels: dot-matrix overlay quads', 'answered', '[]', 'luv has no text layer a game can load without luvcraft/core (docs/luv-capabilities.md, HUD and instruments); the Slug copy costs ~400 lines, a dot-matrix overlay ~40 and reuses the triangle-list pipeline that also draws the placeholder stars. Added by task 23: Placeholder scene: lit Earth sphere and a PLACEHOLDER label.', 'On-screen PLACEHOLDER labels are 5x7 dot-matrix glyphs drawn as screen-space quads by the overlay pipeline in src/scene.lisp (glyph table *glyphs*), not Slug text; real text (instruments, HUD) waits for the Slug text copy the design plans.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:15:05Z', '2026-10-14T17:15:05Z', 23, NULL, 'rule', 23, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (8, 'Live image: ./sly wraps luv''s client from the flake input', 'answered', '[]', 'luv''s ./sly serves only its own checkout and games (docs/luv-capabilities.md, live image). Redefining five functions on top of luv''s 2,400-line client keeps its other commands (eval, failures, resume, describe, xref, …) current with the luv pin, instead of a copy that drifts. Verified end to end in run 34 by task 26: Live image dev loop for luv-in-space.', './sly at the repo root builds build/sly-client from luv''s sly-client system (flake input) with play, screenshot, stop-playing, status and the dependency core redefined in scripts/sly-client.lisp; sly-server.lisp is luv''s, adapted to this root and the luv-in-space system.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:23:33Z', '2026-10-14T17:23:33Z', 26, NULL, 'rule', 26, '2026-10-14, task 27: Verified from a clean clone by `task 27: AGENTS.md Commands, verified from a clean clone; skeleton decisions recorded`: ./sly start (~5 s), play, status, screenshot, eval, failures, stop-playing, stop all work; AGENTS.md Commands documents them.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (9, 'Layout of systems and directories', 'answered', '[]', 'Follows `decision 3: luv ASDF systems the game depends on` (luv only; tests add luv/test-support) and `decision 1: How the game consumes luv` (luv''s sources from the flake input on CL_SOURCE_REGISTRY, so the root .asd is found from the repo root). One .asd keeps the game and its tests together as luv''s own programs do; new modules go under src/ and are listed in the .asd. Verified from a clean clone by `task 27: AGENTS.md Commands, verified from a clean clone; skeleton decisions recorded`.', 'luv-in-space.asd at the root with systems luv-in-space (src/) and luv-in-space/tests (tests/, Parachute); scripts/ holds the agent commands (test, screenshot, build.lisp, the sly client); ./sly and sly-server.lisp at the root; docs/ the design documents.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:25:48Z', '2026-10-14T17:25:48Z', 27, NULL, 'rule', 27, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (10, 'Test and screenshot commands', 'answered', '[]', 'Relies on `decision 2: Screenshots and GPU test: offscreen capture on a hidden canvas, headless on lavapipe`. No window opens, so an agent needs no display and leaves no process running. Ran from a clean clone (`task 27: AGENTS.md Commands, verified from a clean clone; skeleton decisions recorded`): tests 18/18 in ~5 s, screenshot ~3 s, with and without DISPLAY. The live image (`./sly`) is the interactive dev loop, `decision 8: Live image: ./sly wraps luv''s client from the flake input`.', 'scripts/test runs luv-in-space/tests (exit 1 on failure); scripts/screenshot <out.png> [--frames N] writes a 1280x800 PNG and exits; both offscreen on a hidden canvas, headless on lavapipe without a display, and enter the dev shell themselves; nix flake check runs scripts/test in the sandbox.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:25:48Z', '2026-10-14T17:25:48Z', 27, NULL, 'rule', 27, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (11, 'Render pipelines are luv live shader pipelines', 'answered', '[]', 'So a shader re-evaluated in ./sly shows on the next frame and a failed build keeps the last good pipeline, as in Luft and Luvcraft; verified by task 30: Live shader pipelines for the scene (red oceans live).', 'Every render pipeline of the game is a luv live shader pipeline (make-live-shader-pipeline) whose role is a keyword with shader-specification-for methods returning the define-shader functions in src/shaders.lisp; the game returns them from luv:application-live-artifacts and calls luv:refresh-application-live-artifacts at the top of each frame.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:30:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:30:44Z', 30, NULL, 'rule', 30, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (12, 'Module layout and package split', 'answered', '[]', 'One game package lets ./sly eval --package LUV-IN-SPACE reach every function while redefining live, with no export lists to keep in step; the code stays a few thousand lines, so clashes are handled with name prefixes. Shaders stay apart because luv.shader''s operators clash with CL. Keeping simulation free of HAL/SDL lets physics tests run without a canvas. A file that needs its own package is a new decision. Extends decision 9: Layout of systems and directories.', 'One ASDF system with flat files in src/ loaded serially in the order of docs/architecture.md "Module layout" (simulation files math … saves first and free of HAL/SDL calls, then shaders, assets and rendering files, then input, audio, title, main, capture); two packages: luv-in-space for all game code, luv-in-space.shaders for every define-shader; tests in luv-in-space.tests, one tests/<module>.lisp per module with logic.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:39:07Z', '2026-10-14T17:39:07Z', 32, NULL, 'rule', 32, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (13, 'Asset storage and attribution', 'answered', '[]', 'The two first assets are small (1.8 MB texture, a few hundred KB of stars), and committing them keeps the build independent of NASA and HYG URLs moving. A size cap keeps the repo cloneable; larger textures for close views come through Nix when the design wants them. ShareAlike binds the derived star table only, so it lives in its own directory with its licence. The package deletes its source copy after the dump, hence an installed assets directory and an environment variable.', 'Runtime assets committed under assets/<kind>/ (each file <= 5 MB, assets/ <= 25 MB), each kind with a SOURCE.md (URL, date, licence, credit, derivation); larger files via Nix fetchurl with a pinned hash, never committed, with the committed version as fallback. Earth: Blue Marble NG 5400x2700 JPEG, credited ''NASA Earth Observatory (Blue Marble Next Generation)'', no NASA logo. Stars: HYG filtered to mag <= 6.5 as assets/stars/hyg-6.5.sexp, made by scripts/convert-stars, under CC BY-SA 4.0 with assets/stars/LICENSE. CREDITS.md at the root collects all credits and the game shows them. The package installs assets/ to share/luv-in-space/assets and the launcher exports LUV_IN_SPACE_ASSETS; the dev shell falls back to the system-relative assets/.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:39:07Z', '2026-10-14T17:39:07Z', 32, NULL, 'rule', 32, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (14, 'Units and time epoch', 'answered', '[]', 'Doubles have the same relative precision in any unit, so km or AU gain nothing and add conversions: at 1 AU a double resolves ~3e-5 m, at 30 AU ~1e-3 m. SI keeps rocket and orbit formulas plain. J2000 matches JPL Horizons, DE440 and the IAU rotation models, so spot checks need no epoch shift; double seconds resolve ~1.2e-7 s at 30 years from J2000. Power-of-two steps make t + dt exact, so the clock does not drift and runs are deterministic. TDB - TT (< 2 ms) and future leap seconds are ignored.', 'SI throughout (m, kg, s, N, rad) as double-float, display units only at the instruments; constants as mu = GM in m^3/s^2 from JPL DE440 (Park et al. 2021) and radii from IAU 2015 B3 / IAU WGCCRE 2015; simulation time as double-float TDB seconds since J2000 (2000-01-01 12:00 TT), UT1 = UTC = TT - 69.184 s for display and Earth''s rotation; physics steps are power-of-two fractions of a second; the game''s start date is a calendar constant in src/units.lisp, chosen by docs/story.md (default 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC).', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:43:19Z', '2026-10-14T17:43:19Z', 33, NULL, 'rule', 33, '2026-10-14, task 51: 2026-10-14, task 51: docs/story.md keeps the start date 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC (decision 50: Story: start date and the passing rock''s date): the best lunar window of 2040 (Moon at Highport''s apogee 2040-01-26) comes ~20 game days after the start; docs/design.md ''The start'' and docs/architecture.md ''Time'' no longer call it a default. src/units.lisp does not exist yet; milestone 2 writes *start-date* with this value.

2026-10-14, task 93: 2026-10-14, task 93: the fixed TT - UTC = 69.184 s applies to past dates too, so J2000.0 is 11:58:50.816 in the game''s UTC (real UTC 11:58:55.816, TT - UTC 64.184 s in 2000); tests/units.lisp and docs/architecture.md ''Time'' use the game''s value.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (15, 'Coordinate frames', 'answered', '[]', 'Shared axes make re-centring a translation only and keep attitudes unchanged across frames. A body-centred frame keeps the ship''s coordinates small (LEO in an Earth frame resolves ~1e-9 m, against ~3e-5 m heliocentric), so roundoff stays below the integrator''s error and the drift tests of plan 9 measure the integrator; elements and instruments read straight from it. The frame''s acceleration enters as the indirect term of third bodies, so the trajectory does not depend on the frame. Ignoring precession and nutation puts Earth''s pole up to ~0.2 deg off by the 2040s: harmless for a game. FRD is the flight-dynamics convention: positive pitch is nose up, positive yaw nose right.', 'All inertial frames share ICRF (EME2000, J2000 equator and equinox) axes, no precession or nutation; the ship is integrated in the inertial frame centred on its dominant body (Sun, Earth, Moon; planets from milestone 4), the innermost body whose Laplace sphere of influence a (m/M)^(2/5) contains it, re-centred at a step boundary with 2% hysteresis; body-fixed frames from IAU WGCCRE 2015 rotation models, Earth''s by the Earth Rotation Angle about ICRF z with x at the prime meridian (the Blue Marble texture''s centre column); ship body and camera axes x forward, y right, z down (FRD), attitude a unit quaternion body -> inertial.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:43:19Z', '2026-10-14T17:43:19Z', 33, NULL, 'rule', 33, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (16, 'Integrator and simulation clock', 'answered', '[]', 'Measured in /tmp with SBCL in the dev shell (task 34): RK4 at h = 2 s in a 400 km circular orbit keeps relative energy error at 1e-11 and position error at 0.36 m after 1000 orbits; velocity Verlet at 1 s conserves energy (2e-13) but drifts 18 km in phase, and loses its energy bound with a state-dependent step (2.4e-6 at e = 0.5). Phase error is what rendezvous, prediction and the autopilot see, so 4th order beats symplectic here; RK4 also takes thrust, mass flow and attitude in one state vector, with no separable-Hamiltonian assumption. The state-dependent step (eta = 1/256) gives ~2,800 steps per orbit at any eccentricity and distance, at ~180 ns per step with the full force model, so even x100,000 in LEO fits the per-frame cap. 1/64 s at low warp gives input latency below one frame and resolves RCS pulses and rotation. Power-of-two steps keep t exact (decision 14: Units and time epoch).', 'Classical fixed-step RK4 on the ship''s whole state (position, velocity, attitude quaternion, body angular velocity, propellant mass), controls held over a step; step 1/64 s at warp x1 and x4 and while thrusting with manual attitude; otherwise h = eta * min over gravitating bodies of sqrt(d^3/mu), eta = 1/256, rounded down to a power of two in [1/64 s, 16384 s] (2 s in LEO); scheduled events on the 1/64 s grid; the frame adds min(elapsed, 0.1 s) x warp to a double accumulator and runs at most 4096 steps per frame (excess dropped: the sim slows, the readout shows the actual rate); rendering lags one step and interpolates (cubic Hermite positions, slerp attitude).', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', 34, NULL, 'rule', 34, '2026-10-14, task 34: 2026-10-14, task 34: docs/architecture.md ''Time and integration'' adds two limits: under thrust above x4 the coarse step is also capped at 1 s, and the per-frame cap is 4096 steps or 4 ms of steps, whichever comes first.

2026-10-14, task 45: 2026-10-14, task 45: docs/architecture.md ''Integrator'' and ''Drift bounds'': Euler''s equations take the full symmetric inertia tensor in body axes (and its inverse) from ship.lisp, not principal axes; the rotation drift test adds a non-diagonal case (diag(1, 2, 3)x10^4 kg m^2 rotated 10 deg about y), same bounds. A refinement, not a change.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (17, 'Gravity model', 'answered', '[]', 'Integrating everything the same way makes the prediction, the autopilot''s refined burns and the flown trajectory one computation, and removes the rails/integrated jump for the ship. Third bodies cost ~40 ns each per evaluation; the Moon''s pull before its sphere of influence and Earth''s just outside its own (8% of the Sun''s) are large, which patched conics would drop. J2 is the largest perturbation in LEO (~1e-2 m/s^2, nodal regression ~5 deg/day at 51.6 deg), visible to a player who waits a day, and costs a few multiplications; the Moon''s J2 and higher terms are invisible at game scale. Drag needs an atmosphere model and only matters below ~300 km; the design may add it later as a force.', 'The ship is integrated n-body against on-rails bodies in its dominant body''s frame (decision 15: Coordinate frames): the central body plus third bodies as point masses with their indirect terms (Earth frame: Sun, Moon; Moon frame: Earth, Sun; Sun frame: Earth, Moon and, from milestone 4, the planets; planet frames: Sun); Earth''s J2 (1.08263e-3, EGM2008) is in, other bodies'' oblateness out; no drag. The ship is never on Kepler rails; patched conics and Kepler elements serve only display (osculating elements), autopilot first guesses and objects far from the ship.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', 34, NULL, 'rule', 34, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (18, 'Ephemerides', 'answered', '[]', 'Both models are a few hundred lines of published coefficients with no data files, accurate to tens of arcseconds (thousands of km for planets, ~20 km for the Moon): far better than a player can see, and the game is self-consistent since autopilot, prediction and flight use the same ephemerides. A DE440 file would be exact but adds an asset and a reader for no visible gain. The Hermite cache makes a lookup cheap (the integrator asks four times per step), keeps the Moon''s interpolation error below 2 cm, and gives the same values whatever order frames ask in (determinism). Table 1 ends in 2050; the story starts in 2040 (default in docs/architecture.md), so the design keeps game time within the decade or a task switches to Table 2.', 'Sun fixed at the heliocentric origin; planets and the Earth-Moon barycentre from Standish, ''Keplerian Elements for Approximate Positions of the Major Planets'' (JPL SSD), Table 1 (J2000 ecliptic elements with secular rates, valid 1800-2050), rotated to ICRF by the obliquity; the Moon from the truncated ELP-2000/82 series in Meeus, ''Astronomical Algorithms'' (2nd ed., ch. 47), precessed to the J2000 ecliptic; Earth = barycentre - mu_Moon/(mu_Earth + mu_Moon) x geocentric Moon; all bodies read through a cache of cubic Hermite nodes (4096 s for Earth and Moon, 65536 s for the planets), each node a pure function of its index. Game time stays before 2050-01-01; beyond it Table 2a/2b replaces Table 1.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', 34, NULL, 'rule', 34, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (19, 'Time-warp rules', 'answered', '[]', 'Warp is a rendering of the same integration, not another model: the step rule makes the cost of each level follow from the state (x100,000 fits in LEO at 4096 steps per frame), so no altitude table is needed for accuracy. Kinematic attitude above x4 avoids integrating fast rotations with long steps, the classic cause of warp instabilities. Thrust up to x1,000 serves long low-thrust burns of tugs. Drop-outs come from the prediction''s events, so the player is never carried past an impact or a planned burn. Promotion keeps a station and a ship nearby under the same forces (a rails station ignoring J2 and the Moon would drift metres per hour against the ship), and the switch is exact at its instant since elements reproduce the state.', 'Levels x1, x4, x10, x100, x1,000, x10,000, x100,000, x1,000,000. x1 and x4 run the fine 1/64 s step with full rotational dynamics; above x4 attitude is kinematic (held inertial or in the orbital frame, rotation rates below 0.01 rad/s or a hold required to enter) and manual RCS is off; thrust is allowed up to x1,000 under attitude hold (the design may lower it). The clock drops warp so that the next predicted event (impact or periapsis below the crash radius, sphere-of-influence change, scheduled burn minus its lead time, entering a Sun hazard zone) arrives no sooner than 3 s real time, and to x1 at the event. Levels beyond the step budget may be chosen; the readout shows the actual rate. Rails: only objects other than the ship (stations, asteroids, other ships) move on Kepler rails (with J2 secular rates around Earth); one within 100 km of the ship is promoted to integration with the ship''s force model via elements -> state at the current t, and demoted beyond 200 km via state -> osculating elements.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', 34, NULL, 'rule', 34, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (20, 'Trajectory prediction on the frame thread', 'answered', '[]', 'Measured ~180 ns per step with the full force model: one LEO orbit (~2,800 steps) costs ~0.5 ms, a translunar coast a few thousand steps, so a whole prediction fits in a few frames'' budgets. On the frame thread it is deterministic, needs no locking over the ship''s state and is live-redefinable like everything else; a worker adds a thread and a dependency for no measured need. Revisit (and record luv/production) if a profile shows prediction over 2 ms per frame, e.g. many promoted objects or the autopilot''s shooting iterations.', 'Trajectory prediction runs on the frame thread, incrementally within a budget of 1 ms per frame, with the same integrator, force model and step rule as the flight (coarse steps, planned burns as scheduled controls); horizon until the first of: one osculating period (two for the map), an SOI change plus one period in the new body, an impact, 30 days in a planet''s sphere or 2 years heliocentric, 20,000 steps; every step''s state is kept (double, body-relative) for ribbons and event search. luv/production is not a dependency; decision 3: luv ASDF systems the game depends on stands.', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', '2026-10-14T17:50:44Z', 34, NULL, 'rule', 34, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (21, 'Physics test bounds and flight scripts', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/architecture.md ''Testing'' (task 35). Bounds come from the measured drift in ''Time and integration'' with a margin, sized so physics tests stay under ~5 s. Data scripts keyed by simulation time keep runs deterministic and replayable, and go through the same controls as input and the autopilot, so an agent can show a burn, an orbit raise and a deorbit with screenshots from a clean checkout.', 'Drift tests run two-body through the integrator and through the clock at every warp level, with bounds ~10x the measured drift: fine 1/64 s step (circular 10 orbits, e=0.5 2 orbits) dE/E and dL/L < 1e-11, position < 0.05 m; coarse step over 1,000 orbits circular < 1e-10 / 5 m, e=0.5 < 1e-9 / 100 m, and e=0.9 over 200 orbits dE/E < 1e-8, dL/L < 1e-9, < 5 km; the clock''s states equal the direct run bit for bit at every level and frame rate. Flights are driven by data scripts flights/<name>.sexp (actions and waits on the 1/64 s grid, writing controls) run by src/flights.lisp headless or live (fly); scripts/fly <name> <out-dir> writes a PNG per :screenshot checkpoint and exits 1 on a failed :expect; milestone 2 ships burn, raise-orbit and deorbit and demonstrates its Done when with them. GPU tests stay image checks on one frame (decision 2, decision 10).', NULL, '2026-10-14T17:55:32Z', '2026-10-14T17:55:32Z', 35, NULL, 'rule', 35, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (22, 'The player''s ship and its propulsion', 'answered', '[]', 'Hydrolox gives the delta-v for LEO to low lunar orbit (3.9 km/s) or a planetary departure on one tank, and it ties the ship to a water-to-propellant depot economy that gives tugs their work (moving water-bearing rock). One propellant pool keeps decision 16''s single propellant mass. Figures, formulas and sources in docs/design.md ''The ship(s)''. Recorded by task 38: Design: the game in one page and the ship(s).', 'A Mule-class tug: welded steel truss 15.0 x 3.6 x 3.6 m (2,131 kg), crew cab on top at the front (2,466 kg, crew 1 plus a jump seat, 180 person-days of consumables), grapple at the nose, LH2 and LOX tanks inside the truss; one RL10C-1-class hydrolox engine (101.8 kN, Isp 449.7 s, MR 5.5, throttle 10-100 %, gimbal trimmed through the centre of mass, unlimited restarts); 24 gaseous O2/H2 RCS thrusters of 445 N, Isp 360 s, in 8 corner blocks, fed from the same tanks (one propellant pool, as decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock integrates it); dry 7,961 kg, propellant 28,000 kg, wet 35,961 kg; delta-v 6.65 km/s empty, 1.74 km/s with a 50 t load, 1.02 km/s with 100 t; inertia as the full tensor from the parts in docs/design.md. Power, boil-off, ullage settling and gimbal cosine loss are not simulated.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (23, 'Start orbit', 'answered', '[]', 'The orbit of the architecture''s drift tests and of most crewed traffic since the ISS; the delta-v table in docs/design.md is computed from it. RAAN and phase are invented; the story (docs/story.md) or the places (task 42: Design: the places, with real data and Δv checked against the ship) may change them and must keep the start sunlit. Recorded by task 38.', '400 km circular (a = 6,778,136.6 m), i = 51.6 deg, RAAN 0, argument of latitude 0 at the start date (decision 14: Units and time epoch, default 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC), tanks full, attitude prograde hold; sunlit at the start.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (24, 'Grapple, not docking; towing in', 'answered', '[]', 'One mechanism serves refuelling, cargo and asteroid work and is flown the same way every time; the pilot never leaves the cab, so docking ports and crew transfer would only cost. Above 100 t delta-v falls below 1 km/s and turning a 90 deg takes over 40 s. How it is flown and its tolerances: docs/design.md ''Flight'' (task 40). Recorded by task 38.', 'The ship has one attachment mechanism, a nose grapple (capture cone with three latching claws) that latches onto a standard grapple fixture on depots, stations, cargo pods and bagged asteroids; no docking port, no crew transfer. A grappled object becomes part of one rigid stack (combined mass, centre of mass and inertia tensor), pushed by the main engine and turned by the RCS; loads are rated to 100 t.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (25, 'Damage model', 'answered', '[]', 'Per-part damage needs effects, UI and repair for something a careful pilot rarely sees; the pillars ''Newton is the game'' and ''small and finished'' in docs/design.md cut it. Recorded by task 38.', 'None: the ship is intact or destroyed. Contacts within the grapple''s tolerance capture, harder ones beyond the crash rule kill (rules in docs/design.md ''Flight'', task 40), and the Sun''s heat kills past its threshold; no part is ever damaged, leaking or repaired.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:56Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (26, 'Refuelling and economy', 'answered', '[]', 'An economy needs prices, a UI and balancing, and a linear quest line does not need it. Depots are named by task 42: Design: the places, with real data and Δv checked against the ship. Recorded by task 38.', 'Refuelling at depots only, grappled to the depot''s fixture: a cockpit switch transfers LH2 and LOX at 10 kg/s (full in ~47 min game time, time warp allowed) and restocks consumables. No money or economy: propellant is free, the story''s employer pays.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:57Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:57Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (27, 'Other ships', 'answered', '[]', 'Traffic shows the economy the story needs at the cost of meshes only; AI pilots and other ships'' burns would cost far more than they show. Recorded by task 38.', 'Other ships are built from the tug''s parts (truss, cab, tanks, engine, grapple fixture) or a capsule from a frustum: other Mule tugs in other liveries, an uncrewed tanker, a crew capsule, a large rock mover; all move on Kepler rails only (decision 19: Time-warp rules), promoted to integration near the ship but never thrusting in view; a story ship that departs switches its elements while beyond 200 km.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:05:57Z', '2026-10-14T18:05:57Z', 38, NULL, 'rule', 38, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (28, 'Web access for task 44: Verify the sourced figures of docs/design.md "The ship(s)"', 'answered', '["Allow WebSearch and WebFetch for pm workers; task 44 runs again", "Drop task 44; the figures stay sourced by name only"]', '`task 44: Verify the sourced figures of docs/design.md "The ship(s)"` must re-read the RL10C-1 data sheet, NASA CECE reports, GO2/GH2 thruster and IVF papers, NIST densities and Al 2219 data online and record URLs and dates read. Its worker (2026-10-14) was denied WebSearch and WebFetch by the session''s permission settings, so no agent can do it. Allow WebSearch and WebFetch for pm workers (e.g. in .claude/settings.json permissions), or drop the task and keep the figures as named-but-unverified sources.', 'Allow WebSearch and WebFetch for pm workers; task 44 runs again', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:09:07Z', '2026-10-14T18:50:04Z', 44, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-14, task 44: 2026-10-14, task 44: pm workers ran WebSearch/WebFetch; docs/design.md ''Propulsion'' now gives every ship source with URL and date read; no figure changed, decision 22 stands.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (29, 'Control scheme: key map, control modes, gamepad', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Flight'' (task 39). Physical keys keep the map on any layout; the WASD/QE and IJKL-HN blocks follow the convention players know. Rate damping as the default makes the heavy tug (1.6 deg/s^2 wet, 0.38 loaded) controllable by keys; holds are the flight computer the autopilot and warp need (decision 19: Time-warp rules). The gamepad costs ~80 lines that cannot be tested headless or here, and the keyboard covers every function (docs/luv-capabilities.md ''Gamepad and joystick''), so it is a late extra.', 'Keyboard and mouse on luv''s physical key names: W/S pitch, A/D yaw, Q/E roll, H/N I/K J/L translate, Shift/Ctrl throttle (ramp 50 %/s, 10 % minimum), Z full, X cut, F fine (10 %), T rate damping, R inertial hold, 1-8 orbital and target holds, 9/0 relative prograde/retrograde, ./,/ warp up/down/x1, P pause, V view, M map, C camera frame, Home reset, Tab map focus, [ ] \ target, O/arrows/Enter/Backspace autopilot, G grapple, Esc and Ctrl+Q quit (Esc opens a menu from milestone 6); right-drag looks, wheel zooms, left click presses switches and picks map targets. Modes: direct RCS, rate damping (default; up to 5 deg/s, fine 0.5 deg/s), attitude holds slewing at <= 5 deg/s with 80 % of the torque; translation always direct. Gamepad: milestone 6, could.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', 39, NULL, 'rule', 39, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (30, 'Views and cameras', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Flight'' (task 39). One renderer (decision 4: Depth strategy, decision 5: Precision of positions) draws all three; the map is the same scene from a far camera rather than a separate 2D chart, so it costs ribbons and markers only (docs/luv-capabilities.md ''Orbit lines and markers''). The eye point follows the cab of decision 22: The player''s ship and its propulsion; docs/design.md ''The cockpit'' (task 41) may move it.', 'Three views: outside (milestone 2; orbit camera around the CoM, 50 deg vertical FOV, distance from 1.2x the stack''s bounding radius to 5 km, in the orbital frame or locked to the ship with C), cockpit (milestone 3; eye at (14.1, 0, -3.6) m FRD from R, 0.9 m behind the 1.2 x 0.6 m window, 70 deg FOV zoomable 20-90 deg, head look yaw +-150 deg, pitch -60..+80 deg), map (milestone 2 Earth and osculating orbit; 3 prediction ribbon, targets, events; 4 Moon, Sun, planets): true scale, focus ship/body/target, camera 50 m or 1.2 R to 1.5e13 m, x1.2 per wheel notch, x10 per Page key; bodies below 6 px become labelled markers. All views available at every warp level and during the autopilot.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', 39, NULL, 'rule', 39, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (31, 'Warp choices within the time-warp rules', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Flight'' ''Time warp'' (task 39), within decision 19: Time-warp rules. One 60 Hz frame at x100 is 1.67 s of thrust, 4.7 m/s wet and 21 m/s dry, too coarse for a cut by key; at x10 0.5-2 m/s. A scheduled cut lands on the 1/64 s grid at any warp, so x100 is safe there and makes the longest burn (1,213 s) 12 s real. Stopping at SOI changes lets the pilot check the capture periapsis. A 180 deg slew at 80 % torque and 5 deg/s takes 40 s wet and 59 s with 100 t, so 60 s covers it and the max() covers the rest.', 'All eight levels of decision 19 offered, none greyed; the readout shows the actual rate when below 90 % of nominal. Thrust under warp: up to x10 with manual throttle, up to x100 for burns with a scheduled cut-off (autopilot, flight scripts'' :after-dv); throttling up above the limit lowers warp to it. SOI drop-out: stop (x1 at the change). Burn lead time: max(60 s, estimated slew to the burn attitude + 10 s). Rotation and translation keys above x4 are ignored with a message, not a warp drop. Refusals and drop-outs show a message with the reason for 3 s.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', '2026-10-14T18:12:48Z', 39, NULL, 'rule', 39, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (32, 'Autopilot scope and safety rules', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md Flight, The autopilot (task 40). Same physics and fuel as the player per milestone 3; the final 100 m to a fixture stay the pilot''s work, the core of piloting.', 'The autopilot plans by patched-conic first guesses refined by shooting with the flight''s integrator, flies finite burns centred on the node with cut-off by delta-v (full throttle unless under 10 s; RCS below 0.5 m/s), and writes controls like the pilot. Milestone 3: circularise, set Ap/Pe, Hohmann to a circular altitude, plane change (inclination or a target''s plane), rendezvous with a place and stop at a range (default 100 m on the fixture axis) and keep station; milestone 4: TLI, capture, TEI and Earth capture, Lambert transfers to asteroids and planets in scope with mid-course corrections. It never changes warp and never grapples. Backspace or any flight key disengages. Safety: no planned path below a body''s crash radius plus 50 km (Earth, planets) or 10 km (Moon); propellant for the plan plus max(2 %, 20 m/s) reserve, abort below half the reserve; mid-burn cut if continuing makes the orbit unsafe, abort after a repeated attitude error over 5 deg; no plan inside 0.551 AU of the Sun or beyond the consumables.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', 40, NULL, 'rule', 40, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (33, 'Grapple tolerances, contact and berthing', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md Flight, Grappling and berthing (task 40); extends decision 24: Grapple, not docking; towing in and decision 25: Damage model. Wider than real berthing so a careful first try works.', 'Capture when the cone''s tip touches a fixture at 0.01-0.30 m/s closing speed, lateral offset <= 0.30 m, angle <= 10 deg, relative rotation <= 0.5 deg/s: claws close in 2 s, a perfectly inelastic coupling into one stack. Other contacts at <= 1.0 m/s bounce (restitution 0.3, no damage); above 1.0 m/s the ship is destroyed. Ship collision shape: three boxes; objects spheres or boxes, tested against promoted objects. Towable fixtures carry up to 100 t; berth fixtures (depots, stations) hold the ship fixed to the station with engine and RCS locked; refuelling only berthed. Milestone 3.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', 40, NULL, 'rule', 40, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (34, 'How death works', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md Flight, Dying and what follows (task 40), within docs/architecture.md Collisions and the Sun and decision 19: Time-warp rules (the life-support alarms are drop-out events of the same kind). Distances computed from the rule, not guessed.', 'Crash spheres: Earth R+100 km, Moon R+0, Venus R+200 km, Mars R+125 km, gas giants 1,000 km above 1 bar, Sun R_sun; contact above 1.0 m/s kills. Sun: hull temperature relaxing (tau 900 s, exact update) to T_eq = ((0.30 S + 100 W/m2)/(2*0.85*sigma))^(1/4): warning 350 K at 0.551 AU (alarm, warp drop-out, autopilot limit), danger 400 K at 0.415 AU, death 450 K at 0.326 AU. Life support (milestone 4): 180 person-days, alarms and warp drop-outs at 30 and 7 days, death at 0. Running out of propellant does not kill. On death the sim stops, the outside view shows the ship and a LOST panel with the cause; Return restarts from the last checkpoint, N a new game; no permanent death; checkpoints in memory before milestone 5 (start state; from milestone 3 also after a completed plan and on berthing); death never fails a quest, its step is flown again.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', 40, NULL, 'rule', 40, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (35, 'Saving and audio', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md Flight, Saving and Sound (task 40); milestone 5 requires saves; audio stays at the architecture''s default milestone 6, after flight, cockpit and story, since agents cannot hear it and the game is complete without it.', 'Saving from milestone 5: checkpoint.sexp (at quest steps, berthing, a completed plan, only when coasting and safe; death restarts from it) and resume.sexp (on quit and every 5 min real time; deleted on death); no save slots, no save key; saves t, ship state, controls, grapple and load, hull temperature, consumables, target, promoted objects, quest state; not warp or autopilot plans. Audio in milestone 6 as a should: synthesised through-the-hull sounds (cab ambience, engine rumble, RCS puffs, valves and grapple, contact thud, alarms, radio static), one audio-sink and a Lisp mixer, U toggles sound, silent without a device.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', '2026-10-14T18:19:02Z', 40, NULL, 'rule', 40, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (36, 'Cockpit instruments: Slug screens, dot-matrix strip, gauges and lamps; Ghostty out', 'answered', '[]', 'Slug text on the screen planes stays sharp at every zoom and needs no render targets or mipmaps; the grapple camera is needed because the grapple tip is 55 deg below the window''s line of sight. Ghostty is cut: no screen runs a program, luv/ghostty needs the full dev shell (not the slim one of the sandbox test, decision 2) and copying Luvcraft''s ~2,150-line terminal wall, and decision 3 would need extending; the COMMS screen gives the terminal look with Slug text. Every instrument of ''Flight'' has a cockpit place, so the pilot can fly from the cockpit alone (milestone 3).', 'The cockpit (milestone 3) is a rack of mismatched screens, a dot-matrix strip, needle gauges, lamps and 18 illuminated push-buttons around the 1.2 x 0.6 m window, laid out in docs/design.md ''The cockpit'' relative to the eye point of decision 30 (default head pitch 16 deg down; two side portholes added). Screens: Slug text (Monaspace Neon, fixed grids) and ribbon plots drawn directly on the screen planes in the scene pass, no screen textures; the grapple camera is the only render target (480x320, every frame while armed). Dot-matrix strip: 5x7 glyphs of decision 7 as instanced amber dots. Gauges: needle meshes with 0.3 s lag; lamps flash in real time. Ghostty cut. Keys added: B propellant transfer, Y acknowledge. Readability at 1280x800: cap height >= 10 px (0.0175 d m), contrast >= 7:1 for screen text, emissive output divided by exposure; colours: white normal, green ok, amber caution, red danger, cyan own ship, magenta target, never colour alone.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:27:50Z', '2026-10-14T18:27:50Z', 41, NULL, 'rule', 41, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (37, 'Message channels the story may use', 'answered', '[]', 'A linear quest line needs orders and radio chatter, both carried by one screen and a lamp; the printer strip is unreadable at 1280x800 (a 58 mm roll holds ~6 legible characters per line at 0.6 m), voices and portraits cost far more than they show (pillar ''small and finished''). docs/design.md ''The cockpit'', ''Messages and radio''.', 'Four channels: RADIO (sender, game time, text; COMMS screen log typed out at 60 chars/s, MSG lamp until read with Y or a click, dot-matrix ''MSG <SENDER>'', first line in the outside/map overlay, light-time delay from the sender, text only, no reply choices; milestone 5), JOB (the current quest step''s job card: title and up to 3 lines at the top of COMMS and one line in the overlay; a step may require Y to acknowledge), SHIP (the flight computer''s own messages, written by the game, not the story), static texts (the ship''s name above the window, up to 3 tape notes chosen by docs/story.md). Cut: printer strip, recorded voices, video-call faces, an inbox.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:27:55Z', '2026-10-14T18:27:55Z', 41, NULL, 'rule', 41, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (38, 'Start orbit: polar', 'answered', '[]', 'task 42 (docs/design.md ''The places''): J2 (decision 17: Gravity model) turns orbit planes at -(3/2) n J2 (R/p)^2 cos i: at 51.6 deg -5.00 deg/day at 400 km and -4.09 at 800 km, so places at different altitudes drift apart ~0.9 deg/day and a rendezvous days later costs hundreds of m/s of plane change. At i = 90 deg cos i = 0: all polar orbits keep their plane at every altitude, so the places stay coplanar all game and presets cost only altitude and phasing. A polar orbit also shows every latitude. Supersedes the inclination and RAAN of decision 23: Start orbit; altitude, tanks, attitude unchanged.', '400 km circular (a = 6,778,136.6 m), i = 90 deg, RAAN 270 deg, argument of latitude 0 at the start date (decision 14: Units and time epoch, default 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC), tanks full, attitude prograde hold; sunlit at the start (25 deg from the Sun, beta -10 deg). Every Earth-orbit place shares the plane (i 90, RAAN 270).', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:41:48Z', '2026-10-14T18:41:48Z', 42, NULL, 'rule', 42, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (39, 'Places in scope', 'answered', '[]', 'task 42 (docs/design.md ''The places''): Delta-v checked against the Mule''s 6.65 km/s (1.74 with 50 t) and its 180 person-days: every place above is reachable with refuelling at Fulmar, Highport or Peary; Venus (no return), Mars (5.6 km/s, 259 d), Kamo''oalewa (cheapest round trip ~360 d, 9 km/s within 180 d), the main belt (9.7 km/s, 472 d) are not. A loaded tug cannot bring rock from Highport to LEO (2.76 km/s), hence the high water plant. Lagrange points need three-body motion; places are on Kepler rails (decision 19: Time-warp rules).', 'Earth orbit (milestone 3 presets, all invented, polar i 90 deg RAAN 270 deg): Tern Station (crew, 420 km), Fulmar Depot (LEO propellant, 500 km), Derelict K-17 (dead 2.5 t upper stage at 800 km, pushed by hand to re-entry), Highport (depot and water plant, 1,000 x 70,000 km), Skua Yard (bagged rocks 20-100 t and one 150 t beside Highport). Moon (milestone 4): Peary Depot, 100 km polar lunar orbit; landing cut. Asteroids: Skua Yard''s rocks and one invented 50 t passing rock captured into Highport''s orbit (milestone 4, should); real asteroids (Kamo''oalewa, Ceres, Vesta, Pallas, Hygiea) map markers only (could). Planets: all eight on rails, point sprites and flat-colour map impostors, no destinations, no textures. Not places: GEO, Lagrange points/NRHO, constellations.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:41:58Z', '2026-10-14T18:41:58Z', 42, NULL, 'rule', 42, '2026-10-14, task 47: 2026-10-14, task 47: asteroid elements read from JPL SBDB (epoch JD 2461200.5) and tabled in docs/design.md ''Asteroids''; Lambert re-run: Kamo''oalewa round trip 4.9 km/s within 360 d, 8.2 within 180 d; 2008 EV5''s one window of the decade opens at the start (6.4 km/s, 180 d), later >= 7 km/s: both stay cut. Moon: CGI Moon Kit 2019 4k colour (~2.0 MB JPEG) and ldem_4 (4.0 MB) fit decision 13.

2026-10-14, task 51: 2026-10-14, task 51: docs/story.md keeps every place name (decision 49: Story: names, call signs and ships); adds Peary Camp (lunar surface, a RADIO location only, never shown) and names the passing rock 2040 BQ7; docs/design.md ''Earth-orbit points of interest'' and ''What the story may use'' say the names are kept.

2026-10-14, task 55: 2026-10-14, task 55: POD 14, a standard 12 t cargo pod, added in Skua Yard (Highport''s orbit, M0 179.87587 deg, inertial, no spin; milestone 5) as a load, not a place; Skua Yard laid out as a string 666.7 m apart at apogee (decision 53: Deliveries, the stranded offer, checkpoint floors and Skua Yard''s layout); docs/design.md ''Earth-orbit points of interest'' and ''Rigid bodies'' list it.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (40, 'Earth''s look and drag', 'answered', '[]', 'task 42 (docs/design.md ''The places'', ''Earth''): half of each orbit is night, and a cloudless Earth looks like a map; each costs one texture (<= 5 MB, decision 13: Asset storage and attribution) and a few shader lines. Drag at 400 km (rho ~2.8e-12 kg/m3, USSA 1976) is 2.7e-7 m/s2 on the Mule, a 42 m altitude loss per day, invisible; it matters only below ~200 km, where the 100 km crash sphere and the autopilot''s 150 km floor already rule. Confirms decision 17: Gravity model (no drag).', 'Blue Marble NG of the start date''s month (January: 200401), one month all game (milestone 2); atmosphere rim and a halo impostor at R+100 km (milestone 2); night lights from NASA Black Marble 2016 (milestone 3, should); a static, unanimated cloud layer from NASA Visible Earth ''Blue Marble: Clouds'' (milestone 3, should); ocean glint could (milestone 6); aurorae, weather, relief, seasonal textures cut. Drag out.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:41:58Z', '2026-10-14T18:41:58Z', 42, NULL, 'rule', 42, '2026-10-14, task 47: 2026-10-14, task 47: sources verified; night lights = BlackMarble_2016_3km.jpg resampled to 5400x2700 (~1.0 MB), clouds = cloud_combined_8192.tif resampled to a 5400x2700 greyscale JPEG (~3.5 MB), Blue Marble January = world.200401.3x5400x2700.jpg (1.8 MB); URLs, credits and sizes in docs/design.md ''The places'', ''Sources''.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (41, 'Weapons: cut', 'answered', '[]', 'Pillar ''the ship is a tool'': a tug''s tools are its grapple and engine, and the game centres on piloting; the developer''s ideas allow low-tech weapons only ''if any''. Weapons need reacting targets, which rails-only other ships (decision 27: Other ships) cannot be, and damage, which the ship does not have (decision 25: Damage model). docs/story.md uses the trade''s own dangers and conflicts told by RADIO instead. Recorded by task 43 in docs/design.md ''Scope'', ''Weapons''.', 'No weapons in the game, low-tech or otherwise; no ship carries one and the quest line has no fight.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:45:48Z', '2026-10-14T18:45:48Z', 43, NULL, 'rule', 43, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (42, 'Scope: what each milestone builds', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 43 wrote the scope table from all sections of docs/design.md and the developer''s ideas of milestone 1, and checked it against each milestone''s Outcome and Done when; no real contradiction remains (milestone 4''s belt and planets are shown, not destinations, inside the milestone''s intent). Real asteroids on the map are raised from ''could'' to ''should'' so milestone 4 shows the belt, as its Outcome names it; this partly supersedes decision 39: Places in scope.', 'docs/design.md ''Scope'', ''Feature list'' is the milestones'' specification. Milestone 2: polar LEO start, integrated n-body flight with J2, the Mule rigid body with engine, RCS mixer and one propellant pool, key map, direct/rate damping/holds, eight warp levels with refusals and drop-outs, outside view, basic map, minimal readout, Blue Marble Earth with atmosphere, stars, Sun glare, untextured Moon, the Mule''s meshes, Earth crash and restart, flight scripts burn/raise-orbit/deorbit, drift tests. Milestone 3: the DIY cockpit (screens, strip, lamps, gauges, buttons, grapple camera), prediction ribbons and targets, Earth-orbit autopilot with safety rules, the five Earth-orbit places as presets on rails, grapple, berthing, refuelling, towing to 100 t, contact rules, re-entry of released objects, SHIP messages, in-memory checkpoints; should: night lights, clouds, other ships as scenery, the cab''s sunlit patch. Milestone 4: Moon texture and Peary Depot, autopilot across SOIs (TLI, capture, TEI), planets on rails as sprites and map impostors (no destinations), the Sun''s heat and death (tested), life support, asteroid meshes; should: the passing rock, real asteroids (Kamo''oalewa, Ceres, Vesta, Pallas, Hygiea) on the map; could: belt band, 16k Moon. Milestone 5: quests, RADIO and JOB, static texts, the story''s names and date, saves, headless play-through; should: story ships moving on rails. Milestone 6: title screen, introduction, F1 help and Escape menu, credits screen, README, nix run, performance 60 Hz at 1280x800 on the developer''s machine measured and documented; should: sound; could: gamepad, ocean glint, Saturn''s rings. Cut: weapons, docking ports and EVA, landing, drag and aerobraking, per-part damage, economy, electric and hypergolic drives, power/boil-off/slosh simulation, other playable ships, auto-warp, gravity assists, destinations beyond the Moon and the passing rock, dense belt field, planet and seasonal textures, Ghostty, printer strip, voices, save slots, debris simulation.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:45:57Z', '2026-10-14T18:45:57Z', 43, NULL, 'rule', 43, '2026-10-14, task 43: 2026-10-14, task 43: docs/design.md ''Scope'' written (feature list, weapons, performance target, milestone checks, what the story may use); ''Asteroids'' raises real asteroids on the map to should; the Moon untextured until milestone 4 and Skua Yard''s rocks plain spheres until milestone 4; docs/architecture.md title.lisp also holds the Escape menu and the credits screen.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (43, 'Warp drop-outs for contact and life support', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Time warp'' and ''Dying and what follows'' (decision 33: Grapple tolerances, contact and berthing, decision 34: How death works) add events that decision 19 did not list; they follow its rule unchanged, so this extends only its list of events. Life-support times are linear in t and need no prediction; contacts are predicted with bounding spheres because box tests at coarse steps would be pointless: the drop-out brings the ship to x1, where the fine-step box test decides.', 'The clock''s drop-out events (decision 19: Time-warp rules) also include a predicted contact with an object (the stack''s and the object''s bounding spheres meeting on the prediction, objects on rails or promoted) and the life-support alarms at 30 and 7 days and the end of consumables (times in closed form, scheduled like a burn); the Sun hazard zone is entering 0.551 AU from the Sun (the 350 K warning distance of decision 34: How death works). Same 3 s real-time rule, x1 at the event, same refusals.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:48:51Z', '2026-10-14T18:48:51Z', 46, NULL, 'rule', 46, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (44, 'Contact, capture and berthing in the simulation', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Grappling and berthing'', ''Towing'' and ''Dying and what follows'' (decision 33, decision 34) name contact, capture, berthing and life support but leave how the simulation does them to docs/architecture.md (task 46). Immovable berth objects keep stations on their rails and their orbits unchanged by a careless pilot; towable objects must take impulses for a capture to conserve momentum. Holding a berthed ship at its station keeps decision 19 (the ship is never on Kepler rails: the station it rides is promoted and integrated, since it is within 100 km).', 'Promoted objects are rigid bodies: towable ones (pods, rocks, K-17) integrated with torque-free rotation and finite mass; berth objects (stations, depots) immovable in contacts, their attitude kinematic (LVLH, inertial or Sun-pointing as places.lisp says). Contact in hazards.lisp after each step: swept bounding spheres on the relative Hermite, then the grapple tip against fixture plates, then oriented boxes and spheres (separating axes) on substeps of at most 0.25 m relative motion, first overlap bisected to 1e-4 s; only approaching contacts count. Response: capture (perfectly inelastic coupling into one stack at the contact pose, linear and angular momentum conserved), bounce (impulse with restitution 0.3 and both bodies'' mass and inertia; berth objects take none), death above 1.0 m/s. Berthed: the ship is not integrated but held at the fixture''s pose on the promoted station, each step; release gives it the fixture point''s velocity. Hull temperature and consumables are per-step scalars outside RK4, updated exactly. Checkpoints are copies of one simulation-state object in memory (clock.lisp), written to files by saves.lisp from milestone 5.', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:48:51Z', '2026-10-14T18:48:51Z', 46, NULL, 'rule', 46, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (45, 'Web access for pm workers still denied after decision 28', 'answered', '["Permission added; task 44 runs again", "Drop task 44; the figures stay sourced by name only"]', '`decision 28: Web access for task 44: Verify the sourced figures of docs/design.md "The ship(s)"` was answered ''Allow WebSearch and WebFetch for pm workers'', but in run 40 of `task 44: Verify the sourced figures of docs/design.md "The ship(s)"` (2026-10-14) both tools were again denied by the session''s permission settings (''Claude requested permissions to use WebSearch, but you haven''t granted it yet''). The repo has no .claude/settings.json and a worker must not grant itself permissions. Add the permission where pm workers'' sessions read it (e.g. permissions.allow ["WebSearch", "WebFetch"] in .claude/settings.json or the pm worker''s launch flags), then answer; `task 47: Verify the sourced figures and asset licences of docs/design.md "The places"` needs the same.', 'Permission added; task 44 runs again', NULL, '2026-10-14T18:51:35Z', '2026-10-14T20:14:20Z', 44, NULL, 'rule', NULL, '2026-10-14, task 44: 2026-10-14, task 44: with the permission in place task 44 ran with web access and verified the RL10C-1, CECE, GO2/GH2 RCS, NIST density and Al 2219 figures (docs/design.md ''Propulsion'', Sources); the L3Harris RL10 spec-sheet URLs answer 404, read through a search engine''s copy.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (46, 'Rails across spheres, hyperbolas and re-entry', 'answered', '[]', 'task 48: docs/design.md ''The places'' needs the passing rock on a geocentric hyperbola inside Earth''s sphere and heliocentric outside it, and Derelict K-17 to burn up after its release at a 90 km perigee; decision 19: Time-warp rules named only Kepler rails with J2 rates. Segments precomputed keep rails exact, deterministic and cheap; exact boundaries need no hysteresis because nothing is switched per step.', 'Rails objects hold a list of conic segments built once per object (physics.lisp) from one element set: forward to 2050-01-01 and, for a definition, back to the start date; a segment ends at leaving its body''s Laplace sphere (closed form), entering a child''s sphere (sampled at the coarse step rule, swept-sphere test, Newton to 1e-6 s) or the crash sphere; switches at the exact boundary, no hysteresis, state carried through the ephemerides as the ship''s re-centring; at most 16 segments; place-state(t) stays a pure function of t. Hyperbolas (e > 1) stored as q, e, i, Omega, omega, t_p, propagated by the hyperbolic Kepler equation with near-parabolic forms; |e-1| < 1e-6 nudged to 1 +- 1e-6; pure two-body (J2 secular rates only for Earth-centred ellipses). An object meeting its body''s crash sphere is removed at the crossing: closed form on rails, the ship''s swept crash test when promoted; marked removed (time, body, point), a SHIP message, no warp drop-out; drag stays out.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:23:46Z', '2026-10-14T19:23:46Z', 48, NULL, 'rule', 48, '2026-10-14, task 48: 2026-10-14, task 48: docs/architecture.md ''Hyperbolic rails'', ''Rails across spheres of influence'', ''Re-entry of objects'' and their tests; physics.lisp holds the segment builder and the shared swept-sphere test; checkpoints carry segment lists and removed objects; the polar start orbit''s node test added; docs/design.md cites the decision instead of task 48.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (47, 'Rigid bodies of the places', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 49 completes what decision 44: Contact, capture and berthing in the simulation needs from places.lisp. Spins about the fixture axis keep the pin and its axis still, inside decision 33''s 0.5 deg/s, so capture needs no matched motion; the rates grade the difficulty. The 0.3 m stand-off: at the 10 deg capture limit the grapple box''s front corners reach 0.20 m ahead of the tip and would touch a flush face (or a sphere, with the 0.30 m offset) before the tip reached the plate. Sun-pointing suits the sunshaded depots and Highport''s wings; LVLH gives Tern an R-bar approach to its zenith fixture. Fulmar''s sunshade grows from 12 to 16 m to shade its tanks; the Mule''s loaded inertia rows move the load 0.3 m forward.', 'Every place object has body axes at its CoM (FRD), a full inertia tensor from its parts, 1-5 boxes or spheres and fixtures (pin, outward axis) as tabled in docs/design.md ''The places'', ''Rigid bodies: attitude, mass, shapes and fixtures''. Attitude: Tern Station LVLH; Fulmar Depot, Highport, Peary Depot Sun-pointing (x to the Sun, z towards ecliptic south); the parked rock mover inertial, immovable like a berth object, no free fixture. Towable objects spin about their fixture axis (+x): K-17 0.3 deg/s, Skua Yard rocks 0.05-0.30 deg/s, the passing rock 0.4 deg/s, cargo pods none. Every fixture plate stands 0.3 m proud of its object''s shapes. Masses: Tern 85 t, Fulmar 60 t, Highport 300 t, Peary 42 t dry, rock mover 1,087 t with its 10 m rock, K-17 2.5 t, a standard cargo pod 12 t (4.0 x 2.5 x 2.5 m). Ships berthed at places as scenery are part of the place''s shapes.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:32:04Z', '2026-10-14T19:32:04Z', 49, NULL, 'rule', 49, '2026-10-14, task 49: 2026-10-14, task 49: docs/design.md ''The places'' adds ''Rigid bodies: attitude, mass, shapes and fixtures'' (tables per object and rock); Fulmar''s sunshade 16 m; ''Inertia'' loaded rows, the control-feel table and the towing turn time recomputed for the 0.3 m stand-off; docs/architecture.md ''Rails'' names the rock mover among berth objects and cites the decision, the contact cost counts Skua Yard''s objects.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (48, 'Story: setting and premise', 'answered', '[]', 'task 51 per plan 11: story. A sober, small setting fits the hard sci-fi brief and decision 41 (no weapons) and decision 26 (no money): danger from the trade, people''s conflicts by radio. The ending is a hook into a longer adventure without needing any destination cut by decision 42. Full text: docs/story.md ''Setting'', ''Characters'', ''Premise''.', 'January 2040, about twenty people in one polar plane living on rock water. The pilot (no name, gender or face, addressed as ''Patience'') is Cairn Water''s new pilot of the Mule tug PATIENCE (M-07), taking over from Mirela Doru, retired at the career dose limit. Cairn Water (Highport, Skua Yard, Peary Depot and Camp, tankers, rock mover, bagger probes, four Mules) employs and pays; Boreal Orbital owns Tern Station, Fulmar Depot and the AURORA capsules; Lindgren Debris (Kiruna) hires the K-17 push. Conflicts told by RADIO only (Boreal wary of rocks near the plane). The prelude ends berthed at Highport after 2040 BQ7 is delivered (or after the lunar return if the passing rock is dropped): Cairn announces a long-haul tug and offers the pilot its seat, told by RADIO only, nothing cut is flown.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', 51, NULL, 'rule', 51, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (49, 'Story: names, call signs and ships', 'answered', '[]', 'task 51 per plan 11: story. Working place names already read well and are cited across docs/design.md and docs/architecture.md, so they are kept rather than renamed. Names and full list: docs/story.md ''Names and terms''.', 'Ship PATIENCE, registration M-07, call sign ''Patience''. Companies Cairn Water, Boreal Orbital, Lindgren Debris. Speakers: CAIRN OPS Rafael Quiroga (Highport), TERN Ana Folau (Tern Station), LINDGREN Sami Lindgren (Kiruna), HIGHPORT Esther Okafor (Highport), GRINDSTONE Ruth Abara (Skua Yard), THRIFT Dag Eliassen (THRIFT), PEARY Tomasz Wierzbicki (Peary Camp on the Moon), MIRELA Mirela Doru (Tromsø); Cairn senders cyan, others white. Places keep their working names (Tern Station, Fulmar Depot, Derelict K-17, Highport, Skua Yard, Peary Depot); Peary Camp a RADIO location only. Other ships: STUBBORN M-04 and THISTLE M-05 (berthed at Highport), THRIFT M-09 and TANKER 3 (moving, milestone 5 should), TANKER 2 (at Fulmar), AURORA 11 (at Tern), GRINDSTONE (rock mover), BAGGER 3 (with the passing rock). Passing rock designation 2040 BQ7. Static texts: plate ''M-07  PATIENCE''; three tape notes as in docs/story.md ''Static texts''.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', 51, NULL, 'rule', 51, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (50, 'Story: start date and the passing rock''s date', 'answered', '[]', 'task 51 per plan 11: story. Computed with Meeus ch. 47 (truncated, precessed to J2000; checked against example 47.a within 0.1 deg) and Highport''s J2 apsidal drift -0.0665 deg/day; the Earth-orbit quests take ~8 days, leaving 12 days of slack before the January TLI; the rock''s date keeps the meeting clear of the Moon and leaves time after either lunar window. Table and method: docs/story.md ''Start date''.', 'Start date stays 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC with the start orbit''s phase and lighting of decision 38 unchanged. Best lunar window of the year: the Moon at Highport''s apogee direction 2040-01-26 02:36 UTC (0.6 deg off), TLI from Highport''s perigee ~2040-01-21 (game day 20); fallback 2040-02-22 (2.2 deg). Passing rock 2040 BQ7: found 2040-01-19, perigee 2040-03-12 12:00 UTC at 7,378 km in the polar plane; the tug meets it ~2 days before perigee near 400,000 km on Highport''s apogee side, the Moon 134 deg away.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', '2026-10-14T19:41:36Z', 51, NULL, 'rule', 51, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (51, 'Story ships flying free: THRIFT, TANKER 3, BAGGER 3', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 54. ''Behind'' Highport put the parked ships on the autopilot''s rendezvous aim line (1 km behind the target) and in the lane a rock towed from Skua Yard (30 km behind) uses; ''ahead'' is used by no approach. Same-orbit objects keep their time gap, so the gap in metres is smallest at apogee (2 and 5 km) and largest at perigee (20.7 and 51.8 km), always inside the 100 km promotion range near Highport. Immovable because story ships move on rails only (decision 27: Other ships) and a bump must not change the rails the story''s trips depend on.', 'THRIFT (wet Mule, 36.0 t) and TANKER 3 (24 m tanker, two LH2/LOX tank pairs, 54.6 t full) park between trips in Highport''s orbit AHEAD of it, M0 = 180.0220 deg (5 km at apogee) and 180.0088 deg (2 km), clear of the autopilot''s aim point 1 km behind Highport, the free fixture''s approach and the Skua Yard tow lane behind. BAGGER 3, a 1.5 m bagger probe (0.5 t, 1.35 km/s), rides the passing rock''s rails shifted by dt = 1 km / v(400,000 km) (0.626 s at v_inf 0.75 km/s) and flies on past perigee after the grapple; the rock''s fixture axis is held inertially against its inbound velocity at 400,000 km, so the bagger sits on the far side from the approach. All three are immovable objects with no player fixture (as the rock mover): THRIFT and TANKER 3 inertial, BAGGER 3 Sun-pointing; shapes, tensors and bounds in docs/design.md ''Rigid bodies: attitude, mass, shapes and fixtures''.', NULL, '2026-10-14T19:47:49Z', '2026-10-14T19:47:49Z', 54, NULL, 'rule', 54, '2026-10-14, task 54: 2026-10-14, task 54: docs/design.md ''Other ships'' adds the bagger probe kind; ''Earth-orbit points of interest'' places THRIFT and TANKER 3 ahead of Highport with the clearance check; ''Asteroids'' gives BAGGER 3''s time-shifted rails and the passing rock''s fixture axis; ''Rigid bodies'' adds their rows and parts; docs/story.md moves THRIFT and TANKER 3 from behind to ahead of Highport and draws BAGGER 3 with the rock; docs/architecture.md contact cost names them.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (52, 'Story: the quest line', 'answered', '[]', 'task 52 per plan 11: story. One skill per quest, each farther and heavier, every leg within the Mule''s 6.65 / 1.74 / 1.02 km/s and 180 person-days with refuelling only at Fulmar Depot, Highport, Peary Depot (running check in docs/story.md ''Running checks''; tightest: BQ7''s loaded 565 of 1,011 m/s). Completion conditions read only simulation state. Full text: docs/story.md ''The quest line''.', 'Nine quests in fixed order: 1 Shakedown (CAIRN OPS, start orbit, attitude and a 2x2.8 m/s out-and-back burn; milestone 2), 2 Home port (TERN, rendezvous and berth at Tern Station; 3), 3 Seventeen (LINDGREN, grapple K-17, push to Pe < 100 km by hand, release, raise own Pe, burn-up; 3), 4 Topping off (Fulmar Depot fixture A, refuel; 3), 5 The long climb (Highport, berth at the plant, refuel; 3), 6 Rock three (Skua Yard 3, 50 t, delivered to the plant; 3), 7 The January window (POD 14, 12 t, to Peary Depot via the TLI near 2040-01-21, refuel, back to Highport; 4), 8 The big one (Skua Yard 5, 100 t; 3, placed after 7, skipped if given after 2040-03-02 12:00), 9 BQ7 (intercept 400,000 km out, grapple, capture at perigee 2040-03-12 into Highport''s orbit, leave in Skua Yard, then the ending; 4 should, skipped if given after 2040-03-06). If the passing rock is dropped, quest 9 is replaced by the ending alone after quest 8 (assay of Skua Yard 5); no quest depends on moving story ships (if dropped, THRIFT''s messages come from Highport). The Sun is no quest (0.551 AU and back ~250 days > 180 person-days). Death restarts the step from its checkpoint; checkpoints carry a Δv floor; a stranded ship gets CAIRN OPS''s offer, Y restores the last checkpoint.', NULL, '2026-10-14T20:01:02Z', '2026-10-14T20:01:02Z', 52, NULL, 'rule', 52, '2026-10-14, task 52: 2026-10-14, task 52: docs/story.md ''The quest line'' written (nine quests, rules, running checks); Premise''s ending and Start date''s slack refer to quest 8; Names and terms add POD 14 and the quest titles; follow-ups task 55, task 56, task 57 in plan 10: game design.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (53, 'Deliveries, the stranded offer, checkpoint floors and Skua Yard''s layout', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/story.md ''Rules for every quest'' (decision 52: Story: the quest line) relies on these; task 55 puts them in docs/design.md (''Towing'', ''Dying and what follows'', ''Saving'', ''Earth-orbit points of interest'', ''Rigid bodies'') and docs/architecture.md (''Removal of objects'', ''Checkpoints'', tests). The yard''s spacing puts every autopilot aim point (1 km behind a target) mid-gap, 333 m from both neighbours, and orders the rocks as the quests take them so each tow leaves forwards into the empty lane to Highport; the offer and floors make sure no state of the quest line is a dead end and no checkpoint holds a ship that cannot finish its step.', 'Delivery: a towable load released with its CoM within 100 m of a receiving fixture''s pin (Highport''s plant, milestone 3; Peary Depot''s berth, milestone 4) at <= 0.05 m/s relative to the object, still within 150 m 600 s later, is removed with cause :delivered (architecture ''Removal of objects'', causes :burned-up, :impact, :delivered); any towable load counts. Stranded offer (milestone 5): outside a berth, Δv with the load below the step''s floor, an escape path (e >= 1 around Earth past perigee, or the Sun dominant) or the step''s object out of reach, held 10 min game time: CAIRN OPS RADIO marked Y; Y restores the last checkpoint as Return does; no warp drop-out. Checkpoint floors (milestone 5): each step has (v_f, m_L); every checkpoint is vetoed while v_e ln((m_dry+m_L+m_p)/(m_dry+m_L)) < v_f; the floor lives in the quest state, clock.lisp reads it. Skua Yard is a string on Highport''s orbit, 666.7 m apart at apogee from the front: Skua Yard 3 (+2,000 m), POD 14 (+1,333 m, M0 179.87587 deg, inertial, fixture aft at apogee, no spin), 5, 1 (the reference, M0 179.87 deg), 2, 4, 6, the rock mover at -4,000 m.', NULL, '2026-10-14T20:06:34Z', '2026-10-14T20:06:34Z', 55, NULL, 'rule', 55, '2026-10-14, task 55: 2026-10-14, task 55: docs/design.md ''Towing'' (Delivery), ''Dying and what follows'' (the stranded offer), ''Saving'' (checkpoint floors), ''Earth-orbit points of interest'' (Skua Yard''s layout, POD 14), ''Rigid bodies'' (POD 14 row), scope table and ''What the story may use''; docs/architecture.md ''Re-entry of objects'' generalised to ''Removal of objects'', ''Checkpoints'' (floors), tests for delivery, floors and the offer; docs/story.md cites them; task 56 gets the approach inside Skua Yard.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (54, 'Autopilot rendezvous: phasing, lunar chain, hyperbolic targets, clear approaches', 'answered', '[]', 'task 52''s quest line budgets <= 50 m/s phasing per rendezvous and uses presets the design did not define: a 48 h single-revolution Lambert search cannot close a large phase gap between orbits of equal period, a TLI aimed only at periapsis altitude and inclination does not reach Peary Depot''s plane, a hyperbola has no period, and the straight path from 1 km behind a Skua Yard object passes its neighbour 667 m behind. Computed cases (docs/design.md ''Rendezvous: search, phasing and the approach''): Fulmar from 400 km <= 33 m/s within 48 h, Highport from Fulmar <= 28, Skua Yard from Highport 0.2, Peary after LOI, Highport after a lunar return and Skua Yard after BQ7''s capture 0. Refines decision 32: Autopilot scope and safety rules; its safety rules apply unchanged.', 'Rendezvous searches two families within a time limit (48 h default, 12 h-10 days on the AUTOPILOT screen; least total dv, ties within 1 m/s to the earlier arrival): A direct (Lambert, departures over the limit, 0.4-1.5 target periods) and B a phasing orbit (a tangential burn, or one merged into the plan''s capture/LOI/Hohmann apsis burn, period T_t - dt/N to catch up or T_t + (T_t - dt)/N to fall back, N whole revolutions <= 32, then A); falling back merged into a capture costs nothing, so phasing after a capture or LOI is free within 2 target periods. Lunar places from Earth orbit: one chain TLI (B-plane aimed at the place''s plane, periapsis 100 km, same sense) - mid-course - LOI with phasing - aim point - terminal; ''TLI: Peary Depot'' is the same chain. Targets on a hyperbola: departures anywhere on the ship''s orbit over up to 7 days, transfers 2-10 days, meeting inbound at least 36 h before the target''s periapsis, aim point 1 km out on the fixture axis. Terminal path: straight segments >= 100 m from the target''s CoM until the stop point and >= 200 m beyond every other object''s bound; aim point candidates in order 1 km behind on the orbit, 1 km radially from the stop point (outward unless the fixture axis points inward), 1 km out of plane; none clear: refused. Skua Yard''s rocks hold their fixture axes as POD 14 (inertial, aft along the orbit at apogee); ''Rendezvous: Skua Yard'' stops the stack''s CoM at the yard''s drop point, Skua Yard 3''s slot (+2,000 m at apogee).', NULL, '2026-10-14T20:34:03Z', '2026-10-14T20:34:03Z', 56, NULL, 'rule', 56, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (55, 'Meeting 2040 BQ7 and the lunar arrival from the polar plane', 'answered', '[]', 'task 56 computed both by Lambert and patched conics. At 400,000 km BQ7 is 17.6 deg past the apse line (true anomaly -162.4 deg), not at the transfer''s apogee: meeting there costs 2,404 m/s leaving 03-05; meeting 1.5 days out leaves a day and a half to grapple before the capture. The design''s 1,193 m/s to Peary assumed v_inf 0.83 km/s, the Moon moving along the ship''s plane; from the polar plane it moves across it. Both still fit: 996 m/s with 50 t after the intercept (565 needed), 1,353 m/s with POD 14 out of 3,866.', 'The tug meets 2040 BQ7 about 1.5 days before perigee near 325,000 km (2040-03-10 ~23:00 UTC), leaving Highport''s orbit 20-60 min after a perigee pass: 2,010 m/s leaving 2040-03-05 (1,683 on 03-03, 2,289 on 03-06), not ~2 days near 400,000 km. A lunar arrival from the polar plane has v_inf 0.99-1.04 km/s (the Moon crosses the plane at right angles), LOI into 100 km 880-925 m/s, not the coplanar 823: Highport -> Peary Depot in the January window 1,273 m/s (TLI 376 from the 2040-01-20 06:01 perigee, LOI 897 with a 4.9 deg plane change), LEO -> Peary Depot 3,983 m/s.', NULL, '2026-10-14T20:34:10Z', '2026-10-14T20:34:10Z', 56, NULL, 'rule', 56, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (56, 'Flight-script actions, start states and the quest play-through', 'answered', '[]', 'task 57 (Architecture: flight-script actions for the quest play-through): milestone 5''s headless play-through needs grapple, berth, delivery, refuelling, acknowledgements and quest waits that the milestone-2 vocabulary lacked, and ''no shortcuts around piloting'' rules out setting state, so every action is a key the player has and the last metres are flown by a closed loop on the cockpit''s own readouts. Chaining by end states through the save format makes every link a save, and re-running the chain instead of caching files keeps no stale state. The play-through stays out of scripts/test for its ~15 s budget.', 'Flight scripts (decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts) gain pilot actions that press the game''s keys: :target, :autopilot <preset> <goal> [:range] / :off, :grapple :arm/:stow, :release (load or berth), :transfer :on/:off, :translate <axis> :dv, :keys … :for, :ack (Y, waits for a Y message), :warp :coast (re-raise after drop-outs, <= x4 in the autopilot''s terminal phase, x1 in :approach), and :approach <fixture> :speed 0.1: a closed-loop pilot in flights.lisp that reads only the grapple range finder and the TARGET screen and writes keys at x1 (fine control, 1/16 s ticks, hold at 20 m until aligned, up to 3 tries, 2 h limit). Waits and expects take flat conditions (:quest-step "n.m" as a string, :date, :periapsis-below/above, :apoapsis-below/above, :settled, :rate-below, :removed, :on-station, :berthed, :latched, :autopilot-done, :quest-line-done) with a :within limit (default 30 days); expects add :dv-below, :burn <kind> :between, :since-burn, :elapsed-below, :date-between, :clearance-above, :aim-point; the autopilot labels its burns (:departure, :mcc, :capture, :phasing, :arrival, :terminal, :trim). Start states: :new-game, :state (date, orbit, berthed, grappled, propellant, removed, quest step; milestone 3), :after <script> (its end state round-tripped through saves.lisp; run first if not yet run in the process), :file <save>. flights.lisp loads after saves.lisp. Milestone 5: flights/quest-1 … quest-9.sexp chained by :after, test play-through in tests/flights.lisp run by scripts/play-through and nix flake check, not scripts/test; expected ~15 s for ~74 days of game time.', NULL, '2026-10-14T20:51:17Z', '2026-10-14T20:51:17Z', 57, NULL, 'rule', 57, '2026-10-14, task 57: 2026-10-14, task 57: docs/architecture.md ''Scripted runs'' rewritten (Steps, Actions, Waits and expectations, Start states, The terminal approach, Running scripts, Scripts by milestone, The quest play-through with quest-1 and quest-3 written out); flights.lisp moved after saves.lisp in ''Files in load order''; docs/design.md ''Scripted runs'' (rendezvous) points at the start states; docs/story.md follow-up marked done. Deliveries close the last ~10 m by hand after the autopilot''s 100 m station, since a 90 m stop point would fall under the design''s 100 m clearance from Highport''s and Peary Depot''s CoM.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (57, 'Story: how it is told, its texts and the story ships'' schedule', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 53 fixes every text the game shows so quests.lisp can copy them and the play-through can test them. THRIFT''s two-way Peary trip and TANKER 3''s return could never switch slots near Highport while the pilot lives there (decision 27: Other ships, 200 km), so each makes one-way moves timed when the pilot is away (the lunar trip, the BQ7 trip). The tape notes did not fit their places at the readability rule''s cap height; the introduction wrongly put everyone in one orbit and the start over the pole (the start is at the ascending node, decision 38: Start orbit: polar). Refines decision 49: Story: names, call signs and ships and decision 52: Story: the quest line (texts, quest 7''s and 9''s cards now name the autopilot chains docs/design.md and docs/architecture.md use).', 'docs/story.md ''How the story is told'' and the quest tables are binding: every RADIO message has an id, trigger, sender, Y flag and exact text (quests 1-9, dated D1-D7, the stranded offer S1); JOB cards are a title and up to 3 lines of <= 26 characters. COMMS rows: 1 card title + step id (inverted), 2-4 card lines, 5 a rule with ''Y TO COPY'' while a Y is due, 6-11 the log (header ''HH:MM SENDER [+1.3s] [Y]'', text wrapped at 26, a grey date line per UTC day); ''NO JOB'' without a quest; the log is rebuilt from the quest state''s sent messages after a restore or load. Radio: digits for values to fly, words for talk, <= 160 characters (the ending 9h excepted), Y is the pilot''s only reply. Light time from the sender''s position, rounded up to the 1/64 s grid, shown as ''+1.3s'' when >= 0.5 s. Story ships: THRIFT leaves Highport''s orbit 2040-01-21 06:00 and parks half a turn ahead of Peary Depot from 2040-01-26 06:00; TANKER 3 parks 2 km ahead of Fulmar Depot 2040-01-22 to 2040-03-07, then back ahead of Highport; a move leaves one slot and appears in the next, not drawn in transit, each switch only while the player is beyond 200 km. Tape notes get line breaks, the third moves from the gauge bezel to the rack beside it. Introduction: ''most of them in one polar orbit'', ''on an orbit over both poles''.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:03:10Z', '2026-10-14T21:03:10Z', 53, NULL, 'rule', 53, '2026-10-14, task 53: 2026-10-14, task 53: docs/story.md ''How the story is told'' written (channels, voice, COMMS rows, light time, other ships at work); every RADIO in per-quest tables with ids, the D table and S1; quest 8''s cards written out; cards 7.1, 7.2, 7.5, 9.2, 9.4-9.6 fixed to the autopilot chains; tape notes broken into lines; introduction corrected; THRIFT and TANKER 3 schedules. docs/design.md: Skua Yard spacing 666.7 m (not 300-800 m), the story ships'' trips, S1 cited, ''Messages and radio'' points at the story. Follow-up task in plan 10 for the story ships'' slots.

2026-10-14, task 53: 2026-10-14, task 53: the follow-up is task 58: Design: parking slots and moves of the story ships THRIFT and TANKER 3, in plan 11: story (plan 10 is closed), not plan 10.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (58, 'Story ships'' slots at Fulmar Depot and Peary Depot, schedules and clear plans', 'answered', '[]', 'task 58: the slots clear the aim points and terminal paths by kilometres (TANKER 3 >= 1,878 m beyond its bound from Fulmar''s fixture A approach; THRIFT 3,670 km from Peary''s). Quest 4''s transfer from a 370 km drift orbit overshoots ahead and passes TANKER 3 258 m beyond its bound, clear but tight; the LOI''s phasing orbit could cross THRIFT''s slot at the LOI pass when the depot leads by half a period, which the January window avoids (LOI 02:37 before THRIFT''s 06:00 arrival) and the plan check covers otherwise. The deferral is evaluated on the step grid so runs stay deterministic; the until-time at Fulmar is needed for a transit gap before the 03-07 arrival.', 'TANKER 3''s Fulmar slot: Fulmar Depot''s elements, u0 180.01666 deg (2 km ahead), 2040-01-22 06:00 until 2040-03-06 06:00 (departure added), then Highport''s slot from 2040-03-07 06:00. THRIFT''s lunar slot: Peary Depot''s elements, u0 180 deg (half a turn ahead, 3,674.8 km), from 2040-01-26 06:00. Both inertial in the LVLH attitude of their slot''s elements at the epoch. A story ship''s schedule is a list of stays (slot, from, until) with transit gaps; a switch record of actual switch times lives in the simulation state (checkpoints, saves); place-state(t) is a pure function of t and the record, nil in transit. Rule after each step: a due switch happens at t if the stack is more than 200 km from the slot concerned, else waits; scheduled times end a step (no warp drop-out). D2, D3 and 7f follow the actual switches. The autopilot''s plan check drops a candidate whose shot path from its first burn to the aim point comes within 200 m beyond the bound of any object other than the target (covers THRIFT at a later month''s LOI).', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:10:19Z', '2026-10-14T21:10:19Z', 58, NULL, 'rule', 58, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (59, 'Rendezvous transfers pass the target clear', 'answered', '[]', 'Found by task 58: a Hohmann-like transfer from a lower orbit arrives slower than the target and falls back through the target''s place before the braking burn. Checked with 2D Lambert transfers and Kepler propagation (throwaway). Extending the existing plan check costs <= 3 m/s and no new mechanism; preferring arrivals from behind would need a new search rule and costs more from below.', 'Refines decision 54: Autopilot rendezvous: phasing, lunar chain, hyperbolic targets, clear approaches. The plan check of decision 58: Story ships'' slots at Fulmar Depot and Peary Depot, schedules and clear plans also holds the shot path >= 200 m beyond the target''s own bound from the first burn until the aim point (the :arrival burn''s start); the terminal path''s 100 m from the CoM applies after it. The least-dv transfer from below passes the target''s CoM 10-27 m away (K-17, Fulmar Depot, Highport at apogee: inside their bounds); the least clear one costs <= 3 m/s more (Tern 0.05, Fulmar 0.75-0.9, K-17 2.4-2.8, Highport 0.3), arriving a few degrees short of or past the Hohmann''s 180 deg. Quest 3''s transfer 205 -> 208 m/s; quest 4''s worst case 89 -> 90 m/s; scripts: rendezvous-fulmar-phasing :dv-below 114 and :clearance-above 234 :only (:fulmar-depot) :until :aim-point, preset-<place> the same with bound + 200 and 3 m/s more.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:16:17Z', '2026-10-14T21:16:17Z', 59, NULL, 'rule', 59, '2026-10-14, task 59: 2026-10-14, task 59: docs/design.md ''The terminal path and crowded targets'' extends the plan check to the target with the table of passes and costs; ''The quest line''s rendezvous'' (Fulmar worst case 90), ''Scripted runs'' (rendezvous-fulmar-phasing <= 114 m/s, >= 234 m), TANKER 3''s Fulmar note, ''Δv checked against the ship'' (K-17 220); docs/architecture.md :clearance-above gains :only and :until :aim-point, used by rendezvous-fulmar-phasing and preset-<place>; docs/story.md quest 3 (208 m/s transfer, 622 m/s, 4,795 kg), quest 4''s start state and the running checks.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (60, 'Design pillars', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md cites the pillars as the reason for many cuts in ''Scope'' (landing, gravity assists, faces, planets as destinations), but no decision recorded them; task 62: Check the documents against the decision log found the gap. They restate the milestone 1 ideas and its ''scope over realism'' constraint.', 'Five pillars that keep or cut a feature, as docs/design.md ''Pillars'' states them: the ship is a tool (every part has a visible job); Newton is the game (all of the ship''s motion integrated, no shortcuts, propellant the limit); the cockpit is home-made (bolted-on screens, gauges, lamps; flyable from it alone); real sky, real places (real bodies and stars with sources, invented places plausible); small and finished (a feature that costs far more than it shows is cut; one ship done well).', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:24:46Z', '2026-10-14T21:24:46Z', 62, NULL, 'rule', 62, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (61, 'Performance target GPU: the default Vulkan device, the Intel iGPU', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/luv-capabilities.md ''Performance'' measured Luvcraft at 24-36 Hz on the Iris Xe and recommends assuming an iGPU; luv has no device selection, so players get the first device; decision 6: Target platforms promises any Vulkan 1.4 iGPU works. Clarifies, does not change, decision 42''s ''developer''s machine''.', 'The 60 Hz at 1280x800 target of milestone 6 (decision 42: Scope: what each milestone builds) is measured on the GPU luv picks by default, the first Vulkan device: on the developer''s machine the Intel Iris Xe, not the RTX 4060; the CPU share rests on the measured ~180 ns per RK4 step.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:27:55Z', '2026-10-14T21:27:55Z', 63, NULL, 'rule', 63, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (62, 'Scope: must rows for each milestone''s Outcome', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 64 checked each milestone''s Outcome and Done when against the must rows of docs/design.md ''Scope''. Milestone 5''s ''other ships at their work'' and milestone 4''s ''the asteroid belt ... rendered'' were covered only by should rows; both cost little (meshes from the tug''s parts that the places'' shapes already include, decision 47: Rigid bodies of the places; five markers and orbit lines), so they become must. Decision 32: Autopilot scope and safety rules gave milestone 4 Lambert transfers to asteroids and planets in scope, but decision 39: Places in scope and decision 42: Scope: what each milestone builds leave none in scope; a manoeuvre with no destination is cut (pillar ''small and finished'').', 'Other ships as scenery at the places (AURORA 11, TANKER 2, STUBBORN, THISTLE, GRINDSTONE): must, milestone 3, not should. Real asteroids on the map (Kamo''oalewa, Ceres, Vesta, Pallas, Hygiea): must, milestone 4, not should. The autopilot''s heliocentric Lambert transfers to asteroids and planets are cut: no asteroid or planet is a destination; 2040 BQ7 is reached by ''Rendezvous: 2040 BQ7'' within Earth''s sphere. A milestone''s Outcome may be covered by a must row of an earlier milestone that it keeps.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:31:46Z', '2026-10-14T21:31:46Z', 64, NULL, 'rule', 64, '2026-10-14, task 64: 2026-10-14, task 64: docs/design.md ''Feature list'' (scenery ships must 3, real asteroids must 4, transfers to asteroids and planets cut), ''The autopilot'' (mid-course corrections kept, transfers cut), ''Asteroids'', ''Each milestone against its Outcome and Done when''; docs/architecture.md ''Files in load order'' milestones per file');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (63, 'POD 14 and Skua Yard''s layout from milestone 3', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 65 found that milestone 3''s scripts rendezvous-skua-yard and skua-yard-apogee-approach (docs/design.md ''Scripted runs'') fly past POD 14 and expect clearance from it, and milestone 4''s rendezvous-peary and return-highport start with POD 14 grappled or delivered, while the feature list, ''Earth-orbit points of interest'', ''Rigid bodies'' and docs/architecture.md places.lisp gave it to milestone 5. One rigid body of an existing kind costs little; moving it earlier makes every script runnable in its milestone and quest 7 flyable from milestone 4 as docs/story.md says. Partly supersedes decision 39: Places in scope (only POD 14''s milestone, 5 in task 55''s consequence).', 'POD 14 (the standard 12 t cargo pod in Skua Yard) and the yard''s layout of decision 53 are built in milestone 3 with the Earth-orbit places, not in milestone 5; quest 7 stays a milestone-5 quest that milestone 4 makes flyable.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:36:25Z', '2026-10-14T21:36:25Z', 65, NULL, 'rule', 65, '2026-10-14, task 65: 2026-10-14, task 65: docs/design.md feature list row ''POD 14 in Skua Yard, the yard''s layout'' moved to milestone 3, ''Earth-orbit points of interest'' (POD 14), ''Rigid bodies'' (cargo pod) and ''What the story may use'' follow; docs/architecture.md places.lisp lists POD 14 under milestone 3.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (64, 'Scripts always re-enter the dev shell', 'answered', '[]', 'Refines decision 10: Test and screenshot commands. An inherited dev shell can be stale or carry a foreign LD_LIBRARY_PATH: an agent session''s host alsa-lib (glibc 2.43) ahead of luv''s libraries made libSDL3.so fail to load (task 68). Re-entry costs ~0.6 s; the shell hook unsets LD_LIBRARY_PATH and activates luv''s native environment.', 'scripts/test, scripts/screenshot and ./sly re-enter ''nix develop'' unless LIS_DEV_SHELL=1 (set by their own re-exec and by the flake check); an inherited LUV_DEV_SHELL or LUV_DEV_ENVIRONMENT_MODE (direnv) is not trusted.', NULL, '2026-10-14T21:42:45Z', '2026-10-14T21:42:45Z', 68, NULL, 'rule', 68, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (65, 'Vectors and quaternions in math.lisp', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/architecture.md ''Files in load order'' asks for specialised arrays but leaves the layout open. Specialised double arrays are what luv''s write-buffer and SBCL''s unboxed arithmetic handle well, print readably for saves and tests, and compare with equalp bit for bit; in-place twins let the RK4 step (decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock, ~180 ns) run without consing while tests and ./sly eval use the pure forms. Scalar-first matches the integrator''s q-dot = 1/2 q (x) (0, omega) as written. One subtract-then-convert function enforces decision 5: Precision of positions.', 'vec3 is (simple-array double-float (3)), a quaternion (simple-array double-float (4)) scalar first (w x y z), unit, body -> inertial; the plain functions return fresh arrays, and each hot one has an in-place twin with the destination first (vec3-add! out a b), all typed double-float and declaimed inline; no structs or CLOS for them, no luv.arithmetic vec3. The single-float boundary is one function, (relative-single p origin &optional out) = (coerce (- p origin) ''single-float) per component, writing into a (simple-array single-float (3)) or a slot of a larger single-float array for write-buffer.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', 72, NULL, 'rule', 72, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (66, 'Calendar dates on the 1/64 s grid', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 14: Units and time epoch fixes TT - UTC = 69.184 s, which is not a multiple of 1/64 s, while decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock puts steps and scheduled events on the 1/64 s grid. Rounding every date-derived time onto the grid keeps t exact under power-of-two steps and makes scheduled times land on step boundaries; the 3.5 ms offset is invisible. Keeping days and seconds apart avoids the ~1e-5 s loss of going through a double Julian date.', 'units.lisp converts UTC calendar dates to t exactly (t = (JDN - 2451545) x 86400 + seconds of day - 43200 + 69.184, integer days and seconds kept apart until the last sum) and back; every game time made from a date (the start, schedules, flight-script :date, story dates) goes through (utc->grid-t …), which rounds to the nearest multiple of 1/64 s; *start-date* is the list (2040 1 1 0 0 0) and (start-t) its grid time, 2040-01-01 00:00:00.0035 UTC. Display truncates to whole seconds, so the start shows 00:00:00.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', 72, NULL, 'rule', 72, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (67, 'Ephemeris cache and the Moon''s series frame', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 18: Ephemerides fixes the models and node spacing; docs/architecture.md ''Ephemerides'' put the table in the game object, but bodies.lisp loads long before main.lisp defines game and tests have no game; nodes are pure functions of their index, so a global cache cannot change a result (Determinism) and stays a cache. Caching the geocentric Moon keeps Earth-frame lookups (the only frame of milestone 2) free of 1.5e11 m cancellations (decision 5: Precision of positions). Precessing the full ecliptic instead of longitude alone removes up to ~20 arcsec of latitude error by 2049 for a few multiplications per node. Horizons vectors are geometric, so the spot checks compare like with like.', 'The Hermite node cache stores two quantities per 4,096 s node: the Earth-Moon barycentre heliocentric (Standish Table 1, analytic velocity) and the Moon geocentric (Meeus ch. 47, velocity by central difference of +-60 s); Earth heliocentric = EMB - mu_Moon/(mu_Earth+mu_Moon) x Moon, and every body''s state in an origin''s frame is built from these in double without differencing two heliocentric values (Moon from Earth = the geocentric Moon itself). The cache is a global defvar in bodies.lisp (a ''cache'' under docs/architecture.md ''Dev loop''), direct-mapped, 64 slots per quantity keyed by node index mod 64, a slot replaced when its index differs; not in the game object. Meeus''s mean geometric ecliptic of date (no nutation, no light time) goes to the J2000 ecliptic by Meeus''s rigorous ecliptic precession (eq. 21.5 with eta, Pi, p), then to ICRF by epsilon = 84381.448 arcsec.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', 72, NULL, 'rule', 72, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (68, 'Orbit elements: one struct for every conic', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/architecture.md stores hyperbolas as q, e, i, Omega, omega, t_p and ellipses as ''elements at an epoch''; one form for both lets the map, the readout and later rails take any osculating orbit without a branch on the type, and q and t_p stay finite as e -> 1. t_p nearest t keeps M = n (t - t_p) within pi, so no precision is lost to a large mean anomaly. The thresholds sit far below any orbit the game flies and above the roundoff of state -> elements in LEO (~1e-15 relative).', 'orbits.lisp holds one struct, orbit: mu, q (periapsis radius), e, i, Omega, omega, t_p (a periapsis passage time: for an ellipse the one nearest the state''s t), with a, p, n, period and apoapsis derived; it serves ellipses and hyperbolas alike (decision 46: Rails across spheres, hyperbolas and re-entry). Singular cases by convention: e < 1e-11 gives omega = 0 (periapsis at the node, the anomaly is the argument of latitude), i < 1e-11 or > pi - 1e-11 gives Omega = 0 (the node at ICRF +x, the anomaly the true longitude); angles in [0, 2 pi). Round trips compare the non-singular combinations (e cos(Omega+omega), e sin(Omega+omega), tan(i/2) cos Omega, tan(i/2) sin Omega, mean longitude at t) where the classical angles are undefined. Parabolas do not exist: e within 1e-6 of 1 is milestone 4''s nudge.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', '2026-10-14T22:01:48Z', 72, NULL, 'rule', 72, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (69, 'Ship state, step rule and kinematic attitude in physics.lisp', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 2 (task 73) puts the state, gravity, RK4 and the step rule in physics.lisp, while docs/architecture.md ''Drift bounds'' took the step rule from clock.lisp; one place for it (physics, loaded before clock) lets tests and prediction call it without the clock. decision 19: Time-warp rules makes attitude kinematic above x4 but leaves how a burn''s direction follows a hold inside a coarse step: evaluating the hold at each RK4 stage keeps the integrator fourth order for thrust along a turning orbital frame and needs no attitude dynamics. The odometer in closed form serves :after-dv cut-offs (decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts) and the dv expectations without an extra integrated variable.', 'physics.lisp holds the ship''s state as a struct ship-state (r, v: vec3 in the dominant body''s frame; q: quaternion body -> inertial; w: body angular velocity vec3; m-p: propellant kg; dv: the delta-v odometer, sum over steps of |F| / mdot x ln(m0 / m1), or |F| h / m when mdot = 0, exact per step since force and mass flow are held over a step) with a deep copy-ship-state; one RK4 step function (rk4-step! state h t inputs) takes body force, body torque, mass flow, the inertia tensor and its inverse about the CoM (held over the step) and a gravity model (:none, :point, :j2, :full = Earth with J2 plus Sun and Moon with indirect terms, Earth frame only in milestone 2) and conses nothing; the coarse step rule (coarse-step r t thrusting) lives in physics.lisp and clock.lisp calls it. Above x4 attitude is kinematic: the step is given an attitude function of (t, r, v) (the active hold''s target, inertial or orbital, with its roll offset), evaluated at each RK4 stage for the thrust direction; q and w are not integrated but set at the step''s end to the function''s value and the target frame''s rate, so a burn in a prograde hold follows the turning velocity exactly.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:07:50Z', '2026-10-14T22:07:50Z', 73, NULL, 'rule', 73, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (70, 'Flight computer: per-step control laws, holds and the RCS mixer', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''RCS forces, torques and accelerations'' says the mixer is solved per frame, but decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock and docs/architecture.md ''The clock'' require that the frame rate never changes a trajectory; closed loops (rate damping, holds) run per frame would. At most 256 fine steps per real second (x4) make a ~30 us solve affordable. Weighted BVLS with QR is a small, deterministic, dependency-free way to get ''torque exactly, force as fits'' (design) without an LP or QP solver; the sqrt braking curve is the bang-bang slew at 80 % torque that ''Control modes'' asks for, the linear tail settles it inside 0.1 deg, and feed-forward of the target frame''s rate keeps an orbital hold within bounds as the frame turns 0.065 deg/s in LEO. The design''s roll rule (''the roll at engagement, or by default the cab away from the body'') is ambiguous; the default for orbital holds and the current attitude for R is the reading that makes holds reproducible in flight scripts. The closed-form translation capability avoids a second solve per step and matches the design''s 1,266 N wet and 1,391 N dry (decision 29: Control scheme: key map, control modes, gamepad).', 'The flight computer (controls.lisp) runs once per fine step, before it, from the state at the step''s start, and the mixer with it; key states are sampled once per frame and held, as decision 16 says for controls. Mixer: duty cycles u in [0, 1] for the 24 RCS thrusters by bounded-variable least squares (Stark-Parker BVLS, Householder QR on the active columns, a fixed iteration limit and fixed column order) on the stacked system [w B_torque; B_force; e I] u = [w tau_c; F_c; 0] with w large enough (about 1e6 per metre) that the commanded torque is met within 1e-6 of the couple whenever it is reachable, and e small (about 1e-4 N) so that among equal fits the least-propellant duty set wins; zero command gives u = 0 exactly. Couples for clamps: pitch and yaw 2 x 445 N x 14.0 m, roll 4 x 445 N x 1.8 m, from the layout; fine control x0.1. Direct: a held rotation key commands the full couple on its axis. Rate damping: tau = I (w_c - w) / 0.5 s + w x I w, clamped per body axis to the couple, w_c 5 deg/s (fine 0.5) on held axes, 0 elsewhere. Holds: target attitude q_t(t, r, v) and its frame rate w_t; error rotation vector phi from q* q_t (shortest arc); w_c = w_t + phi-hat min(5 deg/s, sqrt(2 x 0.8 alpha |phi|), 0.5 /s x |phi|), alpha the 80 % couple over the inertia about phi-hat, then the rate law above clamped to 80 % of the couple; settled when |phi| < 0.1 deg and |w - w_t| < 0.02 deg/s, unsettled when |phi| > 0.2 deg or |w - w_t| > 0.05 deg/s. Holds 1-6 engage with the design''s default roll (cab -z radial out; towards normal for the radial holds), R with the current attitude; roll keys during a hold change a roll offset about the hold axis at the rate-damping rate, kept by the hold. Translation: a held key commands the axis''s nominal torque-free force (+-x 1,780 N; +-y, +-z 890 N x (1 + min(a_f, a_r) / max(a_f, a_r)) with a_f, a_r the front and rear blocks'' arms from the CoM), x0.1 in fine control. All flight-computer state (mode, hold, roll offset, inertial target, settled) lives in the controls struct, copied with the simulation state.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:08:04Z', '2026-10-14T22:08:04Z', 73, NULL, 'rule', 73, '2026-10-15, task 101: 2026-10-14, task 101: controls.lisp''s mixer uses w = 1e5 per metre, not 1e6: at 1e6 rounding in the torque rows (w² × |B_τ|) swamps the force rows'' gradient, so a translation beside a rotation lost most of its force; at 1e5 torques are met within 1e-7 N m. The BVLS frees a column only when its gradient exceeds its own rounding bound (16 eps Σ|A||A u|), and stops at the KKT point in 5-23 solves (limit 96), 2-10 us per call. A saturating lateral command gets 1,592 N wet, not the nominal 1,266 N: the axial nozzles'' yaw/pitch couples balance more of the front lateral thrust; the keys still command 1,266 N (docs/design.md ''RCS forces, torques and accelerations''). With the CoM 0.22 m below the axis a pure +x command also fires a small z-nozzle pitch couple to trim the aft nozzles'' moment.

2026-10-15, task 102: 2026-10-14, task 102: controls.lisp''s holds read decision 70 thus: the target frame''s rate is v̂ × a / |v| with point-mass gravity (exact for the orbital frame, n (r × v)^ on a circle); while a roll key turns the roll offset its rate is fed forward on body x; fine control scales the holds'' 5°/s cap and 80 % clamp too; R on an active inertial hold releases it like holds 1-6; a kinematic step (attitude-fn) reports a hold settled. A 90° slew settles in ~25 s wet and dry (design: 21 s and 19 s at full torque, 80 % here), R after a 2°/s tumble in 6.6 s; a settled hold step costs ~5 µs at :full. quat-from-basis! and quat-from-mat3! (math.lisp) are the non-consing twins the hold law needed.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (71, 'The Mule''s mass model in ship.lisp', 'answered', '[]', 'Checked by a throwaway script (task 73): these shapes give the design''s CoM rows to 0.01 m and the tensor''s diagonal within 4.2 % (dry I_xx 5.16e4 against 4.95e4, others within 2.5 %), I_xz to the table''s two figures, so the ''Inertia'' table stands and the tests compare against it within 5 %. A 1 kg cut in an estimated part keeps 7,961 / 35,961 kg and every delta-v, load and test figure (85,961 kg wet + 50 t) in docs/ unchanged.', 'ship.lisp builds the Mule''s mass properties from docs/design.md ''Parts'' with these shapes: truss as rods (4 longerons 112.65 kg each at y, z = +-1.8 along x 0-15; 11 frames at x = 0, 1.5 ... 15 of 4 tubes 3.6 m, 16.06 kg each; 40 face diagonals 3.9 m, 17.39 kg, one per bay and face, centred at the bay''s middle; every member x1.15), cab a thin-walled cylinder r 1.5 x 4.5 along x, engine a solid cylinder r 0.725 x 2.2, tanks thin-walled cylinders r 1.6 over their listed lengths (domes ignored), grapple a solid cylinder r 0.8 x 1.6, RCS blocks, gas conditioning, equipment bay, radiators and wings as solid boxes of their listed sizes, cabling and plumbing a box 15 x 0.2 x 0.2; propellant as docs/design.md ''Inertia'' says (solid cylinders r 1.52 from the tanks'' aft ends at x 0.50 and 9.76, lengths f x 8.86 and f x 3.72 for the fill fraction f, LH2 1/6.5 and LOX 5.5/6.5 of the mass). The parts'' listed masses sum to 7,962 kg against the stated dry 7,961 kg of decision 22: The player''s ship and its propulsion; cabling and plumbing (an estimate) becomes 199 kg so the sum is 7,961 kg and every figure derived from it stands. Dry sums (mass, first and second moments about R) are precomputed; per step only the propellant terms and the shift to the CoM are added.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:08:15Z', '2026-10-14T22:08:15Z', 73, NULL, 'rule', 73, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (72, 'Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 3 (task 74). docs/architecture.md ''Checkpoints'' lists the sim state''s contents for every milestone but leaves milestone 2''s struct, the shape of the step, and how a dv cut-off lands on the grid open. One step function shared by the frame, scripts and prediction is what makes ''the clock''s states equal the direct run bit for bit'' and ''a predicted orbit equals the flown one'' true by construction. The cut-off rule gives the same grid point at fine steps and within one fine step at coarse ones (x100 burns, decision 31: Warp choices within the time-warp rules). A step budget for prediction keeps tests deterministic; decision 20: Trajectory prediction on the frame thread''s 1 ms is ~4,096 coarse steps at the measured cost.', 'clock.lisp holds a struct sim (t, central body :earth, ship-state, controls, gravity model :full by default, the armed dv cut-off or nil, the loss record or nil, the step count) with a deep copy-sim, equalp-comparable; (new-game-sim) builds the start state of decision 38: Start orbit: polar (elements -> state through orbits.lisp, q and w the prograde hold''s target and frame rate, 28,000 kg, new-controls, settled). One function (sim-step! sim level &key until) does one step with every rule: fine 1/64 s at x1 and x4, coarse-step above with the kinematic attitude function, shortened to land on until (a scheduled time on the grid: a script action, a date, an apsis wait) and on the dv cut-off, then flight-step!, then the crash test of hazards.lisp; the live frame (clock-frame!), run-flight and the prediction all call it, so they agree bit for bit. A dv cut-off ends at the first 1/64 s grid point where the odometer reaches the target: at the fine step the step that crosses it, at a coarse step the engine-only closed form h = m0/mdot (1 - exp(-dv_left mdot/|F|)) rounded up to the grid; then the throttle is cut. A separate struct clock (warp level, accumulator, paused, rate meter, messages, the previous ship state for interpolation, a per-step hook for flight scripts) is not part of the sim state and not copied by checkpoints. The frame''s 4 ms cap reads the wall clock through a defparameter (*frame-step-time-limit*, 0.004) that tests bind to nil; the prediction''s per-frame budget is counted in steps (*prediction-steps-per-frame*, 4,096, about 1 ms), so nothing else in the simulation reads a wall clock.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:14:47Z', '2026-10-14T22:14:47Z', 74, NULL, 'rule', 74, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (73, 'Warp levels above x4: entry, holds and lowering', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 3 (task 74). decision 19: Time-warp rules and decision 31: Warp choices within the time-warp rules require a hold or low rates to enter kinematic attitude but don''t say what attitude a rate-damped ship holds there (plan 14 already expects the clock to set an inertial hold), what happens when the hold is released above x4, or how often drop-outs are checked; at x1,000,000 a frame is up to 16,667 s of game time, so a once-per-frame check could miss the 3 s rule by a whole frame. A snap keeps the documented kinematic model (no attitude dynamics above x4); the per-step check makes drop-outs exact on the step grid at every frame rate.', 'Entering a level above x4 with no hold (direct or rate damping, |w| < 0.01 rad/s) engages an inertial hold at the current attitude, as R would, shown in the readout and kept when warp comes down; entering under a hold that has not settled snaps the attitude to the hold''s target at the first coarse step (kinematic attitude, decision 69: Ship state, step rule and kinematic attitude in physics.lisp). Switching holds above x4 (1-6, R) is allowed and snaps likewise; releasing the hold (T, the active hold''s key again) lowers warp to x4 with the message ''WARP x4: HOLD RELEASED''. The clock checks before every step, not once per frame: throttle on above the thrust limit (x10 manual, x100 with a dv cut-off armed) lowers warp to the limit (''WARP x10: ENGINE ON''); above x4 without a hold lowers to x4; the next drop-out event at Delta = t_e - t lowers warp to the highest level L with Delta >= 3 s x L (nominal), so below 12 s it is x1 and x1 at the event; the frame''s remaining accumulator is scaled by new/old level. Messages are text plus a 3 s real-time stamp in the clock, worded as docs/design.md ''Time warp'': ''WARP REFUSED: ENGINE ON, LIMIT x10'' (x100), ''WARP REFUSED: ROTATING, SET A HOLD'', ''WARP REFUSED: IMPACT IN <duration>'', ''WARP x<L>: PERIAPSIS BELOW 100 KM'' (Earth; ''MOON IMPACT AHEAD'' for the Moon).', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:14:57Z', '2026-10-14T22:14:57Z', 74, NULL, 'rule', 74, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (74, 'Crash test on the step''s Hermite and the death state', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 3 (task 74). docs/architecture.md ''Collisions and the Sun'' prescribes a chord test minus h^2 |a|max / 8 without saying where |a|max comes from; the Hermite''s own second derivative bounds its distance from the chord exactly and costs a few multiplies, so no tunnelling holds for the curve actually tested. Stopping at the exact impact state gives the death view (camera fixed, ship at the impact) and the LOST panel''s facts from one record; the start-state checkpoint makes the off-grid t harmless.', 'hazards.lisp tests each step against Earth (R + 100 km) and the Moon (R) as decision 34: How death works sets: p(s), s in [0, 1], the cubic Hermite of the ship''s position relative to the body from the two relative states (the Moon''s from body-state at both ends); the sag bound is exact for the cubic, max(|p''''(0)|, |p''''(1)|) / 8 (p'''' is linear in s), subtracted from the chord''s closest approach; only below the crash radius is |p(s)|^2 minimised by Newton from the chord''s parameter (endpoints included), and when the minimum is inside, the first crossing |p(s)| = R_c found by bracketed Newton to 1e-9 in s. Death: the sim''s ship takes the Hermite state at the crossing (position, velocity; attitude by slerp, w and m_p of the step''s end), t becomes the impact time (off the grid; the sim then takes no steps), and the loss record holds cause :crash, body, t, speed relative to the body, latitude and longitude (bodies.lisp latitude-longitude). The renderer''s previous state stays the step''s start.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:15:04Z', '2026-10-14T22:15:04Z', 74, NULL, 'rule', 74, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (75, 'Prediction in milestone 2: the step function on a copy', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 3 (task 74). decision 20: Trajectory prediction on the frame thread fixes the method (the flight''s integrator, coarse steps, budgeted, states kept) but milestone 2 needs only events for drop-outs, alarms and script waits. Running the same step function on a copy makes the ''predicted state after one orbit equals the flown one bit for bit'' test hold at a coarse level and puts crash detection in the prediction for free; osculating Kepler events would be off by seconds under J2 and the Moon, too much for the 3 s rule at x4. Ending waits on the grid keeps scripts'' times exact (decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts).', 'prediction.lisp predicts by running sim-step! on its own copy of the sim at a coarse level (x1,000: kinematic attitude, the active hold or the inertial hold the step function engages), with the armed dv cut-off as the scheduled control; it keeps every step''s t, r, v (double, Earth-relative) in a preallocated buffer of 20,000 steps, resumable across frames within *prediction-steps-per-frame*. Horizon from the current t: until the copy''s crash, one osculating period (two when asked, for the map), or 20,000 steps. Events: :apoapsis and :periapsis from sign changes of r.v on the stored Hermite, root to 1e-6 s; :impact from the copy''s loss record (time, body, point); :cutoff when the copy cuts the throttle. Restart from the flown sim when the controls'' signature changes, every frame while the throttle is on, when the flown state is more than 1 m from the prediction''s Hermite at its t, when the flown t passes the prediction''s end, and on restore or death; while coasting otherwise only the tail is extended. A flight-script wait on an event ends at the first 1/64 s grid point at or after the predicted time (passed to sim-step! as until). Milestone 2''s drop-out event is :impact; milestone 3 adds burns and contacts, milestone 4 SOI changes, the Sun zone and life support.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:15:11Z', '2026-10-14T22:15:11Z', 74, NULL, 'rule', 74, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (76, 'Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 4 (task 75). docs/luv-capabilities.md ''Sun glare and brightness'' proposes HDR, a tone curve, bloom and an exposure rule but leaves them untried; docs/design.md ''The Sun'' and ''Views'' want glare from the bloom chain, stars fading near the Sun and a fixed map exposure. A mip-chain bloom without a threshold spreads the Sun''s 4.6e4 into a wide glare while leaving a lit Earth (1.0) nearly unbloomed, and avoids threshold popping; applying exposure only at the tone map keeps the HDR target within half-float range. The overlay after tone mapping keeps text crisp and unbloomed. One stated radiance scale lets the parts that write shaders (Earth, Sun, stars, meshes) agree without tuning against each other.', 'Each frame is three stages: (1) the scene pass into an HDR :rgba16-float target of the canvas size with the reversed-Z depth of decision 4: Depth strategy (stars, impostors, meshes, ribbons in the order of docs/architecture.md ''Rendering precision''); (2) post.lisp: a bloom mip chain (downsample to 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16 with a 13-tap filter, upsample with a 9-tap tent, added level by level; no threshold, mixed in at *bloom-strength* 0.04), then one tone-map pass into the canvas or capture target: exposure multiply, ACES filmic (Narkowicz fit, after Luvcraft''s aces-filmic), sRGB encoding in the shader unless the target format is -srgb; (3) the overlay pass (dot-matrix labels, the readout) straight into the canvas target after tone mapping, compare :always, no depth. Radiance scale: a white Lambert surface lit face-on by the Sun at 1 AU has radiance 1.0; the Sun''s disc 4.6e4 at its centre (1361 W/m2 / 6.8e-5 sr against 1361/pi), kept below rgba16-float''s 65,504 because exposure is applied only in the tone-map pass. Exposure rule (post.lisp, CPU): target *exposure-sunlit* 1.0 when the Sun''s centre is within the view''s half-diagonal plus 5 deg, or when a body (Earth, Moon) covers at least 5 % of the frame (pi rho^2 over the frame''s solid angle, capped at 1) times its lit fraction (1 + cos phase)/2 at least 0.05; otherwise *exposure-deep* 16.0; the map view uses *exposure-map* (fixed, set by the map''s part). The exposure eases toward its target in log2 with a 1.0 s time constant of real elapsed time (also while paused); it snaps to the target on the game''s first frame and in captures. All values are defparameters that the Earth/Sun/stars part may retune.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:22:12Z', '2026-10-14T22:22:12Z', 75, NULL, 'rule', 75, '2026-10-15, task 111: task 111: post.lisp builds the three stages. luv''s Vulkan passes only clear (load-op :load rejected), so the bloom''s way up writes its own levels U3..U1 (each the downsampled level plus the tent of the one below) and the tone map mixes U1 into the scene at *bloom-strength*; the overlay shares the tone map''s pass. The exposure rule as built is decision 93: Exposure rule as built: hidden Sun, coverage within the view.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (77, 'The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 4 (task 75). docs/architecture.md ''The clock'' says rendering lags one step and interpolates, but not how the fraction is taken from the accumulator nor what happens after a restore or a death; ''GPU tests'' wants scenes set from a state, which needs a capture of a given sim that does not advance. Removing scene.lisp before Earth, stars, ship and readout exist needs a picture in between; placeholders that cite the task which will plan their replacement keep the PLACEHOLDER rule traceable (the parts'' plans don''t exist yet). decision 13 asks the first task adding an asset to do the packaging; doing it here with the Earth texture lets the loader be tested on the real file and the packaging be seen working in nix run.', 'main.lisp''s game holds a clock and a sim from plan 15''s (new-game-sim), the start-state checkpoint, a camera and a renderer; each canvas frame computes the elapsed real time from luv''s frame time (0 on the first frame), calls clock-frame!, then fills a preallocated render-state and encodes the frame from it. render-state (render.lisp, pure, no GPU): render time t_r = t_prev + s (t - t_prev) with s = clamp(acc / (t - t_prev), 0, 1); ship position by cubic Hermite in double from the previous and current r, v; attitude by quat-slerp; m_p, controls and loss from the sim; when the clock has no previous state, its previous t is not below the sim''s t, or the sim is lost, the sim''s state as it is. Bodies (Sun, Moon, Earth''s body-fixed matrix) are taken at t_r. The camera''s position is stored relative to the ship (an offset in double) and the eye is ship + offset. capture.lisp''s (capture-state pathname sim &key camera frames width height) opens a hidden canvas on a copy of the sim with the clock paused and the exposure snapped, so the PNG shows exactly that state; capture-scene (scripts/screenshot) is capture-state of (new-game-sim). Until the parts that replace them land, part 4 draws placeholders named after the plan-writing task of their part: the Earth as a true-scale UV-sphere mesh sampling the Blue Marble through body-fixed-matrix, and screen-space stars (PLACEHOLDER(76)); no ship mesh (PLACEHOLDER(77)); a dot-matrix label in overlay.lisp (PLACEHOLDER(80)). Part 4 commits the first asset, assets/earth/blue-marble-5400.jpg (Blue Marble NG January, world.200401.3x5400x2700.jpg, decision 40: Earth''s look and drag) with assets/earth/SOURCE.md and CREDITS.md, so that the packaging of decision 13: Asset storage and attribution is checked with a real asset in nix build and nix run; part 5 uses it for the impostor.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:22:22Z', '2026-10-14T22:22:22Z', 75, NULL, 'rule', 75, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (78, 'Sky in numbers: stars, Sun, Moon and Earth''s look', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md ''Earth'' and ''The Sun'' and decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure fix the look but leave the numbers open: physical star brightness (Sirius at 6e-11 of sunlight) cannot be seen at any exposure that also shows a lit Earth, so the slope is halved as sky renderers do; Vulkan textures in luv carry one mip level (hal/vulkan/gpu.lisp writes mip 0 only), hence the 1/8 copy and sample-level 0, which also avoids the seam the polynomial atan2''s derivative jump would give. Recorded by task 76: write plan: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars.', 'Stars (stars.lisp): every HYG star of mag <= 6.5 but the Sun as one instanced additive quad at infinity (clip z 0, compare :always, no depth write), a Gaussian of sigma *star-sigma* 0.7 px (+0.15 px per magnitude brighter than 3.0, at most 1.6 px) in a quad of 4 sigma; peak radiance L(m) = *star-radiance-limit* 1.25e-3 x 10^(0.2 (6.5 - m)): half Pogson''s slope, so mag 6.5 shows at the deep exposure (16) and the stars fade at the sunlit one (1.0) except the brightest few, dim; colour from B-V by Ballesteros (2012) to a temperature, a blackbody through the CIE 1931 2-degree functions (Wyman-Sloan-Shirley 2013 fit) to linear sRGB (D65), normalised to luminance 1, mixed toward white by *star-saturation* 0.6; no colour index: white; positions J2000 RA/Dec as ICRF directions, no proper motion. Sun (impostors.lisp): ray-traced sphere at its true distance, centre radiance 4.6e4 (decision 76) with linear limb darkening I/I0 = 1 - 0.6 (1 - mu), colour (1.0, 0.97, 0.94) scaled to luminance 1; glare only from post.lisp''s bloom. Moon: ray-traced grey Lambert sphere, albedo 0.12, lit by the Sun, no earthshine, no texture until milestone 4. Earth: ray-traced sphere R_earth (equatorial) sampling the Blue Marble with sample-level 0 (Vulkan textures in luv have one mip level), mixed with a CPU box-filtered 1/8 copy (675 x 338) by the texel''s footprint in pixels so the far Earth does not shimmer; radiance = texture (linear) x max(n.s, 0) x (1 AU / d_sun)^2; an additive rim on the lit limb (*rim-strength*); a halo impostor of radius R_earth + 100 km, additive, depth-tested without depth write, its radiance the ray''s chord through the shell over the tangent chord (2 sqrt((R+100 km)^2 - R^2)) to the power *halo-power* 1.5, times *halo-radiance* 0.35, blue (0.30, 0.55, 1.0) on the day side, mixed toward (1.0, 0.45, 0.20) where the sun''s elevation at the ray''s closest point is within 6 deg of the horizon, and zero 6 deg below it. Bodies whose angular radius exceeds 45 deg, or that contain the eye, draw as a full-screen triangle (the halo then takes the far root); otherwise a camera-facing quad of the tangent cone. Draw order in the scene pass: stars, Sun, Moon, Earth, Earth''s halo. All values defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:27:55Z', '2026-10-14T22:27:55Z', 76, NULL, 'rule', 76, '2026-10-14, task 76: 2026-10-14, task 76: plan 17: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars builds it (stars.lisp, impostors.lisp); docs/design.md ''Earth'' and ''The Sun'' and docs/architecture.md take its numbers as the pieces land.

2026-10-15, task 113: task 113: src/stars.lisp builds the stars as decided: 8,920 HYG stars in one static instance buffer (direction, magnitudes brighter than 3.0 in w, colour x 10^(0.2 (6.5 - m))), *star-radiance-limit* and the sigma values as uniforms (live), *star-saturation* applied when the renderer starts; the tests'' quads moved to their own :quads pipeline. The fading at the sunlit exposure is decision 95: Star fading at the sunlit exposure as built.

2026-10-15, task 115: task 115: Earth and its halo built as decided (src/impostors.lisp, src/shaders.lisp), the formulas the decision leaves open in decision 97: Earth and halo impostors as built: rim, footprint mix, halo depth and colour ramps; the 1/8 copy by downsample-pixels in src/assets.lisp; the UV-sphere placeholder Earth and PLACEHOLDER(76) gone.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (79, 'Sunlight at a point and the sunlit ship in the exposure rule', 'answered', '[]', 'With decision 76 alone, a sunlit ship at 40 m seen against black space (Earth and Sun out of view) gets the deep exposure 16 and burns out white; the ship is in view in every outside frame, so it decides the exposure there. Earth''s shadow on the ship is needed anyway (the ship''s meshes are dark at night) and later for the Sun''s heat. Recorded by task 76: write plan: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars; partly refines decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure (the exposure target only).', 'bodies.lisp gains (sun-visible-fraction p t &optional (origin :earth)): the fraction of the Sun''s disc seen from p, occulted by Earth (radius R_earth) and the Moon (R_moon) as discs, by the exact area of two overlapping circles in angle (no limb darkening, no refraction), the larger occultation of the two kept; pure, double, no HAL, so milestone 4''s hull heat (zero in Earth''s shadow) uses it too. render-state gains sun-fraction, the ship''s, at t_r. Refining decision 76''s exposure rule: the ship counts as a body in the outside view, its coverage pi rho^2 over the frame''s solid angle with rho = asin(*ship-bounding-radius* / d), d the camera''s distance, and its lit fraction the ship''s sun-fraction; the target is *exposure-sunlit* when that product is at least 0.005 (about 240 m for the 10 m bounding radius), as well as by the Sun and body tests of decision 76. *ship-bounding-radius* 10.0 m until the meshes part computes the stack''s.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:28:03Z', '2026-10-14T22:28:03Z', 76, NULL, 'rule', 76, '2026-10-14, task 76: 2026-10-14, task 76: plan 17: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars, piece 3, adds sun-visible-fraction to bodies.lisp, sun-fraction to render-state and the ship term to post.lisp''s exposure-target.

2026-10-15, task 114: task 114: src/bodies.lisp sun-visible-fraction (inline, conses nothing), render-state''s sun-fraction, post.lisp''s ship term (*ship-bounding-radius* 10 m, *exposure-ship-coverage* 0.005) as decision 96: Impostors part one as built: the ship term''s eye-inside case and the GPU checks'' exposures records; on the live ship the fraction reaches 0 between 1,172 s and 1,551 s after the start (shadow entry 21.9 min).

2026-10-15, task 117: 2026-10-14, task 117: the ship term''s radius is the stack''s computed ship-bounding-radius at the render state''s m_p (12.57 m wet), *ship-bounding-radius* removed; the sunlit exposure now reaches out to about 300 m.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (80, 'The Mule''s meshes in numbers: geometry, draws, materials and lights', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 6 (task 77). docs/design.md ''How they look'' lists the parts and materials but leaves sizes of joints, the decal, the lights'' brightness and timing, the material values and the light model open; the cockpit of milestone 3 must match the outside window and portholes. Instancing only the repeated primitives keeps a handful of draws while the unique parts stay one static buffer; the engine as its own draw lets the bell follow ship-trim-angle as plan 14 intends. The body frame''s coordinates are under 20 m, so single floats hold the mesh to 1e-6 m, and only R''s position relative to the eye goes through double (decision 5: Precision of positions). Earthshine matters in LEO (Earth covers half the sky); the term is the irradiance of a uniformly lit disc, scaled by Earth''s lit phase.', 'meshes.lisp generates the Mule in ship body axes (FRD, metres from R, single floats; the body frame spans < 20 m) from docs/design.md ''How they look'' and ''Parts''. Vertex: position, normal, material id, two parameters (axial coordinate along a tube, cone or bell; or decal u, v), 9 singles. A model is one static baked mesh (unique parts: tanks with hemispherical domes, cab, grapple, wings, radiators, boxes) plus instance groups of unit primitives (8-sided tube, box, small cone) with per-instance origin, unit basis and scale (16 singles; normal = basis (n / scale), normalised): 88 truss members (4 longerons, 11 frames of 4, 40 face diagonals), 4 cab struts at x 11.0 and 14.5 from (x, +-1.8, -1.8) to (x, +-0.9, -2.3), 4 engine thrust-frame struts in the plane x = 0 from the aft corners to 0.3 m from the gimbal, 4 grapple mount struts from the front corners to the grapple cylinder''s aft rim, 2 wing booms, 8 RCS blocks (0.3 along x), 24 RCS nozzles (cones 0.12 long, exit 0.08, pointing along each thruster''s exhaust, against its force in ship.lisp), 3 claws. The engine (bell frustum exit 1.45 at x -2.2, throat 0.3 at x -0.6; chamber cylinder 0.5 x 0.6 from x -0.6 to 0; two turbopump boxes 0.3 x 0.25 x 0.25 at y +-0.35) is its own baked mesh drawn rotated by ship-trim-angle about y through the gimbal (0, 0, -0.5), so its axis passes through the current CoM. Cab: cylinder r 1.5 from x 10.5 to 15.0 about (y 0, z -3.5), flat ends; window 1.2 x 0.6 centred (15.0, 0, -3.6) (dark glass: albedo 0.02, specular, faint emission 0.005 for the screens behind it); portholes r 0.175 centred x 14.1 on the wall 37 deg below the y axis, (14.1, +-1.198, -2.597) (where a ray 90 deg right/left and 40 deg down from the cockpit eye (14.1, 0, -3.6) of decision 30: Views and cameras meets the wall; milestone 3''s cockpit uses the same); hatch 0.8 x 1.2 at +y centred x 11.4, outlined in red hand paint (a 0.05 m border); the stencil decal on both cab sides, 2.8 x 0.7 m from x 12.1 to 14.9, centred 25 deg above the y axis, reading left to right from outside: Cairn Water''s cairn (three stacked grey ellipses 0.6, 0.45, 0.3 m wide) then ''M-07'' in black stencil letters 0.6 m tall on a 5 x 7 grid with stencil bridges, drawn on the CPU into a 512 x 128 texture (meshes.lisp, uploaded once). Grapple: cylinder 0.6 x 0.8 from x 15.0, cone from 0.6 to 1.6 over x 15.8 to 16.6, claws 0.1 x 0.1 x 0.6 at 120 deg on radius 0.85 from x 16.0. Wings: 2.0 (x) x 6.0 (y) x 0.05 at (5.0, +-5.3, 0), cells on the -z face; radiators 4.0 (x) x 1.5 (z) x 0.05 vertical fins at (8.0, +-1.2, 2.55). Materials (linear albedo; Blinn-Phong specular weight, exponent; a defparameter table uploaded each frame so ./sly retunes it): steel 0.45 0.45 0.47, 0.15, 16, darker x0.6 within 0.06 m of each tube end (weld nodes); LH2 foil gold 0.80 0.55 0.20 and LOX foil silver 0.75 0.75 0.78, 0.6, 64, normal perturbed by value noise of the body position (amplitude 0.25, cell 0.08 m); cab white paint 0.80 0.80 0.78, 0.05, 8; stencil black 0.04; cairn grey 0.35; red paint 0.50 0.06 0.04; solar cells 0.03 0.04 0.09, 0.4, 128; radiators 0.85, 0.05, 8; boxes (equipment bay, gas conditioning) aluminium 0.60 0.60 0.62; nozzle 0.08 with a throat band 0.35 0.33 0.30 over the bell''s first 0.15 m. Light on the decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure scale: sun term = max(n.s, 0) x (1 AU / d_sun)^2 x sun-fraction (decision 79: Sunlight at a point and the sunlit ship in the exposure rule); earthshine = *earth-albedo* 0.3 x (R_earth / |r|)^2 x (1 + rhat.shat)/2 x (1 AU / d_sun)^2 x max(n.(-rhat), 0), tinted 0.85 0.92 1.0; *ship-ambient* 0.0005; no self-shadowing (cut: a shadow map for one ship costs more than it shows). Draws: after the impostors in the scene pass, opaque, depth written, compare :greater; per draw a single rotation mat3-from-quat of the interpolated q and the translation eye-relative-single of R''s position r - q c (c the CoM from ship-mass-properties at the render state''s m_p), plus the gimbal rotation for the engine. Lights, additive camera-facing Gaussian billboards depth-tested without write, at least 1.5 px across, radiance scaled down by the area when enlarged: navigation red (-y) and green (+y) at the truss''s top front corners (15.0, +-1.8, -1.8), radius 0.05 m, radiance 50; white strobe on the cab top (12.75, 0, -5.05), radius 0.08 m, radiance 400 for *strobe-on* 0.1 s every *strobe-period* 1.2 s of render time, the phase reduced in double on the CPU; engine glow at the nozzle exit''s centre, radius 0.8 m, colour 0.45 0.6 1.0 (luminance 1) x *engine-glow-radiance* 1.5 x throttle, only while the engine fires (throttle > 0, propellant > 0, not lost). The grapple''s floodlight and range-finder light come with the grapple (milestone 3). A per-frame emissive colour added to every hull fragment (zero here) and a lights-on flag are the slots plan 2''s part 9 (the death glow) and milestone 4 (lights out) drive. The stack''s bounding radius = the largest distance from the current CoM to any mesh vertex, replacing *ship-bounding-radius* 10.0 of decision 79.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:33:36Z', '2026-10-14T22:33:36Z', 77, NULL, 'rule', 77, '2026-10-14, task 77: 2026-10-14, task 77: plan for part 6 of plan 2 (The Mule''s meshes) builds meshes.lisp, the ship''s pipelines, lights and glow with these numbers; post.lisp''s *ship-bounding-radius* 10.0 is replaced by the computed radius.

2026-10-15, task 117: 2026-10-14, task 117: meshes.lisp draws the Mule (hull, 137 instances in three instanced draws, the engine as instance 1 turned by its trim); the material table is a storage buffer rewritten each frame; post.lisp''s *ship-bounding-radius* is gone, the exposure''s ship term uses ship-bounding-radius at m_p; the earthshine''s whole-disc phase is replaced by the lit part of the visible cap (decision 99: Earthshine from the lit part of the visible cap).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (81, 'Input in milestone 2: held keys per frame, taps, gating and the shared key map', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 7 (task 78). docs/design.md ''Controls'' and docs/architecture.md ''The clock'' fix the key map, held keys, focus loss, real-time ramps and the per-frame sample (decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock, decision 29: Control scheme: key map, control modes, gamepad) but not how a tap shorter than a frame survives the per-frame sample, what keys do while paused, lost or above x4 (where a pitch key would release the hold the warp needs, decision 73: Warp levels above x4: entry, holds and lowering), nor where the key map lives when flights.lisp, a simulation file loaded before input.lisp, must press the same keys (decision 56: Flight-script actions, start states and the quest play-through). Keeping events out of controls until the frame makes the zero-order hold exact and the whole input path testable with synthetic events and no canvas.', 'input.lisp keeps its own input state in the game (held physical keys, a tap latch, the right-drag flag); canvas events only change that state or run a discrete command, and once per frame, before clock-frame!, input-frame! writes the held rotation and translation keys into the sim''s controls and runs the real-time throttle ramp. A key pressed and released between two frames counts as held for exactly one frame (the design''s shortest tap). Repeat events (canvas-key-event-repeat-p) are ignored; focus loss clears held keys and latches, ends a drag and leaves relative pointer mode; the throttle lever keeps its value. The held-key map (W/S pitch -/+, A/D yaw -/+, Q/E roll -/+, H/N +x/-x, I/K -z/+z, J/L -y/+y; opposite keys cancel) is data in controls.lisp with (controls-apply-held-keys! controls keys), so flights.lisp''s (:keys ...) action and input share it without loading input.lisp; Shift-left/Control-left ramp the lever at 0.5 per second of real elapsed time (min(elapsed, 0.1 s)), a press from off giving 0.1, below 0.1 off. Discrete keys (F, Z, X, T, R, 1-6, . , /, P, C, Home, M, Tab, Page keys, Escape, Control+Q) act at the press. Gating: paused: only P, C, Home, M, Tab, Page keys, the mouse and quit act, flight keys are ignored (the ramp stops); above x4 (clock-kinematic-p): rotation and translation keys are ignored with the clock message ''RCS OFF ABOVE x4'' once per press, holds, T, R, F and the throttle act; lost (sim-loss): flight keys ignored, part 9 binds Return and N. The window title lists the main keys by QWERTY position (layout characters come with milestone 6''s F1 help); luv exports no key-name-to-layout-character function, a wishlist item.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:37:37Z', '2026-10-14T22:37:37Z', 78, NULL, 'rule', 78, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (82, 'Outside view camera: angles in a frame, toggles that keep the picture, distance and easing', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 7 (task 78). decision 30: Views and cameras and docs/design.md ''Views'' set the outside view''s range, frames and default but not how the camera is parametrised, what C does to the picture, the drag direction and rate, or whether the wheel eases; angles in the chosen frame make Home, C and the frame-following behaviour one formula, and pure functions of the render state keep the camera testable without a GPU (docs/architecture.md ''Dev loop'').', 'camera.lisp''s outside-view (game state, not in the sim or its checkpoints; kept across a restart) holds the frame mode (:orbital or :locked), azimuth, elevation, distance and target distance. The eye sits at the CoM (ship-state r) plus distance x (cos el cos az, cos el sin az, -sin el) in the frame''s FRD basis: :orbital x prograde, z radial in (Earth below), from the interpolated r, v; :locked the ship''s body axes from the interpolated q. The camera looks at the CoM with the frame''s +z as down; elevation clamped to +-85 deg. Default (and Home): az 180 deg, el 20 deg, 40 m (decision 30: Views and cameras, part 4''s outside-camera-default!). C converts the current eye direction into the other frame''s angles, so the picture does not move at the toggle. Right-drag (relative pointer mode while held): az += 0.25 deg per pixel of delta-x, el -= 0.25 deg per pixel of delta-y (mouse right moves the camera right around the ship, mouse up raises it). Wheel: target distance x 1.2 per notch (scroll up nearer), clamped to [1.2 x the stack''s bounding radius, 5 km]; the distance eases to the target exponentially in log with tau 0.15 s of real time as the map''s zoom does, snapping in captures. The bounding radius is the largest distance from the current CoM to a corner of a part''s box in ship.lisp''s parts table (about 12.6 m wet, so the minimum is about 15 m; decision 30''s ''12 m'' was an estimate of the same rule). All numbers defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:37:37Z', '2026-10-14T22:37:37Z', 78, NULL, 'rule', 78, '2026-10-14, task 78: Sign correction (task 78, same day): the drag rule meant is the parenthetical one, mouse right moves the eye to the viewer''s right around the ship; with the eye direction (cos el cos az, cos el sin az, -sin el) in FRD that is az -= 0.25 deg per pixel of delta-x (from az 180 deg the eye''s y component becomes positive). el -= 0.25 deg per pixel of delta-y stands (luv''s delta-y is positive downwards, so mouse up raises the eye). plan 19: Input and the outside view uses this sign.

2026-10-15, task 121: 2026-10-14, task 121: built in src/camera.lisp. outside-min-distance measured 14.64 m wet, 13.58 m half, 13.62 m dry (box corners from the CoM, x1.2; the decision''s ''about 15 m'' estimate), in docs/design.md ''Views''. C keeps the eye offset exactly; the roll follows the new frame''s +z as down, which equals the old one when body z is radial in (the orbital holds). Home joined the window title (''C/Home camera'').');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (83, 'Map camera: framing on opening, focus, zoom, drag and exposure', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 8 (task 79). docs/design.md ''Views'' and decision 30: Views and cameras give the map''s range, steps and opening distance but not its basis, azimuth, field of view, what Tab keeps, what Home and C do, nor the value of decision 76''s *exposure-map*; and its two sentences on the opening focus contradict each other. A basis fixed at opening keeps the orbit still on screen while the ship goes round it; az -45 deg was checked by hand: at az -90 deg a 400 km ship seen from 2.5 radii sits 21.7 deg off Earth''s centre, behind the 22.0 deg limb. Recorded by task 79: write plan: The basic map.', 'camera.lisp''s map-view (game state, not in the sim or its checkpoints; kept across a restart) holds the focus (:earth or :ship), a basis fixed when the map opens or Home is pressed (n = unit r x v of the render state, e1 = unit r, e2 = n x e1; n = ICRF z when |r x v| < 1e-9 |r||v|), azimuth, elevation, distance and target distance. Eye = focus position + distance x (cos el cos az e1 + cos el sin az e2 + sin el n), looking at the focus with n''s projection as up; el clamped to +-85 deg; vertical FOV 50 deg, near 0.1 m; the camera is stored relative to its anchor (decision 5). M opens the map framed on the orbit: focus :earth, az -45 deg (behind the ship''s motion and on its side, so a LEO ship lies in front of Earth''s disc, not behind its limb), el 30 deg above the orbit plane on the normal side, distance 2.5 x max(apoapsis radius, |r|) (|r| for a hyperbola), clamped; M again returns to the outside view as it was. docs/design.md ''Views'' says both ''the ship (default when the map opens)'' and ''opening the map frames the ship''s orbit: focus the dominant body''; the framing rule wins, Tab reaches the ship. Tab cycles :earth -> :ship -> :earth keeping basis, az, el and distance (clamped to the new focus''s range: ship 50 m, Earth 1.2 R_earth, both up to 1.5e13 m); the anchor jumps. Home re-frames as on opening; C does nothing in the map. Wheel x1.2 per notch (up nearer), Page-up /10, Page-down x10 on the target distance; the distance eases in log with tau 0.15 s of real time (decision 82''s rule), snapping in captures. Right-drag: az -= 0.25 deg per pixel of dx, el -= 0.25 deg per pixel of dy (decision 82''s signs). Exposure: *exposure-map* 4.0 (the geometric middle of decision 76''s 1.0 and 16.0: Earth''s ocean and land below white, stars to about magnitude 4 visible), snapped to on entering the map and to the outside target on leaving (no 1 s ease across views). Flight keys act in the map with decision 81''s gating unchanged. All numbers defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:43:34Z', '2026-10-14T22:43:34Z', 79, NULL, 'rule', 79, '2026-10-14, task 79: plan 20: The basic map builds it (camera.lisp map-view, input.lisp''s map commands replacing PLACEHOLDER(79), post.lisp''s *exposure-map*); docs/design.md ''Views'' takes the opening focus and numbers as the pieces land.

2026-10-15, task 122: 2026-10-14, task 122: built in src/camera.lisp (map-view in the game''s map-view slot, map-view-open!, -cycle-focus!, -zoom!, -drag!, map-camera-update!). camera-anchor-position! resolves the camera''s anchor (:ship r, :earth the origin); render.lisp''s frame uniforms and post.lisp''s exposure-target use it, so a map camera anchored on Earth draws right once task 123 wires the view in.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (84, 'Map ribbons and markers: osculating orbit, adaptive segments, overlay markers and visibility', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 8 (task 79). docs/design.md ''Views'' fixes the line (2 px, cyan, impacts red), the markers'' existence and the 6 px rule; docs/luv-capabilities.md ''Orbit lines and markers'' leaves adaptive segments and marker shapes ''assumed''. Uniform segments fail near the ship: 512 chords over a LEO orbit have a sagitta of ~130 m, a visible offset at the 50 m ship-focus distance, so segments are split by their error in pixels. Markers in the overlay keep their colours and edges exact (no bloom, no tone map) and need no new shader; a CPU occlusion test gives one visibility rule for markers and labels, testable without a GPU. The impact marker comes from the osculating orbit because the line it marks is osculating in milestone 2 (plan 15''s note named the prediction; the alarm keeps it). Recorded by task 79: write plan: The basic map.', 'ribbons.lisp draws, in the map view only, the ship''s osculating orbit about Earth from the render state (state->orbit with mu_earth), recomputed each frame into preallocated double buffers relative to Earth''s centre (no single-float cache, docs/architecture.md ''Floating-origin pitfalls''): an ellipse as 256 segments uniform in eccentric anomaly, each split into 2^k (k <= 8) so that its chord''s sagitta seen from the eye is at most *ribbon-max-error-px* 0.25 px, at most *ribbon-max-segments* 8192 in total (k lowered from the farthest segments first when over); a hyperbola as the branch out to Earth''s Laplace sphere radius on both sides, split by the same rule. Each segment is one instance with both endpoints eye-relative (double difference, then single), expanded to 2 px in the vertex shader, endpoints behind the eye clipped at w = near; in the scene pass after the meshes, compare :greater, no depth write, additive; radiance = linear colour x *ribbon-radiance* 1.0 / the current exposure, so the line reads the same at any exposure. Colour cyan #40D0FF; a segment whose midpoint radius is below the crash radius (R_earth + 100 km) red #FF3030. Markers and their labels are screen-space triangles in the overlay pass after the tone map (decision 76''s stage 3, decision 7''s pipeline), in exact colours: the ship a cyan ring of 12 px, always drawn, even behind Earth (findable at every zoom); apoapsis and periapsis cyan diamonds of 9 px labelled ''AP <n> KM'' / ''PE <n> KM'' (altitude above R_earth rounded to whole km; no AP on a hyperbola); the impact a red X of 12 px labelled ''IMPACT'' at the osculating orbit''s next inbound crossing of the crash radius after t_r (orbit-radius-crossings), so it sits on the drawn line (the prediction''s impact stays the readout''s alarm, part 9); Earth, the Moon and the Sun, when their projected diameter H tan(asin(R/d)) / tan(fov/2) is below 6 px, a grey #8A8A80 dot of 7 px labelled ''EARTH'', ''MOON'', ''SUN'', drawn in addition to their impostor. A marker other than the ship''s, and its label, is hidden when its point is behind the eye or the segment from the eye to it meets Earth''s or the Moon''s sphere (double, CPU). Labels in paper white #E8E4D8 dot-matrix glyphs, 8 px right of the marker''s centre, vertically centred. All numbers defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:43:48Z', '2026-10-14T22:43:48Z', 79, NULL, 'rule', 79, '2026-10-14, task 79: plan 20: The basic map builds it (ribbons.lisp, overlay.lisp markers and the completed glyph table); the map''s impact marker comes from the osculating orbit, not from prediction-event as plan 15''s closing note said.

2026-10-15, task 127: 2026-10-15, task 127: map-markers (src/ribbons.lisp) computes the apsides and the impact from r, v directly (eccentricity vector, no state->orbit), consing nothing; the ship''s ring is drawn last so the 100 AU dots don''t cover it; label overlap resolved by decision 103: Map labels: placement order and overlap.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (85, 'Minimal readout: layout, formats, colours and alarms', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 9 (task 80). docs/design.md ''Instruments'' lists the milestone-2 rows and ''Readability at 1280 x 800'' the sizes, colours and the never-colour-alone rule, but not the layout, the units and decimals of each value, where the alarms come from, nor how the readout keeps the other parts'' GPU region checks valid. Osculating apsides match plan 20''s map markers; the impact time comes from the prediction as decision 84: Map ribbons and markers: osculating orbit, adaptive segments, overlay markers and visibility left to this part. An opaque box keeps the 7:1 contrast true over a sunlit Earth without changing the overlay pipeline. Recorded by task 80: write plan: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel.', 'overlay.lisp draws the milestone-2 readout top left in the outside and map views as 5x7 dot-matrix text (decision 7: Placeholder labels: dot-matrix overlay quads) with a 2 px dot pitch (cap 14 px), cells 12 x 22 px (docs/design.md ''Readability at 1280 x 800'': 0.86 x cap, 1.6 x cap), 16 px margin, on an opaque near-black #0A0C0C box sized to its rows (contrast exact, no blending in the overlay pipeline). The glyph table adds % / ! + ( ) , ° × … to plan 20''s A-Z 0-9 space - . :, and every string is upcased; a character missing from the table is a test failure, not a blank. Rows, labels grey #8A8A80, values paper white #E8E4D8: ''ALT'' altitude |r| - R_earth in km, one decimal; ''SPD'' |v| in Earth''s inertial frame, m/s with one decimal below 1,000 m/s, else km/s with three; ''AP'' and ''PE'' osculating apsis altitudes (state->orbit, mu_earth) in km with one decimal, AP ''ESCAPE'' when e >= 1; ''THR'' lever in whole % and ON (throttle > 0 and propellant > 0) or OFF; ''PROP'' whole kg and whole % of 28,000 kg; ''MODE'' DIRECT, RATE DAMP or HOLD <PROGRADE|RETROGRADE|NORMAL|ANTI-NORMAL|RADIAL OUT|RADIAL IN|INERTIAL> with SETTLED or SLEWING, then FINE when on; ''WARP'' ×<nominal level>, then ''ACT ×<rate>'' in amber with ''!'' when the clock''s actual rate is below 90 % of nominal (3 significant digits), then PAUSED; ''UTC'' format-utc of t_r truncated to seconds. Values from the render state (interpolated, t_r), so they move smoothly. Alarms below the rows: amber ''! PE BELOW 100 KM'' while the osculating periapsis radius is below R_earth + 100 km; red inverted (red block, #0A0C0C text) flashing at 2 Hz in real time ''<EARTH|MOON> IMPACT IN <duration>'' while the clock''s prediction has an :impact event (time to it from t_r, the same duration text as the clock''s warp messages). Then the clock''s live messages (clock-messages, 3 s each), newest last, at most 3, amber with ''!'', cut at 40 characters with ''…''. The readout and alarms are a pure function of the render state, the clock and the real time (a list of lines with colour and style), tested without a GPU; captures take :readout (default t) so GPU tests that check scene regions can turn it off. All sizes and colours defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:47:39Z', '2026-10-14T22:47:39Z', 80, NULL, 'rule', 80, '2026-10-14, task 80: 2026-10-14, task 80: plan 21: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel builds it (overlay.lisp readout-lines and readout-vertices!, the extended glyph table, capture-state''s :readout).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (86, 'Death view, LOST panel and restart in milestone 2', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 2''s part 9 (task 80). docs/design.md ''Dying and what follows'' and decision 34: How death works fix what is shown (outside view, camera fixed, 3 s orange glow for an Earth impact, the LOST panel with body, speed, latitude, longitude, Return and N) but not the glow''s curve and brightness, where the panel sits, how the restart resets the views and input, nor whether the camera may move. A fixed camera follows from the stopped ship, so letting the mouse orbit it costs nothing and keeps decision 81''s rule that views act while lost. Clearing input on restart keeps the N press from leaking into a translation. Recorded by task 80: write plan: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel.', 'main.lisp notices the death on the first frame whose sim has a loss (the game keeps the loss it last saw): it switches game-view to :outside and keeps the outside view''s offset, so the camera stays where it was relative to the ship, which no longer moves (the render state takes the sim''s impact state, decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset); the mouse may still orbit it and M still opens the map (decision 81: Input in milestone 2: held keys per frame, taps, gating and the shared key map). It records the real time of the death (the game''s summed elapsed). The ship''s emissive slot of plan 18 (decision 80: The Mule''s meshes in numbers: geometry, draws, materials and lights) carries the entry glow for a :crash into Earth: orange #FF6A1A at *death-glow-radiance* 4.0 (on the radiance scale of decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure, divided by the current exposure so it reads the same lit or in shadow), full for the first 1 s of real time after the death, then falling linearly to 0 at 3 s; a Moon impact has no glow (no atmosphere). The LOST panel appears at once, centred horizontally with its top at 60 % of the height (the ship, at the view''s centre, stays visible): an opaque #0A0C0C box in the readout''s text: ''LOST: CRASH'' as a red inverted block (steady), then ''<EARTH|MOON>  <UTC of the impact>'', ''SPEED <speed relative to the body, as the readout''s SPD>'', ''LAT <|lat|, 2 decimals>° <N|S>  LON <|lon|, 2 decimals>° <E|W>'', a blank line, ''RETURN  RESTART FROM START'', ''N       NEW GAME'' (keys paper white, texts grey). The readout stays, showing the impact state. Return and N are in *key-map* and act only while lost (flight keys are ignored then; N is translate-back otherwise); in milestone 2 both call (game-restart! game): clock-restart! with the start-state checkpoint (equalp to (new-game-sim)), the view :outside, the outside view reset as Home does, the map view closed, the input''s held keys, taps and drag cleared (so the restarting N does not translate), the death record cleared, the exposure snapped. A capture of a lost sim shows the glow at its peak and the panel (capture-state''s :death-elapsed, default 0). All numbers defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:47:52Z', '2026-10-14T22:47:52Z', 80, NULL, 'rule', 80, '2026-10-14, task 80: 2026-10-14, task 80: plan 21: Minimal readout, messages and the LOST panel builds it (main.lisp''s death record and game-restart!, death-glow-radiance into plan 18''s emissive slot, lost-panel-lines, Return and N in *key-map* while lost).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (87, 'Flight scripts in milestone 2: additions, apsis conditions, live fly and scripts/fly', 'answered', '[]', 'task 81 (plan for part 10 of plan 2: masterplan). docs/architecture.md ''Scripted runs'' leaves open how milestone 2''s scripts restart after a crash, check the predicted impact, centre a burn on an apsis and show the map, and its ''within 1%'' for raise-orbit cannot hold on osculating apsides under J2 in a polar orbit. Driving headless and live runs through one per-step function keeps them bit-identical; ignoring the player''s flight keys during a live flight keeps input-frame! from overwriting the script''s keys.', 'flights.lisp reads a script with *read-eval* nil, double floats, a scratch package, one form, and rejects any symbol that is not a keyword; actions, waits and expects of a later milestone are rejected at read time with ''NOT BEFORE MILESTONE <n>''. Additions to decision 56''s vocabulary: (:restart) presses Return while lost (clock-restart! with the run''s start state headless, game-restart! live); (:expect :start-state) holds when the sim equals the run''s start state (equalp); (:expect :impact-as-predicted <s>) compares the loss''s t with the :impact time the prediction gave when the last (:wait :impact) began; event waits take :before <s> ((:wait :apoapsis :before 18.5) ends at the grid point at or after the predicted time less s, for burns centred on an apsis); (:screenshot "<label>" [:view :map]) captures the map instead of the outside view. Apsis conditions (:apoapsis-above/below, :periapsis-above/below) use the prediction''s apsides, the radius at the next predicted passage less R_earth, not the osculating elements the readout shows, since under J2 a polar 400 km orbit''s osculating periapsis swings 367-400 km over one orbit and the true radius only 393-400 km (a throwaway RK4 run, not committed); with an impact predicted first, the periapsis is the osculating one at the impact state. run-flight steps a clock through the clock''s one-step function (lowering rules, the hook, sim-step!; factored out of clock-frame! if plan 15 kept it inline) with no accumulator, calling prediction-update! before each step as the frame does; (:warp :coast) raises in the hook, per step, so live and headless take the same steps. run-flight takes :model (default the sim''s :full) so tests can fly a script two-body. Scripts: burn (x1: 10 s full prograde, about 28.4 m/s, apoapsis about 500 km), raise-orbit (Hohmann 400 -> 800 km: 109.1 m/s cut by :after-dv, then 107.6 m/s centred on the apoapsis with :before, both under (:warp :coast)), deorbit (retrograde hold, 102.3 m/s for a 50 km periapsis, 50 km below the crash radius as plan 15''s test, wait :impact, :lost :crash, :impact-as-predicted 0.1, (:restart), :start-state). raise-orbit''s 1% of the 400 km raise is checked exactly in a two-body run (:model :point: both apsides 800 +- 4 km); the script''s own full-model bands are 800 +- 8 km (1% of the apsis altitude), the worker measures and widens with a comment if J2 needs it. Live: (fly name) restarts the game at the script''s start state, sets the view outside, installs the hook and returns; (flight-status) prints the record so far; while a flight runs input-frame! leaves the controls alone (flight keys ignored as when lost; P, C, Home, M, the mouse and quit act); it writes no PNGs (./sly screenshot does). scripts/fly <name> <out-dir> writes <n>-<label>.png per checkpoint by capture-state and prints one line per checkpoint, burn and failure; --from and <name>.end.sexp come with saves.lisp in milestone 5. Done-when screenshots are not committed: scripts/fly regenerates them bit for bit.', NULL, '2026-10-14T22:52:31Z', '2026-10-14T22:52:31Z', 81, NULL, 'rule', 81, '2026-10-14, task 81: 2026-10-14, task 81: plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when builds on it; its pieces update docs/architecture.md ''Scripted runs'' (the additions, apsis conditions on the prediction, clock-step!, what scripts/fly writes before milestone 5).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (88, 'Warp keys while paused', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 19: Input and the outside view lists . , / among the keys acting while paused and its tests check that . acts while paused; decision 81''s paused list omitted them. docs/design.md ''Time warp'' says only flight inputs are ignored while paused, and warp keys are not flight inputs. If the clock of plan 15 refuses a level change while paused, the clock''s rule stands and input only forwards the command.', 'While paused, . , / act: they change the nominal warp level (with the clock''s refusals and messages) that P''s resume restores; the simulation stays paused. This completes the paused list of decision 81: Input in milestone 2: held keys per frame, taps, gating and the shared key map, which named P, C, Home, M, Tab, Page keys, the mouse and quit.', NULL, '2026-10-14T23:01:04Z', '2026-10-14T23:01:04Z', 88, NULL, 'rule', 88, '2026-10-14, task 88: 2026-10-14, task 88: task 120: input.lisp part two: the key map''s commands, the throttle ramp, gating and the window title implements it.

2026-10-15, task 120: task 120: input.lisp part two: the key map''s commands, the throttle ramp, gating and the window title: . , / run while paused (input.lisp *flight-commands* excludes them), tested in tests/input.lisp input-gating-paused.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (89, 'Ephemeris test bounds and the start Sun''s RA', 'answered', '[]', 'Measured in task 95: cubic Hermite truncation over 4,096 s reaches 2.5 cm for the Moon independent of the velocity difference step; decision 18''s 2 cm was an estimate, and halving the spacing for 1 cm buys nothing visible. Horizons astrometric ICRF at 2040-01-01 00:00 UT gives RA 280.454 deg, Dec -23.086 deg; the plan''s 281 deg +- 0.5 deg would fail by 0.05 deg. beta = -9.6 deg and 25.2 deg from the Sun stay within task 96''s 1 deg bounds.', 'The Moon''s Hermite-vs-series bound is 3 cm (2.5 cm measured at 4,096 s nodes, which decision 18: Ephemerides fixes); the Earth = EMB - f x Moon identity is tested within 1e-4 m, not 1e-6 m (one ulp of a 1.5e11 m heliocentric value is 3e-5 m); the Sun at the start is near RA 280.5 deg in ICRF (281 deg is of date), docs/design.md ''The start'' says so.', NULL, '2026-10-14T23:20:59Z', '2026-10-14T23:20:59Z', 95, NULL, 'rule', 95, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (90, 'rk4-step! calling convention: dry mass, attitude function, inline', 'answered', '[]', 'Refines decision 69: Ship state, step rule and kinematic attitude in physics.lisp so the step path conses nothing (plan 14''s rule): a full call boxes double-float arguments (16 bytes per call for t) and an attitude function returning fresh quats conses per stage. Passing m-dry keeps m_p integrated inside RK4. Task 99, docs/architecture.md ''Integrator''.', '(rk4-step! state h t model force-body torque-body mdot m-dry inertia inertia-inverse &optional attitude-fn): the caller passes the dry mass (the stage mass is m-dry + m_p); the kinematic attitude function is called as (fn q-out w-out time-cell r v), writing the attitude and the frame rate (inertial axes) into q-out and w-out, the time in a one-element double-float array; rk4-step! is declaimed sb-ext:maybe-inline and hot callers (flight-step!, test loops) declare it inline.', NULL, '2026-10-14T23:40:40Z', '2026-10-14T23:40:40Z', 99, NULL, 'rule', 99, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (91, 'Render targets in gpu.lisp: cached per name', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 16 asks for targets keyed by (extent, format); two targets of the same extent and format (post''s ping-pong, the HDR target and a same-size scratch) would collide, so the name is the key and (extent, format) the validity check (task 108).', 'render-target caches one texture and view per name (a keyword: :depth, :hdr, a bloom level), valid while its extent and format match; a change remakes it and the old pair goes through luv''s deferred destroy. The renderer owns every buffer, texture, view, sampler and bind group it creates (gpu-create) and destroys them in destroy-renderer; views of foreign textures (swapchain, capture) stay cached per texture and go with the device.', NULL, '2026-10-15T01:00:47Z', '2026-10-15T01:00:47Z', 108, NULL, 'rule', 108, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (92, 'Blue Marble tests: Antarctic row and Earth-versus-background start check', 'answered', '[]', 'The January Blue Marble (world.200401) draws the Arctic Ocean in dark ocean colour (polar night, row 5 mean about sRGB 2, 5, 20), so plan 16''s ''row 5 above 150'' fails on the real file; Antarctica in its summer is white (row 2694 mean about 240). The textured placeholder Earth''s open Pacific is dark (about sRGB 2, 5, 20, no atmosphere yet), so at the start pose the lower third''s mean (about 8) falls below the upper third''s with the label and stars (about 27); the new check still proves Earth fills the view below the horizon and is lit. Part 4''s exposure and part 5''s atmosphere may restore a luminance margin.', 'tests/assets.lisp checks the mean of row 2694 (Antarctic summer ice) above 150 per channel instead of row 5; tests/render.lisp''s start frame checks that over 95 % of the lower third differs from the background colour (the upper third''s commonest colour), under 50 % of the upper third does, and the lower third is brighter than the background, instead of the lower third''s mean luminance 20 above the upper third''s', NULL, '2026-10-15T01:19:25Z', '2026-10-15T01:19:25Z', 110, NULL, 'rule', 110, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (93, 'Exposure rule as built: hidden Sun, coverage within the view', 'answered', '[]', 'task 111: post.lisp: HDR target, bloom, tone map, exposure. decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure states ''pi rho^2 over the frame''s solid angle'' without saying whether the body must be in view, and the Sun''s test without occlusion; its own test list wants Earth at phase 170 deg filling the view to be deep, which puts the Sun 10 deg off the view axis behind Earth, and a camera looking away from a shadowed Earth to be deep. Occlusion and in-view coverage satisfy both and match what a camera sees; the flat lens is accurate enough for a 5 % threshold.', 'In exposure-target (src/post.lisp) the Sun counts only when no body nearer than it covers its centre (angle to the body''s centre below its angular radius), and a body''s coverage is the overlap of its disc (angular radius asin(R/d), the whole sky inside it) with the cone of the frame''s solid angle, in the flat lens formula, over that cone''s area, capped at 1; lit fraction (1 + cos phase)/2 with the phase at the body between the Sun and the eye. The aspect comes from the target''s extent.', NULL, '2026-10-15T01:34:54Z', '2026-10-15T01:34:54Z', 111, NULL, 'rule', 111, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (94, 'Render targets in gpu.lisp: cached per name', 'answered', '[]', 'Supersedes `decision 91: Render targets in gpu.lisp: cached per name` in its last clause only: leaving foreign views to the device let a GC reclaim them first, and luv''s leak check printed ''Leaked GPU VULKAN-GPU-TEXTURE-VIEW'' in scripts/test (`task 144: gpu.lisp: destroy-renderer leaves colour views of swapchain and capture textures to the GC`). A Vulkan image view''s teardown needs only the live device, so destroying it after the canvas closed is safe; stop''s order (canvas, renderer, device) stays.', 'render-target caches one texture and view per name (a keyword: :depth, :hdr, a bloom level), valid while its extent and format match; a change remakes it and the old pair goes through luv''s deferred destroy. The renderer owns every buffer, texture, view, sampler and bind group it creates (gpu-create) and destroys them in destroy-renderer; views of foreign textures (swapchain, capture) stay cached per texture, are destroyed by forget-color-view when their texture goes on a resize, and by destroy-renderer, before the device.', NULL, '2026-10-15T01:39:55Z', '2026-10-15T01:39:55Z', 144, NULL, 'rule', 144, '2026-10-15, task 144: src/gpu.lisp destroy-renderer destroys the cached colour views; scripts/test prints no leaked-view message.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (95, 'Star fading at the sunlit exposure as built', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 17: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars asks for every star of mag >= 1.0 below 8 of 255 at *exposure-sunlit* and mag 6.5 at least 20 at *exposure-deep*; with decision 78: Sky in numbers: stars, Sun, Moon and Earth''s look''s half-Pogson slope these contradict (16x exposure = 6 magnitudes at half slope; the sRGB toe lifts dim values), so a mag 1.0 star at sunlit reads 21 (measured with tone-map-srgb). decision 78 is the binding answer and says ''the stars fade at the sunlit one except the brightest few, dim''; about 170 stars brighter than mag 3 stay faintly visible next to a lit Earth, the 16 brighter than mag 1 dim. Decided by task 113: stars.lisp: the stars at infinity, their colours and the :stars pipeline.', 'decision 78''s numbers stay (L(m) = 1.25e-3 x 10^(0.2 (6.5 - m)), exposures 16 and 1.0, ACES and sRGB): at the sunlit exposure stars of mag 3.0 and fainter show below 8 of 255, mag 1.0 at 21, Sirius at 58; tests/stars.lisp checks mag >= 3.0 below 8, mag >= 1.0 below 24, Sirius below 80, mag 6.5 at the deep exposure >= 20 (26).', NULL, '2026-10-15T01:49:26Z', '2026-10-15T01:49:26Z', 113, NULL, 'rule', 113, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (96, 'Impostors part one as built: the ship term''s eye-inside case and the GPU checks'' exposures', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 17: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars piece 3 leaves the impostor''s uniform layout open, gives impostor-params! an aspect it does not need, and two GPU checks whose numbers do not hold as written; decision 79 does not say what the ship term is with the camera at the ship. Recorded by task 114: impostors.lisp part one: impostor machinery, the Sun, the Moon, sun-visible-fraction.', 'src/impostors.lisp: impostor-params! (out index centre radius offset-term camera) writes a 16-float block (centre, radius; axis u, c.c - r^2; axis v; shade) into the frame uniforms and returns :quad, :full-screen or nil (cone wholly behind the camera); the full-screen form is a triangle drawn from the same corner buffer. The shader''s ray comes from frag-coord and the clip rows; h^2 is r^2 - |b d - c|^2 and the normal (b d - c - h d)/r, so no large terms cancel. decision 79''s ship term counts only when the eye is outside the ship''s bounding sphere (offset > *ship-bounding-radius*; a camera at the ship, as the tests'' and the cockpit''s, adds nothing) and uses the flat lens overlap with the frame''s cone as decision 93 does for bodies. tests/render.lisp checks the Sun''s limb darkening at *exposure-sunlit* 1e-5 instead of 2e-5 (at 2e-5 the centre and 0.8 R read 229 and 220 in ACES''s shoulder, at 1e-5 202 and 186) and counts the Moon''s lit pixels with the bloom off (with it, the deep exposure''s bloom spreads the disc to 1.17 of its area).', NULL, '2026-10-15T02:11:15Z', '2026-10-15T02:11:15Z', 114, NULL, 'rule', 114, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (97, 'Earth and halo impostors as built: rim, footprint mix, halo depth and colour ramps', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 17: Earth, Moon, Sun and stars piece 4 and decision 78: Sky in numbers: stars, Sun, Moon and Earth''s look name the rim, the footprint mix and the halo''s day/dusk ramp without their formulas, and leave the halo''s depth (depth-tested against Earth, but a full-screen triangle has no depth of its own) open; the check ''the lit disc''s diameters within 2 px'' fails at the sunlit exposure because the January Blue Marble''s ocean is near black there. Recorded by task 115: impostors.lisp part two: Earth, its atmosphere rim and halo; PLACEHOLDER(76) goes.', 'src/impostors.lisp and src/shaders.lisp (task 115): the rim is (1 - mu)^4 x clamp(n.s + 0.2, 0, 1) x *rim-strength* 0.3 x (1 AU / d)^2 in the halo''s day colour (docs/luv-capabilities.md run 32''s term); the 1/8 copy''s weight is clamp(ln(footprint) / ln 8, 0, 1), footprint = t x pixel angle / max(mu, 0.02) over pi R / 2700 (full texels), computed from the hit, not derivatives; the halo''s radius is body-crash-radius of Earth; its colour mix(dusk, day, clamp(sin e / sin 6 deg, 0, 1)) and its strength clamp(1 + sin e / sin 6 deg, 0, 1), e the Sun''s elevation at the chord''s point nearest Earth''s centre; the halo writes :frag-depth at the chord''s start (its end with the eye inside) and compares :greater without writing, so the Moon and ships in front hide it; the frame block grows to 29 vec4s (halo-day replaces the unused body vec4, sun.w carries (1 AU / d)^2, Earth''s and the halo''s impostor blocks, halo-dusk). tests/render.lisp measures Earth''s roundness from 40,000 km with *exposure-sunlit* 8, the halo and bloom off: at exposure 1 the Blue Marble''s open Pacific falls to the background (runs of 83 and 32 px of 121), and decision 78 keeps the texture as albedo, without a gain.', NULL, '2026-10-15T02:23:10Z', '2026-10-15T02:23:10Z', 115, NULL, 'rule', 115, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (98, 'Bloom''s way up: a round 19-tap kernel', 'answered', '[]', 'task 145: the tent''s square footprint showed where the Sun''s far bloom levels saturate (a rounded square). The round kernel alone makes the glow round within 2 % (row against diagonal x sqrt 2, at 320x200 and 1280x800), at the cost of 19 instead of 9 taps on three small passes; per-level falloff or a 1/32 level would change the glare''s size and still leave compact square tails.', 'The bloom chain''s up passes blur the smaller level with a round 19-tap kernel (centre, 6 taps at 1 texel, 12 at 2 texels offset 15 deg, bilinear, Gaussian weights of sigma 1 texel, normalised) instead of the 3x3 tent; the downsample stays Jimenez''s 13-tap, the tone map''s last step the tent; no threshold, *bloom-strength* 0.04 and per-level weights unchanged (decision 76, decision 78).', NULL, '2026-10-15T02:28:35Z', '2026-10-15T02:28:35Z', 145, NULL, 'rule', 145, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (99, 'Earthshine from the lit part of the visible cap', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 117: with the whole-disc phase the Mule''s Earth-facing side stayed lit at the middle of the night pass (u = 180 deg of the start orbit: phase 0.05, earthshine 0.013, about 140 of 255 at the deep exposure 16), though the Earth below it is dark; from 400 km only a cap of 19.8 deg half-angle is visible, wholly past the terminator there. The share is 0.98 at noon (the disc''s 1.0) and falls to 0 a cap''s width past the terminator; it underestimates far from Earth (2/pi at noon from very far), which matters only after milestone 2.', 'The earthshine on the Mule (decision 80: The Mule''s meshes in numbers: geometry, draws, materials and lights) uses, in place of Earth''s whole-disc phase (1 + rhat.shat)/2, the lit share of the cap of Earth seen from the ship: max(0, cos theta) of the Sun''s zenith angle averaged over theta0 +- lambda, theta0 the Sun''s zenith angle at the sub-ship point, lambda = acos(R_earth / |r|) the cap''s half-angle; 0 when the whole cap is past the terminator. Otherwise as decision 80: *earth-albedo* 0.3 x (R_earth / |r|)^2 x that share x (1 AU / d_sun)^2 x max(n.(-rhat), 0), tinted 0.85 0.92 1.0 (meshes.lisp earth-lit-cap).', NULL, '2026-10-15T02:54:34Z', '2026-10-15T02:54:34Z', 117, NULL, 'rule', 117, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (100, 'The lights'' clock and the glows'' numbers', 'answered', '[]', 'task 118 measured the lights in Earth''s shadow, where the exposure is 16: decision 80''s radiances (50, 400, 1.5) bloomed into blobs tens of pixels wide that hid the cab, and the engine glow''s centre came out white with red above blue. A sim-time strobe at x100 is lit in about one frame of twelve at random. Real time while running keeps a steady 1.2 s blink at every warp level and stays deterministic for paused captures.', 'The strobe''s phase runs on the lights'' clock: real seconds summed while the sim clock runs (render-state lights-t, advanced in main.lisp''s draw, 0 for a new game and frozen while paused, so captures are deterministic and show the strobe lit), not the sim''s render time, which at warp aliases the 0.1 s flash into random flicker. Glows are Gaussian billboards with sigma = the light''s radius, a quad of 3 sigma, sigma at least 0.75 px (1.5 px across), the radiance scaled by the area ratio when enlarged; navigation lights and the strobe pulled towards the eye by one radius. Numbers changed from decision 80: The Mule''s meshes in numbers: geometry, draws, materials and lights, because at the deep exposure (16, decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure) its values saturate and bloom over the cab: navigation lights radiance 5 in pure linear red (1 0 0) and green (0 1 0) (pure primaries keep their hue when the tone map saturates), on brackets 0.15 m outboard of the truss''s top front corners, (15.0, +-1.95, -1.95), since the longerons cut them at the corners; the strobe radius 0.05 m, radiance 20; the engine glow *engine-glow-radiance* 0.1 at full throttle (faint in sunlight, unsaturated and blue at the deep exposure; 1.5 saturates to white). Timing (0.1 s every 1.2 s), the engine glow''s colour 0.45 0.6 1.0 and radius 0.8 m as decision 80. The lights-on slot turns off the navigation lights and the strobe, not the engine glow.', NULL, '2026-10-15T03:11:45Z', '2026-10-15T03:11:45Z', 118, NULL, 'rule', 118, '2026-10-15, task 118: 2026-10-14, task 118: meshes.lisp draws the lights and the engine glow with these numbers (:ship-glow pipeline, ship-glows!, strobe-on-p, engine-glow-radiance); render-state gains lights-t, advanced in main.lisp''s draw while the clock runs; docs/design.md ''How they look'' and docs/architecture.md ''Files in load order'' and ''GPU tests'' updated.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (101, 'Ribbon depth check from 10 degrees above the orbit plane', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 20: The basic map asks for the eye in the orbit plane (el 0). Edge-on, the near and far halves of the circular start orbit project onto one line, so the far half''s pixels cannot be told apart from the near half''s. At 10 deg the far half lies apart (about 16 px at 320x200); the test classifies each orbit point as seen or hidden by Earth in double on the CPU and requires no cyan pixel within 1 px of a hidden point that is more than 3 px from any seen one (338 such pixels at 320x200, none cyan; task 125).', 'tests/render.lisp checks that Earth hides the ring''s far side with the map camera 10 deg above the orbit plane, not 0 deg', NULL, '2026-10-15T03:56:23Z', '2026-10-15T03:56:23Z', 125, NULL, 'rule', 125, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (102, 'Overlay colours: sRGB integers, linear vertices, encoded in the overlay fragment', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 84 and decision 85 want exact colours after the tone map; before, the overlay wrote its value raw, so a -srgb canvas and a unorm canvas stored different bytes. Matches ribbons.lisp''s sRGB-integer colours (task 126).', 'marker-vertices! and label-at! take colours as sRGB integers (#40D0FF); vertices carry the linear value; the :overlay fragment sRGB-encodes when the target format does not, so the stored pixel is the given colour on either canvas format. The tests'' :quads pipeline (HDR) keeps the raw fragment (quads-fragment).', NULL, '2026-10-15T04:01:10Z', '2026-10-15T04:01:10Z', 126, NULL, 'rule', 126, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (103, 'Map labels: placement order and overlap', 'answered', '[]', 'Task 127 (decision 84: Map ribbons and markers: osculating orbit, adaptive segments, overlay markers and visibility) drew every label to the right of its marker. At 100 AU the ship, the apsides, the impact, Earth and the Moon fall on one pixel and the Sun ~9 px beside it, so the labels overprinted into an unreadable block and plan 20''s done-when (labelled EARTH and SUN at 100 AU) failed; in LEO the ship''s label and an apsis label collide whenever the ship is at that apsis. Bodies go first because at the zooms where they have markers the orbit''s own markers sit on Earth''s pixel; the ship before the orbit''s points because it is what the player looks for. Trying the left side keeps two nearby labels (Earth and the Sun at 100 AU) both readable; no leader lines or stacking, which would part a label from its marker.', 'overlay.lisp places the map''s marker labels in the order Earth, Sun, Moon, ship (M-07), impact, periapsis, apoapsis: each 8 px right of its marker''s centre (decision 84) when its box overlaps no label placed before, else with its right edge 8 px left of the centre, else dropped (the marker itself still drawn). *marker-label-order* is a defparameter.', NULL, '2026-10-15T04:16:10Z', '2026-10-15T04:16:10Z', 127, NULL, 'rule', 127, '2026-10-15, task 127: 2026-10-15, task 127: src/overlay.lisp place-map-labels! and *marker-label-order*; docs/design.md ''Views'' -> Map markers as built; tests/ribbons.lisp map-labels-placement and the 100 AU Earth/Sun check.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (104, 'Map labels on a near-black backing', 'answered', '[]', 'The overlay has no blending, so colours stay exact only with opaque fills. A box, as the readout has (decision 85: Minimal readout: layout, formats, colours and alarms), reads better than a 1 px outline around each dot at a 2 px dot pitch (an outline would fill most of the cell anyway) and costs one quad per label instead of up to 35 x 8. The 2 px margin keeps the box clear of the 12 px ship ring and 9 px diamonds, whose outer edges lie 6 px from the centre with the label 8 px from it. Drawing backings before shapes keeps every marker visible where a neighbour''s backing would overlap it.', 'Each map marker label kept by place-map-labels! gets an opaque #0A0C0C box behind it: its glyph cells (label-width x 7 dots) grown by *label-backing-px* 2 px on every side. map-marker-vertices! draws all backings first, then the marker shapes, then the glyphs, so a backing never covers a marker or another label''s dots; place-map-labels! keeps its boxes as they are (decision 103: Map labels: placement order and overlap).', NULL, '2026-10-15T04:26:03Z', '2026-10-15T04:26:03Z', 148, NULL, 'rule', 148, '2026-10-15, task 148: 2026-10-15, task 148: src/overlay.lisp label-backing!, *label-backing-colour*, *label-backing-px*, map-marker-vertices! draws backings, shapes, glyphs; tests/overlay.lisp label-backing; tests/render.lisp marker-checks checks a backing pixel; docs/design.md ''Views'' -> Map labels line.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (105, 'Readout box: placement, padding and the overlay''s capacity', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 85: Minimal readout: layout, formats, colours and alarms gives the 16 px margin and the cells but not whether the margin places the box or the text, nor the capacity; task 130 chose the box at the margin with an inner padding, so the text never touches the box''s edge, and one buffer so the overlay is one draw.', 'The readout''s opaque #0A0C0C box has its top left 16 px (*readout-margin-px*) from the frame''s top and left edges; the text sits *readout-padding-px* 8 px inside it, in lines of 22 px with 12 px cells; an inverted run''s block covers its cells grown by *readout-invert-px* 2 px. The map''s markers and the readout share one overlay buffer per frame slot of *overlay-max-vertices* 73,728 (a multiple of 6: twice the fullest readout, 29,130 vertices, plus the old marker capacity 12,288), cut past it.', NULL, '2026-10-15T04:40:04Z', '2026-10-15T04:40:04Z', 130, NULL, 'rule', 130, '2026-10-15, task 130: 2026-10-14, task 130: src/overlay.lisp readout-vertices!, readout-box-size, *readout-margin-px*, *readout-padding-px*, *overlay-max-vertices*; src/render.lisp encode-overlay; tests/overlay.lisp readout-vertices, overlay-capacity; tests/render.lisp readout-checks.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (106, 'Captures freeze the game, not the clock''s pause', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 77: The frame in main.lisp: render state, capture of a given sim, part 4''s placeholders and first asset paused the clock to show exactly the given state; since the readout is drawn (decision 85: Minimal readout: layout, formats, colours and alarms) every capture read WARP ×1 PAUSED. Freezing by a game flag keeps the state exact (no steps) and the readout the player''s; the prediction still updates so impact alarms show. Skipping clock-frame! entirely also keeps the rate meter empty (no ACT ×0). Refines decision 77''s ''clock paused''. Task 151.', 'A capture''s game is frozen (game-frozen-p, start :frozen): game-frame! runs only prediction-update!, no clock-frame! steps, the lights'' clock still; clock-paused stays nil, so the readout shows WARP ×1 without PAUSED and no ACT caution.', NULL, '2026-10-15T04:54:00Z', '2026-10-15T04:54:00Z', 151, NULL, 'rule', 151, '2026-10-15, task 151: task 151: game-frozen-p and start :frozen in main.lisp; with-capture-game starts frozen and game-load-state! no longer pauses (capture.lisp); readout-checks in tests/render.lisp asserts WARP ×1; docs/architecture.md updated.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (107, 'Death glow radiance 1.0', 'answered', '[]', 'At 4.0 the ACES tone map saturates the glow: the Mule at the peak reads yellow-white with an orange fringe from bloom. Captures of a lost sim at the peak (task 152), lit (day side) and in Earth''s shadow, at 4.0, 1.5, 1.0, 0.75, 0.5: in shadow 1.0 is a clear orange and 1.5 already turns yellow-orange; in sunlight 1.0 turns the truss, wings, tanks and engine orange while the white cab stays pale pink-white, because the emission adds to the sunlit term (no radiance turns lit white paint orange without saturating the rest); below 1.0 the lit ship reads salmon rather than glowing.', '*death-glow-radiance* 1.0 (was 4.0 in decision 86: Death view, LOST panel and restart in milestone 2); colour #FF6A1A, ÷ the exposure, full for 1 s, linear to 0 at 3 s, none for the Moon, all unchanged.', NULL, '2026-10-15T04:55:57Z', '2026-10-15T04:55:57Z', 152, NULL, 'rule', 152, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (108, 'run-flight''s prediction during burns and the hook''s order in clock-step!', 'answered', '[]', 'task 134 (plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when). With the throttle on, every prediction-update! restarts the prediction: measured 1.27 ms each (1,519 coarse burning steps), so a 10 s burn at x1 (640 steps) cost 0.82 s headless against the 1.5 s budget of all of plan 22''s piece-1 tests; once per game second it is 18 ms. A frame at 60 fps updates about once per step at x1 too, so headless and live differ in when the prediction is refreshed during a burn either way; the prediction steers a step only through drop-outs (an impact within 3 s x level, at most x100 during a burn: 300 s) and refusals, which a 1 s old prediction during a burn decides as a fresh one does. Reading the level after the hook lets (:warp …), (:warp :coast) and (:throttle …) act on the step they are applied before, with the thrust limit enforced by the hook''s own clock-check-level!; a hook that changes nothing sees the same behaviour as before (plan 15''s clock tests unchanged).', 'Headless, run-flight calls prediction-update! before every step while the throttle is off, and once per *flight-burn-prediction-interval* (1 s of game time) while it is on; clock-step! reads the warp level after the hook, and the flight hook applies clock-check-level! itself after its actions and the coast raise.', NULL, '2026-10-15T05:17:52Z', '2026-10-15T05:17:52Z', 134, NULL, 'rule', 134, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (109, 'Live fly: the restart at once, Return and N ignored, the record in ./sly log', 'answered', '[]', 'task 136 (plan 22: Flight scripts and the Done when). decision 87 says (:restart) presses Return while lost and live calls game-restart!; headless and live must take the same states, so the restart cannot wait for the player. To see the LOST panel live, fly a script without (:restart) (or stop it before) and press Return by hand. A flight''s end callback runs on the canvas thread, whose output is the image''s log, not the ./sly eval connection that started it.', 'Live, (:restart) calls game-restart! with the flight''s start state in the hook run after the crash frame (flight-frame!), so a live deorbit shows the LOST panel only for that frame; while a flight runs Return and N are ignored with the flight and warp keys (the script owns the restart); the end''s record lines go to the image''s output, read by ./sly log, and (flight-status) returns them.', NULL, '2026-10-15T05:36:37Z', '2026-10-15T05:36:37Z', 136, NULL, 'rule', 136, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (110, 'Objects in the simulation: places.lisp''s load position, the object struct and promotion checks', 'answered', '[]', 'task 157 (plan for part 1 of plan 3: masterplan). docs/architecture.md ''Files in load order'' put places.lisp after autopilot.lisp, but clock.lisp''s start state must hold the objects; places.lisp needs only physics.lisp''s type and ship.lisp''s tensor helpers, so it moves up. Table values as data: some tensors (Highport''s I_xz) came from a throwaway script whose details are not all in the docs, so recomputing them at load could disagree with the design. A Kepler solve per object per step at x1,000,000 (4,096 steps x 12 objects) would cost ~10 ms a frame; a next-check time bounded by 25 km/s (two speeds below Earth''s escape speed at the crash radius, 11.09 km/s, plus margin) makes promotion checks rare far away and exact near 100 km. Sharing the Moon and Sun reads keeps nine promoted objects at Skua Yard at ~0.4 us each per step.', 'places.lisp loads after ship.lisp and before controls.lisp (it needs physics.lisp''s object type and ship.lisp''s shape-tensor helpers; clock.lisp''s new-game-sim builds the objects from it; autopilot.lisp, later, reads it). places.lisp holds the design''s table values as data (mass, tensor, shapes, fixtures, bound), not recomputed from the parts at load; tests check them against the formulas and parts. physics.lisp''s object is a struct of an immutable definition (key, name, kind :towable / :berth / :fixed, mass, tensor and inverse, shapes, fixtures, attitude mode, spin, bound, definition rails) shared between copies, and a mutable part copied in place by the sim (status :rails / :promoted / :removed, the rails segment list (immutable, replaced whole at a demotion), the promoted ship-state, the next promotion check time, the removal record). sim-objects is a simple-vector in the fixed order of *objects*; sim-assign! copies it consing nothing. Each step after flight-step!: promoted objects stepped by rk4-step! with the same h and model (towable: no force or torque, attitude integrated; berth and fixed: the mode''s kinematic attitude function), sharing the step''s Moon and Sun reads; then the ship''s crash test, the objects'' crash tests and rails removals, then promotion (within 100 km of the ship) and demotion (beyond 200 km). A rails object''s distance is tested only at its next check time, t + max(0, d - 100 km) / 25 km/s rounded down to the grid (every step while the ship is faster than 12 km/s), kept in the sim so it is deterministic and copied by checkpoints.', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:07:31Z', '2026-10-15T06:07:31Z', 157, NULL, 'rule', 157, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (111, 'Fixture axes of Derelict K-17 and Skua Yard''s rocks', 'answered', '[]', 'task 157. docs/design.md gives K-17''s spin (0.3 deg/s about its long axis, the fixture on the axis) but not where the axis points, and the rocks'' spins but not their axes. In a 100 min LEO an in-plane inertial axis would swing the stop point once around the target every orbit; the orbit normal of a polar orbit is both inertial and fixed in the orbital frame, so a hand approach and the autopilot''s station-keeping see a constant geometry, and the spin about the axis of least inertia stays steady in torque-free motion. The rocks'' axes follow docs/design.md ''The terminal path and crowded targets'' (at apogee the stop point is 100 m behind the target in the line, at perigee 100 m ahead), which assumes POD 14''s orientation for every yard object.', 'Derelict K-17''s fixture axis (+x, its spin axis) is fixed in inertial space along its orbit''s anti-normal (the LVLH +y at the epoch), so in its polar orbit, whose plane J2 does not turn, the fixture faces the same side of the orbit at every point and the stop point 100 m along it stays 100 m out of the plane. Skua Yard''s six rocks: +x is the LVLH attitude at the epoch turned 180 deg about z, as POD 14''s, so every fixture faces back along the orbit at each apogee and forward at each perigee; each spins about +x at its tabled rate.', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:07:31Z', '2026-10-15T06:07:31Z', 157, NULL, 'rule', 157, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (112, 'Grapple, stack and berth in the sim: state, notices to the clock, the latest checkpoint', 'answered', '[]', 'task 158 (plan for part 2 of plan 3: masterplan). docs/architecture.md ''Checkpoints'' lists grapple, load, berth in the sim state and ''Collisions and the Sun'' the contact response, but not how the sim reports events the clock shows (the clock, not the sim, owns messages, and the prediction steps a copy whose events nobody shows), where the latest in-memory checkpoint lives, how a load''s state is known while latched, or how a step is redone. Notices in the sim keep sim-step! the one step function for frame, scripts and prediction; the checkpoint in the clock keeps it out of the sim it snapshots; r as the stack''s CoM keeps rk4-step! unchanged.', 'clock.lisp''s sim gains: the grapple (state :stowed / :armed / :closing / :latched, the attached object''s index and fixture index, its pose relative to the ship: the object''s CoM in ship body axes from R and the relative quaternion, the closing end time, 2 s after capture on the grid), berthed (true when attached at a :berth fixture: the ship is not integrated but set each step from the station''s state and the fixed pose; engine and RCS locked), transfer (B on), the excluded object (released, until its shapes and the stack''s no longer overlap), the pending delivery (load, receiving object and fixture, release time), notices (a list of (key text) written by sim rules: captures, NO CAPTURE, removals, deliveries, TANKS FULL) and a checkpoint request flag (berthing; plan 3''s autopilot part sets it on a completed plan); all copied by copy-sim and sim-assign! and compared by equalp. The ship-state''s r and v stay the CoM of the whole stack; ship-mass-properties takes the latched load (mass, tensor, pose) and adds it to the sums. A latched towable object gets status :latched: not stepped, not demoted, its stored state written from the stack after each step, so object-state-into! answers for it. clock-step! drains the notices after each step into clock-message! (key per kind) and a SHIP log in the clock (the last 200 entries: game time, text; warp messages too; a read mark Y sets), and on a request takes the clock''s latest checkpoint (clock-checkpoint) when checkpoint-safe-p holds (no impact and no contact predicted, throttle off); the prediction''s copy clears both after each step. The LOST panel''s Return restores clock-checkpoint (else the start) and reads RESTART FROM CHECKPOINT when there is one; N restores the start state and clears it. Contact responses that need a shorter step restore the step''s start from a preallocated scratch outside the sim, saved only when a promoted object''s bounding sphere is within reach of the step.', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:12:44Z', '2026-10-15T06:12:44Z', 158, NULL, 'rule', 158, '2026-10-15, task 158: plan for task 158 (part 2 of plan 3: masterplan) builds it: sim fields, notices, clock-log, clock-checkpoint in its pieces 2-5.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (113, 'Grapple and contact details the design leaves open', 'answered', '[]', 'task 158 (plan for part 2 of plan 3: masterplan). docs/design.md ''Grappling and berthing'' and docs/architecture.md ''Collisions and the Sun'' give the tolerances, the response and the shapes but leave open: a touch below the 0.01 m/s floor, the order of the reasons, occupied fixtures, G''s gating, what the locked engine says, where refuelling works (the design names depots, places.lisp has no depot flag), object-object contact, the range finder''s exact quantities, the drop-out''s event definition (an overlap that starts the prediction would refuse warp forever beside a load just released) and the contact death''s flash and panel. Each is chosen to keep the design''s numbers and the milestone-2 patterns (decision 81 gating, decision 86 panel and glow).', 'Grapple contacts count only at free fixtures of :towable or :berth kind (occupied ones sit behind their berthed ship''s shapes); with the grapple stowed a fixture is no grapple contact. At a tip crossing the checks run in this order and the first failure is the message: a towable object over 100 t ''LOAD <n> T OVER 100 T RATING''; closing speed above 0.30 m/s ''NO CAPTURE: TOO FAST'', below 0.01 m/s ''NO CAPTURE: TOO SLOW'' (a fifth reason: the claws need a push to trigger); offset ''OFF AXIS''; angle ''MISALIGNED''; relative rotation ''ROTATING''; each a bounce along the fixture axis, death above 1.0 m/s as any contact. Capture messages ''LATCHED <NAME>'' and ''BERTHED <NAME>''. G acts unpaused and not lost at every warp level (stowed -> armed -> stowed; latched releases; closing: ''RELEASE LOCKED: CLAWS CLOSING''); release refused ''RELEASE REFUSED: ROTATING'' above 0.5 deg/s or ''RELEASE REFUSED: ENGINE ON''. Berthing cuts the throttle and sets rate damping; while berthed throttle and RCS keys give ''ENGINE LOCKED: BERTHED'' once per press and change nothing; the release keeps rate damping. B works berthed at a depot (a depot flag in object-def: Fulmar Depot, Highport; Peary Depot in milestone 4): 10 kg/s exactly per step until 28,000 kg (''TANKS FULL'', off), off at release; elsewhere ''NO PROPELLANT HERE'' or ''NOT BERTHED''. Y acts at any time but lost: it marks the SHIP log read (the cockpit''s lamps are its readers). Contact is only ship (stack) against object: objects never collide with each other (a released load drifts through a station; deliveries take it in first). While berthed, the ship counts as part of the immovable station in contacts with other objects. The range finder: the nearest free fixture of a promoted object whose pin lies ahead of the cone''s tip (body x) within 500 m and whose axis faces the ship; range is the tip''s distance to the plate''s plane along the axis, closing speed its rate, offset the pin''s body y and z from the tip, angle between +x and the inward axis, relative rotation |w_ship - w_object|. The contact drop-out event is the stack''s and an object''s bounding spheres coming to meet on the prediction (the centre distance falling below the sum of bounds), not an overlap at the prediction''s start, excluding the berth station, the latched load and the excluded object; its message ''WARP xL: CONTACT IN <duration>''. A contact death: loss cause :contact with the object and normal speed; LOST panel ''LOST: CONTACT'', ''<OBJECT NAME>  <UTC>'', ''SPEED <normal speed>''; the ship''s emissive white #FFFFFF at radiance 1.0 divided by the exposure, full for 0.1 s of real time, linear to 0 at 1 s.', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:12:58Z', '2026-10-15T06:12:58Z', 158, NULL, 'rule', 158, '2026-10-15, task 158: plan for task 158 (part 2 of plan 3: masterplan) builds it in its pieces 2-7; docs/design.md ''Grappling and berthing'', ''Controls'', ''Dying and what follows'' to state it as built.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (114, 'Targets and target holds in the sim', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 3 (task 159). docs/design.md ''Controls'' and ''Control modes'' give the keys and directions but leave open what a target is, the cycle order, the roll of target holds, degenerate directions, and how a target moving on rails enters the per-step and kinematic hold laws (decision 70: Flight computer: per-step control laws, holds and the RCS mixer). A default-roll rule like holds 1-6 has a singular direction (radial, the R-bar approach) where the roll would flip 90 deg; transporting the roll of engagement has none and is what the design''s ''the roll at engagement'' says. Quadratic extrapolation keeps the kinematic step free of a Kepler solve per RK4 stage.', 'The sim holds the target as a key (sim-target: nil, a place key of *places* or a towable object''s key), copied by checkpoints. Candidates in cycle order: the five places of *places* (Skua Yard its drop point), then the towable objects not already a place (Skua Yard 1-6, POD 14) in *objects* order; removed and latched objects are skipped; the rock mover (scenery) is not a target. ] next, [ previous (wrapping; from none ] the first, [ the last), \ clears; they act while paused and at every warp level, not while lost, not while a flight runs. When the target''s object is removed or latched the step clears it, an active target hold releases to rate damping, and the notice ''TARGET LOST: <NAME>'' goes out. Holds 7 target, 8 anti-target (nose along +/- r_T - r_ship), 9 relative prograde, 0 relative retrograde (nose along +/- v_ship - v_T) join hold-kind; without a target they are refused with ''NO TARGET''. Their roll is the roll at engagement, transported: the hold''s attitude is the shortest-arc rotation carrying the previous step''s target attitude''s nose to the new direction, applied to it (no singular direction, no default roll); engaging starts from the ship''s attitude. The direction is frozen (the last target attitude kept) below 1 m range (7, 8) or 0.01 m/s relative speed (9, 0). The frame rate fed forward is d x (d/dt d) / |d|^2 from the relative motion (7, 8) or point-mass gravity''s difference (9, 0). The step writes the target''s state at its start (rails or promoted; r, v and point-mass gravity a) into the controls; above x4 the kinematic attitude function takes the target at stage time tau as r_T + v_T tau + a_T tau^2 / 2 (under 0.1 m at Highport''s 64 s steps). Instruments take the target at the render time t_r the same way from its state at the sim''s t (rails exactly). Relative values: range |r_T - r_ship|, closing speed -(r_rel . v_rel)/|r_rel| (positive closing), relative velocity v_ship - v_T in body axes (F, R, D).', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:20:32Z', '2026-10-15T06:20:32Z', 159, NULL, 'rule', 159, '2026-10-15, task 159: 2026-10-15, task 159: plan 25: Predicted trajectories, targets and target holds builds it in its pieces.

2026-10-15, task 195: 2026-10-15, task 195: Targets and the relative values: sim-target, the candidates, ] [ \, target-check! and target-relative-into! built (clock.lisp, controls.lisp, render.lisp, input.lisp); the render-time extrapolation refined by decision 143: The target at the render time: the sim''s gravity and the jerk.

2026-10-15, task 196: 2026-10-15, task 196: Target holds 7-0 built (controls.lisp, clock.lisp, input.lisp, overlay.lisp): the per-step law stores each step''s transported attitude as the last target attitude, a kinematic flight-step! the step''s end one; an exactly opposite direction turns half a turn about the cab''s axis; 7-0 are flight keys (ignored paused, lost, flying) refused with ''NO TARGET'' (message key :target); clearing the target by \ also releases an active target hold; the target''s rails drift per decision 144: The target hold''s rails target: the position''s drift beside the Kepler velocity. A 90 deg slew settles in 24.9 s for each hold.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (115, 'Planned burns as scheduled controls in the sim', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 3 (task 159) puts ''planned burns as scheduled controls in the prediction'' before the autopilot part (part 4). The milestone''s rules want every autopilot command on the step grid and the sim one copyable object; decision 75: Prediction in milestone 2: the step function on a copy predicts by running sim-step! on a copy, so the plan must live in the sim and be executed by the step function for the copy to fly it. A closure schedule could not be compared or saved; plain data can.', 'The sim holds the autopilot''s engaged plan (sim-plan: nil or an immutable burn-plan) and its cursor (index of the current burn, phase :pending / :holding / :burning / :done, the RCS burn''s odometer target); copy-sim and sim-assign! copy them, equalp compares. A burn-plan is a vector of planned-burns (label, hold-at and start on the 1/64 s grid, inertial attitude quaternion, throttle 0.1-1 or an RCS sign +1/-1 along body x, dv) and the hold between burns (:prograde by default). One executor, plan-step-controls! (clock.lisp), runs at the start of every sim-step-ticks! before the cut-off check: at hold-at it engages the inertial hold at the burn''s attitude; at start it sets the throttle and arms sim-cutoff at odometer + dv, or holds the +/-x translation key with its own odometer target, released at the first step start where the odometer reaches it; after a cut it engages the coast hold and moves to the next burn, :done after the last. step-h lands on the next hold-at or start like on until. The flight (live, scripts) and the prediction''s copy run the same executor, so a plan flown at a coarse level equals its prediction bit for bit. The prediction steps its copy at x4 (fine, RCS on) while an RCS burn runs, else at x1,000. Planning, the throttle rule, trims, aborts, input gating while engaged and the completion checkpoint are the autopilot part''s (autopilot.lisp), which sets and replaces sim-plan.', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:20:45Z', '2026-10-15T06:20:45Z', 159, NULL, 'rule', 159, '2026-10-15, task 159: 2026-10-15, task 159: plan 25: Predicted trajectories, targets and target holds builds it in its pieces.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (116, 'Prediction in milestone 3: horizon, target nodes, closest approach, relative nodes, budget', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 3 (task 159), extending decision 75: Prediction in milestone 2: the step function on a copy and decision 20: Trajectory prediction on the frame thread (two periods for the map, events for closest approach and nodes). plan 23: Objects on rails and the Earth-orbit places measures up to ~5 us per coarse step with Skua Yard''s nine objects promoted, against ~0.4 us without, so a budget in steps would overrun 1 ms tenfold near the yard. Coplanar orbits (every place shares the start orbit''s plane, decision 38: Start orbit: polar) would give relative nodes from rounding noise; 0.01 deg is the autopilot''s plane tolerance (docs/design.md ''The autopilot'').', 'The clock''s prediction runs two osculating periods while coasting (map, AUTOPILOT plot and closest approach read one prediction) and one while the throttle is on (it restarts every frame then). With a target it stores the target''s r, v at every node in a parallel buffer, from the copy sim (object-state-into!: rails, or promoted and stepped with the ship). Events added: :closest-approach at each local minimum of |r_T - r_ship| (r_rel . v_rel from - to +), bisected on both Hermites to 1e-6 s, with its distance as the event''s value; :relative-ascending / :relative-descending where the ship''s path crosses the target''s orbit plane (r_ship . (r_T x v_T)^ from - to +, or + to -), bisected to 1e-6 s, counted only where the planes are at least 0.01 deg apart; :burn-start (value: the burn''s index) when the copy''s executor starts a burn. prediction-closest-approach gives the least local minimum after the flown t (time, distance), nil without one. Events hold a double value beside time and kind; capacity 128. Restart also when the target key or the plan (eq) changes, and when the flown target is more than 1 m from the stored target path at the flown t. The per-frame budget counts work, not steps: 1 per copy step, 1 per promoted object stepped in it, 1 per rails evaluation of the target; *prediction-work-per-frame* set from a measurement to about 1 ms (no wall clock in the prediction).', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:20:55Z', '2026-10-15T06:20:55Z', 159, NULL, 'rule', 159, '2026-10-15, task 159: 2026-10-15, task 159: plan 25: Predicted trajectories, targets and target holds builds it in its pieces.

2026-10-15, task 198: 2026-10-15, task 198: src/prediction.lisp milestone 3''s form: the coasting horizon (two periods for the clock''s), the plan flown by the copy (x4 during an RCS burn, :burn-start), the target''s node buffer, event values, the target and plan restart rules, the work budget in units; the budget stays 4,096 per decision 146: The prediction''s work budget: 4,096 per frame, not 1 ms; docs/architecture.md ''Trajectory prediction'', ''Testing''.

2026-10-15, task 199: 2026-10-15, task 199: :closest-approach, :relative-ascending, :relative-descending (target-search!), prediction-closest-approach, prediction-relative-node in src/prediction.lisp, with decision 147: Closest approach: a noise floor for constant ranges; docs/architecture.md ''Trajectory prediction'', ''Testing''.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (117, 'Autopilot in milestone 3: planning jobs, goal measures, burns, execution rules in the step, trims', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 4 (task 160). docs/design.md ''The autopilot'' and decision 32: Autopilot scope and safety rules give the method, the tolerances and the safety rules but not how frame-spread planning stays deterministic, how the goals are measured under J2 (a circular polar orbit''s radius swings ~3 km per orbit, the inclination''s short-period terms reach ~0.02 deg), how ''each frame'' checks land on the grid, how a trim is found, or the burn labels for the orbit manoeuvres. A snapshot makes the plan independent of frame timing; the barrier keeps a plan''s burns in the future without changing warp; 0.1 % of the radius (6.8 km in LEO) covers the J2 swing, means over a period remove short-period terms; impulsive closed forms per step are cheap and deterministic, where a shot per frame is neither; max(0.2 m/s, 1 %) keeps tiny burns from trimming their own noise; re-shooting the remaining burns absorbs an error more cheaply than an extra burn.', 'Planning is a job on a snapshot: copy-sim of the flown sim at the request, the preset and goal; the plan is a pure function of them (the same bit for bit whether run at once, headless, or spread over frames). While a job runs the clock steps the sim no further than the snapshot''s t + 60 s (*plan-barrier*; it stalls, warp unchanged), and a plan''s first hold-at is >= snapshot t + 120 s (*plan-min-lead*). The frame''s work budget (*prediction-work-per-frame*, decision 116) is shared: with a job running the prediction takes at most half, the job the rest; headless (run-flight, tests) jobs run to completion at once. A shown plan is stale at engagement when the flown t has passed its first hold-at - 10 s or the flown r is more than 1 m from the job''s final shot at the flown t; Return then re-plans from now (''PLAN STALE: REPLANNING''). First guesses from osculating Kepler elements (vis-viva at the apsis, Hohmann, 2 v sin(di/2) at the cheaper node: the equatorial nodes for an inclination, the prediction''s relative nodes for a target''s plane; the one at the larger radius, the earlier on a tie); shooting refines each burn''s dv vector in the LVLH axes at its node (T, N, R) by Newton with a finite-difference Jacobian and the minimum-norm step, burn times re-centred on the node after each iteration, at most 8 iterations (''NOT CONVERGED''). Shots are sim-step! runs of a snapshot copy with the candidate sim-plan at *prediction-level* until the last cut + one osculating period, under the sim''s model. Goal measures on the shot after the last cut: apoapsis and periapsis are the largest and smallest |r| over that period (the apsis events), within max(0.1 % of the goal radius, 1 km); inclination is the mean osculating inclination over it, within 0.01 deg; a target''s plane is the angle between the period''s mean unit h and the target''s h at its middle, within 0.01 deg. Burns: centred on the node, t_burn by the rocket equation at the mass then; full throttle unless under 10 s at full, then the throttle that makes 10 s, floor 10 %; under 0.5 m/s on the RCS along +/-x, the sign that keeps the nose within 90 deg of the coast hold''s direction (prograde), so small retrograde trims need no slew; attitude fixed inertial with the nose along dv and the cab (-z) radial out at the node (towards the normal when dv is within 10 deg of radial); hold-at = start - max(60 s, slew estimate + 10 s), the slew estimate the bang-bang time at 80 % of the stack''s couple and 5 deg/s from the previous burn''s attitude or the coast hold''s at hold-at; all times on the 1/64 s grid. Labels: a Hohmann''s first :departure and second :circularise, ''Circularise'' :circularise, ''Set apoapsis/periapsis'' :apsis, ''Change plane'' :plane, trims :trim (decision 56''s list gains :circularise, :apsis, :plane). A plane change asked with a Hohmann merges into the burn at the larger radius. Planning refuses (reason shown) when any node of the final shot from the snapshot to its end is below Earth''s crash radius + 50 km (''BELOW 150 KM'') or within the Moon''s radius + 10 km (''TOO NEAR THE MOON''), when propellant for the plan (rocket equation per burn, RCS at 360 s) plus the reserve max(2 % of the plan''s dv, 20 m/s) exceeds m_p (''NEEDS <n> KG''), when the ship is not in Earth''s sphere, or when a goal altitude lies outside 150-300,000 km (milestone 3 keeps apoapses below the Moon''s sphere). Execution rules run in the one step (clock.lisp, beside plan-step-controls!), so they land on the grid: during a burn, abort (''AUTOPILOT ABORT: UNSAFE ORBIT'') at the first step where the osculating periapsis after cutting now is at or above Earth''s crash radius + 25 km but the one after the burn''s remaining dv applied impulsively along the thrust axis is below it (half the planning margin, so a plan at the 150 km floor never trips it); abort (''PROPELLANT LOW'') when m_p falls below the remaining burns'' propellant plus half the reserve; an angle over 5 deg between the thrust axis and the burn''s direction cuts the burn, the inertial hold re-settles and the burn resumes with its remaining dv once settled, a second time aborts (''ATTITUDE ERROR''); a capture, bounce, latch or berth notice in the step aborts (''CONTACT'', ''LATCHED'', ''BERTHED''). Abort and disengage: throttle off, translation keys released, rate damping, sim-plan nil, the plan kept by the clock''s autopilot for the map in grey; an abort is a sim notice (SHIP log, AUTO lamp). Measurement at each cut: the actual v against the shot''s post-cut state propagated by Kepler to the actual cut time; over max(0.2 m/s, 1 % of the burn''s dv) the cursor goes :replan and a job from that step''s sim re-shoots the remaining burns (after the last leg one :trim burn, only if the goal measure on the post-cut path misses its tolerance), installed exactly at the cut + 60 s (the barrier) in every mode; at most 2 replans per plan, then the plan ends with ''AUTOPILOT: GOAL MISSED''. A burn running on the RCS caps warp at x4 (''WARP x4: RCS BURN'', refusal ''RCS BURN, LIMIT x4''); the next burn''s hold-at is a warp drop-out event (''WARP xL: BURN IN <duration>'', x1 at hold-at). The last cut sets the cursor :done, engages the coast hold, sends ''AUTOPILOT: PLAN COMPLETE'' and sets the sim''s checkpoint request (decision 112); the autopilot is then disengaged (rendezvous keeps station: task 161).', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:29:26Z', '2026-10-15T06:29:26Z', 160, NULL, 'rule', 160, '2026-10-15, task 160: 2026-10-15, task 160: plan 26: Autopilot: planner, orbit manoeuvres, execution and safety builds it in its pieces 1-5; decision 56''s burn labels gain :circularise, :apsis, :plane.

2026-10-15, task 202: 2026-10-15, task 202: the planning job, shots, goal measures and refusals as built, refined by decision 151: Autopilot shooting: what Newton steers, the finite difference, the shot''s setup and horizon, refusal order; decision 56''s labels :circularise, :apsis, :plane in docs/architecture.md ''Waits and expectations''.

2026-10-15, task 203: 2026-10-15, task 203: the execution rules in the step, completion, aborts, engage and disengage, the RCS burn''s x4 and the :burn drop-out as built, refined by decision 152: Autopilot execution in the step as built: when each rule runs, the warp look-ahead, completion, contact reasons, shot aborts.

2026-10-15, task 205: 2026-10-15, task 205: measurement at each cut, replans and the :trim burn as built, refined by decision 154: Autopilot measurement, replans and trims as built.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (118, 'Autopilot preset menu and input in milestone 3', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 4 (task 160). docs/design.md ''The autopilot'' (''Picking a preset'', ''Interrupting'') and ''Controls'' name the keys and step sizes but not the menu''s rows, the value ranges, the repeat rate of held keys, what Backspace does outside a running plan, gating, or how input and the executor share controls. One list with inline values needs no second screen on the 22 x 8 AUTOPILOT screen; letting held keys reach controls while engaged would overwrite the executor''s translation key every frame; the 300,000 km cap keeps milestone 3''s apoapses out of the Moon''s sphere, whose crossing is milestone 4''s.', 'The clock holds the autopilot model (clock-autopilot; not in the sim, not in checkpoints): the menu (open or closed, the selected row, each row''s value), the planning job, the shown plan, the engaged flag mirrored from the sim, the last refusal or abort text. The menu is one list, one manoeuvre per row with its goal value inline: ''CIRCULARISE AP'' (Left/Right toggle AP/PE; default the next apsis), ''SET AP <alt> KM'', ''SET PE <alt> KM'', ''ORBIT <alt> KM'' (a Hohmann to a circular altitude) with an optional second value ''INC <deg>'' or KEEP for a merged plane change, ''PLANE <deg>'' or ''PLANE TARGET'' (offered only with a target); task 161 appends ''RENDEZVOUS <PLACE>'' rows. Altitudes start at the current orbit''s value rounded to 10 km and stay within 150-300,000 km; inclinations at the current one rounded to 0.5 deg, within 0-180 deg. O opens and closes the menu; Up/Down select (wrapping); Left/Right change the selected value: one step at the press, then 8 steps per second of real time while held, steps x10 after 1 s held (10 km -> 100 km, 0.5 deg -> 5 deg); Return plans the selected row (state planning, ''PLANNING...''), a second Return on a shown plan engages it (a running plan is replaced only then); Backspace disengages a running plan (''AUTOPILOT OFF''), else drops a shown or refused plan back to the menu, else closes the menu. After a disengage or abort, Return re-plans the same row from the new state and engages as soon as it is shown. Gating: the menu, planning and engaging act while paused and at every warp level; not while lost; while a flight runs its keys are ignored (flights drive the model through the same functions). While engaged input-frame! writes no held keys and runs no throttle ramp (the executor owns the controls); a new press of any flight key (rotation, translation, Shift-left, Control-left, Z, X, F, T, R, 1-0) disengages with ''AUTOPILOT OFF: PILOT INPUT'' and acts from that frame. The readout''s MODE shows ''AUTOPILOT'' before the hold while engaged. The window title gains ''O autopilot''.', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:29:39Z', '2026-10-15T06:29:39Z', 160, NULL, 'rule', 160, '2026-10-15, task 160: 2026-10-15, task 160: plan 26: Autopilot: planner, orbit manoeuvres, execution and safety builds it in its piece 4; task 161 appends the rendezvous rows.

2026-10-15, task 204: 2026-10-15, task 204: built as decision 153: Autopilot preset menu as built: rows, states, keys and the clock; src/autopilot.lisp (autopilot-model, autopilot-key!, autopilot-held!, autopilot-menu-lines, autopilot-plan-summary), src/clock.lisp (barrier, budget split, the model''s notices), src/input.lisp (keys, gating, pilot input), src/overlay.lisp (''>'' glyph); docs/design.md ''Controls'', ''The autopilot''; docs/architecture.md; AGENTS.md.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (119, 'Rendezvous planning in milestone 3: Lambert, search, aim and stop points, checks, shooting, burns', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 5 (task 161). docs/design.md ''Rendezvous: search, phasing and the approach'' and decision 54: Autopilot rendezvous: phasing, lunar chain, hyperbolic targets, clear approaches give the families, grids, aim points and checks, but leave open the solver, how the clearance enters a Kepler search whose least-dv transfers pass through the target (decision 59: Rendezvous transfers pass the target clear), how a ship already 1 km from its target finds a transfer (skua-yard-apogee-approach: 0.4 periods of Highport are 9.5 h), the shooting''s parameters and tolerance, how a co-orbital aim point is defined, what is refused, the stop point for a loaded stack, and the menu rows. A universal-variable solver is short, robust and well documented (Vallado, Curtis give test cases); a time-shifted rails state keeps aim point (1) exactly on the target''s orbit under J2; filtering in Kepler keeps shots few, the shot check keeps the rule exact; milestone-4 merges are left as room, not stubs.', 'The Lambert solver is a universal-variable single-revolution solver (orbits.lisp, lambert-into!), prograde in the sense of the ship''s angular momentum, its plane from r1 x r2 or, within 0.5 deg of 180 deg, the ship''s orbit normal; candidates within 0.5 deg of 180 deg are skipped. A rendezvous plan is [:plane] [:phasing] :departure :arrival, then the terminal phase: a :plane burn first at the cheaper relative node (plan 26''s change-plane guess) when the planes differ by 0.01 deg or more, the search starting after its cut. The search runs in two-body Kepler with J2-secular rails for the target and the other objects: family A departures every 1/64 ship period over the limit, transfer times 0.4-1.5 target periods in 32 steps, plus, when the ship is within 50 km of the aim point, 0.02-0.4 target periods in 16 steps (the close grid, for a ship already beside its target); family B a tangential burn at one of 64 points of the ship''s next revolution into T_ph = T_t - dt/N or T_t + (T_t - dt)/N, N 1-32 while N T_ph fits the limit, then an A transfer departing within one T_ph of its end; in milestone 3 B is a burn of its own (merging into a capture, LOI or Hohmann burn is milestone 4''s). Cost: the impulsive sum of every burn; a candidate is kept only if its Kepler arc from its first burn to the aim point stays >= bound + 200 m from the target and every other object (decision 59), scanned at 1/256 of each coast and refined by golden section where under 10 km; the best three per family and N refined by golden section in departure and transfer time; least total dv wins, within 1 m/s the earlier arrival. Each Lambert solve and each Kepler clearance scan counts work units set from a measurement against a coast step (decision 116''s budget). Aim point (1) is the target''s own rails state shifted back in time by 1 km / |v_T| (so co-orbital by construction); (2) and (3) are fixed in the target''s terminal frame (its attitude with any spin about the fixture axis removed) 1 km from the stop point, radially out (in when the fixture axis points inward) or out of plane (the clearer side), with the velocity of that frame''s point. The stop point is the fixture''s pin plus the range (default 100 m, 100-1,000 m) along its outward axis, for the grapple cone''s tip; a place without a fixture (Skua Yard''s drop point) stops the stack''s CoM. A named fixture, else the place''s first free berth or towable fixture; an occupied or missing one refuses ''NO FREE FIXTURE''; a stop point under 100 m from the target''s CoM refuses ''RANGE TOO SHORT''. The aim point is the first candidate whose straight terminal segment (aim to stop point in the terminal frame) keeps >= 100 m from the target''s CoM and >= bound + 200 m from every other object (their positions relative to the target at the arrival and every 300 s for 1,800 s); none: ''NO CLEAR APPROACH''. Shooting: parameters the departure''s and arrival''s dv in LVLH (6; the phasing and plane burns fixed from their guesses), residuals the post-cut position and velocity against the aim point''s at the arrival burn''s cut (6), Newton as decision 117, tolerance 100 m and 0.1 m/s; the shot path from the first burn to the arrival burn''s start must keep >= bound + 200 m from the target and every other object (the shot copy''s promoted objects, the stack''s CoM to their CoM); a failing candidate is dropped and the next shot, up to 8, then ''NO CLEAR TRANSFER''. No :trim after :arrival: the terminal phase absorbs the arrival''s error; measurement and replans of decision 117 apply to :phasing and :departure. The plan''s propellant counts a terminal allowance of 20 m/s beside decision 117''s reserve. Engaging a rendezvous sets sim-target to its target. Limit: 48 h default, 12 h-10 days in 12 h steps. Menu rows after decision 118''s: ''RDV <PLACE> <h> H'' for the five places, then ''RDV TARGET <h> H'' offered with a target that is not a place; Left/Right change the limit.', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:36:38Z', '2026-10-15T06:36:38Z', 161, NULL, 'rule', 161, '2026-10-15, task 161: plan 27: Autopilot: rendezvous, phasing, clear approaches and station-keeping builds it in its pieces.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (120, 'Rendezvous terminal phase and station-keeping in the step', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 5 (task 161). docs/design.md ''The autopilot'' gives the terminal phase (RCS and low throttle along the relative-retrograde hold, closing at most range / 100 s, nulling below 0.02 m/s, +/-5 m at x1 and x4) but not where the law runs, its deadbands, which point is controlled, how a rotating stop point (Tern''s LVLH turns 0.065 deg/s, sweeping 0.12 m/s at 102 m) is followed, what warp is allowed, or when it gives up. w = -p / 100 s is the design''s speed rule as a velocity field: straight towards the stop point, so the flown path is the checked segment. The RCS gives 0.05 m/s^2 wet (0.8 mm/s per fine step), the 10 % throttle 0.28 m/s^2 for the larger braking; manual RCS is off above x4, so close work runs on fine steps. A law that acts only on fine steps keeps the step function the one function the prediction and shots run, without the law flying the copy at coarse steps. Station-keeping keeps the nose on the target, as the pilot''s :approach needs (decision 56: Flight-script actions, start states and the quest play-through).', 'After the :arrival burn''s cut the plan cursor goes :terminal, then :station; a law in controls.lisp (pure: relative state in, keys and throttle out) run by clock.lisp right after plan-step-controls!, on fine steps only (on a coarse step it releases the keys and the throttle, so the prediction''s copy coasts). Controlled point: the grapple cone''s tip for a fixture, the stack''s CoM for a point; stop point and its velocity from the target''s terminal frame (attitude without spin about the fixture axis; v_T + w x offset), written into the controls'' target scratch at the step''s start. With p the controlled point less the stop point and u their relative velocity: the wanted velocity w = -p / 100 s, its magnitude capped at 10 m/s in :terminal and 0.05 m/s in :station; the error e = u - w; RCS translation (full, not fine) per body axis on while |e| along it exceeds 0.01 m/s, off under 0.003 m/s; the engine at 10 % throttle while -e lies within 10 deg of +x and exceeds 0.3 m/s along it, off under 0.05 m/s. Attitude: hold :relative-retrograde in :terminal (frozen while slow, decision 114), hold :target in :station. :station is reached at |p| <= 1 m and |u| <= 0.02 m/s: notice ''ON STATION: <NAME>'', the sim''s checkpoint request, :autopilot-done true; it then holds the range within +/-5 m until disengaged. The clock lowers warp to x4 when :terminal begins (''WARP x4: TERMINAL PHASE'') and refuses higher levels in :terminal and :station (''WARP REFUSED: STATION-KEEPING, LIMIT x4''). Aborts beside decision 117''s: the target cleared by the step (removed or latched) ''TARGET LOST''; :terminal longer than 7,200 s of game time ''APPROACH TIMED OUT''. While :terminal or :station the clock''s prediction treats the ship as under thrust (one period, restarted each frame).', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:37:09Z', '2026-10-15T06:37:09Z', 161, NULL, 'rule', 161, '2026-10-15, task 161: plan 27: Autopilot: rendezvous, phasing, clear approaches and station-keeping builds it in its pieces.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (121, 'Skua Yard''s stop point while the drop point is occupied', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 5 (task 161). decision 54: Autopilot rendezvous: phasing, lunar chain, hyperbolic targets, clear approaches puts ''Rendezvous: Skua Yard'' at the drop point, empty after quest 6; but the milestone''s preset-skua-yard flies it from the start state, where Skua Yard 3 sits at the drop point, so the clearance rule would refuse the preset and milestone 3''s Done when could not be met. The yard''s lane ahead of Skua Yard 3 is empty up to Highport, 27 km ahead at apogee (docs/design.md ''Earth-orbit points of interest''), and a time shift keeps the point on the yard''s orbit under J2 like the yard''s own gaps.', '''Rendezvous: Skua Yard'' stops the stack''s CoM at the drop point (Skua Yard 3''s slot) when no object''s bound + 200 m reaches it; while one does (Skua Yard 3 itself until quest 6 tows it, or a rock released there), it stops at the yard''s front: the drop point''s rails shifted 1.043 s ahead in time (1 km at apogee, ~10 km at perigee), the yard''s own time-gap rule. Chosen at planning; a stop point taken stays for the plan.', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:37:09Z', '2026-10-15T06:37:09Z', 161, NULL, 'rule', 161, '2026-10-15, task 161: plan 27: Autopilot: rendezvous, phasing, clear approaches and station-keeping builds it in its pieces.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (122, 'Flight scripts in milestone 3: autopilot steps, the record, close work, start states', 'answered', '[]', 'task 162 (part 6 of plan 3: masterplan). docs/architecture.md ''Scripted runs'' and decision 56: Flight-script actions, start states and the quest play-through name the milestone-3 vocabulary but leave open how a script plans through decision 117''s frame-spread jobs without making live and headless differ, how burns are labelled when the terminal law switches the throttle many times, how clearance is measured cheaply, how :autopilot-done learns of an abort, the start states'' attitudes and velocities, and what the prediction costs in close work: measured 2026-10-15, a prediction restart is 1.2 ms, so refreshing it before each fine step of a ~1,800 s terminal phase would take ~140 s headless. Engaging at the barrier makes the engage time independent of how long planning takes; at x1 and x4 every step is the fine step, so a 10 s old prediction changes no state, only warp messages. Reading notice keys from the log keeps the sim''s structure as plans 24-26 build it.', '(:autopilot <preset> <goal> [:range m] [:limit s]) asks the clock''s autopilot model for the plan (autopilot-request!: the menu''s Return on that preset and goal) on a snapshot of the sim at the step''s t; headless the job runs to completion at once (decision 117), live over frames; the step lasts until the plan is shown and t >= snapshot t + *plan-barrier* (60 s), then engages it (the second Return, autopilot-confirm!), so headless and live engage at the same t and fly bit for bit alike; a refusal fails the script with its text. :limit is 12 h steps from 43,200 to 864,000 s, :range 100-1,000 m, else a read error. (:autopilot :off) is Backspace. The flight reads the clock''s SHIP log since its mark, whose entries keep their notice key: :autopilot-done holds once the plan engaged last completed (an orbit plan''s completion notice, a rendezvous''s ON STATION) and, for a rendezvous, while it keeps station; a wait on it fails at once on that plan''s abort, missed goal or disengage, with the text. While a plan is engaged the record''s burns come from the plan cursor, one :burn entry per planned burn with its label (start, cut, dv by the odometer) and one :terminal entry from the :arrival cut to ON STATION; throttle-based burns only while none is engaged. The engage''s t and odometer are the reference of :dv-below, :elapsed-below, :burn, :since-burn and :clearance-above; :clearance-above keeps per object the least CoM distance since the engage, on each step''s relative Hermite for promoted objects (plan 27''s shot-path function) and >= 100 km for objects on rails (promotion''s invariant), the latched load and removed objects skipped, and separately up to the :arrival burn''s start for :until :aim-point. :on-station is docs/story.md''s ''On station at F'' (tip within 150 m of the pin, <= 0.10 m/s relative at the pin); for a place without a fixture (Skua Yard) the stack''s CoM against its stop point by stop-point-into!''s rule at the current t. :target calls sim-set-target!; :grapple, :release, :transfer press G or B through grapple-key! and transfer-key! (refusal texts fail); :translate lowers warp to x1 and holds the key until the odometer has grown by the value (600 s limit). (:warp :coast) caps at x1 during :approach and :translate, x4 while the plan is :terminal or :station or a promoted object other than the load is within 1 km + its bound of the stack''s CoM and the ship is not berthed. run-flight refreshes the prediction before every step unless it would restart (the throttle on, translation keys held, a plan :terminal or :station): then once per 1 s of game time above x4 (decision 108) and once per 10 s at x1 and x4 (*flight-close-prediction-interval*), where every step is fine whatever the prediction says. Start states: one function stated-sim builds (:start :state ...) from new-game-sim with the sim''s t set to the date; dates (:periapsis|:apoapsis place [:before s] [:after UTC]) from the place''s rails, grid-ceiling; :circular in the polar plane with :u, or :lead (place deg) from the place''s rails argument of latitude; :near place :behind/:above/:at-slot at the place''s velocity; :on-axis puts the grapple tip on the axis at m from the pin, +x along the inward axis, +z along the object''s +z made perpendicular (else its +y), velocity the object''s at the ship''s CoM (v + w x offset), w 0; :berthed and :grappled use plan 24''s berth and capture poses with that roll rule, :grappled with :orbit puts the stack''s CoM on the orbit in the prograde hold''s attitude; :removed records the removal at the date; objects within 100 km promote on the first step. :consumables, :elements around another body and the presets :tli, :capture, :return are ''NOT BEFORE MILESTONE 4'', :quest, :ack, :offer, (:start :after/:file) ''NOT BEFORE MILESTONE 5''. (:screenshot ... :view :cockpit) is accepted and drawn from the outside view until the cockpit view exists (PLACEHOLDER(165)).', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:47:08Z', '2026-10-15T06:47:08Z', 162, NULL, 'rule', 162, '2026-10-15, task 162: 2026-10-15, task 162: plan 28: Flight scripts of milestone 3: vocabulary, start states and the headless preset runs builds it in its pieces 1-4.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (123, 'Milestone 3''s flight scripts run in scripts/test-slow, not scripts/test', 'answered', '[]', 'task 162 (part 6 of plan 3: masterplan) asks to measure the preset scripts'' game time against scripts/test''s ~15 s budget (docs/architecture.md ''Rules for tests''), already ~13 s used before plans 23-27 add theirs. From docs/design.md''s figures the scripts cover ~10.5 days of game time: Tern Station ~20 h (A after ~19 h of waiting), Derelict K-17 ~14 h, Fulmar Depot ~36 h, Highport and Skua Yard up to the 48 h limit plus the terminal phase, rendezvous-fulmar-phasing <= 48 h 47 min, rendezvous-skua-yard <= 26 h, skua-yard-apogee-approach <= ~10 h, the two approaches ~20-40 min each. Measured 2026-10-15 with today''s step: a 48 h coast under (:warp :coast) 0.17 s, 1,800 s of fine steps 0.53 s, a prediction restart 1.2 ms. So each script flies in ~0.2-1 s, but each rendezvous plan shoots Newton iterations over coasts of up to 48 h (~0.1 s per shot, tens of shots), 1-4 s a plan by estimate: ~30-100 s for the ten scripts twice, far over the budget.', 'The ten milestone-3 scripts (preset-tern-station, preset-fulmar-depot, preset-k-17, preset-highport, preset-skua-yard, approach-tern, approach-k-17, rendezvous-fulmar-phasing, rendezvous-skua-yard, skua-yard-apogee-approach), each flown twice and compared bit for bit, run in a second Parachute system luv-in-space/slow-tests (tests/slow-flights.lisp, package luv-in-space.slow-tests, depending on luv-in-space/tests for its helpers and running only its own package) through scripts/test-slow, which re-enters the dev shell like scripts/test (decision 64), prints a line per script (game time, steps, wall time, PASSED or FAILED) and exits 1 on a failure; nix flake check runs it as a second check. scripts/test keeps the reader and the per-action tests on short crafted runs (under ~1 s for this part) and the milestone-2 scripts. Every task that changes the simulation, the autopilot or flights.lisp runs scripts/test-slow before committing (AGENTS.md ''Commands''). Tests that plan 27 piece 6 marks for ''task 162''s slow command'' move there. Milestone 5''s play-through may join the system; decision 56''s scripts/play-through stays its own command.', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:47:17Z', '2026-10-15T06:47:17Z', 162, NULL, 'rule', 162, '2026-10-15, task 162: 2026-10-15, task 162: plan 28: Flight scripts of milestone 3: vocabulary, start states and the headless preset runs builds scripts/test-slow and the luv-in-space/slow-tests system in its piece 5, the presets in piece 6.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (124, 'Meshes of the places: models, scenery liveries, lights, far dots, the grapple''s claws, floodlight and range finder', 'answered', '[]', 'task 163 (plan for part 7 of plan 3: masterplan). docs/design.md ''Earth-orbit points of interest'', ''Other ships'', ''How they look'' and ''Rigid bodies'' give the places'' parts, shapes, masses and some lights, but not the fixture''s look, the scenery ships'' liveries, which lights each place carries and their blink, how far a place is still drawn, how the claws move, the floodlight''s beam and brightness, or how a latched load is posed for drawing. Baking scenery ships into the place keeps one model per object definition, as the shapes already include them; meshes inside the shapes keep what is seen and what collides the same; a dim area-scaled dot keeps a sunlit place visible from tens of km as real stations are, without a mesh; a floodlight sized for the deep exposure lights Highport''s shadowed plant fixture as the design says (''lit by their own floodlight''); posing attached bodies from the ship''s pose avoids interpolation jitter at warp.', 'meshes.lisp builds one model per object definition (decision 110: Objects in the simulation: places.lisp''s load position, the object struct and promotion checks) in the object''s body axes about its CoM (FRD, single floats), with decision 80''s vertex, instance and material scheme; scenery ships (AURORA 11 at Tern''s node, TANKER 2 at Fulmar Depot''s fixture B, STUBBORN and THISTLE at Highport''s arms, GRINDSTONE''s Mule cab) are other models baked into the place''s model under a transform (bake-model!), the tanker from the Mule''s builder generalised to truss length, bays, tank pairs, cab or nose fixture (the Mule''s own output unchanged: 7,302 hull vertices). Every non-thin vertex lies within the object''s shapes grown by 0.05 m; wings, booms and antennas are thin parts, as the Mule''s. Standard fixture: a disc plate Ø 1.0 x 0.05 m whose outer face centre is the pin, normal the outward axis; a pin Ø 0.08 x 0.25 outward; three guides 0.06 x 0.20 x 0.30 at 120 deg on radius 0.42 leaning 30 deg out; a stand-off tube Ø 0.25 back to the surface; material ''fixture'' (aluminium 0.85, specular 0.5, exponent 32) with a black cross target (two 0.05 m bars) from the plate''s centre coordinates, bright on K-17''s old stage. Free berth fixtures carry four steady white lamps (radius 0.04 m, radiance 5) at radius 0.55 m, 45 deg off the body axes; occupied ones are dark. Liveries: Cairn Mules STUBBORN (M-04) and THISTLE (M-05) as PATIENCE with their registrations; GRINDSTONE''s cab shows the cairn only (its registration worn away); Boreal Orbital: Tern''s modules, node and AURORA 11 white 0.80 with a 0.6 m pale green band (linear 0.45 0.70 0.50) round each module''s middle and the capsule''s service section, ''TERN'' stencilled on the middle module''s sides; POD 14 white with ''POD 14'' stencilled on both long sides; Fulmar Depot and Highport foil tanks, white plant and spine, no lettering; K-17 ''scorched'' grey 0.25 with darker streaks of value noise and ''peeling foil'' (silver foil, normal noise amplitude 0.6, patches where the noise exceeds 0.55 showing scorched steel); rocks ''rock'' 0.07 0.065 0.06 under a procedural ''net'' (cords 0.04 m wide every 0.45 m along latitude and longitude about +x, albedo 0.55 0.50 0.40); the capsule''s frustum white, its service section white with the band. The decal texture becomes an atlas of 128 x 512 rows (M-07, M-04, M-05, TERN, POD 14), the stencil glyphs gaining 1 4 5 D E N O P R T and space; a model picks its row. Lights (decision 100''s billboards, radiances and blink): Tern red at the -y wing tip (-2.92, -39.0, 0) and green at +y, a strobe on the node''s zenith side; Highport red and green at the wing tips (0.02, -/+70.0, -4.84) and strobes at both truss ends'' -z faces; Fulmar Depot one strobe on the aft spine end''s -z face; the berthed Mules and GRINDSTONE their Mule navigation lights and strobe, TANKER 2 a strobe; K-17, the pod and the rocks dark. Each object''s strobes run on the lights'' clock offset by 0.37 s x its index in *objects*, so the places do not blink together. Far objects: below 2 px of projected bound the mesh is not drawn; the object is a glow of area S/4 of its shapes (rocks pi r^2), radiance 0.5 x the Sun term x (1 + cos phase)/2 x the sun-fraction at the object, so the glow rule''s dimming by area makes it a dot that fades as 1/d^2; its lights stay. Claws: each hinged at its base (x 16.0, radius 0.85) about the tangent; stowed 0 deg (as built), armed 30 deg out, closing from 30 to -8 deg linearly over the sim''s 2 s closing time, latched -8 deg; the shown angle follows closing and latched exactly by render time and eases at 90 deg per real second between stowed and armed, snapping in captures. Floodlight: lamp at (15.45, 0, -0.32) on the grapple cylinder''s top, aimed along +x, full within 15 deg and fading to 0 at 25 deg (smoothstep of the cosine), radiance on a white face-on surface *floodlight-intensity* 4 / max(d, 2 m)^2 (0.04 at 10 m, 0.64 at the deep exposure 16), no shadows; on while armed, closing or latched, lighting the hull and every object; its lamp a white glow radius 0.06 m, radiance 10. Range finder: a red (1 0 0) aperture glow radius 0.02 m, radiance 10, at (15.45, 0, 0.32), and its spot, a red glow radius 0.03 m, radiance 10, where the ray from the tip (16.6, 0, 0) along +x first meets a promoted object''s shape within 500 m (CPU, pulled 0.05 m towards the ship); while armed or closing. A latched load and the station while berthed are drawn from the ship''s render pose and the grapple''s relative pose (decision 112), never jittering against the Mule; other promoted objects by the Hermite between the clock''s previous object states and the current, objects on rails exactly at the render time. The exposure rule counts each object in view like the ship (a sphere of its bound, coverage x its sun-fraction at least 0.005), and the latched load''s bound joins the stack''s for the outside view''s least distance.', NULL, '2026-10-15T06:55:26Z', '2026-10-15T06:55:26Z', 163, NULL, 'rule', 163, '2026-10-15, task 163: 2026-10-15, task 163: plan 29: Meshes of the places, other ships and the grapple builds it in meshes.lisp, render.lisp, post.lisp, camera.lisp, shaders.lisp and clock.lisp (the previous objects for drawing).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (125, 'Map in milestone 3: the trajectory line, line styles, plan display, markers, labels, click and focus', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 8 (task 164). docs/design.md ''Views'' and ''The autopilot'' name the lines (predicted trajectory, target''s orbit magenta, dashed after a planned burn, the goal orbit thin, a kept plan grey), the markers (closest approach, relative nodes, burns with their dv, targets at every zoom, places below 6 px) and the clicks, but not how a prediction of up to 20,000 nodes becomes a ribbon within decision 84''s 8,192 segments and 0.25 px, how dashes stay continuous, what a plan looks like before engaging (the clock''s prediction only flies sim-plan, decision 115) or past the prediction''s two-period horizon (a 48 h phasing plan), nor the new markers'' shapes, labels and order. Merging by the conic''s own sagitta rule keeps decision 84''s error bound and costs O(n) per frame; screen-pixel dash phases stay the same at every zoom; the osculating AP/PE agree with the readout; the target always drawn mirrors the ship''s rule; every marker kind keeps its own shape or label so colour is never alone (docs/design.md ''Readability at 1280 x 800''); markers >= 8 px except decision 84''s 7 px body dot. SDL''s own click count gives the platform''s double-click interval.', 'ribbons.lisp draws the ship''s line in the map from the clock''s prediction (decision 116), not the osculating orbit: its first point the render state''s r at t_r, then along the stored interval''s Hermite to each stored node after t_r up to the horizon or the impact; node intervals merged in aligned power-of-two runs (at most 64 nodes) while the run''s sagitta (chord^2 / 8 rho, rho = |v|^3 / |v x a| with point-mass gravity at its first node) seen from the eye stays within *ribbon-max-error-px* 0.25 px and the run holds no break (a dash start, a crash-radius crossing, a burn''s start or cut), single intervals split into 2^k pieces (k <= 8) on the Hermite by the same rule, k lowered farthest first and then the merge tolerance doubled until the pieces fit *ribbon-max-segments* 8,192; red below the crash radius as in decision 84; the impact marker at the prediction''s impact point; with fewer than two stored nodes after t_r the milestone-2 osculating orbit is drawn instead. AP and PE stay the osculating ones of decision 84, as the readout''s (decision 85). A ribbon instance gains a style vec4 (16 floats): the dash phase in screen pixels at both ends (negative: solid), the width in px, a radiance factor; dashes are 8 px on, 6 px off, the phase summed along the ribbon from the CPU-projected endpoints so it runs on across segments; every map line goes into one instance buffer (*map-lines-capacity* 32,768 segments) and one draw. Lines: the ship cyan #40D0FF 2 px; the target''s osculating orbit about Earth from the render state''s target r, v magenta #E040E0 2 px solid (red below the crash radius); the goal orbit (burn-plan''s) cyan 1 px solid (''thin''); a kept plan grey #8A8A80. The plan shown: before engaging, the coast prediction solid to its horizon plus the shown plan''s final shot (kept in the autopilot model) dashed from its first burn''s start; engaged, the prediction (it flies the plan) solid until the start of the first burn not yet started, dashed after, and past the prediction''s end the shot dashed to its end; after a disengage or abort the kept plan''s remaining burns (start > t_r) and its shot from the first of them grey dashed, until it is dropped or replaced. Burn markers: a plus of 12 px at the burn''s centre time ((start + the shot''s post-cut t) / 2) on the drawn path, labelled ''BURN <n> <dv> M/S'' (n its 1-based index in the plan; dv whole at 100 m/s and above, one decimal from 1, two below), cyan, grey when kept. Target: a magenta box (square outline) of 12 px labelled with its display name, at every zoom and even behind Earth like the ship. The other target candidates (places, Skua Yard as its drop point, towable loads; not the rock mover) whose bound projects below 6 px: a grey #8A8A80 box of 8 px labelled with their name, hidden behind Earth or the Moon. Closest approach (prediction-closest-approach): a cyan triangle of 10 px at the ship''s point labelled ''CA <d>'' (whole metres below 1 km ''CA 340 M'', one decimal below 100 km ''CA 12.3 KM'', whole km above) and a magenta triangle at the target''s point, unlabelled. Relative nodes: the next ascending and descending ones as magenta diamonds of 9 px, ''AN <angle>'' / ''DN <angle>'' with the angle between the ship''s and the target''s orbit planes at the node (two decimals below 1 deg, one below 10, whole above). Rendezvous aim point: a magenta ring of 9 px ''AIM'', at the target''s position at t_r plus the plan''s aim offset turned by the terminal frame at t_r, and the terminal path aim -> stop point as a cyan dashed segment, both drawn while the plan''s arrival is ahead. Label order (decision 103 extended): Earth, Sun, Moon, ship, target, impact, burns in order, CA, AIM, PE, AP, AN, DN, then the other candidates in target-candidates order; markers capacity 48. Clicks in the map: a left press picks the clickable marker (the target and the candidates; the ship and Earth for focus only) whose centre is nearest within *map-pick-px* 10 px; one click on a candidate sets it as the target (sim-set-target!; gated as ] [ \); a press with clicks >= 2 (SDL''s double click, canvas-pointer-event-clicks) also focuses it: ship :ship, Earth :earth, a candidate :target; Moon and Sun ignored until milestone 4. Focus :target: the anchor is the render state''s target r at t_r, least distance max(50 m, 2 x its bound); Tab cycles :ship -> :earth -> :target -> :ship (the target only when one is set); when the target is cleared the focus becomes :ship keeping angles and distance (clamped). Closest approach, nodes and the trajectory come from the clock''s prediction only, never from a shot. Captures in the map view run the prediction to completion first. All numbers defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-15T07:04:12Z', '2026-10-15T07:04:12Z', 164, NULL, 'rule', 164, '2026-10-15, task 164: 2026-10-15, task 164: plan 30: The map of milestone 3 builds it in its pieces 1-6.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (126, 'Cockpit structure and view in milestone 3: frames, draw order, Slug text, head look, exposure, cab light, portholes, bezels, tape labels', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 9 (task 165). docs/design.md ''Views'', ''The DIY look'', ''Layout'' and ''Readability'' and decision 30: Views and cameras and decision 36: Cockpit instruments: Slug screens, dot-matrix strip, gauges and lamps; Ghostty out give the eye point, spans, field of view and the look, but not the frame the cockpit is built in, how the eye-inside cab exterior is kept out of the view, the text''s fonts and atlas, the head look''s signs and wheel, V''s gating, the cockpit''s exposure, the strips'' level, or the bezels and label texts; and two rows disagree with the built hull (the portholes'' position, the strips 8 mm outside the lining). Body axes relative to the eye keep the cockpit exact in single floats with no double conversion; one atlas for both fonts makes all cockpit text one draw; a fixed exposure keeps a room-lit cab and the window view readable together, as the map''s fixed exposure does; the portholes follow decision 80: The Mule''s meshes in numbers: geometry, draws, materials and lights because the hull is built.', 'Frames: the cockpit''s geometry is built in ship body axes (FRD) relative to the eye point (14.1, 0, -3.6) m from R (*cockpit-eye*, camera.lisp), single floats (all within 3.6 m); its draws use a cab block whose clip rows are the head camera in body axes, the world camera''s projection rotated by the interpolated q, so no world position reaches a cockpit shader and both agree in depth (decision 4, decision 5). The world camera in :cockpit sits at r + q (e - c), c the CoM encode-ship uses, oriented q * head. The cockpit is drawn only in :cockpit; the Mule''s cab exterior (parts cab, window, portholes, hatch, decals) is baked last in the hull and skipped in :cockpit. Scene-pass order in :cockpit: stars, impostors, the Mule without its cab, objects, glows, the cab''s opaque meshes and instances, cockpit text, then the window and porthole glass last. Text (text.lisp, adapted from Luvcraft''s text.lisp and its :slug-world-text vertex shader, attributed): fonts copied from luv''s fonts/ to assets/fonts/: MonaspaceNeon-Regular.ttf (screens), MonaspaceNeon-Bold.ttf (the name plate, emphasis), IosevkaAile-Bold.ttf (tape labels, engravings), with LICENSE-Monaspace.txt and LICENSE-Iosevka.md; one Slug atlas for both fonts over a fixed repertoire (printable ASCII and the design''s extra characters), made once per renderer, so all cockpit text is one instance buffer, one bind group, one draw; grid runs (monospace cells, glyph ids from the cmap, no shaping, consing nothing) for screens and shaped runs (HarfBuzz, made once) for labels; an instance is Luvcraft''s 24 floats plus a vec3 (kind, factor, spare): kind 0 emissive, ink x factor / exposure; kind 1 lit, ink albedo x the cab''s irradiance at the glyph''s origin; reversed-Z compare :greater, no depth write, premultiplied alpha, runs 1 mm in front of their plane; *text-max-glyphs* 8,192. Head look: yaw positive right within +-150 deg, pitch positive down (the layout table''s elevation sign) within -60 (up) to +80 deg (down); right-drag 0.25 deg per pixel, mouse right turns the head right, mouse up raises it; wheel: tan(fov/2) x 1.2 per notch (up narrower), fov within 20-90 deg, eased in log over tau 0.15 s of real time, snapped in captures; Home: yaw 0, pitch 16 deg, fov 70 deg; the cockpit-view is game state, kept across a restart. V toggles :outside <-> :cockpit, acting while paused, at every warp and while the autopilot flies; ignored in the map and while lost; M from the cockpit returns to it; a loss still shows :outside (decision 86); a restart sets *default-view*, :outside until plan 3''s part 10 draws the instruments (that part flips it to :cockpit); until then the cockpit view also draws the minimal readout (PLACEHOLDER(166)). Exposure in the cockpit fixed at *exposure-cockpit* 4.0, snapped on entering and leaving, as the map''s (decision 83). Cab light: two warm-white LED strips (1.0 0.85 0.65) along x from 10.7 to 14.8 at (dy +-0.40, dz -1.21) (the docs'' dz -1.25 lies 8 mm outside the 1.40 m lining), each as 8 point samples, inverse square, Lambert; level set so a white 0.8 surface at the button block''s centre has radiance 0.04 on decision 76''s scale; strip faces emissive 0.5; *cab-ambient* 0.002; no shadows from cockpit parts; no earthlight (cut). Portholes: the outside mesh''s (decision 80): centred x 14.1 on the wall 37 deg below the y axis; their inside opening centre (0, +-1.118, +0.943) from the eye, direction +-90 deg, 40.1 deg down; this replaces the layout table''s (-0.30, +-1.07, +1.00), 42 deg, which disagreed with the built hull. Interior hatch matches the exterior: 0.8 (x) x 1.2 m around the +y wall at x 11.4, corners r 0.2, a handwheel 0.4. Openings are cut by discard in the lining and front-wall shaders (CPU twin cab-opening-p). Bezels: ORBIT grey ruggedised (border 25 mm, depth 50 mm, four bolts) under a cardboard hood; ATTITUDE black plastic (15, 40); TARGET green military surplus (30, 60) under a sheet-metal hood; AUTOPILOT beige office monitor (35, chin 50, depth 80); SHIP a bare panel with taped edges (5 mm lip, 30 mm tape); COMMS black plastic (20, 60); the dot-matrix strip a bare green board in an aluminium channel; screen faces albedo 0.02. Tape labels (Iosevka Aile Bold, capitals, white marker 0.85 on grey tape 0.12, rotated by a fixed pseudo-random angle within +-2 deg per label, ragged ends by discard): ORBIT ''ORBIT''; ATTITUDE ''ATT [WASD QE]''; TARGET ''TGT NEXT ] PREV [ CLR \'' (keys that are brackets go unbracketed); AUTOPILOT ''AUTOPILOT [O] [RET]''; SHIP ''SHIP  XFER [B]''; COMMS ''COMMS  ACK [Y]''; dot-matrix strip ''WARP [. , /] PAUSE [P]''; button block ''COMPUTER  DAMP [T]''; throttle quadrant ''THR [SHFT/CTRL]'' and ''[Z] MAX [X] CUT''; hull gauge ''HULL TEMP''; caution lamps ''ACK [Y]''; status lamps ''STATUS''; the window frame''s lower bar ''VIEW [V]  MAP [M]  HOME [HOME]''; each fits its strip at the readability rule''s cap height, else it is shortened and docs/design.md says so; space under the TARGET and SHIP screens and beside the hull gauge stays free for milestone 5''s tape notes. Name plate: steel 0.40 x 0.07 m centred (0.89, 0, -0.43), ''M-07  PATIENCE'' in Monaspace Neon Bold, black paint, cap 30 mm, lit.', NULL, '2026-10-15T07:14:22Z', '2026-10-15T07:14:22Z', 165, NULL, 'rule', 165, '2026-10-15, task 165: 2026-10-15, task 165: plan 31: Text and the cockpit''s structure and view builds it in its six pieces; docs/design.md ''Layout'' (the portholes'' row) and ''The DIY look'' (the strips'' height, the interior hatch) are corrected by its pieces 3 and 4.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (127, 'Cockpit screens'' contents in milestone 3: lines, modes, plots, colours and updates', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 10 (task 166). docs/design.md ''Instruments and how each is drawn'' gives each instrument''s place and sample text but not the colours'' radiance, what each screen shows in each state, the lines the table leaves out (TARGET''s hint, COMMS''s job card in milestone 3, SHIP lines 6-8), how the ATTITUDE plot fills the body''s disc, or how the AUTOPILOT screen shares the orbit plot, the menu and the plan on 22 x 8; and ''MASS 35.5 t  ACC 2.87 m/s²'' is 26 characters on a 22-column grid. The inverse tone map makes every screen colour the design''s hex, which the contrast rules are written against; the per-pixel plot fills a disc that may wrap past the rim, which a CPU outline cannot; the AUTOPILOT modes follow decision 118''s states.', 'Colours: every screen, lamp and LED is emissive with its ink the inverse of the tone map at the cockpit''s fixed exposure (emissive-for-srgb, post.lisp, CPU twin of a shader function), so the tone-mapped pixel is the design''s sRGB colour; the screen faces add a backlight that maps to #0A0C0C; text paper white #E8E4D8, labels and units grey #8A8A80, green #40D040, amber #FFB000 with ''!'', red #FF3030 inverted and flashing at 2 Hz, cyan #40D0FF own ship, magenta #E040E0 target. Updates: text lines are pure functions (screen-lines) of the render state, the clock, its prediction, the autopilot model and the real time, rebuilt at 10 Hz of real time or at once when a mode signature changes (view, hold kind and settled, fine, damping, grapple state, target, the model''s state and selection, warp, pause, berth, log length); the ATTITUDE plot, its markers, the rate bars, the strip, lamps, gauges and lever every frame, consing nothing. A value too long for its field is cut with ''…''. ORBIT (24 x 8): 1 ''EARTH  SOI --:--'' (SOI dark, PLACEHOLDER(4)); 2 ''ALT  400.00 km''; 3 ''SPD 7.669 km/s'' (m/s below 1,000); 4 ''VS  +0.0 m/s''; 5 ''AP 400.0 km T-45:12'' and 6 ''PE 400.0 km T-91:00'' (times from the prediction''s apsis events, as H:MM:SS above an hour, ''AP ESCAPE'' on a hyperbola, a periapsis below the crash radius red inverted); 7 ''INC 90.00° ECC 0.0001''; 8 ''PER 92.6 min'' (h above 10 h). ATTITUDE (19 x 8): rows 1-7 left: the sky plot, a disc of 0.150 m on the face''s left, azimuthal equidistant about +x with -z up, the rim the tail; drawn by a :cab-attitude fragment shader that inverts the projection per pixel (the dominant body''s disc of angular radius asin(R/r) about the nadir filled dark blue-grey #24303A, 10-degree rings grey at 30 %, the rim grey), markers as glyphs of the text atlas at cap >= 0.0175 d: prograde ○ with a tick towards the velocity''s projected motion, retrograde ⊗, normal ▲, anti-normal ▼, radial out ■, radial in □, the target ◆ magenta, the hold''s direction a cyan cross of 2 px lines; right of the plot three vertical bars P Y R, full scale +-5°/s, green within 0.01 rad/s, amber beyond; row 8 the mode (''HOLD PRO  SETTLED'', ''SLEWING'', ''DAMP'', ''DIRECT'', ''+FINE'', ''AUTOPILOT'' first while engaged). TARGET (24 x 8), grapple stowed: 1 the name magenta; 2 ''RNG 12.345 km'' (m below 1 km, ''RNG 340.2 m''); 3 ''CLS +10.2 m/s'' (closing positive); 4 ''F +1.2 R -0.3 D +0.0'' (m/s, the relative velocity in body axes); 5 ''CA T-03:12 0.84 km'' (none: ''CA --''); 6 the autopilot''s rendezvous phase when engaged on this target (''TERMINAL'', ''ON STATION 100 m''); 8 ''TGT ] NEXT [ PREV'' grey; no target: 1 ''NO TARGET'', 3 ''PICK ] [  OR MAP CLICK'' grey. Grapple armed: the camera image over the whole face, rows 1-3 and 8 on opaque #0A0C0C bands: 1 ''RNG 12.4 m CLS 0.12 m/s'', 2 ''OFF Y+0.05 Z-0.10'', 3 ''ANG 2.1° ROT 0.1°/s'', each value green inside its tolerance (grapple-reading''s flags), amber with ''!'' outside; 8 ''ARMED'', ''NO FIXTURE IN 500 m'', ''CLOSING'', ''LATCHED <NAME>'' green, ''BERTHED <NAME>'' green. AUTOPILOT (22 x 8), by the model''s state: closed or off: the orbit plot over rows 1-7 (the plane seen from its normal side, scaled so the apoapsis or the farthest predicted point fills 90 % of the plot''s half-height; the body''s disc grey; the predicted trajectory cyan 2 px; Ap and Pe diamonds; the ship''s ring; the target''s orbit projected into the plane magenta; burns as cyan ticks with the path after the first dashed), row 8 ''PRESETS [O]'' grey, or the kept plan in grey after a disengage; menu or refused: rows 1-7 a window of autopilot-menu-lines keeping the selection visible, the selection inverted (paper white block, dark text), row 8 ''PLAN [RET]  CLOSE [O]'' or the refusal amber ''! BELOW 150 KM''; planning, shown, engaged: rows 1-4 the plan (''PLANNING…'' alone while planning; else ''BURN 1/2 T-12:30'', ''DV 56.2 m/s  20 s'', ''TOTAL 112 m/s 905 kg'', ''LEFT 27.1 t  ARR 02:14'', from autopilot-plan-summary), rows 5-7 the orbit plot at a third of the height, row 8 ''ENGAGE [RET]  DROP [BKSP]'' (shown) or ''ENGAGED  OFF [BKSP]'' green; an abort''s text red inverted on row 8 until acknowledged. SHIP (22 x 8): 1 ''PROP 27,547 kg 98 %'' (amber ''!'' when PROP is lit); 2 ''DV 6.61 km/s''; 3 ''MASS 35.5 t 2.87 m/s²'' (the full-throttle acceleration; ACC dropped to fit 22 columns); 4 ''THR 100 %  ENG ON'', berthed ''XFER 10 kg/s'' or ''XFER OFF [B]'' (the engine is locked there); 5 ''LOAD 50.0 t <NAME>'' or ''BERTHED <PLACE>'' or empty; 6 ''LIFE --- d'', 7 ''SUN --- AU'', 8 ''HULL --- K'' grey (PLACEHOLDER(4)). COMMS (26 x 11): rows 1-3 the job card: ''JOB'' grey and ''NONE'' (PLACEHOLDER(5)); row 4 a rule of ''-''; rows 5-11 the log, clock-log''s last 200 entries as ''HH:MM TEXT'' in game UTC, wrapped at 26 with continuation lines indented 6, amber, typed out at 60 characters per second of real time in arrival order with a block cursor after the last typed character; newest at the bottom; the wheel scrolls 3 lines per notch, a scroll position away from the bottom stays put while new lines arrive and the bottom follows them.', NULL, '2026-10-15T07:20:42Z', '2026-10-15T07:20:42Z', 166, NULL, 'rule', 166, '2026-10-15, task 166: 2026-10-15, task 166: plan 32: Cockpit instruments, buttons, the grapple camera and messages builds it in its pieces 1, 2, 4, 5 and 7; docs/design.md ''Instruments and how each is drawn'' (SHIP line 3 shortened to ''MASS 35.5 t 2.87 m/s²'') to state it as built.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (128, 'Cockpit devices, buttons, picking, the grapple camera and messages in milestone 3', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 10 (task 166). docs/design.md ''Instruments and how each is drawn'', ''Interaction'' and ''Lamps in detail'' name the devices and their colours but leave open the alarms that light MSTR, what AUTO, RCS and WARP show in each state, how a button is lit or engraved (a 4-character name and its key do not both fit a 36 mm lens at the readability rule''s cap), the gauges'' scales, the strip''s cell and marquee timing, the grapple camera''s field of view axis, exposure, noise and post pass, and how the cockpit reads messages. The choices keep decision 36, decision 37, decision 113 and decision 118, reuse the clock''s message keys, the SHIP log of decision 112 and the render targets of decision 94, and show the keys on the strip so the cockpit still teaches the key map.', 'Lamps (lens quads in the lamp columns, engraved Iosevka Aile Bold at cap >= 0.0175 d): unlit a dark lens of its colour at albedo 0.04 lit by the cab; lit emissive at the colour (emissive-for-srgb); flashing in real time, on in the first half of each period: red 2 Hz, amber 1 Hz. MSTR: an onset of a red alarm (PERI, an autopilot abort) makes it flash red, of an amber one (PROP) light amber steady, until acknowledged by Y or a click on it (the same :acknowledge command, which also sets clock-log-read); the other lamps show their condition regardless. PERI red steady while the osculating periapsis is below the crash radius or the prediction holds an impact. PROP amber below 5 % of 28,000 kg or below the shown or engaged plan''s need. TEMP and LIFE dark (PLACEHOLDER(4)). WARP white steady above x1, amber flashing for 3 s of real time after a :refused or :lowered message. RCS amber while warp is above x4 or |w| > 0.01 rad/s with no hold. AUTO white while planning or a plan is shown, green engaged, red flashing after an abort until acknowledged or the next engage. GRPL white armed, green closing, latched or berthed, amber flashing 3 s after a ''NO CAPTURE'' or over-rating message. MSG dark (radio is milestone 5''s, PLACEHOLDER(5)). ENG on the throttle quadrant white while the engine fires. Lamp state is a pure function lamp-states of the render state, the clock (its message stamps by key: clock-message-stamp) and the autopilot model. Buttons: 18 lenses 0.036 m in three rows of six at 0.042 m pitch, each engraved with its name only (at most 4 characters: PRO, RETR, NML, ANML, RAD+, RAD-; TGT, ATGT, RPRO, RRET, INRT, DAMP; FINE, GRPL, XFER, AP, WRP-, WRP+; RETRO is RETR to fit, the keys are not engraved since two lines do not fit a 36 mm lens at the readability rule''s cap); lit while their function is on: the active hold''s button amber slewing and green settled, DAMP green with rate damping, FINE green, GRPL white armed and green latched, XFER green transferring, AP white with a plan shown and green engaged, WRP+ white above x1, WRP- never; every press lights its lens white for 0.15 s of real time. A click runs the same command as the key through input.lisp''s gating (a refused one gives the key''s message); AP is Return with a plan shown, Backspace engaged, else O. The key of the hovered button shows on the dot-matrix strip''s line 3 as ''<NAME> [<KEY>]'' while no message runs there, so the cockpit still teaches the key map. Picking: the pointer''s ray from the eye through the cockpit camera (double, body axes from the eye) against every clickable rectangle (18 buttons, MSTR, the AUTOPILOT menu rows while the menu shows, the COMMS face); the nearest hit within the rectangle wins; the hovered button or lamp gets a 2 px paper-white outline (a :cab-lines rectangle 2 mm outside its lens); ignored while the right button is held, in other views, while lost and while a flight runs; the wheel over the COMMS face scrolls its log instead of zooming. Gauges: the PROP gauge Ø 0.10 m on the throttle quadrant, 0-100 % over 270 degrees, a red arc below 5 %; the hull gauge Ø 0.12 m, 150-500 K over 270 degrees with the arcs of docs/design.md drawn, its needle at the 150 K stop (PLACEHOLDER(4)); needles as thin boxes (2 mm wide, 0.42 x the radius long, paper white), each following its value by a first-order lag of 0.3 s in real time, snapped in captures. The throttle lever: a box 0.012 x 0.012 x 0.10 m on a pivot, 0-60 degrees for 0-100 %, -10 degrees at off, no lag. The dot-matrix strip: 19 x 3 cells of 5 x 7 dots, dot pitch 2.45 mm, cell 6 x 9 dots, round dots Ø 1.9 mm as instanced quads, lit amber #FFB000 emissive, unlit at 6 % of it; line 1 ''YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS'' UTC; line 2 ''WARP x1000 ACT x490'' (ACT shown below 90 % as the readout''s, 3 significant digits with K or M; ''ACT '' dropped when the line would exceed 19), ''PAUSED''; line 3 the messages: every new clock-log entry queued (at most 8, oldest dropped), each shown once: up to 19 characters static for 3 s of real time, longer ones scrolled once at 12 characters per second from the right edge until their end leaves the left; then the next; idle the hovered button''s key, else blank. The grapple camera: while the grapple is armed, closing or latched, a scene pass before the frame''s own into the cached render targets :grapple-hdr (rgba16-float) and :grapple-depth, 480 x 320, from the tip (16.6, 0, 0) m from R (the camera at r + q (tip - c)), oriented q, vertical field of view 50 degrees, near 0.1 m, the scene without the cockpit (encode-frame :interior nil) without bloom; its exposure exposure-target for that camera, snapped; then :grapple-post into :grapple-image (rgba8-unorm): the tone-mapped luminance in grey plus noise uniform in +-4/255 from a hash of the pixel and the frame counter; the TARGET face samples it through :cab-screen-image (inverse tone map at the cockpit''s exposure, so the face shows the image''s values); the crosshair (2 px lines through the centre, gaps of 10 % at the middle, paper white) and the pin''s mark (◆ magenta, the pin projected through the grapple camera) drawn over it; off when stowed. Messages: the COMMS log and the strip both read clock-log; the cockpit keeps its own read cursor (entries seen) and each entry''s arrival real time for typing out; nothing of it is in the sim.', NULL, '2026-10-15T07:21:07Z', '2026-10-15T07:21:07Z', 166, NULL, 'rule', 166, '2026-10-15, task 166: 2026-10-15, task 166: plan 32: Cockpit instruments, buttons, the grapple camera and messages builds it in its pieces 2, 3, 6 and 7; docs/design.md ''Lamps in detail'', ''Interaction'' (RETRO engraved RETR, keys on the strip) and ''Instruments and how each is drawn'' (grapple camera) to state it as built.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (129, 'Overlay of the outside and map views in milestone 3: the message line and the autopilot panel', 'answered', '[]', 'plan 3: masterplan part 11 (task 167). docs/design.md ''Interaction'' and ''The autopilot'' say only that the outside and map views gain ''the latest message line and the autopilot panel in the overlay''; where they sit, what they show per autopilot state, how long a message stays and how it avoids repeating the readout''s live messages are open. The readout''s format and box are reused so contrast and readability stay exact (decision 85, decision 105); top right keeps the panel clear of the readout at 1280 x 800 (the readout''s widest line is ~40 cells, 496 px); the bottom centre keeps the message line clear of the LOST panel (top at 60 % of the height); severity by notice key keeps ''never colour alone'' (decision 85''s ''!'' and inversion); the plan lines are shared with the AUTOPILOT screen (decision 127) so the two never disagree; the message line persists after its 3 s in the readout so a message missed during warp can still be read.', 'Both are lines in the readout''s format (runs of text, sRGB colour, :plain or :inverted, flash phase; decision 85), pure functions drawn by readout-vertices! on its opaque #0A0C0C box with 12 x 22 px cells, only in :outside and :map (the cockpit has its strip, COMMS and AUTOPILOT screens), switched off with the readout by a capture''s :readout nil. Message line: the newest entry of clock-log (the SHIP log of decision 112), one line, its box centred horizontally with its bottom 16 px (*readout-margin-px*) above the frame''s bottom edge; text ''HH:MM:SS '' in game UTC grey #8A8A80, then the entry''s text cut at 60 characters (*message-line-chars*) with ''…''; the entry''s colour by its notice key through *message-severity* (a defparameter alist, default :info): :info paper white #E8E4D8, :caution amber #FFB000 with ''! '' before the text (warp refusals and drop-outs, NO CAPTURE and over-rating, release refusals, ENGINE LOCKED, PLAN STALE, NO TARGET and the planner''s refusals), :danger red inverted flashing at 2 Hz (autopilot aborts, AUTOPILOT: GOAL MISSED) while the entry is not yet acknowledged (clock-log-read below it; Y), then red inverted steady; 30 s of real time after its arrival (*message-line-fresh* 30) an :info or :caution entry turns grey and stays until a newer entry arrives; no entry, no line. Arrival is tracked on the render side by a preallocated message-line struct (the newest entry seen, eq, and the real time it was first seen), updated once per frame by main.lisp''s draw and reset by game-restart!; captures show the newest entry fresh (:message-age, default 0). To avoid showing a message twice, the readout''s live messages (decision 85''s rows) leave out the newest clock-messages entry while it is the message line''s entry; nothing else of decision 85 changes. Milestone 5 adds RADIO''s first line and the JOB card''s one-line form above the message line (decision 37). Autopilot panel: shown while clock-autopilot''s state is not :closed; its box top right, 16 px from the top and right edges, at least 30 cells wide (*autopilot-panel-chars*, the box does not jump as lines change) and at most 30 (longer lines cut with ''…''). Line 1 ''AUTOPILOT'' grey then the state word: MENU, PLANNING…, PLAN, REFUSED, ENGAGED green, OFF. :menu and :refused: a window of at most 8 rows of autopilot-menu-lines keeping the selection visible, the selection paper white inverted, the others paper white; last line ''PLAN [RET]  CLOSE [O]'' grey, or the refusal amber ''! <REASON>''. :planning: ''PLANNING…'' alone. :shown and :engaged: the plan''s four lines of decision 127 (''BURN 1/2 T-12:30'', ''DV 56.2 M/S  20 S'', ''TOTAL 112 M/S 905 KG'', ''LEFT 27.1 T  ARR 02:14'', from autopilot-plan-summary; one function autopilot-plan-lines in overlay.lisp, which the AUTOPILOT screen of cockpit.lisp also calls), then one row per burn up to 6 (''<n> <LABEL> <dv> M/S <duration>'', label the burn label''s name upcased, dv as the map''s burn markers of decision 125): done burns grey, the current burn paper white with ''>'' before it, later ones paper white; while a rendezvous plan is :terminal or :station the phase line of the TARGET screen (''TERMINAL'', ''ON STATION 100 M'') green; last line ''ENGAGE [RET]  DROP [BKSP]'' grey (shown) or ''ENGAGED  OFF [BKSP]'' green. :off: the kept plan''s burn rows grey; last line the abort''s ''ABORT: <REASON>'' red inverted, flashing until acknowledged (as the message line), else ''OFF  RE-PLAN [RET]  CLOSE [O]'' grey. Draw order in the overlay: the map''s markers, the readout, the autopilot panel, the message line, the LOST panel. *overlay-max-vertices* becomes twice the sum of the fullest readout, panel and message line plus the map markers'' capacity, rounded up to a multiple of 6. All numbers defparameters.', NULL, '2026-10-15T07:26:13Z', '2026-10-15T07:26:13Z', 167, NULL, 'rule', 167, '2026-10-15, task 167: 2026-10-15, task 167: plan 33: The outside and map views'' overlay of milestone 3 builds it in its three pieces; docs/design.md ''Instruments'', ''The autopilot'', ''Interaction'', ''Messages and radio'' to state it as built.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (130, 'Earth''s night lights and clouds in numbers: conversion, packing, radiance, fade, cloud albedo and shadow', 'answered', '[]', 'plan ''Earth''s night lights and clouds'' (part 12 of plan 3: masterplan); docs/design.md ''Earth'' gives sources, sizes and the ''should'', decision 40: Earth''s look and drag and decision 13: Asset storage and attribution the sources and the size rule. Open were: how to resample reproducibly (convert-stars'' pattern: pinned hashes, a script, identical reruns), Black Marble''s non-black background (it would light the whole night side; removing it in the asset keeps the shader one multiply and the JPEG small), GPU memory (a 5400x2700 RGBA8 texture is 58 MB; packing clouds into the night texture''s alpha saves one and a binding), and the brightness numbers, set against the tone map (tone-map-srgb) at the exposures of decision 76: Frame passes, radiance scale and exposure: the night side is seen at the deep exposure 16 (Earth covers the frame unlit), the terminator from a sunlit view at 1.0. The 6° fade matches the halo''s dusk band (decision 78: Sky in numbers: stars, Sun, Moon and Earth''s look). 5 km is a typical cloud-top height; the cap keeps grazing light from smearing the shadow across the map.', 'Conversion: scripts/convert-earth [out-dir] (needs network, enters the dev shell, like scripts/convert-stars): downloads BlackMarble_2016_3km.jpg and cloud_combined_8192.tif into a mktemp -d directory, checks their pinned SHA-256s, resamples with ImageMagick from the flake''s pinned nixpkgs (-strip, Lanczos, 5400x2700!): night lights in linear light (-colorspace RGB … -colorspace sRGB), with Black Marble''s dark land and ocean background removed in linear light (max(0, x − F)/(1 − F), F fixed in the script from the image''s unlit Sahara and open Pacific), JPEG quality 92, assets/earth/black-marble-5400.jpg (~1 MB); clouds greyscale in the file''s own values, quality 90, assets/earth/clouds-5400.jpg (≤ 5 MB, ~3.5 MB); two runs give identical files, their SHA-256s in SOURCE.md. Packing: one RGBA texture, night lights in RGB (sRGB-decoded), cloud cover in A (linear, 0 clear to 1 overcast), merged when the renderer starts, with its 1/8 copy for the far Earth as the day map has (decision 78). Night lights: radiance *night-lights-radiance* 0.08 × texel (linear), so a city core reads ~237 of 255 at the deep exposure 16 and ~70 at 1.0; faded in by smoothstep from n·s = +0.05 (lights off) to −0.10 (full, 6° below the horizon, as the halo''s dusk band); dimmed under clouds by (1 − 0.6 α). Clouds: white of albedo *cloud-albedo* 0.9 mixed over the ground by α, lit by max(n·s, 0) like the ground; shadow on the ground (1 − *cloud-shadow* 0.5 × α''), α'' sampled at the ground point moved toward the Sun by h·tan(z) with h = 5 km, capped at 40 km (≈5 texels), so the offset shows near the terminator; fixed to the ground, not animated. The tunables are live (defparameter, frame uniforms); a task that changes one records it as a consequence of this decision.', NULL, '2026-10-15T07:29:37Z', '2026-10-15T07:29:37Z', 168, NULL, 'rule', 168, '2026-10-15, task 168: 2026-10-15, task 168: plan 34: Earth''s night lights and clouds builds on it (scripts/convert-earth, merge-alpha-pixels, earth-fragment, three pieces).');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (131, 'Flight screenshots and live runs in milestone 3: the screen state, screenshot waits, live shots, the cockpit capture script', 'answered', '[]', 'The Done when needs flight screenshots that show milestone 3''s screens (panel, message line, cockpit screens), but a :screenshot entry held only a sim while those read the clock; timed screenshots inside burns and the terminal phase would otherwise need :after steps, which split coarse steps and change trajectories; live fly wrote no PNGs (decision 87), and a ./sly screenshot cannot be timed at x100,000. K-17 for the cockpit capture: a grapple latch (not a berth), the cheapest rendezvous to a towable load (~14 h). Task 169.', 'A :screenshot entry keeps, beside its copy of the sim, a copy of the clock''s screen state (clock-screen-snapshot in clock.lisp: the nominal warp level, the live messages, the SHIP log and its read mark, the autopilot model), and capture-game-state :screen loads it into the frozen capture game, so flight PNGs show the warp, the message line, the autopilot panel, the strip, COMMS and the lamps as at that step (plain captures keep decision 106''s WARP x1). Waits added for screenshots, conditions checked after each step so they add no step boundary: (:wait :in-burn <label>) (the engaged plan''s cursor inside that labelled burn), (:wait :terminal) (cursor :terminal), (:wait :range-below <m>) (the target''s CoM range). Screenshot steps in the scripts use only these and the existing condition waits, never :after or event waits, so each script''s states equal its version without them bit for bit. (:approach ... :screenshots (<view> ...)) records one screenshot entry per view, labelled approach-20m-<view>, at the moment the 20 m hold goes on. Live: (fly name :shots <dir>) pauses the clock at each :screenshot step (the hook sets the flight''s pending shot; clock-frame! stops the frame''s steps when the hook paused it; the render state shows the sim''s state), and a thread of the image sets the game''s view to the step''s view (outside reset to its default pose, cockpit head at Home, the map framed on opening), waits 10 frames, writes the window''s PNG to <dir>/<nn>-<label>.png, unpauses; the view stays the step''s; record and states equal run-flight''s. The cockpit rendezvous and capture is flights/cockpit-k-17.sexp: Rendezvous: K-17 by autopilot, then (:approach :k-17), latched, every screenshot :view :cockpit; in scripts/test-slow. Screenshots are not committed (decision 87).', NULL, '2026-10-15T07:34:25Z', '2026-10-15T07:34:25Z', 169, NULL, 'rule', 169, '2026-10-15, task 169: 2026-10-15, task 169: plan 35: Flight screenshots, the live runs and the Done when builds it in its pieces 1-4.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (132, 'Rails details: frames of ω, two-body rails, polar node and a crash end at the start', 'answered', '[]', 'task 183: Rails and attitude modes of the objects. plan 23 leaves the frame of ω, a two-body switch and the polar node''s roundoff (cos of the double nearest 90° is 6e-17, Ω drifting ~3e-14 rad over 10 years) open; these choices keep the rails consistent with the integrator''s conventions and the plan''s tests exact.', 'object-attitude-into! writes ω in inertial axes (the form rk4-step!''s attitude function takes); object-state-into! writes ω in body axes, as a ship-state. make-rails takes :j2 nil for pure two-body rails (tests, piece 4''s two-body re-entry). Ω̇ is set to 0 when |cos i| < 1e-15, so the polar places keep Ω = 270° bit for bit. A rails start below the crash radius ends its segment at the start (:crash), so the sim removes it at once.', NULL, '2026-10-15T08:18:34Z', '2026-10-15T08:18:34Z', 183, NULL, 'rule', 183, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (133, 'The places'' data: rock radii, the yard''s attitude, display names and the target state', 'answered', '[]', 'task 184: The places: the twelve object definitions and the five presets. The design''s table rounds the rocks'' r to 1 cm but computes I from the exact r (Skua Yard 3: 1.81 m gives 6.55e4, the table 6.58e4), so r to the mm keeps r within 0.005 m and I at (2/5) m r² to 1e-9 while every value rounds to the table. Between the epoch and apogee the yard moves 0.12° in mean anomaly but 0.021° in true anomaly, so the epoch''s LVLH would miss plan 23''s 0.01° for the fixture facing back along the velocity at apogee; all yard objects share a, e, ω, so one apogee''s LVLH serves them all. The yard''s M₀ to 1e-5° (2.3 m at apogee) makes its gaps 665.6 and 667.9 m: the test allows 1.5 m, not 1 m. place-target-state-into! needs the sim''s objects, which do not exist before task 185.', 'Rocks hold r to the mm ((3m/4πρ)^(1/3): 1.337, 1.611, 1.814, 2.122, 2.285, 2.616 m), I = (2/5) m r² of that r, the pin at r + 0.3 m and the bound pin + 0.8 m, as literals; they round to the design''s table. POD 14 and the six rocks share one attitude: the LVLH attitude at the yard''s first apogee (Highport''s elements at M₀ 179.87°, 30.8 s after the start) turned 180° about z, not the LVLH at the epoch. K-17''s q₀ maps body x, y, z to the epoch''s LVLH y, z, x. The rock mover''s display name is GRINDSTONE (docs/story.md), the place names TERN STATION, FULMAR DEPOT, DERELICT K-17, HIGHPORT, SKUA YARD; fixtures are named berth, A, B, plant, arm 1, arm 2 and fixture. place-target-state-into! moves to task 185: The sim holds the objects: promotion, demotion and stepping, which builds sim-object.', NULL, '2026-10-15T08:25:09Z', '2026-10-15T08:25:09Z', 184, NULL, 'rule', 184, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (134, 'Objects in the sim: the shared third-body reads, the principal-axis test and rechecks', 'answered', '[]', 'task 185: The sim holds the objects: promotion, demotion and stepping. plan 23 says the ship''s step fills the buffer; filling it in the step function before flight-step! is the same single read and keeps flight-step! and rk4-step! free of a fill flag. ''Within 1° of a principal axis'' needs eigenvectors, which math.lisp lacks, and for K-17 (Iy = Iz) and the spherical rocks eigenvectors are arbitrary within the degenerate eigenspace, so a test against them would wrongly freeze a steady spin; ω ∥ I ω is exactly the steady-spin condition of torque-free motion. A lost sim''s t is the impact time, off the grid, so no promotion runs there. Placing the ship (tests, milestone 3''s start states) leaves stale check times, which a recheck clears.', 'sim-step-ticks! reads the step''s Moon and Sun once (third-bodies-step-into!) and passes the buffer to flight-step! and every promoted object''s object-step! (rk4-step!''s optional last argument; without it rk4-step! reads them itself, bit for bit the same). A demoted towable object spins on rails when the angle between ω and I ω is at most 1° (ω along a principal axis; a degenerate tensor''s whole eigenspace counts), else it is held inertial. sim-update-objects! runs after each step unless the ship is lost; sim-recheck-objects! makes every rails object''s check due now (new-game-sim, and any start state or test that places the ship).', NULL, '2026-10-15T08:35:45Z', '2026-10-15T08:35:45Z', 185, NULL, 'rule', 185, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (135, 'The stack in ship.lisp: the load''s form, loaded inertia bounds, the range finder''s details', 'answered', '[]', 'task 187 (piece 1 of plan 24: Grapple, contact, berthing, towing, deliveries and refuelling). The plan asks the loaded rows within 0.5 % in I_xx, I_yy, I_zz, but the unloaded model already differs from the table by up to 4 % in I_xx (tests/ship.lisp''s ship-inertia-table allows 5 %), and the loaded rows'' figures rest on the table''s unloaded ones; measured: wet + 50 t I_xx 1.568e5 against 1.55e5 (1.2 %), all inside the combined bound. decision 113 leaves ''within 500 m'' and ''offset'' per axis or radial open; the design''s 0.30 m is the cone''s radius less the guides'', so radial.', 'ship-mass-properties takes the load as a stack-load (object def, CoM in ship axes from R, quaternion object body -> ship body), a struct the sim''s grapple can hold or fill; nil is bit for bit the unloaded path. The loaded ''Inertia'' rows are tested at 0.5 % plus the unloaded ship''s own deviation from its row (decision 71''s model is 2.4 % over the table''s wet I_xx, a throwaway script''s), CoM within 0.005 m, I_xz within 1e3 kg m^2. The range finder''s 500 m is the tip-to-pin distance; the offset''s tolerance is radial (sqrt(y^2 + z^2) <= 0.30 m); readings hold the object''s index in sim-objects and the fixture''s index in its definition; flags per tolerance: closing, offset, angle, rotation.', NULL, '2026-10-15T08:52:17Z', '2026-10-15T08:52:17Z', 187, NULL, 'rule', 187, '2026-10-15, task 187: 2026-10-15, task 187: src/ship.lisp builds stack-load, ship-mass-properties with a load, stack-bound, grapple-range-into!; tests/ship.lisp tests the loaded rows by this bound.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (136, 'The SHIP log''s stamps and read mark, restarts and the latest checkpoint', 'answered', '[]', 'task 188: The sim''s grapple fields, notices, the SHIP log, the latest checkpoint and G, B, Y. decision 112 gives the log''s content and the read mark but not their form, nor what a restart does to them; the log records what the ship said, so a restart keeps it, and a flight script is a new game.', 'clock-message! stamps each log entry with clock-game-t, the sim''s t the clock last saw (the step''s t after a step); clock-log is newest first, the read mark clock-log-read the newest entry when Y was pressed (eq), so entries after it are unread and all are once it falls off the 200; clock-restart! keeps the log, the read mark and clock-checkpoint; N (game-new-game!) and a live fly clear clock-checkpoint; Return is game-return!, its own :restart command, N :new-game; lost-panel-lines takes whether the clock holds a checkpoint.', NULL, '2026-10-15T09:01:23Z', '2026-10-15T09:01:23Z', 188, NULL, 'rule', 188, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (137, 'Contact in the step: the scratch, a response at the step''s start, the impulse at g and the contact panel', 'answered', '[]', 'task 189 (piece 3 of plan 24: Grapple, contact, berthing, towing, deliveries and refuelling). decision 112 places the scratch outside the sim and decision 44 the response on the grid, but not the zero-length step a fine-step response gives, which point both bodies take the impulse at when t_c and g differ, where objects stand at a contact death, or how the panel shows a speed near the 1.0 m/s limit (format-speed''s one decimal reads 1.0 for 1.01). Re-taking r_o at g keeps angular momentum exact; stopping objects at t_c keeps the death view consistent at coarse steps; two decimals tell a death from a bounce.', 'The step''s start lives in *contact* (hazards.lisp), outside the sim, saved by contact-save! when a candidate is within twice its relative reach; a bounce at the fine step is answered on the step''s start (sim-step! returns h = 0, the prediction replaces its last node); at g the impulse uses n and the point kept from the stack''s CoM at t_c with r_o re-taken from the CoMs at g and the normal speed at g, so the ratio is exactly the restitution and both bodies take it at one point; a contact death puts the ship and every promoted object on their step''s Hermite at t_c; the LOST panel shows a contact''s normal speed to 0.01 m/s.', NULL, '2026-10-15T10:29:43Z', '2026-10-15T10:29:43Z', 189, NULL, 'rule', 189, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (138, 'Berthed pose: the ship''s R point in the station''s axes, the engine off', 'answered', '[]', 'task 190 (decision 112 says r = r_s + q_s p with p fixed). Holding R rather than the CoM keeps the hull on the fixture when propellant transfer (task 191) moves the CoM; the throttle cannot fire berthed, so it must not shorten coarse steps or lower the warp either, which keeps a berthed run bit for bit the same whatever the controls say.', 'While berthed the grapple''s com holds the ship''s R (body origin) in the station''s axes from its CoM and its q the ship''s axes to the station''s; each step r = r_s + q_s p + q CoM(m_p); berthed, the engine counts as off for step-h and the warp limits, and no inertial hold is engaged above x4.', NULL, '2026-10-15T10:45:32Z', '2026-10-15T10:45:32Z', 190, NULL, 'rule', 190, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (139, 'Release in the sim: the ship alone at the stack''s motion, exclusion by the shapes, the berth release''s coast bound', 'answered', '[]', 'task 191 (piece 5 of plan 24: Grapple, contact, berthing, towing, deliveries and refuelling). A point held fixed to a station is not a free path (Hill: the radial and cross-track tide); released from Tern Station''s fixture at 500 km the free ship leaves the held path by 0.16 m in 100 s (Fulmar Depot 0.11 m) with its velocity exact, so 1 cm over 100 s cannot hold for any release; quadratic growth under the tide''s bound plus 1 cm at 10 s catches a velocity error of 1 mm/s. The ship''s own CoM, not the stack''s, is the integrated point after the release (decision 112: r is the stack''s CoM).', 'grapple-release! (hazards.lisp): a latched load keeps the state latched-load-update! wrote (the stack''s motion at its CoM) and is :promoted; the ship''s r moves from the stack''s CoM to its own, v + ω × (r_ship − r_stack), ω kept; berthed, the ship keeps berth-hold!''s state and is integrated again, the transfer off; rate damping kept; the grapple :armed with no object; sim-excluded the object, cleared by exclusion-update! after each step once the bounding spheres or the shapes (stack-object-contact!) no longer meet or the object is not promoted. Refusals in that order: ROTATING above 0.5°/s of the ship''s |ω|, ENGINE ON with the throttle up and propellant left. The transfer adds min(10 h, 28,000 − m_p) per berthed step before berth-hold!, off with TANKS FULL at full. Task 191''s ''coasting 100 s within 1 cm of the held pose''s path'' is tested as: within 1 cm for the first 10 s, and within the tide''s 3 n² d t² / 2 for 100 s.', NULL, '2026-10-15T10:52:09Z', '2026-10-15T10:52:09Z', 191, NULL, 'rule', 191, '2026-10-15, task 191: task 191: src/hazards.lisp grapple-release!, exclusion-update!, transfer-step!, delivery-start! (PLACEHOLDER(192)); src/clock.lisp calls them; tests/hazards.lisp release-*, transfer-fulmar, berth-checkpoint; docs/architecture.md ''Collisions and the Sun'' and ''Testing'' state the coast bound.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (140, 'Deliveries in the sim: the check''s place, the receiver''s pin and the removal''s point', 'answered', '[]', 'task 192: decision 53 and decision 112 leave open the receiver''s velocity at the pin, the point''s form and where the notice comes from; object-remove! writes only BURNED UP (task 188''s test keeps a :delivered removal silent).', 'delivery-start! at a towable release takes the first receiving fixture of a promoted object within 100 m and 0.05 m/s of its pin''s motion (v + ω × q pin); delivery-update! runs after each step with a delivery pending, beside exclusion-update! (and in contact-respond! for a response at the step''s end), reading both the load and the receiver by object-state-into! (rails or promoted); a load not promoted or on rails, a receiver without state, or 150 m clears it; grapple-capture! of the load clears it too; step-h lands on release + 600 s as on until; the removal''s point is (receiver key, fixture name), e.g. (:highport "plant"), and the notice (:delivery "<NAME> DELIVERED") is written by delivery-update!, not object-remove!.', NULL, '2026-10-15T11:00:50Z', '2026-10-15T11:00:50Z', 192, NULL, 'rule', 192, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (141, 'The contact drop-out in the prediction: per step, the copy''s contact death, the checkpoint check', 'answered', '[]', 'task 193 builds decision 113''s contact drop-out; these are the details it leaves open. One event per step keeps the event list in time order per kind; the copy''s contact death always comes after its spheres'' meeting, so the :contact event already covers it and the PERIAPSIS BELOW message no longer reads it.', 'contact-crossing! after each step of the prediction''s copy: objects a contact candidate at the step''s start and end, the relative Hermite against stack-bound at the step''s end plus the object''s bound (hermite-crossing, its sag bound); the earliest s > 0 of the step is one :contact event (time, object index); s = 0 (spheres already meeting) is none. A copy''s death by contact is no :impact. checkpoint-safe-p asks prediction-dropout-p (any drop-out event); dropout-message takes the event''s kind.', NULL, '2026-10-15T11:09:04Z', '2026-10-15T11:09:04Z', 193, NULL, 'rule', 193, '2026-10-15, task 193: 2026-10-15, task 193: src/prediction.lisp contact-starts!, contact-crossing!, the :contact event, prediction-dropout-p; src/clock.lisp checkpoint-safe-p and dropout-message; docs/architecture.md ''Time warp'', ''Trajectory prediction'', ''Checkpoints'', ''Testing''.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (142, 'Lateral RCS capability with the stack''s CoM ahead of the front blocks', 'answered', '[]', 'docs/design.md "RCS forces, torques and accelerations" gave ~1,077 N for wet + 50 t, but at its own "Inertia" row''s CoM (x 15.21 m) the rule gives 1,064.8 N, which is also the mixer''s measured maximum for a 10 kN command (task 256). The figure is corrected rather than the rule bent; the CoM-between case keeps the nominal 2F(1 + min/max) without the axial couple, as the design sets.', 'Front lateral pair up to 2F, its torque cancelled by the axial couple 4Fd, the aft pair with the couple left: wet + 50 t 1,065 N, wet + 100 t 958 N; docs/design.md''s ~1,077 N corrected to 1,065 N', NULL, '2026-10-15T11:21:30Z', '2026-10-15T11:21:30Z', 256, NULL, 'rule', 256, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (143, 'The target at the render time: the sim''s gravity and the jerk', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 114: Targets and target holds in the sim gives the render-time target as the quadratic with point-mass a; task 195''s bound (a promoted target within 1 mm over a 2 s coarse step) fails with it: the point-mass jerk alone gives ~1.6 mm at τ = −1 s and ~13 mm at −2 s, J2 left out of a another ~10 mm. Model gravity (J2; the third bodies'' ~1e-6 m/s² left out, no ephemeris read per frame) plus the jerk keeps it under 1e-5 m at −2 s for one gravity call per frame. Supersedes decision 114 in part: only the instruments'' extrapolation.', 'Instruments take a promoted target at the render time t_r from its state at the sim''s t as r + v τ + a τ²/2 + j τ³/6 (v + a τ + j τ²/2), a the sim''s gravity model (:full read as :j2), j point-mass gravity''s jerk; rails targets exactly. The kinematic hold''s quadratic of decision 114 stays.', NULL, '2026-10-15T11:50:30Z', '2026-10-15T11:50:30Z', 195, NULL, 'rule', 195, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (144, 'The target hold''s rails target: the position''s drift beside the Kepler velocity', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 114: Targets and target holds in the sim extrapolates the target over a kinematic step as r_T + v_T τ + a_T τ²/2. On rails (decision 19: Time-warp rules) the position moves with J2''s secular rates (Ω̇, ω̇, Ṁ), but rails-state-into! gives v as the Kepler velocity of the elements, ~11 m/s from the position''s rate in LEO; over a 2 s step at x1,000 the quadratic then misses Tern Station by 22 m and the nose by 1.7e-6 rad, over Highport''s 64 s steps by ~700 m. The drift costs no Kepler solve and brings the error to 2.2e-8 rad. The relative holds keep the Kepler v, the velocity a promoted target gets and the one to be matched, and the one the instruments show (task 195).', 'For a target on rails the target hold''s scratch keeps the rails v (the Kepler velocity of the mean elements) for holds 9 and 0 and, beside it, the drift of the rails position''s rate from it (rails-rate-into!: × (n + Ṁ)/n, plus Ω̇ ẑ × r and ω̇ ĥ × r); holds 7 and 8 extrapolate the position as r_T + (v_T + drift) τ + a_T τ²/2. A promoted target has no drift.', NULL, '2026-10-15T12:05:57Z', '2026-10-15T12:05:57Z', 196, NULL, 'rule', 196, '2026-10-15, task 196: 2026-10-15, task 196: controls.lisp keeps the drift in the target scratch (slots 10-12), clock.lisp writes it from rails-rate-into! (physics.lisp) via target-rails-segment; the kinematic hold''s nose within 2.2e-8 rad of the true direction at x1,000 (1.7e-6 without). Follow-up for the instruments'' closing speed added to plan 25.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (145, 'When a planned engine burn ends in the executor', 'answered', '[]', 'task 197: Planned burns in the sim. decision 115: Planned burns as scheduled controls in the sim leaves open how the executor sees a cut and what a manual cut does; the cut lands at a step''s end, so the next step start is the first chance to engage the coast hold. A throttle cut by anyone ends the burn; input gating while a plan is engaged is plan 26: Autopilot: planner, orbit manoeuvres, execution and safety''s.', 'plan-step-controls! ends an engine burn at the first step start where the throttle is off, sim-cutoff is nil or the odometer has reached it (cutting it then itself); the coast hold engages at that step start, one step after the cut step. Running out of propellant leaves the burn :burning (an abort is the autopilot''s). sim-set-plan! engages a plan from its first burn without touching the controls.', NULL, '2026-10-15T12:20:00Z', '2026-10-15T12:20:00Z', 197, NULL, 'rule', 197, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (146, 'The prediction''s work budget: 4,096 per frame, not 1 ms', 'answered', '[]', 'task 198 (plan 25: Predicted trajectories, targets and target holds, piece 4) was to set the budget to ~1 ms from a measurement (decision 116: Prediction in milestone 3: horizon, target nodes, closest approach, relative nodes, budget). Measured, ~1 ms is ~900 units at the start orbit, and that breaks two rules the prediction serves: at high warp the clock steps up to 4,096 steps (~4 ms) a frame (decision 72: Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp), so a 900-unit prediction falls behind the flight and the warp drop-outs come too late (clock-dropout-to-impact and clock-dropout-to-contact fail: the impact 1 h ahead at x1,000,000 is no longer found before the first frame''s steps); and under thrust it restarts every frame (decision 75: Prediction in milestone 2: the step function on a copy) and one LEO period (~2,800 steps) never completes in one 900-unit frame. The prediction''s step costs 1.1 us, not decision 20''s 180 ns (the copy''s crash, contact and objects'' tests, the contact buffer, the third bodies); a cheaper step, or a clock that never outruns the prediction and a burn that keeps the last prediction until the new one completes, would allow ~1 ms again (follow-up task).', '*prediction-work-per-frame* is 4,096 work units (decision 116''s units: 1 per copy step, 1 per promoted object stepped, 1 per rails evaluation of the target), as many as the clock''s *frame-step-limit*; measured ~4.6 ms at the start orbit (:full, 1.13 us per unit) and ~2.7 ms at Skua Yard with its nine objects promoted (0.66 us per unit). It is spent only while the prediction restarts or catches up (high warp, thrust); a complete coasting prediction extends by the flown steps.', NULL, '2026-10-15T12:30:19Z', '2026-10-15T12:30:19Z', 198, NULL, 'rule', 198, '2026-10-15, task 198: 2026-10-15, task 198: *prediction-work-per-frame* 4,096 in src/prediction.lisp, the figures in docs/architecture.md ''Trajectory prediction''; task 260 profiles the step.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (147, 'Closest approach: a noise floor for constant ranges', 'answered', '[]', 'decision 116: Prediction in milestone 3: horizon, target nodes, closest approach, relative nodes, budget counts every sign change of r_rel . v_rel from - to +. A co-orbital target 1 km ahead (task 199''s test) has a constant range, and the integrated ship against the Kepler target gives sign changes from rounding noise at a cosine of ~1e-12 (two per two periods). A real shallow minimum (Fulmar Depot 13,649 km away, closing at ~15 km/s) reads ~9e-6 at its nodes, a 1 mm/s approach to 10 m ~2e-4: 1e-9 keeps both and drops the noise. Events of one step are found out of order (apsis, closest approach, node, contact), so push-event! inserts by time.', 'A local minimum of the range is a :closest-approach only where, at one of the bracketing nodes, |r_rel . v_rel| / (|r_rel| |v_rel|) >= *closest-approach-min-cosine* 1e-9; events are inserted in time order.', NULL, '2026-10-15T12:54:09Z', '2026-10-15T12:54:09Z', 199, NULL, 'rule', 199, '2026-10-15, task 199: 2026-10-15, task 199: *closest-approach-min-cosine* and the ordered push-event! in src/prediction.lisp; docs/architecture.md ''Trajectory prediction''.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (148, 'The closing speed of a rails target: the position''s rate', 'answered', '[]', 'task 259: On rails the position moves with J2''s secular rates, ~11 m/s from the Kepler v in LEO (decision 144: The target hold''s rails target: the position''s drift beside the Kepler velocity). The closing speed is read as the range''s rate, so it must match it. F R D is the velocity to null: the Kepler v is the one a promoted target gets (promotion happens before the close work that matches velocities) and the one holds 9 and 0 point along, so the instruments and the relative holds agree. target-relative-into! takes the rate as an optional argument (default v-t).', 'The instruments'' closing speed (TARGET''s CLS) of a target on rails takes the rate of its rails position (rails-rate-into! on target-rails-segment after target-state-into!), so it equals −d(range)/dt; the relative velocity F R D and render-state-target-v stay on the Kepler v of the elements, as decision 144''s holds 9 and 0 do. A promoted target is unchanged.', NULL, '2026-10-15T13:07:46Z', '2026-10-15T13:07:46Z', 259, NULL, 'rule', 259, '2026-10-15, task 259: 2026-10-15, task 259: controls.lisp target-relative-into! takes an optional rate of the target''s position for the closing speed; render.lisp render-state-update! passes a rails target''s rails-rate-into!; tests/render.lisp render-state-target-closing-speed (CLS within 1e-3 m/s of the range''s centred difference; the Kepler v misses by 10.8 m/s); docs/design.md TARGET''s CLS, docs/architecture.md.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (149, 'The prediction''s work budget stays 4,096 per frame after task 260''s cheaper step', 'answered', '[]', 'task 260: The prediction''s per-step cost and a ~1 ms frame budget, following decision 146: The prediction''s work budget: 4,096 per frame, not 1 ms. ~1 ms is ~1,540 units at the start orbit and ~170 steps at Skua Yard. Keeping the warp drop-outs safe at that budget needs two rules: (1) the clock never steps past the prediction''s computed extent, keeping 3 s x level of computed lead (else an impact is found later than the drop-out rule''s 3 s of real time; clock-dropout-to-impact checks every step), which runs high warp at the prediction''s rate: x1,000,000 in LEO at ~x185,000 actual instead of ~x490,000; (2) under thrust a per-frame restart reaches ~170 one-second steps at Skua Yard, short of the 300 s lead x100 needs, so the clock would stall unless the running prediction is kept until it completes, and the readers (the overlay''s impact, the map, the AUTOPILOT plot) then need a second, complete prediction so the events don''t vanish at each restart. Both trade warp speed and a double buffer for ~1.5 ms in catch-up frames, inside a frame whose own steps are capped at 4 ms (decision 72: Simulation state and the one step function in clock.lisp); decision 20: Trajectory prediction on the frame thread''s 2 ms revisit threshold is passed only in those frames, not in steady state, so no worker thread. The cuts: rk4-step! skips the attitude function at the stages without force; third-bodies-into! reads the EMB and Moon Hermites once; third-bodies-step-into! reuses the last step''s end (*third-bodies-last*); body by eq; copy-doubles! for small copies; latched-load-update! returns early without a load.', '*prediction-work-per-frame* stays 4,096 work units (decision 116''s units), as many as the clock''s *frame-step-limit*: the prediction is the flight''s own step on a copy, so it steps as far per frame as the clock may. task 260 made the step cheaper, bit for bit: measured (i5-12500H, 2026-10-15, :full, two periods) 0.65 us per unit at the start orbit (was 1.13), 0.45 us with Tern Station targeted (0.68), 0.52 us at Skua Yard with nine objects promoted, 0.59 us with Skua Yard 3 targeted (0.66): 4,096 units are ~2.7 ms at the start orbit and ~2.4 ms at Skua Yard, spent only in frames where the prediction restarts or catches up (thrust, a control change, high warp); coasting it extends by the flown steps.', NULL, '2026-10-15T13:20:53Z', '2026-10-15T13:20:53Z', 260, NULL, 'rule', 260, '2026-10-15, task 260: 2026-10-15, task 260: src/physics.lisp (rk4-step! forceless stages, third-bodies-into!, *third-bodies-last*), src/bodies.lisp (body), src/math.lisp (copy-doubles!), src/hazards.lisp (latched-load-update!), src/clock.lisp, src/prediction.lisp (*prediction-work-per-frame* docstring); docs/architecture.md ''Trajectory prediction'', ''Integrator''; docs/design.md ''Performance target''.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (150, 'Autopilot first guesses: node states, circular orbits, ties, plane turns, goal forms', 'answered', '[]', 'task 201 (plan 26 piece 1): decision 117 fixes the method but not these details. Kepler states keep the guesses exact against the closed forms (Hohmann within 1e-9) and cheap; the shot only gives times. 1e-3 is ~13 km of apsis spread at LEO, above J2''s ~3 km swing, so a near-circular orbit''s noisy apsides are not used. Rotating about r-hat leaves the radial velocity alone, the cheapest plane change at that point. Per-burn fields belong with the burn; the plan''s vector stays read-only and shared.', 'First guesses (autopilot-first-guess) take each node''s state by Kepler from the snapshot''s osculating elements at the coast shot''s times (apsis events, equator crossings bisected on the shot''s Hermite, relative-node events); an orbit with e < 1e-3 (*circular-guess-e*) has no apsis to burn at, its burn goes at the earliest point whose hold-at is >= snapshot t + 120 s; two nodes'' radii within 0.1 % (*node-tie*) are a tie, the earlier wins; a plane change turns the velocity about r-hat (Rodrigues), so its dv is 2 v_h sin(di/2) with v_h the horizontal speed (the full speed on a circle); a merged or off-node inclination takes the least turn about r-hat that reaches it (the nearest reachable where the latitude forbids it), the target''s plane the turn of h-hat towards the target''s h-hat about r-hat; no node in the shot or its two extrapolated periods refuses ''NO NODE AHEAD''. Goals: :circularise :apoapsis|:periapsis, :set-apoapsis / :set-periapsis an altitude in m, :change-orbit (altitude inclination-or-nil-or-:target), :change-plane an inclination in rad or :target. The shot''s post-cut state and Isp live per burn on planned-burn (cut-t, cut-r, cut-v, isp; a guess''s are the node''s after the impulsive dv); burn-plan''s constructor takes coast-hold, preset, goal, goal-orbit, reserve-dv and reserve-mass as keywords.', NULL, '2026-10-15T13:45:14Z', '2026-10-15T13:45:14Z', 201, NULL, 'rule', 201, '2026-10-15, task 201: 2026-10-15, task 201: src/autopilot.lisp (new), src/controls.lisp (planned-burn cut-t, cut-r, cut-v, isp; burn-plan preset, goal, goal-orbit, reserve-dv, reserve-mass), luv-in-space.asd, tests/autopilot.lisp; docs/architecture.md ''Files in load order'', AGENTS.md ''Layout''.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (151, 'Autopilot shooting: what Newton steers, the finite difference, the shot''s setup and horizon, refusal order', 'answered', '[]', 'task 202: Autopilot planning job, shooting and the orbit manoeuvres. decision 117: Autopilot in milestone 3: planning jobs, goal measures, burns, execution rules in the step, trims names Newton on the goal measures, but under J2 a circular orbit''s largest and smallest |r| jump between near-equal extrema and its radius swings ~7 km, so Newton on them oscillated (the Hohmann at :full did not converge in 8 steps); a time-fitted harmonic of r leaked the mean radius and depended on the window''s start. Mean |r| and the mean eccentricity vector are smooth and blind to the window''s start; with these the :j2 and :full Hohmann converge in one step. A 0.01 m/s finite difference was below the cut''s grain and gave zero columns. The plan''s test of a 120 km goal expects ''BELOW 150 KM'', so the 150 km bound of ''GOAL OUT OF RANGE'' is the menu''s, not the job''s.', 'The goal measure of decision 117 (largest and smallest |r| over the period after the last cut, mean osculating inclination, the target plane''s angle) decides convergence: a shot with every residual within its tolerance ends the iteration, no further step. Newton steers by smooth residuals over the same window, each over its tolerance: a circular goal by the mean |r| and the mean osculating eccentricity vector times the radius (a Hohmann''s two burns: both; circularise''s one burn: the eccentricity vector only, since a burn keeps its own point on the orbit), set Ap / Pe by the largest and smallest |r| (the far one at the goal, the near one kept at the coast shot''s radius at the burn''s node; circularise''s goal radius is that radius too), a plane by the plane''s residual alone (no apsis terms). The Jacobian''s finite difference is 1 m/s per LVLH component (a cut lands on the 1/64 s grid, so delivered dv is quantised at ~0.044 m/s at full throttle wet). Burns are rebuilt on their first-guess nodes each iteration with the LVLH axes of the first guess''s node states. A shot (shot-sim-assign!) is the snapshot with the throttle off, no cut-off, the plan from its first burn and its coast hold engaged at once, as the autopilot''s engage; prediction-shoot! runs it with a fixed horizon (:until) or, after the plan, to the last cut plus the osculating period there, capped 3 burn half-durations plus 600 s past the last node; the prediction records each burn''s cut time. Refusals: at the job''s start ''NOT IN EARTH ORBIT'', ''GOAL OUT OF RANGE'' (goal altitude above 300,000 km only), ''NO TARGET'' (a target''s plane without a target); after the first guess its ''NO NODE AHEAD'' and ''NEEDS <n> KG'' (first-guess propellant plus reserve, n rounded up), so a plan that cannot burn is not shot; a shot that never reaches its last cut, or 8 steps without meeting the tolerance, ''NOT CONVERGED''; on the final shot ''BELOW 150 KM'' (a node under Earth''s crash radius + 50 km, or a crash), ''TOO NEAR THE MOON'', ''NEEDS <n> KG''. A goal below 150 km is not out of range: the menu''s ranges keep it out, a job given one refuses it by the floor.', NULL, '2026-10-15T14:15:29Z', '2026-10-15T14:15:29Z', 202, NULL, 'rule', 202, '2026-10-15, task 202: 2026-10-15, task 202: src/autopilot.lisp (shot-sim-assign!, the goal measures, the planning job, autopilot-plan-now), src/prediction.lisp (:until, prediction-shoot!, the cut times); tests/autopilot.lisp, tests/prediction.lisp; docs/design.md ''The autopilot'', docs/architecture.md ''Trajectory prediction'', ''Waits and expectations'', ''Testing''.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (152, 'Autopilot execution in the step as built: when each rule runs, the warp look-ahead, completion, contact reasons, shot aborts', 'answered', '[]', 'task 203: Autopilot execution and safety in the step. decision 117: Autopilot in milestone 3: planning jobs, goal measures, burns, execution rules in the step, trims gives the rules but not where in the step each runs, how the clock caps warp from a burn''s first step when the throttle only comes on inside the step, how ''disengaged'' after completion differs from an abort, what a plain bounce (no notice) aborts as, or what a planner shot does when its own candidate trips a rule (a set Pe 120 km plan aborts ''UNSAFE ORBIT'' in its shot and would otherwise read ''NOT CONVERGED'').', 'plan-step-checks! runs in sim-step-ticks! right after plan-step-controls!, so the flight, the prediction''s copy and the planner''s shots obey it alike. Completion: at the step start after the last cut (phase :done) the notice ''AUTOPILOT: PLAN COMPLETE'', the checkpoint request and sim-plan nil, the coast hold kept (not rate damping). The unsafe-orbit, propellant and attitude rules run only while :burning (propellant changes only then); the Δv left is the cut-off (RCS target) less the odometer; the propellant left is the rocket equation from the current stack mass for that Δv and the later burns. The attitude rule compares the nose with the burn attitude''s nose on fine steps; a cut stores the Δv left in the cursor (sim-plan-resume), :holding resumes once the inertial hold reports settled; retries count per plan (sim-plan-retries), so a second cut anywhere in the plan aborts. Contact: a contact answered in the step (contact-test! non-nil, not lost) aborts ''BERTHED'' when berthed, ''LATCHED'' after a towable capture (grapple :closing), else ''CONTACT'' (bounces and refused captures; plain bounces write no notice of their own). Abort and disengage (sim-plan-stop!): throttle off, cut-off cleared, translation keys released, rate damping, sim-plan nil. Engaging (sim-engage-plan!, also a shot''s start) cuts the throttle, clears the cut-off, releases all keys, sets the plan from burn 0 with retries, replans and resume 0 and engages the coast hold. Warp: the clock looks one step ahead (plan-burn-due: :burning, or the start reached, or a resume settled), so the RCS cap x4 (''WARP x4: RCS BURN'', refusal ''WARP REFUSED: RCS BURN, LIMIT x4'') and the engine''s x100 (''WARP x100: ENGINE ON'') hold from a burn''s first step; the drop-out kind :burn is the flown sim''s next hold-at while :pending, from sim-plan, not from the prediction. Shots: the prediction keeps its copy''s abort notice (prediction-abort); a shot aborted ''UNSAFE ORBIT'' is refused ''BELOW 150 KM'', another abort by its reason. The clock''s autopilot slot holds the plan last engaged (the map''s grey plan) until task 204 makes it the menu''s model; clock-restart! clears it.', NULL, '2026-10-15T14:33:38Z', '2026-10-15T14:33:38Z', 203, NULL, 'rule', 203, '2026-10-15, task 203: 2026-10-15, task 203: src/clock.lisp (sim cursor fields, plan-step-checks!, contact-abort!, sim-engage-plan!, sim-plan-stop!, plan-burn-due, autopilot-engage!, autopilot-disengage!, clock-autopilot, the warp rules), src/prediction.lisp (prediction-dropout-event with the sim, prediction-abort), src/autopilot.lisp (shot aborts refused), src/overlay.lisp and src/render.lisp (MODE AUTOPILOT); tests/clock.lisp, tests/overlay.lisp; docs/architecture.md, docs/design.md, AGENTS.md.');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (153, 'Autopilot preset menu as built: rows, states, keys and the clock', 'answered', '[]', 'task 204: Autopilot menu, input and frame-spread planning. decision 118: Autopilot preset menu and input in milestone 3 names the rows, ranges, keys and states but not how ORBIT''s second value is reached with only four arrows, when values are taken, what O and the arrows do in each state, what follows completion, how refusals reach the SHIP log (decision 129 lists them as caution messages), the exact repeat count, or where the job''s buffers live. A separate INC row keeps one value per row; KEEP at the opening value avoids an unreachable sentinel; state = visibility matches decision 129''s ''panel shown while the state is not :closed''.', 'The menu is *autopilot-rows* (autopilot-row structs: key, kind, value on opening, text, preset and goal, offered-p): CIRCULARISE AP/PE, SET AP, SET PE, ORBIT, ORBIT INC (its own row holding the ORBIT''s merged plane change; KEEP while at the value it opened with, which keeps the current plane exactly; Return on it plans the ORBIT), PLANE <deg>, PLANE TARGET (offered only with a target); task 161 appends rows. Values are set when the menu opens (not when reopened :off): Ap, Pe and the semi-major axis''s altitude rounded to 10 km within 150-300,000 km, inclinations to 0.5 deg within 0-180. autopilot-menu-lines marks the selection ''> '' (the glyph table gains ''>''). State and visibility are one: open is state /= :closed; O closes from any state (dropping a job or a shown plan; an engaged plan keeps flying) and opens to :menu, or :engaged with a plan running. The arrows and Return act only with the menu open; changing the selection or a value drops a shown, refused or planning plan back to :menu (:engaged when a plan runs). Return in :menu, :refused or :engaged plans the selected row (a running plan is replaced only by a second Return); in :off it re-plans and engages once shown; in :shown it engages or, stale, writes ''PLAN STALE: REPLANNING'' and re-plans to engage. Refusals write ''PLAN REFUSED: <REASON>'' (message key :autopilot-refused) besides the model''s text. Engaged plans end :off after a disengage or an abort notice (text kept), :closed after completion or a missed goal or a plan gone without a notice. The held repeat counts repeats k = 1, 2 ... at k/8 s of real time held after the press''s own step, ×10 for k > 8, so 1.5 s held gives 13 steps; another or no arrow ends it. Pilot keys that disengage: *flight-keys*, the throttle keys and *flight-commands* less G and B, only when they act (not paused, lost or in a flight), above x4 too. The barrier lands the step''s until on the job''s snapshot t + 60 s and at it empties the accumulator; the job is made at the first plan and reused (not in new-clock: ~4 MB of predictions per clock). autopilot-plan-summary is a plist: :burns :index :total-dv :propellant :left :next-in :next-dv :next-duration (start to the shot''s cut) :arrival.', NULL, '2026-10-15T14:50:46Z', '2026-10-15T14:50:46Z', 204, NULL, 'rule', 204, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (154, 'Autopilot measurement, replans and trims as built', 'answered', '[]', 'task 205: Autopilot measurement, replans and trims. decision 117: Autopilot in milestone 3: planning jobs, goal measures, burns, execution rules in the step, trims gives the threshold, the 60 s installation and the replan limit, but not how a shot''s candidate (whose cut states are impulsive guesses) avoids tripping the measurement, where the cursor waits, how the clock knows a headless run from frames, what the remaining burns'' first guess is, how a trim''s node and guess are found, what a goal met or a refused replan does, or how GOAL MISSED leaves the controls.', 'Only a planner''s plan is measured: burn-plan carries its goal measure (set by shot-cut-plan); candidates in their shots and hand-made plans have none, so shots and old tests never replan. The measurement runs in plan-step-controls! at the cut transition (the step start after the cut), against the burn''s shot cut state carried by Kepler to that t. Over max(0.2 m/s, 1 % of the burn''s dv) the cursor goes :replan with the cursor index left on the burn cut and plan-replan-t the cut''s t; plan-next-time is then cut + 60 s (*plan-barrier*, moved to clock.lisp), so every mode steps exactly onto it. With 2 replans made the cut ends the plan with the notice ''AUTOPILOT: GOAL MISSED'' (sim-plan nil, coast hold kept, no checkpoint request; the model :closed). The clock''s model has its own replan job (separate from the menu''s, made at the first replan): clock-step! starts it from the sim right after the cut''s step; before a step at or past cut + 60 s it installs the result: a plan via sim-install-plan! (from its first burn, retries kept, replans + 1, the message ''AUTOPILOT: REPLANNED''), no plan (goal met after the last burn) sets the plan :done so it completes in that step as usual, a refusal aborts with its reason (''AUTOPILOT ABORT: <reason>''). Without a budget (headless clock-step!, run-flight) the job finishes there at once; under clock-frame! it takes the frame''s job share before the menu''s job and the sim waits at cut + 60 s until it ends. The replan''s first guess keeps each remaining burn on its old node (the middle of its start and shot cut) with its old inertial dv vector, node states by Kepler from the snapshot after the earlier remaining burns; Newton then runs as for any plan with the original goal measure, preset and goal. After the last burn the goal measure is taken on the snapshot''s coast shot (two periods); missed, one :trim burn starts from a first guess chosen by trim-goal: plane-only goals the plane change; set Ap/Pe the set Ap or set Pe of whichever apsis is further off in tolerances; circular goals circularise at the apsis nearer the goal radius.', NULL, '2026-10-15T15:07:03Z', '2026-10-15T15:07:03Z', 205, NULL, 'rule', 205, '');
INSERT INTO decision (id, subject, status, options, body, answer, reason, created_at, closed_at, created_by_task, signoff, kind, decided_by_task, consequences) VALUES (155, 'Parachute checks compiled with make-instance notinline', 'answered', '[]', 'Every Parachute check (is, true, false, fail) expands to a make-instance with its own quoted form as an initarg, so SBCL''s PCL builds a distinct constructor per call site and compiles it on its first run: ~10 ms for each of ~200 small tests, ~2.5 s of scripts/test (task 262, measured by sb-sprof: 64 % of the cold small tests'' time in install-optimized-constructor). Declaimed notinline in tests/package.lisp (loaded and compiled before every other test file), make-instance takes the generic path: no run-time compile, all 57,382 checks unchanged, scripts/test 16.3-17.2 s -> 13.4-13.9 s wall at load ~2. Only the test image is affected; src/ is compiled before it. Prefer this over shortening tests while a run fits the budget.', 'tests/package.lisp declaims make-instance notinline for the test system', NULL, '2026-10-15T15:30:47Z', '2026-10-15T15:30:47Z', 262, NULL, 'rule', 262, '2026-10-15, task 262: 2026-10-15, task 262: tests/package.lisp (declaim notinline make-instance); docs/architecture.md ''Testing'' ''Time budget''; docs/luv-wishlist.md; AGENTS.md ''Commands'' (~13.6 s).');

